var e=JSON.parse(`[["In 1815 M. Myriel has been Bishop of Digne for nine years. He is the son of a magistrate of Aix, married young, and is said to have spent his early life in society and gallantry. The Revolution scattered his family; he emigrated to Italy, where his wife died, and he came back a priest. No one knows what changed him.","By 1804 he was curé of Brignolles. A parish errand took him to Paris, where, waiting in Cardinal Fesch's anteroom, he caught Napoleon's eye. Asked who the old man staring at him was, Myriel answered that the Emperor was looking at a good man and he at a great one, and each could profit by it. Shortly afterwards he was astonished to be made Bishop of Digne.","He arrived with his sister, Mademoiselle Baptistine, a thin, gentle spinster ten years younger than himself, and their only servant, Madame Magloire, a plump, bustling, asthmatic old woman. The town gossiped about his past for a while, then forgot it. Installed in the episcopal palace with due honours, he left Digne waiting to see what sort of bishop he would be."],["The episcopal palace is a huge stone mansion; the hospital beside it is a low, narrow building holding twenty-six patients in five or six small rooms. After visiting it, the Bishop summons the director, counts the mismatch aloud, and announces that there has been a mistake: the hospital has his house and he has theirs. The next day the patients move into the palace and the Bishop moves into the hospital.","He has no property of his own, so he draws up a household budget dividing his fifteen-thousand-franc salary among seminaries, missions, prisons, poor teachers, debtors' families and the poor, keeping one thousand livres for himself. Madame Magloire grumbles. When he is reminded that the department owes him a carriage allowance, he claims it, a senator writes an angry letter calling priests greedy, and the three thousand francs are promptly budgeted for hospital soup, foundlings and orphans.","Money soon flows through his hands from the rich to the poor, and none of it sticks. He taxes the wealthy heavily for episcopal fees and gives the proceeds away. The country people pick out the name among his baptismal names that means something to them and call him Monseigneur Bienvenu, Monseigneur Welcome. He likes it: Bienvenu, he says, makes up for the Monseigneur."],["The diocese of Digne is nearly all mountain and nearly roadless, with thirty-two curacies and hundreds of chapels. The Bishop visits them anyway, walking, riding a spring-cart or a donkey, with the two women when they can manage it and alone when they cannot. Arriving at Senez on an ass, he tells the scandalised mayor that he rides the animal Jesus used from necessity, not vanity.","On these rounds he talks rather than preaches. To cantons hard on the poor he describes Briançon, where widows and orphans mow first; to greedy villages, Embrun, where the parish harvests a sick man's field; to litigious ones, the valley of Queyras, where the mayor settles everything for nothing. To villages without schools he describes the travelling schoolmasters of Queyras, identified by the quill pens in their hats. When examples fail he invents parables, and because he believes what he says, he persuades."],["The Bishop laughs like a schoolboy and jokes about his short stature. He needles the Comtesse de Lô for cataloguing her sons' inheritances, sums up the miser Géborand's Sunday sou as purchasing paradise, and when the Marquis de Champtercier says he has poor of his own, replies, 'Give them to me.' He preaches on the window tax and the misery of peasant hovels, and speaks the southern dialects so that he can enter every house.","His rule is to examine the road a fault has travelled. He holds that falling is human and uprightness, not sainthood, is the rule, and that the faults of women, children and the poor are the faults of the strong and the rich; society that leaves souls in darkness is guilty of the sins committed there. Hearing a drawing-room praise a prosecutor who tricked a woman into denouncing her lover, a counterfeiter facing death, he asks where the prosecutor will be tried.","When the prison chaplain falls ill and the curé refuses to attend a murderer about to be executed, the Bishop goes himself, spends the whole day and night with the man, rides the tumbril and mounts the scaffold beside him, and sends him to his death consoled. Afterwards he tells his sister he has 'officiated pontifically', but the sight of the guillotine crushes him for a long time. He mutters that death belongs to God alone and thereafter avoids the place of execution.","He is tireless at deathbeds and in bereaved homes, knowing when to be silent and when to speak, seeking not to erase grief but to turn it toward hope."],["He sleeps little, meditates an hour, says mass, breakfasts on rye bread dipped in milk from his own cows, then works through the business of a diocese. Whatever time is left goes first to the sick and needy, then to what he calls 'gardening', whether digging, reading or writing. Around midday he walks through town in his purple cloak with his long cane, and children and old people come out to their doorsteps as for the sun. He visits the poor while he has money and the rich when he has none.","He wears the warm purple cloak even in summer so that no one will notice how long his cassocks have lasted. Supper with his sister is boiled vegetables and oil soup unless a visiting curé gives Madame Magloire an excuse for fish or game. Afterwards he writes: learned manuscripts, including a dissertation on the first verse of Genesis, and notes in the margins of whatever book he is reading, one of which lists the names Scripture gives to God and ends with Solomon's, Compassion, as the most beautiful. At nine the women go up to bed and leave him alone on the ground floor."],["The house has three rooms downstairs for the Bishop, a dining-room, bedroom and oratory in a row, with an alcove bed in the oratory for visiting curés, and three upstairs for the women. The old hospital pharmacy is the kitchen; a stable in the garden holds two cows, half of whose milk goes to the hospital every morning. In hard winters he sits in a boarded-off corner of the cowshed he calls his 'winter salon'. The furniture is white wood and straw chairs; money raised for a new altar always goes to the poor instead.","His bedroom holds an iron hospital bed, a bookcase, a cold painted chimney, a worn copper crucifix, a table heaped with papers and two portraits of long-dead priests he found there and left. Everything is whitewashed and scrupulously clean, the one luxury he permits because it takes nothing from the poor. He admits he still owns six silver knives and forks, a soup-ladle and two massive silver candlesticks inherited from a great-aunt; Madame Magloire locks the silver in a cupboard by his bed each night, but the key is never removed.","The garden has four box-edged plots: three for Madame Magloire's vegetables and one for the Bishop's flowers, which he defends as being as useful as the useful, 'more so, perhaps'. No door in the house can be locked. He had the bolts stripped from the street door, which is fastened only by a latch anyone can push. The women have learned to live with it. His notes explain him: the physician's door should never be shut, the priest's always open, and one should not ask the name of a man who asks for shelter. To a curé who worries for him he quotes the psalm: unless the Lord guard the house, they watch in vain who guard it."],["Cravatte, a lieutenant of the broken band of Gaspard Bès, has taken refuge in the mountains above Barcelonette and terrorises the countryside, even robbing the sacristy at Embrun. When the Bishop arrives at Chastelar on his rounds, the mayor begs him to turn back. The Bishop refuses any escort: the shepherds he has not seen for three years need to hear of God, and so, he adds, do the brigands. He is not in the world to guard his life but souls.","He crosses the mountain on a mule with a child for a guide, meets no one, and spends a fortnight with the shepherds. Wanting to end with a pontifical Te Deum, he finds the village sacristy has only threadbare chasubles. While they search, two unknown horsemen deposit a chest at the presbytery and ride off. It holds the gold cope, diamond mitre, cross and crosier stolen from Notre Dame d'Embrun, with a note: 'From Cravatte to Monseigneur Bienvenu.'","The curé murmurs 'God, or the Devil'; the Bishop repeats firmly, 'God!' Back at Chastelar he tells his sister he set out with only his faith and came back with a cathedral's treasure, and that evening he says that the real robbers are prejudices and the real murderers vices; a priest should take no precaution against his fellow man. Whether the treasure went back to the cathedral or to the hospital, Hugo declines to say, noting only a scrap among the Bishop's papers weighing the two."],["The senator who complained about the carriage allowance is a clever self-made man who has marched to his goal untroubled by conscience, and who likes to tease 'that good old fellow the Bishop'. At dessert, pleasantly tipsy, he proposes that senator and bishop are two augurs who cannot look at each other without winking, and confesses his philosophy.","It is a long speech: there is neither good nor evil, only vegetation; immortality is a fairy tale; man should eat rather than be eaten; after death comes total liquidation. Religion, he allows, is fine for the barefoot poor who have nothing else, but he reserves Naigeon and the philosophers for himself.","The Bishop claps his hands and praises this 'marvellous' materialism: how agreeable to feel irresponsible, to swallow places, sinecures and treacheries, and enter the tomb with one's digestion complete. It is a philosophy accessible only to the rich, he observes, who are good-natured enough to let belief in God be the people's philosophy, much as the goose stuffed with chestnuts is the truffled turkey of the poor."],["Writing to the Vicomtesse de Boischevron, Baptistine reports that Madame Magloire has discovered painted ceilings and old frescoes under the whitewash of their upstairs rooms, which will make her chamber 'a regular museum'. They are poor and cramped, she says, but she is always happy: her brother gives everything to the poor and sick.","She describes his ways: the door never fastened, walking out in rain and winter, fearing no road or encounter. Last year he went alone into a country of robbers and came back with a trunk of the cathedral jewels, saying 'This is the way I have been robbed!' She has stopped opposing him. She and Madame Magloire pray together, tremble together and fall asleep; if anything happened to him it would be the end of her. She answers the Vicomtesse's genealogical question about the Faux family and sends greetings.","Hugo adds that the women understand the Bishop better than he understands himself. When he is acting as a bishop they become two shadows in the house, never interfering by a word or a sign, and confide him to God."],["An old member of the Convention, G—, lives alone in a hidden valley near Digne, spoken of with horror as almost a regicide and an atheist. The Bishop feels the general estrangement himself but tells himself he owes the lonely soul a visit. When word comes that G— is dying, he sets out at sunset and finds a clean low hut, a white-haired man in a wheelchair smiling at the sun, and a shepherd boy offering him milk.","G— says he will die in three hours and sends the boy to bed. The Bishop, unexpectedly cold, begins severely: he congratulates G— on not having voted for the king's death. G— answers that he voted for the death of the tyrant ignorance, for the end of prostitution, slavery and the night of childhood. They spar over '93 and the guillotine, over Louis XVII and the brother of Cartouche, over Marat and Bossuet, over the crimes of the monarchy set against those of the Terror. G— apologises for using the Bishop's wealth against him in argument; the Bishop, when challenged as a prince of the Church, answers 'I am a worm'.","The Bishop's last defence is that progress should believe in God. G—, weeping, says 'O ideal, thou alone existest', and affirms the infinite as a person, a God. The Bishop, moved from coldness to emotion, takes his hand and asks whether it would not be regrettable to have met in vain. G— recounts a life of combat for the oppressed, of poverty and persecution, and asks what the Bishop wants. 'Your blessing,' says the Bishop, and kneels. When he lifts his head G— is dead.","He spends the night in prayer and afterwards points heavenward when asked about G—. From then on he redoubles his tenderness toward children and sufferers. The town's coteries mutter; to a dowager who asks when he will receive the red cap he replies that those who despise the colour in a cap revere it in a cardinal's hat."],["The meeting with G— left the Bishop more gentle, not a 'philosophical bishop'. Hugo turns to his attitude toward the events of the age. Napoleon had made him a baron and summoned him to the 1811 synod of bishops in Paris, but he attended only one sitting and went home, saying he had embarrassed those princes of the Church like an open door letting in outside air. He was shocked by their clocks, carpets and liveries, which cried to him of people who were hungry and cold. An opulent priest, Hugo agrees, is a contradiction.","The Bishop leaned ultramontane rather than gallican, and from 1813 applauded hostile manifestations against Napoleon, refused to see him on his return from Elba, and ordered no prayers for him during the Hundred Days. Of his two brothers, a general and a prefect, he was for a time harsh with the general for pursuing the Emperor after Cannes. Hugo judges that it would have been well had Monseigneur Bienvenu not been a royalist: one may resist power when it is rising, but to applaud a great man's fall is unworthy.","In everything else he was just, humble and kind, and tolerant even in politics. When the town-hall porter, an old guardsman of Austerlitz who scoffed at Louis XVIII and refused to wear a cross without the Emperor's face, lost his place, the Bishop reproved him gently and made him beadle of the cathedral. After nine years Digne loves its bishop even while it adores the emperor he snubbed."],["Influential bishops, the 'big mitres', are surrounded by young abbés hoping for parishes and prebends, and carry their satellites upward toward Rome. Monseigneur Bienvenu, poor and retiring, has none. His canons and vicars are plain old men walled up in the diocese like himself, and the young priests he ordains hurry off to be recommended to the archbishops of Aix or Auch. A saint living in abnegation is a dangerous neighbour who might infect you with incurable poverty.","Hugo then attacks success itself as a hideous thing whose false resemblance to merit deceives the crowd: the lucky man is called clever, the rich man great, and the world confuses the stars of heaven with the stars that ducks' feet make in a puddle."],["Hugo declines to examine the Bishop's orthodoxy: he believed to the extent of his powers and his faith never curdled into hypocrisy. What marked him was an excess of love that overflowed onto all creation. He had none of the harshness priests reserve for animals. His sister once heard him say of a hideous spider, 'Poor beast! It is not its fault!' and he sprained his ankle avoiding an ant. Stories of his youth suggest he had once been passionate, even violent; his gentleness was a conviction slowly worn into him like a hollow made by dripping water.","At seventy-five he looks sixty: short, plump, ruddy, with all his white teeth and a 'fine head' people forget is fine because he is so amiable. On first meeting he seems merely a good fellow; after a few hours in his company something august appears, like a smiling angel slowly unfolding its wings.","His day is full of prayer, offices, alms, consolation, gardening and study, but it is not complete without an hour or two in the garden at night after the women have gone to bed. Sitting on a bench against an old vine, he gazes at the stars past his stunted fruit trees, not studying God but dazzled by him. A little garden to walk in and immensity to dream in: flowers on earth and all the stars in the sky."],["Lest the preceding chapter make the Bishop sound like a solitary philosopher with a private religion, Hugo stresses that his wisdom came from his heart. No systems, many works. He would have feared the heights where Swedenborg and Pascal slipped into madness; he was not a genius and not a prophet. He simply loved.","The universe seemed to him an immense malady, and without trying to solve the enigma he strove to dress the wound. 'Love each other' was his whole doctrine. When the senator told him this was nonsense, he replied that if so the soul should shut itself up in it like the pearl in the oyster. He noted the exterior of the great mysteries without scrutinising them and kept a grave respect for darkness."],["An hour before sunset a thickset man of about forty-six walks into Digne, sunburnt, shaven-headed, in torn clothes, with a new soldier's knapsack and an enormous knotty stick. He reports at the town-hall, then goes to the best inn, the Cross of Colbas, and orders food and lodging, showing his purse. The landlord, Jacquin Labarre, sends a scullion to the town-hall with a note and on reading the reply tells him there is no room and no dinner. When the man insists, Labarre whispers that he knows his name, Jean Valjean, and what he is, and orders him out.","A humbler tavern in the Rue de Chaffaut takes him in until a fishmonger who refused him a ride that morning tips off the host, who throws him out too. Children stone him. The prison turnkey tells him to get himself arrested if he wants a bed. Peering into a cottage where a father dandles a child, he asks to pay for soup and a corner of the shed; the peasant, recognising 'the man', takes down his gun and bolts the door. He crawls into a sod hut for warmth and is driven out by the dog whose kennel it is. 'I am not even a dog!' he says.","He walks out into the fields, is turned back by the desolation of the night, re-enters through a breach in the walls, shakes his fist at the cathedral and lies down on a stone bench by a printing office. An old lady leaving the church, the Marquise de R—, asks what he is doing. He has had a mattress of wood for nineteen years, he says, and tonight one of stone; he has knocked at every door and been driven away. She gives him her four sous and points across the square to a small low house beside the Bishop's palace. 'Have you knocked at that one? Knock there.'"],["The Bishop has spent the evening on his unfinished treatise on Duties. At eight Madame Magloire fetches the silver from the cupboard by his bed, and he joins the women in the dining-room, where a fire burns and the table is laid. Hugo pauses to describe the two over-sixty women: Madame Magloire plump and peasant-like in her checked apron, Mademoiselle Baptistine slender and lady-like in a puce silk gown bought in 1806 and worn ever since.","Madame Magloire has heard in the shops that a barefoot vagabond with a terrible face, refused by Jacquin Labarre, is loose in the town, that the police are useless because the prefect and mayor are at odds, and that sensible people should bar their doors tonight. The Bishop warms himself and asks mildly whether they are in great danger. Encouraged, she asks leave to have the locksmith Paulin Musebois replace the old locks, since Monseigneur has a habit of saying 'come in' to anyone.","At that moment there is a heavy knock on the door. 'Come in,' says the Bishop."],["The door swings open and the man stands there with his knapsack and cudgel, hideous in the firelight. Madame Magloire cannot even cry out; Baptistine looks at her brother and grows calm. Before anyone can speak the man states his case in a loud voice: his name is Jean Valjean, he has spent nineteen years in the galleys, was freed four days ago, is walking to Pontarlier, has been refused everywhere because of his yellow passport, and has a hundred and nine francs fifteen sous to pay with. Will they let him stay?","'Madame Magloire, you will set another place,' says the Bishop. The man, thinking he has not been understood, unfolds the passport that calls him a very dangerous man. The Bishop orders white sheets on the alcove bed and invites him to sit and warm himself. Valjean is stupefied: a bed with a mattress and sheets, nineteen years since he slept in one, and this man calls him 'sir'. He takes the Bishop for an innkeeper, then for the curé, and asks what he must pay. Nothing, says the Bishop, and sighs when he hears that the money took nineteen years to earn.","The Bishop shuts the door, has the silver placed near the fire, and, remarking that the lamp gives poor light, has Madame Magloire bring the two silver candlesticks. Valjean says the priest does not despise him though he has hidden nothing. The Bishop touches his hand: this is not his house but the house of Jesus Christ, whose door asks whoever enters not whether he has a name but whether he has a grief; and he already knew his name, which is 'my brother'. Over soup, bacon, mutton, figs and the Bishop's old Mauves wine, he tells the convict that if he leaves the galleys with goodwill he is worthier than any of them, then notices something missing from the table: Madame Magloire brings out the other three sets of silver, as is the custom when a guest sups."],["Baptistine writes to Madame de Boischevron that the man ate like a starving creature and then remarked that the carters who had refused him kept a better table than the priest. Her brother answered that they were more tired than he. Told he must be too poor to be a real curé, he said the good God is more than just.","He asked whether Monsieur Jean Valjean was bound for Pontarlier and praised the region: paper mills, tanneries, foundries, and above all the cheese-dairies called fruitières, where the poor peasants pool their cows and hire a cheese-maker, the grurin. He returned to this comfortable trade again and again, as though to let the man see a refuge without advising him outright. He mentioned that his own family was ruined in the Revolution and that he once lived by the work of his hands in Franche-Comté.","What struck Baptistine was that her brother never said a word that could remind the man of what he was, never asked where he came from or what his history was, and once stopped short when a phrase about the innocent mountaineers might have wounded him. He supped with Jean Valjean exactly as he would have supped with the curate. Mother Gerbaud came to the door with her baby and the Bishop borrowed fifteen sous from his sister to give her. Then he said grace, told the man he must need his bed, and the women went up, sending Madame Magloire back down with an old goat skin from the Black Forest to keep him warm."],["After saying good night to his sister the Bishop takes one silver candlestick, gives the other to his guest, and leads him through his bedroom to the alcove in the oratory. As they cross the room Madame Magloire is locking the silver into the cupboard by the head of the bed, her last task every evening. A fresh white bed is ready; the Bishop promises warm milk from his cows before the man sets out.","Then, without transition, the man folds his arms, fixes a savage gaze on his host and asks with a monstrous laugh whether he has really thought about lodging a stranger so close to himself: how does he know he has not been an assassin? 'That is the concern of the good God,' says the Bishop. He raises two fingers in blessing over the man, who does not bow, and returns to his room without looking back.","He kneels briefly before the curtain that hides the altar, then walks and meditates in his garden until midnight. The convict, too tired to use the white sheets, snuffs the candle with his nostrils as convicts do and drops onto the bed fully dressed. Soon everyone in the little house is asleep."],["Jean Valjean came from a poor peasant family of Brie and never learned to read. Orphaned early, he was raised by an older sister, and when her husband died he, at twenty-five, took the father's place and fed her seven children by pruning trees, haymaking and any labour he could get. One hard winter in 1795 there was no work and no bread. One Sunday evening he smashed the grating of Maubert Isabeau's bakery at Faverolles and seized a loaf; the baker caught him with his arm still bleeding.","He was convicted of burglary at night and, his poaching counting against him, sentenced to five years in the galleys. Chained at Bicêtre in April 1796, he wept and could only say 'I was a tree-pruner at Faverolles', raising his hand seven times as if touching seven heads. At Toulon he became number 24,601. His sister and the children vanished from his life; once, in his fourth year, he heard that she was in Paris with only the youngest boy, who waited an hour in the cold each winter morning for a school to open. He never heard of them again.","In his fourth year he escaped and was recaptured after two days: three more years. In the sixth year another attempt, with resistance: five more. In the tenth, three more; in the thirteenth, four hours of freedom cost three years more. Nineteen years in all for a pane of glass and a loaf of bread. He went in sobbing and came out impassive; he went in despairing and came out gloomy. What had happened in that soul?"],["In the galleys Valjean made himself his own tribunal. He admitted his theft was wrong and foolish. But he also asked whether a willing labourer should have lacked bread, whether nineteen years was not a monstrous overweight on the scale of punishment, whether society had the right to crush a man first by its lack of foresight and then by its pitiless foresight. He judged society and condemned it to his hatred; then he judged Providence, which made society, and condemned that too. Light entered his soul on one side and darkness on the other.","He went to the convicts' school at forty and learned to read, write and cipher, feeling that to strengthen his mind was to strengthen his hate. Hugo asks whether a soul can be wholly deformed by misfortune or whether some incorruptible spark remains, and admits Valjean himself could not have mapped his own state: he hated in the shadows, in advance of himself, with flashes of rage that lit the precipices of his destiny. His repeated, useless escapes were the instinct of a wolf finding its cage open.","He was the strongest man in the galleys, worth four men at the capstan, nicknamed Jean the Jackscrew after holding up a loosened caryatid of the Toulon town-hall balcony with his shoulder. He was even more supple than strong, able to climb a wall's angle to a third storey by back, elbows and heels. He rarely spoke and never laughed, conscious of a vast pyramid of laws, prejudices and men, the Emperor shining at its summit, pressing down on him. By the time he left he was capable of sudden instinctive reprisals and of cold, reasoned evil, and his passport's 'very dangerous man' was not unearned. He had not shed a tear in nineteen years."],["A man falls overboard and the vessel does not halt. He surfaces, calls, waves; no one sees him. The waves toss and half devour him, the birds sing uselessly above him like angels over human distress, night falls, and at last, exhausted, he lets go and sinks.","Hugo names the image: the sea is the inexorable social night into which the penal laws fling the condemned, the immensity of wretchedness. The soul going down in that gulf may become a corpse. Who shall resuscitate it?"],["When he heard 'Thou art free', a ray of true light entered him, but it soon paled. He had reckoned his nineteen years of earnings at a hundred and seventy-one francs; after deductions for Sundays and holidays and local levies he was handed a hundred and nine francs fifteen sous, and understood none of it. He felt robbed.","The day after his release, unloading bales at an orange-flower distillery in Grasse, he worked well and a gendarme checked his yellow passport. In the evening the master handed him fifteen sous instead of the thirty the other men earned; when he protested, the master looked him in the eye and said, 'Beware of the prison.' The State had robbed him wholesale, now an individual robbed him at retail. One gets free from the galleys, Hugo observes, but not from the sentence. Then came Digne."],["The cathedral clock strikes two and Valjean wakes, disturbed by the novelty of a bed. He cannot sleep again. Memories old and new churn in his mind, but one thought drives out the rest: the six sets of silver and the ladle he saw Madame Magloire put in the little cupboard by the head of the Bishop's bed, on the right as you enter from the dining-room. Solid old silver; the ladle alone worth two hundred francs, double what he earned in nineteen years.","For an hour his mind wavers, not without some struggle. At three he sits up, removes his shoes and sets them softly on the mat, and sits motionless, oddly haunted by the checked suspender of a convict named Brevet he once knew. When the clock strikes the quarter he rises, examines the window, which opens on the garden with only a pin for a fastening, and notes the low white wall beyond.","He takes from his knapsack a short pointed iron bar, a miner's candlestick of the kind convicts quarrying stone around Toulon could get hold of, pockets his shoes, shoulders his knapsack and pulls down his cap, sets his cudgel by the window, and creeps toward the Bishop's bedroom with the iron in his right hand. The door is ajar; the Bishop has not closed it."],["He pushes the door with a fingertip; it yields silently, then on a third push a rusty hinge shrieks like the trumpet of Judgment. He stands petrified for minutes, certain the whole house must wake. Nothing stirs. He steps in among the shadowy papers and folios and hears the Bishop's even breathing.","At the bed he stops short. A cloud parts and moonlight falls on the Bishop's face as he sleeps almost dressed against the cold, his ringed hand hanging over the edge, his whole face lit with hope and confidence, like an infant's slumber on an old man's head. Valjean stands in the shadow with the iron candlestick, frightened by this luminous old man; the confidence terrifies him. He seems about to crush that skull or kiss that hand. Slowly he takes off his cap and stands meditating, cap in one hand, club in the other, while the crucifix over the chimney stretches its arms over both of them.","Then he jams his cap back on, strides past the bed to the cupboard, raises the iron to force it, finds the key in the lock, seizes the basket of silver, crosses the room without caring about noise, climbs out of the oratory window with his cudgel, stuffs the silver into his knapsack, throws the basket away, crosses the garden, leaps the wall like a tiger and flees."],["At sunrise the Bishop is in his garden when Madame Magloire runs up asking where the silver basket is. He has just found it, empty, in a flowerbed where it crushed a plant. She cries that the man has stolen the silver and points to the torn coping where he went over the wall into Cochefilet Lane. The Bishop asks gently whether the silver was ever theirs: he had kept it wrongfully for a long time; it belonged to the poor, and the man was evidently a poor man. When she asks what Monseigneur will eat with, he proposes pewter, then iron, then wood, and at breakfast remarks cheerfully that one needs no spoon to dip bread in milk.","As they rise from the table there is a knock. Three gendarmes hold a fourth man by the collar: Jean Valjean. The brigadier addresses the Bishop as 'Monseigneur', and Valjean, stupefied, murmurs, 'So he is not the curé?' The Bishop hurries forward: 'Ah! here you are! I am glad to see you. But I gave you the candlesticks too, which are silver like the rest. Why did you not carry them away with your forks and spoons?' Valjean stares at him with an expression no tongue can render. The gendarmes, who stopped a man walking like one running away, are told it was a mistake and release him.","The Bishop fetches the two candlesticks from the chimneypiece and puts them in the trembling man's hands, adding that next time he need not come through the garden, since the street door is never fastened with anything but a latch. He dismisses the gendarmes. Then, low, he tells Valjean never to forget that he has promised to use this money to become an honest man. Valjean, who remembers no promise, is speechless. 'Jean Valjean, my brother, you no longer belong to evil, but to good. It is your soul that I buy from you; I withdraw it from black thoughts and the spirit of perdition, and I give it to God.'"],["Valjean leaves Digne as if fleeing, retracing his steps all morning without eating, torn between rage and an emotion he resists; he almost wishes the gendarmes had kept him. Toward sunset he sits behind a bush on a deserted plain some three leagues from Digne. A boy of about ten comes singing up the path, a Savoyard with a hurdy-gurdy and a marmot-box, tossing his coins. A forty-sou piece rolls to Valjean, who sets his foot on it.","The child asks politely for his money and gives his name, Little Gervais. Valjean tells him to go away. The boy pleads, cries, tugs at the iron-shod shoe; Valjean seems not to hear, then reaches for his cudgel and roars 'Who's there?' and 'Will you take yourself off!' The terrified child runs. Only after the sun has set does Valjean see the coin shining in the earth. He recoils as if from an open eye, snatches it up and runs after the boy, shouting 'Little Gervais!' across the darkening plain. He meets a priest on horseback, presses ten francs on him for the poor, asks after the child, then begs 'have me arrested, I am a thief'; the priest spurs away.","At a crossroads he shouts one last time, collapses on a stone with his face on his knees, cries 'I am a wretch!' and weeps for the first time in nineteen years. Hugo explains that the Bishop's pardon had been the greatest assault his hardness had ever suffered; he had resisted it with pride, half aware that he must now rise higher than the Bishop or fall lower than the convict. The theft from the child was not the man but the beast acting by habit, and it was a thing he was no longer capable of. Seeing it, he saw himself: the convict Jean Valjean standing before him like a stranger, and behind him a light that took a human form and was the Bishop. As he wept, the Bishop grew and Valjean shrank until only the Bishop remained.","What he did afterwards no one knows. Only the Grenoble carrier, passing through Digne at three in the morning, saw a man kneeling in prayer on the pavement in the dark before the door of Monseigneur Welcome."],["Hugo piles up the trivia of 1817: Louis XVIII counting it the twenty-second year of his reign, hairdressers' shops painted with fleurs-de-lys, the army dressed in Austrian white, Napoleon at Saint Helena turning his old coats, the rotting columns of the Emperor's Champ de Mai platform, the Medusa still unreported, Chateaubriand dictating at his window while cleaning his teeth, a useless toy called a steamboat puffing under the Tuileries windows, Saint-Simon and Fourier unnoticed, Byron introduced to France as 'a certain Lord Baron'.","Through it runs the royalist settling of scores: regicides and Bonapartists hounded, turncoats strutting, journals insulting exiles, six months in prison for a seditious joke. Hugo defends these forgotten details: there are no trivial facts in humanity, and the physiognomy of the centuries is made of the physiognomy of the years. In this year, he ends, four young Parisians arranged 'a fine farce'."],["The students are provincials who have become Parisians: Tholomyès of Toulouse, Listolier of Cahors, Fameuil of Limoges and Blachevelle of Montauban, four ordinary handsome young men of twenty. Each has a mistress: Blachevelle loves Favorite, so called because she has been to England; Listolier adores Dahlia; Fameuil idolises Zéphine; and Tholomyès has Fantine, called the Blonde for her sunny hair. The girls are working-women not yet wholly divorced from their needles. Favorite, Dahlia and Zéphine are experienced and 'philosophical'; Fantine, the youngest, is still in her first illusions and is the only one none of the men addresses as 'thou'.","Hugo sketches the others' families and falls: Favorite's brutal mathematician father and the pious old woman who installed herself as her mother; Dahlia drawn to idleness by nails too pretty to work with; Zéphine's roguish 'Yes, sir'. Fantine has no family at all. Born in Montreuil-sur-Mer under the Directory, she never knew father or mother, was given her name by a passer-by, went into service at ten and came to Paris at fifteen to seek her fortune. Beautiful and pure as long as she could be, she worked for her living and then, because the heart too has its hunger, she loved Tholomyès: an amour for him, a passion for her.","Tholomyès is thirty, rich on four thousand francs a year, already wrinkled, toothless and balding, with a weeping eye, but all the gayer for it; his universal doubt makes him the group's leader. One day he takes the other three aside: the girls have been begging for a surprise for a year, and their parents keep writing. He whispers something that makes all four grin, and the result is a pleasure party the following Sunday."],["Hugo notes how much the outskirts of Paris have changed since 1817, then follows the party through all the country follies of the day: up at five, by coach to Saint-Cloud to admire the dry cascade, breakfast at the Tête-Noir, ring-throwing, Diogenes' lantern, roulette for macaroons at the Pont de Sèvres, bouquets, reed-pipes and apple tarts everywhere. The girls chatter like escaped warblers. An old poet seeing them pass exclaims that there is one Grace too many.","Favorite, the eldest at twenty-three, leads the romp; Zéphine and Dahlia cling together in English poses; Listolier and Fameuil discuss their professors to Fantine; Blachevelle carries Favorite's shawl; Tholomyès dominates in elephant-leg nankeen trousers, with a two-hundred-franc rattan and a cigar.","Fantine is a joy to behold: splendid teeth made for laughter, thick blond hair that will not stay up, a mauve barege gown and a muslin canezou that hides and reveals at once while the others wear low necks without disguise. Hugo dwells on her sculptural beauty, her deep blue eyes and arched feet, and above all on the reserve and chaste astonishment that separate Psyche from Venus. Though she would refuse Tholomyès nothing, her face in repose is virginal, and gravity can fall over her gaiety without transition. Love is a fault, Hugo concedes; Fantine was innocence floating high over fault."],["Nature itself seems on holiday at Saint-Cloud. The couples run, sing, chase butterflies and exchange kisses freely, except Fantine, hedged by her dreamy wildness and in love. 'You always have a queer look about you,' Favorite tells her. Hugo rhapsodises on the fairy who made fields and forests for lovers, and on Watteau, Lancret and Diderot gazing at such idylls.","After breakfast they view the newly arrived Indian shrub drawing all Paris to Saint-Cloud, then Tholomyès hires asses and they ride back by Vanvres and Issy. At Issy they find Bourguin's park open, visit the manikin hermit and the cabinet of mirrors, and shake the swing between two chestnut trees; Tholomyès pushes each beauty in turn and sings the old ballad 'Gallega' in Spanish. Fantine alone will not swing, and Favorite mutters at her airs.","They cross the Seine by boat, walk from Passy to the Étoile barrier, and about three o'clock, frightened at their own happiness, slide down the Russian mountains on the heights of Beaujon. From time to time Favorite demands the surprise. 'Patience,' says Tholomyès."],["Worn out, the eight strand themselves at Bombarda's branch restaurant in the Champs-Élysées, in a large ugly room with an alcove and a bed, two windows over the quay and river, a mountain of bouquets and hats on one table and the debris of dinner on the other. The idyll begun at five in the morning has reached half past four in the afternoon.","Outside, the Champs-Élysées are light and dust: the horses of Marly, bodyguards with clarions, the white flag over the Tuileries, the Place de la Concorde renamed Place Louis XV and full of promenaders wearing silver fleurs-de-lys, girls singing the Bourbon air. Hugo quotes the police chief Anglès assuring the King that the suburban populace is heedless, puny and harmless, and answers him: this despised cat can become a lion, and the little man of the faubourgs, given a pike or a gun, has made the 10th of August and Austerlitz. Then he returns to the couples, whose dinner is drawing to its close."],["Fameuil and Dahlia hum, Tholomyès drinks, Zéphine laughs, Fantine smiles, Listolier blows a wooden trumpet bought at Saint-Cloud. Favorite gazes at Blachevelle and declares she adores him; asked what she would do if he stopped loving her, she promises to scratch him, throw him in the water and have him arrested. Blachevelle swells with vanity.","In a low voice Dahlia asks whether she really idolises him. 'I detest him,' says Favorite; he is stingy. She loves the little fellow across the way, an actor's son who declaims in the garrets and earns twenty sous a day copying for an attorney, and who once offered to eat her gloves as fritters. 'How I lie!' she says. She is sad besides: it has rained all summer, butter is dear, and dining in a room with a bed in it disgusts her with life."],["Amid the uproar Tholomyès calls for deliberation: no haste, no zeal, let them mingle majesty with the feast. Shouted down, he recovers his empire with a pun and then defends puns as the droppings of the soaring mind, before preaching limits in everything, even apple turnovers, and the wisdom of the man who knows when to effect his own arrest. Favorite admires his name Félix, which means prosperous.","He rattles on: a recipe of lemonade, hard labour and cold baths for doing without love; distrust woman, who hates the serpent out of professional jealousy; glory to wine, with the liquid measures of Spain by way of proof. He addresses each girl in turn, praising Favorite's lips and advising Dahlia to call herself Rosa. Of Fantine he says she is a dreamer, a phantom with a nymph's form and a nun's modesty strayed into a grisette's life, gazing into the azure without knowing what she sees: 'I, Tholomyès, I am an illusion; but she does not even hear me.' He warns the girls not to marry, not to eat sugar, and urges the men to steal each other's mistresses without remorse.","The other three answer with a nonsense studio song about Clermont-Tonnerre failing to be made pope. Tholomyès drains and refills his glass and ends with a toast to mirth and folly, to summer, to the Luxembourg and the Odéon arcades: 'Embrace me, Fantine!' He makes a mistake and embraces Favorite."],["Zéphine prefers the dinners at Édon's, Blachevelle the silver knife handles at Bombarda's; Tholomyès, gazing at the dome of the Invalides, remarks on those with a silver chin. Asked whether he prefers Descartes or Spinoza he answers 'Désaugiers', praises the Madeira from three hundred and seventeen fathoms above the sea, and launches into Bombarda, Egypt, Greece and Aspasia, the goddess-prostitute who was Socrates plus Manon Lescaut.","He is stopped only by a horse falling on the quay in front of the restaurant: an old, thin Beauceron mare dragging a heavy cart, which refuses to go further, takes a cut of the whip and falls never to rise. Tholomyès closes with a melancholy quatrain on the jade's life. 'Poor horse!' sighs Fantine, and Dahlia mocks her for crying over horses.","Favorite folds her arms and demands the surprise. The moment has come, says Tholomyès; the ladies must wait a moment. It begins with a kiss on the brow, and each man gravely kisses his mistress's forehead; then all four file out with their fingers on their lips. Favorite claps: it is beginning to be amusing. 'Don't be too long,' murmurs Fantine. 'We are waiting for you.'"],["Left alone, the four girls lean from the windows and watch the men leave the café arm in arm, wave, and vanish into the Sunday crowd. They speculate on the present; Favorite wants it to be gold. Their attention drifts to the mail-coaches and diligences thundering down the Champs-Élysées toward the Passy barrier in sparks and dust. One halts for a moment and sets off again at a gallop, which surprises Fantine; Favorite explains loftily that travellers often arrange to be picked up on the quay.","After a long wait the waiter brings a paper the gentlemen told him not to deliver for an hour. Favorite reads it aloud. 'Our Beloved': they have parents who implore their prodigal sons' return; being virtuous, they obey. At this moment five horses are carrying them to their papas and mammas by the Toulouse diligence, away from the abyss, which is the girls. They return to society and duty to become prefects and fathers of families; the girls should mourn them in haste and replace them with speed. For nearly two years they have made them happy and bear them no grudge. Signed Blachevelle, Fameuil, Listolier, Félix Tholomyès. Postscript: the dinner is paid for.","The girls look at one another. Favorite pronounces it a very pretty farce, decides it must have been Blachevelle's idea and is in love with him for it; Dahlia says it was Tholomyès's, and they cheer him and burst out laughing. Fantine laughs with the rest. An hour later, back in her room, she weeps. It was her first love; she had given herself to Tholomyès as to a husband, and the poor girl has a child."],["Spring 1818, Montfermeil, near Paris. Outside a cook-shop called the Sergeant of Waterloo, kept by a couple named Thénardier, two little girls sit laughing in the loop of a huge rusted chain hung under a wrecked cart, swung by their mother, who sings as she rocks them. A young woman with a heavy carpet-bag and a sleeping child in her arms stops to watch. It is Fantine, worn and poorly dressed, though still beautiful under the plain cap.","The narrator fills in the ten months since the picnic. Deserted, Fantine lost her friends Favorite, Zéphine and Dahlia along with the men; her letters to Tholomyès went unanswered. She can barely read and cannot write. Unable to find work in Paris and unwilling to be known as an unmarried mother, she decided to go back to her native Montreuil-sur-Mer, sold everything for about two hundred francs, dressed herself in linen and put every scrap of finery on her child, and set out at twenty-two with eighty francs and a small cough. Hugo dismisses Tholomyès for good: he became a rich provincial lawyer and a stern juryman.","Fantine sits down with the innkeeper's wife, who introduces herself as Madame Thénardier, and tells a half-true story of a dead husband and lost work. Her child, Cosette (really Euphrasie), wakes and is soon digging in the dirt with the other two. When Madame Thénardier says the three look like sisters, Fantine seizes her hand and asks her to keep Cosette for her. A man's voice from inside the shop drives the bargain: seven francs a month, six months paid in advance, fifteen francs more for expenses, fifty-seven francs in all, and the child's fine wardrobe handed over. Fantine pays nearly everything she has, sleeps at the inn, and leaves the next morning weeping in the street.","Once she has gone, Thénardier tells his wife the money will cover a note for a hundred and ten francs due the next day; her children, he says, made a good mousetrap. \\"Without suspecting it,\\" she answers."],["The narrator pauses to say who the Thénardiers are, promising to complete the portrait later. They belong to a bastard class of coarse people who have risen a little and clever people who have sunk, combining the vices of both. Small natures that turn monstrous if anything warms them; souls that walk backwards into darkness. Thénardier is a man one distrusts on sight, shadowy behind and threatening in front. He says he was a sergeant in the 1815 campaign and painted his inn sign himself to commemorate a feat of arms; the narrator pointedly reserves judgement on how much of this is true.","Madame Thénardier is twelve or fifteen years younger than her husband and just literate enough to devour the sentimental romances then setting Paris portresses aflame. That reading gave her a languishing manner over a coarse frame, a Pamela with a Megaera underneath. It also named the children: the elder is Éponine, the younger narrowly escaped Gulnare and is called Azelma. Hugo closes with a note that this anarchy of baptismal names, which gives the ploughboy an Arthur and the viscount a Jacques, is one small eddy of the Revolution's levelling."],["The inn is failing. Fantine's fifty-seven francs pay off Thénardier's note, but the next month they pawn Cosette's wardrobe for sixty francs, and once that is spent they treat her as a charity child: cast-off rags, leftovers eaten from a wooden bowl under the table beside the dog and cat. Her mother, settled at Montreuil-sur-Mer, has a letter written every month and is always told Cosette is doing wonderfully. Before the first year is out Thénardier demands twelve francs instead of seven; later, having somehow learned that the child is illegitimate and that the mother cannot afford exposure, he raises it to fifteen with a threat to send the child back. Fantine pays each time.","Madame Thénardier adores her own daughters and so hates the stranger, who absorbs the blows that would otherwise fall on them. Éponine and Azelma copy their mother. The village thinks the Thénardiers generous for raising an abandoned child and assumes the mother has forgotten her. Before she is five Cosette is sweeping, carrying and running errands, and her mother's payments have begun to fall behind.","After three years the rosy child has become thin, pale and uneasy, with nothing left of her prettiness but her large sad eyes. Not yet six, she sweeps the street in rags before daylight in winter. The neighbourhood calls her the Lark, because she is small, trembling and awake before anyone else; only this lark never sings."],["Book V opens by turning from Montfermeil to the mother. Fantine, who left her province ten years before, finds Montreuil-sur-Mer prosperous. The town's old industry, imitation jet and black glass trinkets, had always languished because the raw materials were dear. About the end of 1815 an unknown man settled there and made two small changes, gum-lac for resin and simply folded sheet-iron for soldered slides, which cut costs, raised wages, improved the goods and tripled profits at once.","Within three years the inventor was rich and had made everyone around him rich. Nothing is known of his origins; he arrived with a few hundred francs at most, in a workman's clothes and speech. The story goes that on the December evening he walked into town, knapsack on his back and thorn club in hand, the town hall caught fire and he ran into the flames to save the two children of the captain of gendarmes, so no one thought to ask for his passport. Afterwards they learned his name: Father Madeleine."],["Madeleine is about fifty, preoccupied and good. His factory has two large workrooms, one for men and one for women, kept strictly apart; he asks good will of the men, pure morals of the women and honesty of all, and is inflexible on that one point in a garrison town full of temptation. Anyone hungry can find work there. Slack seasons and misery vanish from the district.","He seems to care little for his own fortune. By 1820 he has six hundred and thirty thousand francs with the banker Laffitte, but has first spent over a million on the town: six hospital beds, two new schools with doubled salaries for the teachers (\\"the nurse and the schoolmaster\\" being, he says, the two chief functionaries of the state), an infant school, a fund for old workmen, a free dispensary in the new quarter that grew up around the factory.","The town's gossips explain him in turn as a fortune-hunter, an ambitious man and an adventurer. He goes to early mass every Sunday, which alarms the local deputy into a rival show of piety that happens to add six more hospital beds. In 1819 the King names him mayor on the prefect's recommendation; he refuses. The same year his process wins him the cross of the Legion of Honour; he refuses that too. Society's drawing-rooms open to the millionaire; he declines them, and is called an ignorant brute. In 1820 the King appoints him mayor again and the whole town begs him to accept. He gives in, apparently moved by an old woman who scolds him from her doorstep for shrinking from the good he could do. Father Madeleine has become Monsieur Madeleine, and Monsieur Madeleine becomes Monsieur le Maire."],["Madeleine remains as simple as on his first day: grey hair, a sunburnt labourer's face, a wide-brimmed hat and a long coat buttoned to the chin. Apart from his duties he lives alone, eats with a book open before him, and his speech grows more polished year by year. He carries a gun on his walks but rarely uses it; when he does, his aim is unnervingly perfect, and he never shoots a harmless creature. Though no longer young he is thought enormously strong, lifting horses and freeing stuck wheels. He goes out with full pockets and comes home with empty ones.","He knows country secrets, how to keep weevils and scurf off wheat, how to guard a rabbit warren with the smell of a guinea-pig, and lectures some villagers on the uses of the nettle they are uprooting, ending: there are no bad plants or bad men, only bad cultivators. He seeks out funerals as other men seek christenings. He slips into poor men's attics at night, sometimes forcing the door, and leaves a gold piece behind.","Rumour makes his bedroom a hermit's cave of skulls and hourglasses. When some mocking young women ask to see the \\"grotto\\", he shows them at once: ugly mahogany, cheap wallpaper, and nothing remarkable but two antique candlesticks on the chimneypiece that appear to be silver, since they are hallmarked. Rumour also credits him with millions on instant call at Laffitte's; the truth is some six hundred and thirty or forty thousand francs."],["The newspapers announce the death of M. Myriel, Bishop of Digne, at eighty-two, in the odour of sanctity. The narrator adds a detail the papers left out: the Bishop had been blind for years and content to be so, with his sister at his side. There follows a long meditation on blindness and being loved, on hearing a sister's step and feeling a hand on your brow and knowing yourself the centre of another soul's care; from that paradise, Hugo says, Monseigneur Welcome passed to the other.","The local paper reprints the notice. The next day M. Madeleine is dressed wholly in black with crape on his hat. The town's little aristocracy concludes he must be related to the Bishop, and his standing rises at once. When a dowager asks whether he is the late Bishop's cousin, he says no; pressed about the mourning, he answers that he was a servant in the Bishop's family in his youth.","People also notice that whenever a young Savoyard boy wanders through town looking for chimneys to sweep, the mayor sends for him, asks his name, and gives him money. The little Savoyards tell one another, and a great many come that way."],["Opposition to Madeleine fades into universal respect; people travel ten leagues to have him settle their disputes. One man escapes the contagion. A tall figure in an iron-grey coat with a heavy cane and battered hat turns to stare after the mayor in the street with a grimace that says: I have seen that man somewhere, and I am not his dupe. His name is Javert; he is the town's police inspector, posted there after Madeleine's fortune was already made, through the protection of M. Chabouillet, secretary to the Paris prefect of police.","Hugo describes him through his theory that every man carries an animal inside him: Javert is the dog born in a litter of wolves. Born in prison to a fortune-teller whose husband was in the galleys, he grew up feeling outside society and saw only two ways back, to attack it or to guard it. He chose the police, served in his youth as a guard in the southern convict prisons, and is an inspector at forty. His face is a flat nose, deep nostrils and huge whiskers; serious, he is a watchdog, laughing, a tiger.","He is made of two feelings pushed to excess: respect for authority and hatred of rebellion, under which he files every crime. The functionary can never be wrong; the convict can never be redeemed. He would arrest his own father and denounce his mother and feel virtuous doing it. He has no vices, reads although he hates books, and allows himself a pinch of snuff when pleased with himself. Vagrants are petrified by his name.","Javert's eye rests constantly on M. Madeleine, who notices and treats him with the same courtesy as everyone else. From words Javert lets slip it is guessed that he has quietly searched the mayor's past elsewhere and knows that someone gathered information in a certain district about a family that had disappeared. Once he says to himself, \\"I think I have him!\\" and then is silent for three days, as if the thread had broken. Madeleine's calm disconcerts him, until one day something happens."],["Passing through an unpaved lane one morning, M. Madeleine finds a crowd around an accident. Father Fauchelevent's horse has fallen with two broken legs and the old man lies caught beneath his heavily laden cart, the whole weight on his chest. Fauchelevent, a former notary and near-educated peasant, had watched this simple workman grow rich while he went bankrupt, hated him for it and tried to injure him at every turn; with nothing left but a horse and cart he had become a carter. Javert, already on the scene, has sent for a jackscrew from the farrier, a quarter of an hour away.","The cart is sinking into the rain-soaked ground and will crush the old man's ribs in five minutes. Madeleine offers five louis, then ten, then twenty to anyone who will crawl under and lift it with his back. No one moves. A voice says it is not will that is lacking but strength: Javert, watching the mayor fixedly, says he has only ever known one man able to do such a thing, a convict in the galleys at Toulon. Madeleine shudders and turns pale.","Fauchelevent screams that he is strangling. Madeleine meets Javert's eye, looks at the motionless peasants, smiles sadly, and drops to his knees under the cart. Twice he fails; the crowd and Fauchelevent himself beg him to come out. Then the mass quivers and rises, twenty arms seize it, and the old man is dragged free. Madeleine stands, pale, muddy and dripping, while Fauchelevent kisses his knees and calls him the good God. His tranquil eye rests on Javert, who is still staring at him."],["Fauchelevent's kneecap is dislocated. Madeleine has him nursed in the infirmary he keeps in his factory, and the next morning the old man finds a thousand-franc note on his bedside table with the words \\"I purchase your horse and cart\\", though the horse is dead and the cart wrecked. His knee stays stiff, and on the recommendation of the sisters and the priest Madeleine gets him a place as gardener in a women's convent in the Rue Saint-Antoine in Paris.","Some time later Madeleine is made mayor. The first time Javert sees him in the mayor's scarf he feels the shudder of a watchdog that smells a wolf in its master's clothes. From then on he avoids him and, when duty forces a meeting, addresses him with profound respect. Hugo adds an invisible sign of the town's prosperity: in seven years the cost of collecting taxes in the arrondissement falls by three quarters, and the finance minister Villèle cites it as a model.","Into this town Fantine returns. No one remembers her. The door of M. Madeleine's factory is like a friend's face; she is admitted to the women's workroom, learns a trade wholly new to her, earns little, but enough. She is making her living."],["Earning her bread honestly, Fantine is briefly almost happy. She buys a mirror, rents a little room, furnishes it on credit, and pays the Thénardiers promptly, writing through a public letter-writer since she can only sign her name. The letters are noticed. Hugo digresses on the idle who spy on their neighbours for the pure itch of knowing, at great cost to themselves and often ruinous to others.","Fantine is watched, envied for her golden hair and white teeth, seen wiping away tears at her bench. Her letters go twice a month to a Monsieur Thénardier, innkeeper at Montfermeil; the letter-writer is made tipsy and talks; it comes out that she has a child. An old woman makes the journey and reports, for her thirty-five francs, \\"I have seen the child.\\" She is Madame Victurnien, fifty-six, widow of a monk who left his cloister in a red cap in '93, soured by him and turned fierce bigot at the Restoration, in favour with the bishop's palace at Arras.","More than a year after Fantine joined the factory, the superintendent of the women's workroom hands her fifty francs from the mayor, tells her she is dismissed, and asks her, in the mayor's name, to leave the neighbourhood. It is the very month the Thénardiers raise their price from twelve to fifteen francs. In debt for rent and furniture, Fantine cannot leave. Overwhelmed with shame, she does not dare go to the mayor; he gave fifty francs because he is good and dismissed her because he is just, she thinks, and she bows to it."],["The mayor almost never enters the women's workroom and has given full authority to its superintendent, a respectable elderly spinster recommended by the priest, who has the charity that gives but not the charity that understands and forgives. She tried, condemned and dismissed Fantine convinced she was doing right, and paid the fifty francs out of a relief fund Madeleine had entrusted to her without asking for accounts.","Fantine seeks work as a servant and is refused everywhere. The second-hand dealer threatens to have her arrested as a thief if she leaves; her landlord tells her she is young and pretty and can pay. She divides the fifty francs between them, gives back most of the furniture, and is left with a bed, no trade and fifty francs of debt. She sews coarse shirts for the garrison at twelve sous a day while her child costs ten, and begins to pay the Thénardiers irregularly.","Her neighbour Marguerite, a pious old spinster who lights her candle for her at night, teaches her the art of poverty: no fire in winter, a petticoat made from a coverlet and a coverlet from a petticoat, meals eaten by the light of the window opposite. Fantine regains a little courage and thinks of sending for Cosette, but cannot share such destitution or pay what she owes or the fare.","At first too ashamed to go out, she feels the town's scorn like a north wind when she does. After two or three months she decides it is all the same to her and walks with her head up and a bitter smile, growing brazen. Madame Victurnien watches her from her window and congratulates herself. The work wears Fantine out; her dry cough grows and her hands are hot. Only combing her beautiful hair in the morning still gives her a moment of pleasure."],["A second winter brings short days and less work. The Thénardiers write that little Cosette is naked in the cold and needs ten francs for a woollen skirt. That evening Fantine goes to a barber, lets down hair that falls to her knees, and sells it for ten francs. She sends a knitted petticoat; the Thénardiers, who wanted money, give it to Éponine, and the Lark goes on shivering. Fantine tells herself her child is clothed in her hair.","Dark thoughts take hold. She comes to hate everyone, and most of all Father Madeleine, whom she blames for everything, and laughs and sings when she passes the factory. She takes a lover out of bravado, a beggar musician who beats her and leaves her. Her cough does not leave her. Then a letter: Cosette has a miliary fever, the drugs are ruinous, send forty francs within the week or the child dies. Fantine laughs wildly at the impossible sum, yet keeps going back to the staircase window to reread it.","In the square a travelling quack dentist offers her a gold napoleon apiece for her two front teeth. She flees in horror, then that evening is seen turning towards the inns. Next morning Marguerite finds her sitting on her bed, grown ten years older, with two napoleons glittering on the table and a black hole in her bloody smile. She sends the forty francs. Cosette was never ill; it was a trick to get money.","Fantine throws away her mirror, moves to an attic under the roof, loses her coquetry, stops mending her clothes. A prison contractor undercuts prices and her seventeen-hour days now earn nine sous. Creditors harass her on the stairs; she feels hunted and something of the wild beast grows in her. Thénardier writes that he must have a hundred francs at once or he will turn the convalescent child out into the street. She decides to sell what is left. The unfortunate girl becomes a woman of the town."],["Hugo steps out of the story to name what has happened: society purchasing a slave from hunger, cold, isolation and destitution, a soul for a morsel of bread. Christ's law governs our civilisation but does not yet permeate it; slavery survives in Europe, falling on the woman alone, on grace, weakness, beauty and maternity, and is called prostitution.","Nothing is left of the Fantine who was. She has become marble in becoming mire; she endures, ignores, no longer avoids anything, a sponge already soaked. She believes it so. But it is an error, Hugo warns, to think fate can be exhausted or that anything has a bottom. Who knows where these driven lives are going? One alone sees the whole of the shadow, and his name is God."],["Hugo opens with a portrait of the small-town idler: the young man with fifteen hundred francs a year who hisses actresses, quarrels with officers, smokes, yawns and never works, dressed in the triple waistcoats, codfish-tail coat, spurs and moustache of the period. Tholomyès, had he stayed in his province, would have been one of them. Hats mark politics: narrow-brimmed morillos for royalists, wide bolivars for liberals.","Eight or ten months after Fantine's fall, towards the first of January 1823, one such dandy in a morillo and a great cloak, known as M. Bamatabois, stands smoking in the snow outside the officers' café and flings an insult at a bare-shouldered woman in a ball-dress each time she passes: how ugly you are, you have no teeth. She never answers or looks at him. Piqued, he creeps up behind her and thrusts a handful of snow between her bare shoulders.","The woman roars, whirls, and hurls herself on him, clawing his face and pouring out gutter oaths in a brandy-roughened voice from a mouth missing its two front teeth. It is Fantine. Officers pour out of the café and a laughing circle forms around the struggling pair. Suddenly a tall man pushes through, grips her by her muddy satin bodice and says \\"Follow me!\\" Her fury dies; she turns pale and trembles. She has recognised Javert. Bamatabois slips away."],["Javert drags Fantine to the police station, sits down and writes in silence while she crouches in a corner like a frightened dog. In his mind he has just witnessed a crime: a creature outside society attacking a citizen and elector. He hands the sergeant his paper and tells Fantine she will have six months. She drags herself across the floor on her knees, begging: six months at seven sous a day, and she owes the Thénardiers over a hundred francs; Cosette will be turned into the street; the gentleman put snow down her back; she was not bad at bottom, she drank brandy only out of misery. Javert tells her the Eternal Father himself could do nothing more, and turns his back.","A man who entered unnoticed and stood listening against the door steps forward: \\"One moment, if you please.\\" Javert recognises M. Madeleine. At the words \\"Mr. Mayor\\" Fantine springs up, stares at the man she blames for everything, laughs, and spits in his face. He wipes it away and says: Inspector Javert, set this woman at liberty. Javert is nearly driven mad; for an instant a hideous comparison between what this woman is and what this mayor might be flickers through his mind. Fantine, bewildered, assumes Javert has freed her and pours out her story to him, cursing the mayor who had her dismissed, explaining how the prison contractors' prices forced her onto the streets, laying his hand on her throat to feel the burning there.","As she reaches the door Javert roars at the sergeant to stop her. \\"I\\" gave the order, says Madeleine. Javert, pale and shaking, insists she insulted a citizen, M. Bamatabois, a householder and elector, and that this is a matter for the police. Madeleine answers that he inquired in the square and the citizen was at fault, that the insult to himself is his own to forgive, that the highest law is conscience. When Javert still refuses, the mayor cites the articles of the criminal code that make him the judge in municipal police matters and the law against arbitrary detention, and says: Leave the room. Javert takes the blow like a soldier, bows to the ground, and goes.","Fantine watches the two men who held her life in their hands fight over her, and the angel turns out to be the man she abhorred. Her hatred melts. Madeleine tells her gently that he knew nothing of her dismissal, that he believes her, that he will pay her debts and bring her child or send her to it, that she need never work again, and that in God's sight she has never ceased to be virtuous. She can only sob, kneels, kisses his hand, and faints."],["Fantine wakes at noon in the infirmary Madeleine keeps in his own house to find him standing by her bed gazing at the crucifix on the wall, praying, he says, to the martyr on high, and adding to himself, for the martyr here below. He has spent the night and morning making inquiries and knows her story in every detail. From that moment he is transfigured in her eyes.","The same night Javert writes a letter and posts it himself to M. Chabouillet, secretary to the prefect of police in Paris. The postmistress and others who recognise his hand assume, after the scene at the station, that he is resigning.","Madeleine writes to the Thénardiers: Fantine owes a hundred and twenty francs; he sends three hundred and tells them to bring Cosette at once to her sick mother. Thénardier tells his wife not to let the child go, since this lark is turning into a milch cow, and replies with a bill for five hundred odd francs, padded with doctors' and apothecaries' charges that were really for Éponine's and Azelma's illnesses. Madeleine sends three hundred more. Thénardier still does not let go, pleading winter and small debts.","The sisters, at first repelled by \\"that woman\\", are won over by her humility; she tells them that having Cosette beside her will be the sign that God has pardoned her. Madeleine visits twice a day and each time promises the child tomorrow, perhaps. But Fantine does not recover: the snow on her bare back has unleashed a chest disease smouldering for years. The doctor sounds her chest, shakes his head, and tells Madeleine to make haste and bring the child. Madeleine shudders, and tells Fantine the doctor says Cosette's arrival will cure her. He declares he will send for Cosette, or go himself, and has Fantine sign a dictated note ordering Thénardier to hand the child to the bearer. Then, the narrator says, a serious incident occurs."],["Madeleine is in his study preparing for a possible trip to Montfermeil when Javert, who has avoided him since the station scene, asks to see him. The mayor keeps his back turned and goes on annotating a docket. Javert, visibly changed, stands in humble, rigid resignation, and announces that an inferior agent has gravely failed in respect towards a magistrate: he himself, towards the mayor. He asks to be turned out, not allowed to resign, since resignation is honourable and he deserves punishment. Six weeks ago, furious over the woman, he informed against the mayor at the prefecture in Paris, as an ex-convict.","The mayor turns livid as Javert explains: a resemblance, inquiries the mayor had made at Faverolles, his strength, the Fauchelevent affair, his marksmanship, a slight drag in his leg, all absurdities, but he had taken him for a certain Jean Valjean, a convict he knew twenty years ago as a guard at Toulon, who after his release robbed a bishop and then a little Savoyard on the highway and vanished eight years ago. Paris replied that Javert was mad, and they were right, because the real Jean Valjean has been found. The paper drops from Madeleine's hand.","Javert tells the story. A wretched old man called Father Champmathieu was arrested last autumn near Ailly-le-Haut-Clocher with a broken branch of stolen apples in his hand. Transferred to the prison at Arras, he was hailed by an ex-convict turnkey, Brevet: you are Jean Valjean, we were at Toulon together. Inquiry found that Champmathieu had been a tree-pruner at Faverolles thirty years before, like Valjean; that Valjean's mother was named Mathieu; that Jean Mathieu, pronounced in Auvergne, becomes Chan Mathieu, then Champmathieu. Two lifers from Toulon, Cochepaille and Chenildieu, were brought to confront him and recognised him. Javert was summoned and recognised him too: the truth is the truth, that man is Jean Valjean. For a convict, climbing a wall and stealing apples is a second offence: the galleys for life. The man only repeats, stupidly, that he is Champmathieu, which Javert takes for cunning. The case is tried at Arras tomorrow; Javert leaves by coach tonight to testify and returns as soon as his deposition is taken.","Madeleine, outwardly indifferent, gives Javert a list of petty police errands, then rises and says he esteems him, that the offence concerns himself alone, and that Javert deserves promotion and shall keep his post. Javert refuses: he has been severe with others all his life and would be a blackguard not to be severe with himself; the kindness that upholds a street-walker against a citizen and a police agent against a mayor is false kindness that disorganises society. He demands the discharge of Inspector Javert. When the mayor offers his hand, Javert recoils: a mayor does not give his hand to a police spy. He will serve until replaced. Madeleine sits listening to the firm steps die away."],["Book VII begins with a warning that not all of what follows was known at Montreuil-sur-Mer, and that two or three improbable details are kept out of respect for the truth. The afternoon after Javert's visit, Madeleine sends for Sister Simplice before going in to Fantine. The two infirmary nuns are Sister Perpétue, a robust peasant who became a nun as another woman becomes a cook, rough with her patients and crabbed with the dying, and Sister Simplice, pale as wax, ageless, gentle, austere and more solid than granite.","Simplice's distinctive trait is that she has never lied, not even the small innocent lie everyone allows himself; to her, Satan has two names, Satan and Lying. She chose her name after Simplice of Sicily, who let her breasts be torn off rather than misstate her birthplace. Her two early faults, a taste for sweets and for letters, she has corrected. She has grown fond of Fantine and devotes herself to her.","Madeleine takes Simplice aside and commends Fantine to her in a singular tone she recalls afterwards. With Fantine, who says she only lives when he is there, he is as usual, answering \\"Soon\\" when she asks for Cosette, but stays an hour instead of half an hour. His face darkens when the doctor murmurs that she is losing ground fast. Back at the town hall, the clerk sees him examining a road map of France and writing figures on a scrap of paper."],["On his way to the horse-hirer Madeleine passes the parsonage, stops, turns back, lifts the knocker, holds it, and sets it down gently without knocking before hurrying on. Master Scaufflaire, a Fleming who lets horses and gigs, is asked for a horse that can do twenty leagues in a day hitched to a cabriolet and set out again the next morning. Madeleine shows him a paper with the figures 5, 6 and 8½, nineteen and a half leagues in all.","Scaufflaire offers his little white Boulonnais horse, which will not be ridden but goes like the wind in harness, and sets his terms: a half-hour rest midway with someone watching that the stable boys do not steal the oats, the mayor travelling alone and driving himself, thirty francs a day, and a light open tilbury instead of a cabriolet. Asked whether he has considered the winter cold and the rain, Madeleine says only that horse and tilbury must be at his door at half-past four tomorrow morning. He goes out, comes back, and leaves a five-hundred-franc note as guarantee for a horse and gig worth a hundred crowns, still refusing to say where he is going.","The Fleming and his wife puzzle over it; she guesses Paris. Then he finds the forgotten paper on the mantel: five leagues to Hesdin, six to Saint-Pol, eight and a half to Arras. He is going to Arras.","Madeleine takes the long way home to avoid the parsonage door and shuts himself in his room at half-past eight. The cashier who lodges beneath him wakes at midnight to footsteps pacing overhead, a cupboard opening and closing, furniture moved, and the red flicker of a fire reflected on the opposite wall through a window flung wide in the bitter cold. An hour or two later the step is still pacing. This, says the narrator, is what had taken place in that room."],["Hugo opens by saying the reader has no doubt divined that M. Madeleine is none other than Jean Valjean, and asks us to enter his conscience as Dante entered his door. After the theft from Little Gervais, Valjean sold the Bishop's silver except the candlesticks, crossed France, built his fortune at Montreuil-sur-Mer, and has since lived by two rules that nearly always agree: to hide his name and to sanctify his life. Where they clashed he sacrificed safety to goodness, keeping the candlesticks, mourning the Bishop, questioning the Savoyards, inquiring at Faverolles, lifting Fauchelevent's cart under Javert's eye.","At Javert's first words his instinct was to run and denounce himself; he stifled it, got through the day, visited Fantine, hired the tilbury so as to be ready for anything, dined with appetite, and then bolted his door and put out his light as if his conscience could be shut out. Slowly he sees that he is master of the situation: the name he dreaded has surfaced and, far from menacing him, has made him safer than ever. Javert has his Jean Valjean. Providence has arranged it; who is he to disarrange it? He relights his candle and declares the matter settled, and feels no joy.","The tide of thought returns. To let an innocent man take his place is hypocritical baseness, a theft of another man's life, a moral murder; the Bishop, more present dead than alive, would find Mayor Madeleine abominable and the convict Jean Valjean admirable. He must go to Arras, free the false Valjean and denounce the real one. He puts his books in order, burns bills owed him by poor tradesmen, writes a letter to the banker Laffitte, pockets his passport. He weighs the chances of mercy and remembers that the forty sous from Little Gervais make him a repeat offender liable to the galleys for life. Then he thinks of Fantine and a new crisis opens: if he vanishes, the factory, the town, the mother and the child all perish, for the sake of an old apple-thief who may deserve the galleys anyway. The true self-sacrifice, he decides, is to bear the reproach of his conscience for others' good. \\"I am Madeleine, and Madeleine I remain.\\"","To cut the last threads, he opens a hidden cupboard in the wall and throws into the fire the blue blouse, trousers, knapsack and iron-shod thorn cudgel he wore into Digne in 1815; among the ashes glints the forty-sou piece. He seizes the two silver candlesticks to melt them too, and a voice within him shouts \\"Jean Valjean!\\", telling him to finish, to forget the Bishop, to let an innocent old man wear his red blouse and drag his chain while the whole town blesses him and one voice curses him in the dark, and that only the curse will reach God. He asks aloud if anyone is there, laughs at himself, and sets the candlesticks back on the mantel. He paces on, recoiling from both resolutions in turn, imagining the galleys at his age, the green cap, the chain, the strangers told \\"that is the famous Jean Valjean who was mayor\\". Remain in paradise and become a demon, or return to hell and become an angel? The chapter ends with him no further forward than when he began, likened to another man who once pushed away a cup in a garden of olive trees."],["After five hours of pacing he falls asleep in his chair and has a dream he later wrote down, which Hugo reproduces from the paper in his hand. He walks on a vast grassless plain, neither day nor night, with the brother of his childhood whom he never thinks of, talking of a neighbour who always worked with her window open. A hairless rider the colour of ashes passes on an earth-coloured horse without a word. His brother leads him into a hollow road and vanishes. He enters a village he thinks is Romainville, where every street, house and garden is empty, yet behind each corner, door and tree a man stands silent against the wall and does not answer when asked where he is. Leaving, he turns and finds the whole crowd of these earthen-faced men silently overtaking him. The first one says: do you not know that you have been dead this long time? He opens his mouth to answer and there is no one there.","He wakes icy cold; the fire is out, the candle almost gone, the window still open, no stars in the sky. A harsh sound from below draws his eye to two red lights in the yard, which he first takes for stars fallen to earth, then recognises as the lanterns of a tilbury harnessed to a small white horse. A tap at his door: the old portress, saying it is five o'clock and the cabriolet is here. He denies having ordered any tilbury, until she says M. Scaufflaire's name, which goes through him like lightning. After a long silence he tells her to say it is well, and that he is coming down."],["Entering Montreuil-sur-Mer before five, the Arras mail-wagon clips the wheel of a tilbury going the other way at a gallop with a single cloaked man in it, who does not stop. He is going to Arras without having decided anything, telling himself there is no harm in seeing with his own eyes, that no one will recognise him, that he holds his destiny in his hand; at bottom he would rather not go, and the nearer he gets the more something in him draws back. The little Boulonnais horse covers five leagues in two hours.","At the inn at Hesdin the stableman points out that the wheel has two split spokes and a strained hub and will not go another quarter league. The wheelwright Bourgaillard can mend it only by tomorrow; he has no spare wheels, no cabriolet to let or sell, only an old calash needing two post-horses, which on these crossroads at ploughing time would mean waiting hours at every relay; the white horse will not take a saddle; no horse for Arras can be bought or hired for any money; the next mail passes at night. Valjean feels an immense joy. It is Providence that broke the wheel; he has honestly exhausted every means and can turn back with nothing to reproach himself for. He breathes freely for the first time since Javert's visit.","But the conversation took place in the street, and a boy who was listening runs off and returns with an old woman who has a cart to hire: a leaky, rusted basket-cart hanging on leather thongs, but it runs on two wheels and can reach Arras. The iron hand has seized him again. He pays, leaves the tilbury for repair, harnesses the white horse and goes on, angry at the joy he felt a moment before. He refuses the boy who asks a tip for finding the cart.","The heavy cart takes four hours for the five leagues to Saint-Pol, where he watches the horse eat as promised, orders breakfast, and cannot swallow the bread, which tastes bitter. Beyond Tinques a road-mender tells him the main road is barred for repairs; the detour by Carency and Mont-Saint-Éloy adds leagues of crossroads in the dark. He takes the advice to hire a second horse and a stable-boy as postilion at Tinques. The whiffletree breaks in a rut and he cuts a branch to replace it. Starving, frozen, he remembers his other night journey on the plain near Digne eight years before. At seven, with three leagues to go, it occurs to him for the first time that he does not even know the hour of the trial, that Assizes sit at nine and such a case is short, and that he will probably arrive when all is over."],["The same day, back at the infirmary, Fantine has had a frightful night and is delirious by morning; the doctor asks to be told as soon as M. Madeleine arrives. At twenty-five she has the face of an old woman. She asks Sister Simplice the time again and again as three o'clock, the mayor's punctual hour, approaches, sits up at the stroke and stares at the door for a quarter of an hour, then falls back. The hours pass; she blames no one, only coughs and says low that he is wrong not to come today, since she is going away tomorrow. Then she sings, in a voice like a breath, an old lullaby about buying linen for a child and the Virgin at her stove, which she had sung to Cosette and not remembered in five years. The nun weeps.","At six Sister Simplice sends a maid to the factory portress, who reports that the mayor left before six that morning alone in a tilbury with a white horse, that some say he took the Arras road and others the Paris road, and that he said not to expect him tonight. Fantine, overhearing the whispering, rears up on her knees and demands in a hoarse voice to know why he cannot come. The maid whispers to the nun to say he is busy with the town council. Simplice blushes at the proposed lie, weighs the blow the truth may deal, and then says calmly: Monsieur le Maire has gone away.","Fantine's face blazes with joy: he has gone to fetch Cosette. She prays, apologises for shouting, lies down obediently, kisses the little silver cross Simplice gave her, and talks on in a flood: he need not even go through Paris, Montfermeil is a little to the left; he made her sign the letter; Cosette is seven now and must be a big girl; tomorrow will be a festival and she must wear her lace cap. The sister, who has no idea of distances, agrees that he will be back tomorrow.","Between seven and eight the doctor finds her wide-eyed and asking whether the child may sleep in a little bed beside her, there is just room. Simplice explains their silence and the patient's guess, which may after all be right; he approves. He finds her pulse stronger and the pressure on her chest eased, prescribes quinine and a calming draught, and tells the sister that if the mayor really arrived tomorrow with the child, great joy has been known to arrest even an advanced organic disease; they may yet save her."],["The cart rolls into the Hotel de la Poste at Arras about eight. Valjean stables the white horse himself, refuses bed and supper, and, told the horse needs two days' rest, goes to the posting office, shows his passport, and buys the vacant seat beside the postboy on the mail-wagon leaving for Montreuil-sur-Mer at one in the morning. Then he wanders the dark unfamiliar streets, loses himself beyond the little river Crinchon, and at last asks an old townsman with a lantern the way to the courthouse. The courts, the man explains, are sitting in the prefecture, the former bishop's palace. Four windows blazing late in the grand square mean the Assizes have not finished; the man wonders if he is a witness. He says he only wants to speak to a lawyer.","In the dim great hall of the old palace, thick with murmuring black-gowned groups, he asks a lawyer what stage they have reached. It is finished, judgment given, for life. His voice falters: so the identity was established? The lawyer is puzzled: there was no identity in question; a woman named Limosin was condemned for killing her child. The lights are for a second case begun two hours ago, a convict up for a second offence, a bandit's face if ever there was one. Valjean has felt, within moments, ice and fire. He listens at the groups: the apple theft is not well proved, but the man was certainly once at Toulon; witnesses are done, speeches remain; the man will be condemned, the prosecutor never misses.","The usher tells him the door will not reopen because the hall is full; there are two or three seats behind the President, but only for public functionaries. He goes slowly down the stairs, leans on the balusters with folded arms, and after a long inner debate tears a leaf from his pocketbook and writes by the street lantern: M. Madeleine, Mayor of Montreuil-sur-Mer. He climbs back through the crowd, hands it to the usher, and orders him to take it to the President."],["Without knowing it, the mayor of Montreuil-sur-Mer is famous across the Bas Boulonnais and the neighbouring departments for his virtue and for the industries he has rescued or financed. The Councillor from Douai presiding over the Arras Assizes knows the name like everyone else, writes a deferential line at the foot of the paper, and says \\"Admit him.\\" The same usher who turned his back now bows to the ground and hands back the note: the President of the Court of Assizes presents his respects to M. Madeleine. He crushes it as though the words left a bitter taste.","He is led through narrow corridors and dark stairs to the wainscoted council-chamber, two candles on a green cloth, and told that the copper handle of the door opposite opens into the courtroom behind the President's chair. Alone, he cannot gather his thoughts. He has not eaten in twenty-four hours and does not feel it. He stands before a framed autograph letter of Pache, mayor of Paris in the year II, and reads it two or three times without taking in a word, thinking of Fantine and Cosette.","His gaze falls on the brass knob and fills slowly with terror; sweat runs down his temples. With a gesture that says who compels me to this, he turns, goes out by the door he came in, and flees along the lantern-lit corridor as if pursued until he staggers against the cold stone wall. For a quarter of an hour he stands there in the dark, hearing within him only the single word Alas. Then, as though someone had overtaken him and were leading him back, he returns to the council-chamber. The knob shines at him like a terrible star; he cannot look away; step by step he nears it, grasps it without knowing how, and the door opens. He is in the courtroom."],["He stands inside the door of a vast, ill-lit, smoky hall and sees, on a bench between two gendarmes, a man of about sixty with bristling hair, a wild uneasy eye and a blouse: himself grown old, as he was the day he entered Digne full of hatred. \\"Shall I become like that again?\\" The President and the prosecutor, who knows him from Montreuil-sur-Mer, bow to him; he barely notices. Judges, gendarmes and gaping heads are the scene of his own trial twenty-seven years ago come to life, except that a crucifix now hangs above the President, which the courts lacked when he was condemned. Everyone in the room is calling the man in the blouse Jean Valjean. He sinks into a chair and hides his face behind a pile of cardboard boxes on the judges' desk. M. Bamatabois is one of the jurors. He looks for Javert and cannot see him.","The defence has just finished. For three hours the crowd has watched this wretched, stupid or cunning man bend under a terrible likeness. The accusation holds that he is not only a fruit-stealer but the ex-convict Jean Valjean, wanted for the highway robbery of the Savoyard Little Gervais eight years ago, to be tried for that later once his identity is fixed; and that for a convict a new theft is a second offence. The man's whole person is a denial, but he speaks with difficulty and stares at the ceiling. If his identity were established and the Little Gervais affair ended in conviction, even death would be possible.","Counsel for the defence, in the pompous dialect of the provincial bar, argued that no one saw his client scale the wall or break the branch, which he said he found on the ground; that the only proof of the theft is his supposed character as a convict; that four witnesses do recognise him as Jean Valjean and the Faverolles and Jean-Mathieu details are awkward, but even granting that, being Valjean does not prove he stole the apples; that his client's obstinate denial of everything was a bad system which his lawyer advised against, but a brutalised and stupid man should not be condemned for defending himself badly. He asked, at most, the penalty for breaking ban, not the galleys for a second offence.","The district-attorney seized on the defence's \\"loyalty\\": the lawyer had as good as admitted the man was Jean Valjean. He blamed the crime on the immoral new romantic literature, painted Valjean as a monster, and listed the four witnesses: Javert, the upright inspector, and the convicts Brevet, Chenildieu and Cochepaille. The accused listened open-mouthed, admiring that a man could talk like that, shaking his head slowly and muttering that this came of not asking M. Baloup. The prosecutor called that stupidity deliberate craft and demanded the full penalty, at that time the galleys for life. The defence rose to reply and visibly lost ground."],["The President asks the accused whether he has anything to add. The man, twisting a dreadful cap in his hands, does not understand at first, then erupts: he was a wheelwright in Paris with Monsieur Baloup, a hard open-air trade that makes a man old at forty and he was fifty-three; his daughter was a laundress, up to her waist in cold water, later at the steaming Enfants-Rouges wash-house that ruined her eyes; her husband beat her; she is dead; they were not happy; ask M. Baloup. He pauses to salute someone in the crowd. The audience laughs, and not knowing why, he laughs too.","The President explains that Baloup has been summoned in vain, being bankrupt and untraceable, and solemnly asks the prisoner two questions: did he climb the Pierron orchard wall and steal the apples, and is he the discharged convict Jean Valjean, yes or no? The man says \\"In the first place\\", stares at his cap and the ceiling, and falls silent. The prosecutor declares his embarrassment condemns him. Stung, the prisoner bursts out again: he is a poor man who does not eat every day, he picked up a broken branch lying on the road after a shower, he has been dragged about for three months, he never heard of Jean Valjean or Jean Mathieu, he does not know where he was born, his parents walked the roads, yes he has been in Auvergne and at Faverolles but can a man not have been there without having been in the galleys? Why is everyone hunting him?","The prosecutor asks to recall the four witnesses. The President notes that Inspector Javert left for his duties as soon as he had testified, so the prosecutor reads his deposition: he needs no circumstantial proof, he recognises the man perfectly as the vicious ex-convict Jean Valjean, nineteen years for theft, five or six escape attempts, suspected also of a theft at the late Bishop of Digne's, whom he often saw as adjutant of the guard at Toulon. Then Brevet is brought in, a sixty-year-old with a businessman's face and a rascal's air, reminded that he cannot swear an oath, and begged to reflect; he insists this is Jean Valjean, who entered Toulon in 1796 and left in 1815, sly then if brutish now. Chenildieu, a small wiry lifer in red cassock and green cap nicknamed I-deny-God, laughs: they were chained together five years. Cochepaille, a half-savage Pyrenean shepherd turned brigand, says he is Jean Valjean, called Jean-the-Screw for his strength.","After each the prisoner mutters, with growing satisfaction, \\"he's a nice one\\", \\"Good!\\", \\"Famous!\\", and when asked what he has to say, repeats \\"Famous!\\". Uproar; the man is clearly lost. As the President calls for silence to sum up, a lamentable, terrible voice beside him cries: Brevet! Chenildieu! Cochepaille! look here! A man among the privileged spectators behind the court has risen, pushed through the half-door and stands in the middle of the hall. The President, the prosecutor, Bamatabois and twenty others exclaim together: M. Madeleine!"],["It is he. His coat is buttoned, his hat in his hand, he is very pale, and his hair, grey when he arrived in Arras, has turned entirely white in the hour he sat there. Before anyone can move he walks to the three witnesses and asks if they recognise him; they shake their heads, and Cochepaille, intimidated, salutes. He turns to the jury and court and says gently: order the prisoner released, have me arrested; he is not the man you seek, it is I, I am Jean Valjean.","In the grave-like silence the President exchanges signs with the prosecutor and asks whether a physician is present; the prosecutor, invoking the honourable mayor's reputation, asks that he be attended and taken home. Valjean cuts him off: he is not mad; they were about to commit a great error; he has hidden under another name, grown rich, become mayor and tried to re-enter the ranks of the honest, and it seems that cannot be done. He robbed the Bishop; he robbed Little Gervais; Jean Valjean was a vicious wretch, though perhaps not wholly by his own fault, for the galleys make the convict: he went in a stupid peasant and came out a firebrand, and later kindness saved him as severity had ruined him. The forty-sou piece he stole from Little Gervais seven years ago lies among the ashes of his fireplace. He wishes Javert were here to recognise him.","Since the convicts do not know him, he knows them. Brevet, do you remember the knitted checked suspenders you wore in the galleys? Chenildieu, your right shoulder bears a deep burn where you tried to efface the letters T. F. P., which still show: is it true? \\"It is true.\\" Cochepaille, near the bend of your left arm is tattooed in blue the date of the Emperor's landing at Cannes, March 1, 1815: pull up your sleeve. A gendarme holds a light to the bare arm, and the date is there. With a smile both of triumph and of despair he says: you see plainly that I am Jean Valjean.","There are no longer judges or prosecutors in the room, only staring eyes and moved hearts; everyone understands at once the history of a man giving himself up so another will not be condemned in his place. No one puts a question or intervenes. He says he will withdraw since they do not arrest him, that he has much to do, and that the district-attorney knows who he is and where he is going and can have him taken when he likes. The crowd parts; the door is found open; at the threshold he tells the prosecutor he is at his command and asks the audience to consider him worthy of pity, though when he thinks of what he nearly did he counts himself to be envied. Less than an hour later the jury acquits Champmathieu, who goes off stupefied, thinking all men fools."],["Day is breaking in Montreuil-sur-Mer. Fantine has had a feverish night full of happy visions and has only just fallen asleep when Sister Simplice, mixing a draught in the dispensary, looks up and finds the mayor standing before her. He has come straight from the courtroom at Arras. The sister tells him Fantine is calmer because she believes he went to Montfermeil to fetch her child, and asks, anxiously, what they will say to her now. \\"God will inspire us,\\" he answers; the nun murmurs that they cannot tell a lie.","In the full daylight the sister sees that his hair, dark the day before, has gone perfectly white. She finds him the little mirror the doctor uses to test whether a patient still breathes; he looks, says only \\"Well!\\" and seems to be thinking of something else. When she suggests he keep away until the child can really be brought, so that no lie is needed, he refuses: he must see Fantine now, because he may be \\"in haste.\\" The sister does not catch the weight of that word.","He goes in alone. Fantine sleeps, her breathing ragged but her face serene, almost transfigured; the narrator compares her to something about to take flight rather than to die. He stands by the bed as he did two months earlier, looking from her to the crucifix: then her hair was gray and his brown; now hers is gray and his is white. She opens her eyes, sees him, and asks quietly, smiling, where Cosette is."],["Fantine shows neither surprise nor doubt; she says she watched him all night in a glory of celestial forms, and asks why Cosette was not laid on her bed for her to find on waking. He cannot answer. The doctor arrives and covers for him: the child is here, but Fantine is still feverish and must be calm before she sees her. Fantine protests, apologizes, promises to be \\"very good\\" so that they will bring the child, and asks him about the journey, the Thénardiers, whether Cosette had clean linen and is pretty.","He holds her hand and tells her only that Cosette is beautiful and well, and that she must stop talking so much because it makes her cough. A little girl playing in the yard begins to sing, and Fantine, radiant, is sure it is Cosette's voice; when it fades she turns against the doctor for keeping her child away. Then she drifts into plans: a little garden the mayor has promised, Cosette running after butterflies, learning her letters, and in five years a white veil for her first communion. She laughs.","He has let go of her hand and sits with his eyes on the floor, lost in thought. Her sudden silence makes him look up: Fantine has raised herself in bed, ghastly, her eyes fixed on something at the far end of the room. She cannot speak; she signs to him to look behind him. He turns and sees Javert."],["The narrator goes back a few hours. Valjean left the Arras courtroom after midnight, caught the mail-coach, reached Montreuil-sur-Mer before six, posted a letter to the banker M. Laffitte, and went to Fantine. At Arras, meanwhile, the district-attorney first insisted on convicting Champmathieu anyway; the defence and the presiding judge prevailed and the jury dismissed the case. The prosecutor then turned on Madeleine instead. He conferred with the president (a royalist, offended that the mayor had said \\"the Emperor\\" rather than \\"Bonaparte\\"), and an arrest warrant was sent by express to Montreuil-sur-Mer for Police Inspector Javert to execute.","Javert, who had returned home straight after giving his evidence, received the order as he was getting out of bed. To a stranger he looked as cool and correct as ever as he climbed the infirmary stairs, but the buckle of his stock sat under his left ear instead of at the nape: for a man who never had a wrinkle in his duty or his uniform, the sign of an inner earthquake. He had requisitioned a corporal and four soldiers, left them in the courtyard, and had the unsuspecting portress point out Fantine's room. He pushed the door open gently and stood in it, hat on, hand in his buttoned coat, his heavy cane hidden behind him, for nearly a minute unnoticed.","When Madeleine's eyes meet his, Javert becomes terrible without moving: the narrator calls it the face of a demon who has found his damned soul. His satisfaction at finally laying hands on Jean Valjean, his pride at having been right all along despite the brief embarrassment over Champmathieu, flood his features. He feels himself the embodiment of justice, order and law, a ferocious archangel, a monstrous Saint Michael with his heel on crime. Hugo insists there is nothing ignoble in him: probity, conviction and duty wrongly aimed remain grand even when hideous, and Javert in his triumph is to be pitied as every ignorant man who triumphs is. It is \\"the evil of the good.\\""],["Fantine thinks Javert has come for her and screams to the mayor to save her. Jean Valjean (the narrator says he will call him by no other name from now on) tells her gently that it is not she Javert wants, then says to the inspector, \\"I know what you want.\\" Javert's only reply is a roar: \\"Be quick about it!\\" He produces no warrant and makes no explanation; for him this arrest is not a beginning but the end of a five-year wrestling match. He strides to the middle of the room and, to Fantine's bewilderment, addresses the mayor as \\"thou\\" and grabs him by the collar. Valjean does not resist.","Valjean asks for a private word; Javert refuses to listen to anything not said aloud. In a low voice Valjean begs for three days' grace to go and fetch the dying woman's child, offering to let Javert come with him. Javert laughs: three days to run away, on the pretext of fetching \\"that creature's child.\\" Fantine hears, and understands that Cosette is not there. She cries out for her daughter and for Monsieur Madeleine. Javert tells her to hold her tongue, rails at a town where convicts are magistrates and prostitutes are nursed like countesses, and declares for all to hear that there is no Madeleine and no mayor, only a thief, a brigand, a convict named Jean Valjean, and that he has him in his grasp.","Fantine rears up in bed, looks from one to the other, tries to speak, and falls back dead, her head striking the headboard. Valjean opens Javert's hand from his collar as he would a baby's and says, \\"You have murdered that woman.\\" When Javert threatens him with the guard and thumbscrews, Valjean wrenches an iron bar from an old bedstead, walks to Fantine's bed, and tells Javert in a near-whisper not to disturb him now. Javert, trembling, stays by the door rather than call the guard and risk losing him.","Valjean gazes at the dead woman with inexpressible pity, bends and whispers something to her that no one hears; Sister Simplice, the only witness, always said afterwards that she saw a smile come to the dead lips. He arranges Fantine's head on the pillow as a mother would a child's, ties her chemise, smooths her hair under her cap, closes her eyes, kneels and kisses her hand. Then he rises and says to Javert, \\"Now, I am at your disposal.\\""],["Javert lodges Valjean in the city prison. Within two hours the town has forgotten everything Madeleine did: he was a galley-slave, his real name some frightful \\"Béjean, Bojean, Boujean,\\" he will be tried for an old highway robbery, and people who always found him \\"too good, too perfect\\" now say they suspected as much. An old lady who reads the royalist Drapeau Blanc calls it a lesson to the Bonapartists. Only three or four people stay loyal, among them the old portress of his house, who that evening, without thinking, hangs his key on its nail and sets out his candlestick as if he were coming home.","Two hours later a hand reaches through her lodge window and takes the key and candle: it is the mayor. He tells her plainly that he broke a bar in a window, dropped from a roof, and is here; she is to fetch Sister Simplice, who is watching over Fantine's body with Sister Perpétue. How he got into the courtyard without opening the gates is never explained. Upstairs he closes his shutters, finds that the portress has tidied his room and laid on the table the two iron tips of his cudgel and the blackened forty-sou piece rescued from the ashes. He writes a label identifying them as the cudgel-tips and the coin stolen from Little Gervais that he mentioned at the Assizes, sets them where they will be seen first, tears up an old shirt and wraps the Bishop's two silver candlesticks in the strips, nibbling a piece of prison bread as he works.","Sister Simplice comes, weeping and trembling; the day has made the nun a woman again. He hands her a note for the curé: everything he leaves behind is to pay for his trial and for the funeral of the woman who died yesterday, and the rest is for the poor. He will not look at Fantine a last time; he is pursued and would only be arrested in her room. Footsteps and the portress's loud protests sound on the stairs, then Javert's voice: there is a light in that room. Valjean blows out the candle and stands in the angle behind the door; the sister kneels at the table. Javert enters, sees the nun and halts, for his veneration of all authority makes a nun a creature who never sins, and he knows Sister Simplice has never lied in her life. He asks whether she is alone; she says yes. He asks whether she has seen the escaped man, Jean Valjean; she says no. He bows and withdraws without even noticing the still-smoking wick. Hugo prays that this lie be counted to her credit in paradise.","An hour later a man in a workman's blouse, carrying a bundle, is walking fast through the trees and mist toward Paris. The curé, judging that a convict and a street-woman deserved no more, keeps as much of the money as he can for the poor and gives Fantine the cheapest possible funeral. She is thrown into the common pit in the free corner of the cemetery, among the first bones that came to hand. Her grave, the narrator says, resembled her bed."],["The narrator, \\"the person who is telling this story,\\" describes a May morning walk in 1861 along the undulating paved road from Nivelles toward La Hulpe, past Lillois and Bois-Seigneur-Isaac, with the reversed-vase church tower of Braine-l'Alleud to the west and a crossroads tavern called At the Four Winds. He reaches a little valley with a stream under the road, an inn with a cart and hop-poles at its door, a girl weeding beneath a yellow poster, ducks on a pond, and a badly paved lane plunging into the bushes.","A hundred paces down the lane he comes to a fifteenth-century wall and a grim Louis XIV stone gateway with two flat medallions, three harrows lying in the flowering meadow before it, a bird singing in a tree. He stoops to examine a large round hollow in the stone at the foot of the gate-pier. A peasant woman comes out and tells him a French cannonball made it, and points out a hole higher up from an iron bullet the size of an egg that did not pierce the wood. He asks the name of the place. \\"Hougomont.\\" Looking over the hedges, he sees in the distance a mound with something on it like a lion, and realizes he is on the field of Waterloo."],["Hougomont, once a château and now a farm, was the first knot under Napoleon's axe at Waterloo. The narrator enters the courtyard: a sixteenth-century arch standing amid ruin, an orchard door, a well, chickens, a chapel with a small bell-tower, a growling dog where the English once stood. Here four companies of English guards under Cooke held out seven hours; Napoleon threw his brother Jérôme, the divisions of Foy, Guilleminot and Bachelu, and nearly all of Reille's corps against it and failed. The northern door, which the French broke in and where the brigade commander Bauduin was killed, still hangs patched on the wall; for years bloody handprints marked its posts. Buildings since pulled down made the yard a maze of angles; the French got in and could not hold it, and answered the fire from cellars and garrets by setting the place alight.","In the ruined wing a cracked spiral staircase shows where the English guards cut away the lower steps and fought from above. The chapel saw a massacre: the French held it briefly, were dislodged, and set it on fire; the wooden Christ's feet burned to stumps and then the flames stopped, which the locals call a miracle, while a cannonball carried off the head of the infant Jesus on a fifteenth-century Saint Anne. The walls carry scrawled names from several nations. At the chapel door was found a corpse still gripping an axe: Sublieutenant Legros. Two wells stand in the yard; one has no bucket because it is full of skeletons. The gardener Guillaume van Kylsom, dragged from his hiding place in the cellar and forced to draw water for the thirsty English, was the last to use it; after the battle three hundred bodies were thrown into it, perhaps too hastily, for legend says feeble voices called from it the following night. Van Kylsom's granddaughter, now gray-haired, remembers being carried to the woods at three years old and imitating the cannon.","The orchard beyond is in three parts, garden, orchard and wood, enclosed by walls and a hedge. In the garden, with its ruined balustrade scarred by bullets, six French light-infantrymen trapped among the currant bushes fought two hundred Hanoverians for a quarter of an hour before dying. In the orchard proper fifteen hundred men fell in under an hour: the English had pierced the south wall with thirty-eight loopholes hidden behind a tall hedge, and Soye's brigade, expecting only a hedge, broke against the wall. \\"Thus Waterloo began.\\" The French took the orchard anyway, climbing with their nails; a Nassau battalion of seven hundred was overwhelmed; Major Blackmann and the German general Duplat died among the apple trees. Every tree still carries its bullet. Hugo totals it: Bauduin killed, Foy wounded, Nassau and Brunswick regiments destroyed, three thousand men cut down, shot or burned in this one hovel, all so that a peasant can now say to a traveller, \\"Monsieur, give me three francs and I will explain the affair of Waterloo.\\""],["Exercising \\"the storyteller's right\\" to turn back, Hugo returns to June 1815, a little before the events of the first part of his book. Had it not rained in the night, he says, the fate of Europe would have been different: a few drops of water decided the downfall of Napoleon. The battle could not begin until half-past eleven because the ground was too soft for the guns to manoeuvre, and that delay gave Blücher time to come up.","Napoleon was an artillery officer whose whole method was to converge his guns on one point and breach the enemy like a citadel; on the 18th he had 240 guns to Wellington's 159 and counted on them. Had the soil been dry the action would have begun at six and been won by two, three hours before the Prussians could turn the tide. Hugo then poses the question many historians raise, whether Napoleon at forty-six was worn out, reckless, no longer able to scent catastrophe, and answers flatly: \\"We do not think so.\\" The plan was by universal admission a masterpiece: strike the centre of the allied line, cut it in two, drive the British on Hal and the Prussians on Tongres, take Brussels, throw the Germans into the Rhine and the English into the sea.","He adds that he does not pretend to write a history of the battle; one scene of the story he is telling is connected with it, but the history itself has been written by Napoleon and by a whole constellation of historians. He is a passer-by on the plain, a bystander bending over ground made of human flesh, with neither military practice nor strategic authority; in his view a chain of accidents dominated both commanders, and where destiny is the culprit he judges as the people do."],["To picture Waterloo, Hugo says, set a capital A on the ground. The left leg is the road to Nivelles, the right the road to Genappe, the crossbar the hollow road running from Braine-l'Alleud to Ohain. The top of the A is Mont-Saint-Jean, where Wellington stood; the bottom left is Hougomont, with Reille and Jérôme Bonaparte; the bottom right is La Belle-Alliance, where Napoleon was. At the centre of the crossbar the final word of the battle was spoken, and there the lion monument now stands. The triangle at the top is the plateau of Mont-Saint-Jean, and the whole battle was the dispute over that plateau; behind it lies the forest of Soignes. The plain is a vast undulating sweep rising toward Mont-Saint-Jean.","Two armies on a field are two wrestlers, Hugo says, each clutching at every bush, wall and fold of ground; a chance hollow can stop a whole army. Both generals had studied the plain; Wellington had looked it over the year before as a possible battlefield. On 18 June Wellington had the good position, above, and Napoleon the bad one, below. Hugo sketches the familiar silhouette of Napoleon on his white horse at Rossomme at daybreak, the small hat, the green coat, the sword of Marengo, and reflects that history's pitiless light now casts shadow where people once saw only rays: the despot's shadow fights the leader's brilliance, and it is a misfortune for a man to leave behind him the night that bears his form."],["The first phase, Hugo says, is well known: a troubled, uncertain beginning, worse for the English than the French. The night's rain had left guns axle-deep in mud; only the trampled wheat and rye made movement possible. Napoleon, who liked to hold his artillery in hand like a pistol, waited for the sun to dry the ground, but the sun never came, and the English general Colville noted the first cannon shot at thirty-five minutes past eleven.","The French left wing attacked Hougomont with more fury than the Emperor wished; it was partly a feint to draw Wellington leftward, and it failed because the English guards and Perponcher's Belgians held, so Wellington needed to send only a few extra companies. Quiot's brigade struck La Haie-Sainte in the centre and Ney pushed the right wing against Papelotte on the English left, aiming to cut the Brussels road, bar the way to any Prussians and roll Wellington back on Hougomont. Both Papelotte and La Haie-Sainte were taken. Hugo notes the raw English recruits in Kempt's brigade, who fought with a dash that displeased Wellington.","After La Haie-Sainte fell the battle wavered, and from midday to four o'clock there is an obscure interval that Hugo describes as twilight: a fog of colbacks, dolmans, Scottish kilts, Brunswick black and English scarlet, pictures rather than lines. A battle has an incalculable ebb; the two plans deform each other; the line undulates like a thread; what the generals planned is overtaken by what the ground devours. Rembrandt paints such a thing better than a map-maker. No narrator, he says, can fix the shape of \\"that horrible cloud which is called a battle.\\" Nevertheless, at a certain moment in the afternoon, the battle came to a point."],["At four the condition of the English army is serious. The Prince of Orange holds the centre, Hill the right, and Picton, who commanded the left, has been shot through the head at the moment the French lost the flag of the 105th. Hougomont still holds but is burning; La Haie-Sainte has been taken, only forty-two of its German defenders surviving, three thousand men slaughtered in that barn. The Scots Greys no longer exist; Ponsonby's dragoons have been hacked apart by Bro's lancers and Travers's cuirassiers, Ponsonby himself dead of seven lance-thrusts; two divisions are annihilated.","One rallying point remains, the centre on the plateau of Mont-Saint-Jean, backed by the village and a stout sixteenth-century farmhouse off which cannonballs bounce. The English have cut embrasures in the hedges and hidden guns in the brushwood so well that Haxo, sent to reconnoitre in the morning, reported no obstacles; a battalion of the 95th lies hidden in the tall wheat. Behind lies the forest of Soignes, into which no army could retreat without dissolving. Wellington feeds the centre everything he has: Chassé from the right, Wincke from the left, Clinton's division, the Brunswickers, Nassau, Kielmansegg's Hanoverians and Ompteda's Germans, twenty-six battalions in all, a masked battery of sandbags where the Waterloo museum now stands, and Somerset's fourteen hundred dragoon guards behind a rise.","Wellington sits his horse all day beneath an elm near the old mill, impassive while bullets rain and his aide Gordon falls beside him. Asked what his orders are if he is killed, he says, \\"To do like me\\"; to Clinton, \\"Hold this spot to the last man\\"; to his Peninsular veterans, \\"Think of old England!\\" Toward four the English line draws back: the regiments vanish behind the crest into the sunken farm road, leaving only guns and skirmishers visible. \\"The beginning of retreat!\\" cries Napoleon."],["The Emperor, unwell and uncomfortable on horseback, had never been in better humour. Riding the lines in the storm at one in the morning with Bertrand and seeing the English campfires, he murmured, \\"We are in accord\\"; Hugo says fate and he were no longer in accord. He slept not at all, mistook a passing column for Wellington decamping, learned at half-past three that the English were not moving, and at five, told by Belgian deserters that they meant to fight, said, \\"So much the better.\\" He breakfasted at a kitchen table on a truss of straw with the map spread out, calling it \\"a pretty checkerboard,\\" joked with Soult and Ney about Wellington's ball in Brussels, and dictated the order of battle. Hugo gathers testimony to his habitual gaiety: the grenadiers whose ears he pinched, the speaking-trumpet joke on the voyage from Elba.","At nine, when the army deployed in five columns with drums and trumpets, he cried twice, \\"Magnificent!\\"; by half-past ten it stood in six lines, \\"the figure of six V's.\\" He pointed out to Haxo the three twelve-pounder batteries that were to open on Mont-Saint-Jean, \\"four and twenty handsome maids,\\" and, seeing the Scots Greys massing, allowed himself one word of haughty pity: \\"It is a pity.\\" He took up his second observation post beyond Rossomme and later a third knoll between La Belle-Alliance and La Haie-Sainte, where balls rebounded about him and where he scolded his terrified peasant guide, Lacoste, tied to a hussar's saddle, for trying to hide behind him. The narrator himself has dug rusted shell fragments from that knoll.","Hugo warns that the field is no longer what it was: to raise the huge earthen mound with the lion, the builders carted away the relief of the ground, so that Wellington on revisiting it exclaimed, \\"They have altered my field of battle!\\" In 1815 the slope up to the plateau from La Haie-Sainte was steep, made worse by mud, and along the crest ran a feature no distant observer could guess at. The road between the villages of Braine-l'Alleud and Ohain crosses the plateau between the two highways, and in 1815 it was a sunken way, a trench sometimes twelve feet deep with crumbling banks; stone crosses record a Brussels merchant crushed by a cart there in 1637 and a peasant killed by a landslide in 1783. On the day of battle this hollow road, bordering the crest and quite invisible, was, Hugo says, terrible."],["On the morning of Waterloo Napoleon was content, and rightly, for his plan was admirable. Hugo then pours out the day's accumulating mischances in one enormous sentence: Hougomont's resistance, Bauduin killed, Foy disabled, Soye's brigade shattered on the hidden wall, guns mired, shells burying themselves harmlessly in mud, Piré's wasted demonstration, Ney's error in massing d'Erlon's four divisions twenty-seven ranks deep to be ploughed by cannonballs, Marcognet's division caught between infantry and cavalry, flags lost, Grouchy's delay, fifteen hundred dead in the orchard of Hougomont and eighteen hundred around La Haie-Sainte. None of it clouded the Emperor's gaze; he never added up the details, only the total, victory, and treated destiny as his equal.","Yet, Hugo observes, a man with the Bérésina, Leipzig and Fontainebleau behind him might have distrusted Waterloo. When Wellington's line drew back, Napoleon half rose in his stirrups with the lightning of victory in his eyes: Wellington cornered against the forest of Soignes would be the conquest of England, Crécy and Agincourt avenged. He swept his glass over the field a last time, studying every bush, the two English barricades on the highways, and the little white chapel of Saint Nicholas at the Braine-l'Alleud crossroads. He bent and asked the guide Lacoste something in a low voice; the guide shook his head, \\"probably perfidiously.\\"","Napoleon straightened, reflected, and concluded that Wellington had retreated and had only to be crushed. He sent an express galloping to Paris to announce the battle won. Then he found his thunderbolt: he ordered Milhaud's cuirassiers to carry the plateau of Mont-Saint-Jean."],["Milhaud's cuirassiers, twenty-six squadrons of giant men on colossal horses with iron breastplates and long sabres, supported by the light cavalry and lancers of the Guard, had been admired by the whole army that morning. The Emperor's order is carried by his aide Bernard; Ney draws his sword and puts himself at their head. In two columns, Wathier's on the right and Delort's on the left, they descend the hill of La Belle-Alliance like a battering ram, vanish into the smoke of the valley, and reappear climbing at a trot through grapeshot up the slope of Mont-Saint-Jean, two immense steel serpents crawling toward the crest. Hugo likens them to the centaurs of Orphic legend storming Olympus.","Behind the crest, unseen and unseeing, the English infantry wait in thirteen squares, two battalions each, in two lines, muskets at the shoulder, listening to the rising thunder of three thousand horses. Then a line of upraised sabres, helmets and gray-moustached heads shouting \\"Vive l'Empereur!\\" bursts over the crest like an earthquake. And at that instant, on the English left, the head of the column rears with a frightful clamour: between the cuirassiers and the English yawns a trench two fathoms deep, the hollow road of Ohain. There is no stopping; the second rank pushes the first in, the third pushes the second; horses fall backward crushing their riders; the force meant to crush the English crushes the French, and the ravine yields only when it is filled with living men, over whom the rest ride on. Nearly a third of Dubois's brigade goes into that abyss. \\"This began the loss of the battle.\\" Local tradition, surely exaggerated, speaks of two thousand horses and fifteen hundred men buried there.","Napoleon had scrutinized the ground before ordering the charge but could not see the road, which made not a wrinkle on the plateau; alerted by the little white chapel, he had probably asked the guide Lacoste about an obstacle, and Lacoste had said no. Hugo says one might almost claim the catastrophe began with that shake of a peasant's head. Could Napoleon have won? No, and not because of Wellington or Blücher, but because of God: a victorious Bonaparte did not fit the law of the nineteenth century; his enormous weight in human destiny upset the balance, and the groans of overfilled cemeteries and weeping mothers had pleaded against him. \\"He embarrassed God.\\" Waterloo is not a battle but a change of front by the universe."],["The masked English battery unmasks at the same moment as the ravine: sixty cannon and thirteen squares fire point-blank into the cuirassiers, and General Delort gravely salutes the battery. Decimated but not discouraged, Delort's column, which Ney had deflected leftward as if sensing an ambush, arrives whole and the cuirassiers fall on the squares at full gallop, swords in their teeth, pistols in hand. The English infantry does not stir: the front rank kneels to receive the horses on bayonets, the second shoots them down, the square opens to let a blast of grapeshot through and closes again. The horses leap into the squares and die there; files of men are ground to dust; the squares become craters and the cavalry a tempest. On the far right the 75th Highlanders are almost annihilated, their piper playing Highland airs on a drum until a cuirassier's sabre cuts off his arm and his song together.","Some Hanoverian battalions give way; Wellington thinks of his cavalry, and Hugo notes that if Napoleon at that moment had thought of his infantry he would have won, which he calls the fatal mistake. Somerset's fourteen hundred dragoon guards with Dornberg's Germans and Trip's Belgians fall on the cuirassiers from behind, so that they fight on every side at once; the dragoons are reduced to eight hundred and their colonel killed. Ney comes up with lancers and light horse; the plateau is taken, retaken and taken again; there are a dozen assaults; Ney has four horses killed under him; half the cuirassiers are left on the plateau. Wellington, three-quarters beaten, says under his breath, \\"Sublime!\\" Seven squares of thirteen are destroyed, sixty guns taken or spiked, six English flags carried to the Emperor at La Belle-Alliance. One cuirassier and his horse were found dead the next day at the Mont-Saint-Jean crossroads, having pierced the whole English line.","But the centre is not broken; everyone holds the plateau, so no one does, and most of it remains with the English. Both armies are exhausted: Kempt asks for reinforcements and Wellington replies there are none, he must let himself be killed; Ney asks Napoleon for infantry and Napoleon demands whether he is expected to make it. The English are in the worse case. Alten's division is almost destroyed, Uxbridge's knee is shattered, Delancey, Van Meeren and Ompteda are dead, Wellington's whole staff decimated; the Cumberland hussars turn bridle and flee to Brussels spreading panic that reaches Louis XVIII at Ghent, and the roads to Brussels are choked with fugitives and wagons. Wellington has no cavalry left but Vivian's and Vandeleur's brigades. His lips blanch; the Austrian and Spanish commissioners think him lost. At five o'clock he looks at his watch and murmurs, \\"Blücher, or night!\\" At about that moment a distant line of bayonets gleams on the heights toward Frischemont."],["Napoleon's painful surprise is famous: he hoped for Grouchy and got Blücher, death instead of life, Saint Helena instead of the throne of the world. Hugo lays weight on a small shepherd who guided Bülow, Blücher's lieutenant. Had the boy led him out of the forest above Frischemont instead of below Plancenoit, the Prussians would have come up against a ravine impassable for artillery and never arrived; by the Prussian general Muffling's own account an hour's delay would have found Wellington no longer standing.","Bülow had been badly delayed: he bivouacked at Dion-le-Mont, set out at daybreak, bogged his divisions to the hubs in mud, and had to wait at the narrow bridge of Wavre while the French-fired street leading to it burned out, so that his vanguard reached Chapelle-Saint-Lambert only at midday. Had the battle begun two hours earlier it would have been over at four and Blücher would have arrived to find Napoleon victorious. At noon the Emperor himself had spotted the cloud on the horizon and asked Soult what he saw toward Chapelle-Saint-Lambert; Soult levelled his glass and said four or five thousand men, evidently Grouchy. Others on the staff said it was trees. The cloud did not move, and Napoleon sent Domon's light cavalry to reconnoitre.","Bülow's weak vanguard could do nothing alone, and he had orders to concentrate before engaging; but at five o'clock, seeing Wellington's peril, Blücher ordered him to attack, saying, \\"We must give air to the English army.\\" The divisions of Losthin, Hiller, Hacke and Ryssel deploy before Lobau's corps, Prince William of Prussia's cavalry debouches from the forest of Paris, Plancenoit catches fire, and Prussian cannonballs begin to rain on the ranks of the Guard held in reserve behind Napoleon."],["Everyone knows the rest, Hugo says, and tells it in one rush: eighty-six Prussian guns thundering, Pirch and Zieten's cavalry led by Blücher in person, Marcognet swept from the Ohain plateau, Durutte from Papelotte, Lobau caught in flank, the French regiments dismantled, the whole English line resuming the offensive, a gigantic breach torn in the French army, English and Prussian grapeshot aiding each other, and into this collapse the Guard entering the line. Knowing they were about to die, they shouted \\"Vive l'Empereur!\\"; history, he says, records nothing more touching than that agony bursting forth in acclamation. As they advanced, at eight o'clock, the clouds parted and let through the sinister glow of a sun they had seen rise at Austerlitz.","Each battalion of the Guard was led by a general: Friant, Michel, Roguet, Harlet, Mallet, Poret de Morvan. When the tall bearskins with their eagle plates appeared in symmetrical lines, the enemy felt a respect for France and those who were winning believed for a moment they were beaten. Then Wellington shouted, \\"Up, Guards, and aim straight!\\"; the red English guards rose from behind the hedges and riddled the tricolour with grapeshot, and the final carnage began. In the dark the Guard felt the army dissolving around it and heard the rout behind it, and still advanced, losing men at every step; not a man was missing from that suicide.","Ney, haggard and great with the grandeur of accepted death, offered himself to every blow. His fifth horse was killed under him; sweating, foaming, his uniform torn open, an epaulette half cut away, his eagle plaque dented by a bullet, a broken sword in his hand, he cried, \\"Come and see how a Marshal of France dies on the field of battle!\\" He did not die. He asked Drouet d'Erlon whether he was not going to get himself killed, and shouted that he wished all the English bullets would enter his bowels. Hugo adds that he was reserved for French bullets."],["The rout behind the Guard is melancholy. The army yields simultaneously at Hougomont, La Haie-Sainte, Papelotte and Plancenoit; the cry of \\"Treachery!\\" is followed by \\"Save yourselves who can!\\" A disbanding army is a thaw: everything cracks, floats and falls. Ney borrows a horse and, without hat, cravat or sword, plants himself across the Brussels road to hold back English and French alike, insulting and clinging to the rout; the soldiers flee past him shouting \\"Long live Marshal Ney!\\" Napoleon tries to build walls out of what remains of his Guard and spends his last squadrons; he gallops along the line of fugitives haranguing and threatening, and the mouths that shouted for him in the morning hardly recognize him. Caissons are unharnessed so the drivers can escape on the horses; wagons overturn and block the road; men walk over the living and the dead; forty thousand men pour over roads, bridges, fields and woods. \\"Lions converted into goats.\\"","At Genappe Lobau rallies three hundred men behind a barricade, but one volley of Prussian canister, whose marks can still be seen on a brick gable, scatters them and Lobau is taken. The pursuit is stupendous. Blücher orders extermination; Duhesme, general of the Young Guard, surrenders his sword at an inn door at Genappe to a hussar who takes it and kills him. Hugo, \\"since we are history,\\" passes sentence: old Blücher disgraced himself. The flight runs through Genappe, Quatre-Bras, Gosselies, Frasnes, Charleroi and Thuin and halts only at the frontier. And who was fleeing in that manner? The Grand Army.","Hugo asks whether this collapse of the bravest army in history was causeless and answers no: the shadow of an enormous right lies across Waterloo, the day of destiny, when something mightier than man made the heroes' brows wrinkle and great souls surrender their swords. Waterloo is the hinge of the nineteenth century; the great man had to disappear for the great century to arrive. \\"God has passed by.\\" At nightfall, in a meadow near Genappe, Bernard and Bertrand seize by the coat a haggard, gloomy man who has dismounted and, bridle over his arm, is walking back alone toward Waterloo: Napoleon, the sleepwalker of a shattered dream, trying once more to advance."],["Several squares of the Guard hold their ground amid the defeat as rocks do in running water, waiting for night and death together. Cut off from an army that no longer exists, each regiment dies alone, some on the heights of Rossomme, some on the plain of Mont-Saint-Jean; with them, Hugo says, died Ulm, Wagram, Jena and Friedland.","At twilight, toward nine, one square remains in the fatal valley at the foot of the slope the cuirassiers climbed, surrounded by the victorious English, under a frightful density of fire. It is commanded by an obscure officer named Cambronne. At each discharge the square shrinks and answers, drawing in its four walls; distant fugitives pause to listen to that ever-diminishing thunder. When the legion is a handful, the flag a rag, the muskets clubs for want of cartridges, and the heap of corpses larger than the group of survivors, a sort of sacred terror falls on the conquerors and the English guns go silent. In the dusk the dying men see the ring of lit matches like tigers' eyes and hear the pieces being loaded, and an English general, Colville or Maitland, shouts, \\"Surrender, brave Frenchmen!\\" Cambronne replies: \\"Merde!\\""],["If a French reader's susceptibilities forbid repeating the word, Hugo says, he will violate the prohibition at his own risk, for it would mean leaving something sublime out of history. Among those giants there was one Titan, Cambronne. To make that reply and then perish is the grandest thing possible, and it was not his fault that he survived being shot. The winner of Waterloo was not Napoleon, put to flight, nor Wellington, giving way at four and despairing at five, nor Blücher, who did no fighting: it was Cambronne. To thunder such an answer at the lightning that kills you is to conquer.","Hugo piles up what the word does: it answers the rainstorm, the wall of Hougomont, the sunken road, Grouchy's delay and Blücher's arrival; it drowns the European coalition in two syllables; it ends Waterloo with carnival and Leonidas with Rabelais; it loses the field and keeps history. Faced with a victory in which no one is truly victorious, this earthworm of war, offered the mockery of life, finds in his soul the lowest word and makes it the loftiest. He invents the word for Waterloo as Rouget de Lisle invented the Marseillaise, and he hurls it not only at Europe in the name of the Empire but at the past in the name of the Revolution; Danton seems to speak, Kléber to bellow.","At Cambronne's word the English voice answers, \\"Fire!\\" The batteries flame, the hill trembles, a last gush of grapeshot rolls out in white smoke under the rising moon, and when it clears nothing is left: the Guard is dead, the four walls of the living redoubt lie prone, with scarcely a quiver among the bodies. So the French legions expired on Mont-Saint-Jean, on the spot where today, Hugo notes, Joseph the postman from Nivelles passes at four in the morning, whistling and cheerfully whipping up his horse."],["Waterloo, Hugo says, is as obscure to those who won it as to those who lost; Napoleon saw panic, Blücher fire, Wellington nothing at all, and the historians, Jomini, Muffling and even the admirable Charras, fumble in its dazzle. In an event stamped with superhuman necessity the part of men amounts to nothing. Taking Waterloo from Wellington and Blücher deprives England and Germany of nothing: in that age Germany had Schiller above Blücher and England Byron above Wellington; civilized peoples are not elevated or abased by a captain's fortune. Often a battle is lost and progress is won. What is Waterloo? Not a victory but the winning number in the lottery, the stake won by Europe and paid by France. It was not worth putting a lion there.","Napoleon and Wellington are not enemies but opposites: on one side precision, foresight, method, war regulated watch in hand; on the other intuition, divination, a flaming glance, faith in a star. Wellington was the Barême of war, Napoleon its Michelangelo, and genius was beaten by calculation; both waited for someone, and the calculator's man, Blücher, came while Grouchy did not. Wellington is classic war taking its revenge for the young Corsican who had humiliated the old school in Italy; the academy's rancour wrote \\"Waterloo\\" beneath Lodi, Arcola and Mantua. In his decline Napoleon found Wurmser in front of him again, and to get Wurmser it sufficed to whiten Wellington's hair. \\"Waterloo is a battle of the first order, won by a captain of the second.\\"","What must be admired is England: not her captain but her army, the Scots Greys, the Horse Guards, the Highlanders playing the pibroch under grapeshot, the raw recruits holding against the veterans of Essling and Rivoli. Wellington, ungratefully, called it a \\"detestable army.\\" England has made Wellington too great and herself too small; the column should bear the statue of a people, not a man, though Hugo expects England, still cherishing the feudal illusion of heredity and hierarchy, to resent that. He marvels at the cleverness of chance, rain, wall, sunken road, Grouchy deaf, one guide deceiving and another enlightening, and concludes it was more massacre than battle: the narrowest front for so many combatants, seventy-two thousand on each side, fifty-six percent French losses, sixty thousand dead. The field is calm now, but at night, he says, a visionary mist rises and the dreamer sees the whole slaughter fought again by spectres."],["There is a respectable liberal school that does not hate Waterloo; Hugo does not belong to it. To him Waterloo is the stupefied date of liberty, an eagle hatched unexpectedly from such an egg. Viewed from the summit, it is intentionally a counter-revolutionary victory: Europe against France, Petersburg, Berlin and Vienna against Paris, the status quo against the initiative, 14 July 1789 attacked through 20 March 1815, the Brunswicks, Nassaus, Romanovs, Hohenzollerns and Habsburgs in solidarity with the Bourbons. The dream was the final extinction of a people that had been in eruption for twenty-six years.","But because the Empire had been despotic, the restored kingdom was forced by reaction to be liberal, and a constitutional order was Waterloo's unwilling result, to the conquerors' regret. Revolution, being providential, cannot really be conquered: before Waterloo it worked through Bonaparte overturning thrones, after it through Louis XVIII signing a charter. Call revolution Progress, and progress Tomorrow; Tomorrow has no bad tools. It uses Wellington to make an orator of General Foy, who falls at Hougomont and rises again in the tribune; it uses the conqueror from without and the gouty invalid from within. By cutting short the demolition of thrones by the sword, Waterloo only turned the revolutionary work into another channel: the slashers had finished, it was the thinkers' turn.","What triumphed at Waterloo, what smiled behind Wellington, trundled barrows of bones to raise the lion's mound and encouraged Blücher's sabres, was the counter-revolution, which murmured the infamous word \\"dismemberment\\"; but arriving in Paris and seeing the crater close at hand, it changed its mind and stammered a charter instead. There is no intentional liberty in Waterloo. The counter-revolution was involuntarily liberal, as Napoleon had been involuntarily revolutionary. On 18 June 1815, \\"the mounted Robespierre was hurled from his saddle.\\""],["End of the dictatorship; a whole European system crumbles. The Empire sinks into a gloom like the expiring Roman world, and Hugo grants that it was wept for by heroic eyes: it had shed over the earth all the light tyranny can give, a sombre light. Louis XVIII re-enters Paris; the dances of 8 July efface the enthusiasm of 20 March; the white flag flies on the Tuileries; the Corsican becomes the antithesis of the Bearnese. Europe adopts the white cockade; the Imperial Guard gives way to the Maison Rouge; the Arc du Carrousel, ashamed of Marengo and Arcola, hides behind a statue of the Duc d'Angoulême; the pauper's grave of the Madeleine is covered in marble for Louis XVI and Marie Antoinette; a column in the moat of Vincennes recalls the Duc d'Enghien; Pope Pius VII, who crowned Napoleon, blesses his fall; at Schoenbrunn a four-year-old shadow may not be called King of Rome. All because, one summer afternoon, a shepherd said to a Prussian in the forest, \\"Go this way, and not that!\\"","This 1815 was a lugubrious April: old poisonous realities under new appearances, a lie wedded to 1789, divine right masked by a charter, prejudices and mental reservations varnished with liberalism, the serpent changing its skin. Napoleon had made man both greater and smaller; the people, that cannon fodder so fond of the cannoneer, looked for him everywhere, and a veteran told that he was dead answered, \\"You don't know him.\\" The kings filled the void with a Holy Alliance, which the field of Belle-Alliance had named in advance.","Against this reconstructed old Europe a new France is sketched, bearing on its brow the star Liberty, and the young are in love at once with the future and with the past, with Liberty and with Napoleon. Defeat has made him greater; Bonaparte fallen seems loftier than Napoleon erect; England guards him with Hudson Lowe and France watches him with Montchenu; Alexander calls him \\"my sleeplessness,\\" and the kings reign ill at ease with the rock of Saint Helena on the horizon. While he dies at Longwood, the sixty thousand dead of Waterloo rot quietly and the Congress of Vienna makes the treaties that Europe calls the Restoration. And what, Hugo asks, does any of it matter to the Infinite, before whose eye a grub hopping between grass blades equals the eagle soaring between the towers of Notre-Dame?"],["Returning \\"of necessity\\" to the battlefield, Hugo notes that the full moon of 18 June aided Blücher's murderous pursuit. The English bivouac in the French camp beyond Rossomme and Wellington goes to the village of Waterloo to write his report to Lord Bathurst; the village, which took no part, carries off all the honour from Mont-Saint-Jean, Hougomont, La Haie-Sainte, Papelotte and Plancenoit. Hugo then turns to one of war's hideous features: the dawn after a battle rises on naked corpses. He will not blame the soldier; every army drags behind it a rearguard of stragglers, half brigands and half lackeys, sham invalids, sutlers in little carts with their wives, marauders who belong to no nation and change sides with the victor. Hoche and Marceau had none; Wellington had few, and ordered anyone caught robbing the dead to be shot, yet that night the dead were robbed all the same.","Toward midnight a man is prowling and climbing toward the hollow road of Ohain, neither soldier nor peasant, a ghoul drawn by the scent of corpses, in a blouse like a greatcoat, with no sack but large pockets, stopping to stoop over something silent on the ground and then fleeing on. Some distance off, hidden behind a hovel at the corner of the Nivelles road, a small sutler's wagon with a wicker hood waits with a starving nag, and in it a sort of woman seated on coffers and bundles; perhaps, Hugo says, there is some connection between the wagon and the prowler. Hougomont and La Haie-Sainte still burn at either end of the English line of fires. The narrator pauses to imagine the death of the cuirassiers in the sunken road, alive and laughing one minute and crushed beneath horses the next; the road is now level with the plain, filled with bodies to the brim, a river of blood running out to the Nivelles highway.","The prowler searches this vast tomb, walking in blood. He stops: a hand projects from under the heap, lit by the moon, with a gold ring on its finger. He crouches, and when he rises the ring is gone. As he stands, the hand closes on the skirt of his coat; an honest man would have been terrified, but he laughs, saying he prefers a ghost to a gendarme. Curious whether the dead man is alive, he digs him out and drags him clear: a cuirassier officer of considerable rank, helmetless, his face a mask of blood from a sabre cut, his limbs apparently unbroken because the dead above him had arched over him. The prowler tears the silver cross of the Legion of Honour from his breastplate, takes his watch from his fob and his purse from his waistcoat. At this stage of his \\"succour\\" the officer opens his eyes and whispers thanks.","The officer asks who won; \\"The English.\\" He tells the man to look in his pockets and take his watch and purse; the prowler pretends to search and says there is nothing there, and the officer says he has been robbed and is sorry, for they should have been his. A patrol approaches and the man makes to go; the officer holds him weakly: \\"You have saved my life. Who are you?\\" The prowler says he too belonged to the French army, that he will be shot if caught, and that the officer must get out of the scrape himself. His rank: sergeant. His name: \\"Thénardier.\\" The officer says he will not forget that name, and gives his own. \\"My name is Pontmercy.\\""],["Jean Valjean has been recaptured. Hugo passes over the details by quoting two newspapers of 25 July 1823. The royalist Drapeau Blanc reports that the man known as M. Madeleine, who had revived the jet-and-black-glass industry of a Pas-de-Calais district and been made mayor, was discovered by the police to be the ban-breaking ex-convict Jean Valjean and has been returned to prison; before his arrest he withdrew more than half a million francs, honestly earned, from M. Laffitte, and no one knows where he has hidden it.","The Journal de Paris adds more: he escaped after his arrest, was retaken three or four days later in Paris as he was boarding one of the little coaches that run to the village of Montfermeil, is believed to have used the interval to withdraw six or seven hundred thousand francs and hide them, and was then tried at the Var Assizes for highway robbery with violence committed eight years earlier on a little Savoyard chimney-sweep. He refused to defend himself; the prosecution proved to its satisfaction that he belonged to a band of robbers in the south; he was condemned to death and refused to appeal; the King commuted the sentence to penal servitude for life, and he was taken at once to Toulon. Some papers presented the commutation as a triumph of the priestly party. In the galleys he was given a new number: 9,430.","Hugo adds, so as not to return to it, that everything Madeleine foresaw during his night of hesitation came true. Montreuil-sur-Mer's prosperity vanished with him: foremen set up as rival manufacturers, workshops closed, methods and products were debased, workers scattered, wages fell, bankruptcy followed, and there was nothing left for the poor. Within four years the cost of collecting taxes in the district had doubled, a fact M. de Villèle pointed out in the Chamber in February 1827."],["Hugo first tells the local superstition: in the Montfermeil forest one may meet at nightfall a black man with horns, digging a hole. Speak to him and you die within the week; dig up his hole after he leaves and you die within the month; flee and you die within the year. The second course, which at least yields a treasure for a month, is the one most often chosen, and a medieval sorcerer-monk named Tryphon left two lines of bad Latin on what the digger finds: a coin, a stone, a corpse, a phantom, or nothing.","Shortly after the time when the prosecuting attorney believed the escaped convict Jean Valjean had been prowling about Montfermeil, villagers noticed that an old road-mender named Boulatruelle, a former galley-slave under police supervision, employed cheaply on the Gagny–Lagny road and thought too humble and too servile toward gendarmes to be honest, had taken to leaving his work early and going into the forest with a pickaxe, where he was seen in the wildest thickets searching and digging holes. Village gossip concluded the devil had appeared and Boulatruelle was after his hoard. Then his excursions stopped and he went back to stone-breaking.","The schoolmaster and Thénardier, the tavern-keeper, remained curious, suspecting a windfall more solid than the devil's. Thénardier shrugged that no one knows who has been in the galleys or will be, and proposed \\"the wine test.\\" Boulatruelle drank enormously and said little, but from his scattered words they pieced together a story: one morning at daybreak he found a shovel and pick hidden in the underbrush; that evening, hidden behind a tree, he saw a stranger \\"whom he, Boulatruelle, knew well\\" (Thénardier's gloss: a comrade of the galleys) going into the densest part of the wood carrying something square like a small trunk; he dared not follow a man three times as strong as he and armed with a pick; two or three hours later, by moonlight, the man came out carrying only the shovel and pick, and in the morning the tools were gone from their hiding place. Boulatruelle concluded that a chest too small for a body, hence full of money, had been buried, and he had dug wherever the ground looked freshly turned, in vain. Montfermeil forgot the matter, except for a few gossips who were sure the devil had come."],["Toward the end of October 1823 the ship of the line Orion, part of the Mediterranean squadron, enters the port of Toulon after heavy weather. Hugo pauses on the twenty-two useless salute guns and reckons the civilized world fires 150,000 pointless shots a day while the poor starve. He then digresses on \\"the Spanish war\\" of 1823: a Bourbon family affair in which France, under the Duc d'Angoulême, marched to crush Spanish liberals, with Napoleon's old soldiers under the white cockade, citadels bought rather than taken, little blood and no honour, a war that wounded the military spirit and enraged the democratic one. The Bourbons mistook it for strength and took the soldier's obedience for the nation's consent; 1830, he says, had its germ in 1823.","He returns to the Orion and to a long hymn to the ship of the line, a floating forest of three thousand cubic metres with a mainmast sixty fathoms tall, a masterpiece of man that a gale can nevertheless snap like straw; this is why crowds gather to stare at warships. The Orion, barnacled and leaking after an earlier scraping had loosened the bolts of her keel, had been further damaged in an equinoctial gale and was anchored near the Arsenal for repairs, watched daily by idlers on the quays.","One morning the crowd sees an accident: a topman bending the main-topsail loses his footing and is left hanging from a footrope, swinging over the sea like a stone in a sling, slowly losing his strength while the newly levied sailors dare not go to him. Suddenly a man in the red jacket and green cap of a life convict climbs into the rigging like a cat; a gust takes his cap and shows a head of perfectly white hair. A convict from the detachment working aboard had asked the officer of the watch for permission to risk his life, and on a nod had broken the chain riveted to his ankle with one blow of a hammer, a thing no one noticed at the time. He runs out along the yard, fastens a rope, lowers himself hand over hand until two men hang over the gulf instead of one, lashes the sailor to the rope, climbs back and drags him up, and carries him along the yard to the top and the arms of his comrades. The crowd applauds, old convict-sergeants weep, and every voice cries, \\"Pardon for that man!\\"","Descending to rejoin his detachment he seems to stagger on a lower yard, and a shout goes up: he has fallen into the sea between the Orion and the frigate Algésiras. Four men put off in a boat; the man never surfaces; they sound and dive until evening and do not find even the body. Next day the Toulon newspaper reports, under the date 17 November 1823, that a convict of the Orion detachment, returning from rescuing a sailor, fell into the sea and drowned, his body presumably caught among the piles of the Arsenal point; he was committed under the number 9,430 and his name was Jean Valjean."],["Hugo describes Montfermeil, on the plateau between the Ourcq and the Marne: in 1823 not yet a resort of plaster villas but a cheap, charming forest village on the road to nowhere, whose one hardship was water. The Gagny end used the woodland ponds; the end around the church, where the Thénardier tavern stood, had only a little spring halfway down the slope toward Chelles, a quarter of an hour away. Better houses paid a water-carrier half a farthing a bucket, but he worked only till seven in summer and five in winter, and after dark anyone who wanted water fetched it himself.","This was the terror of little Cosette. The Thénardiers had profited from her twice over, making the mother pay and the child serve, and when the mother's payments stopped they simply kept her as their servant; it was she who ran for water, and she took great care there was always some in the house so that she would never be sent to the spring at night.","Christmas of 1823 is mild and lively: Paris showmen have set up booths in the main street and the Church Square, spreading into Boulanger Alley where the Thénardiers' inn stands, and a menagerie shows a Brazilian vulture with a tricolour cockade for an eye, which retired Bonapartist soldiers come devoutly to see. On Christmas Eve the public room is full of carters and peddlers drinking and smoking by candlelight; Madame Thénardier tends the supper roasting on the fire while her husband drinks with the customers and talks politics about the Spanish war, amid talk of vintages, milling and mowing. Cosette sits in her usual place on the crossbar of the kitchen table, in rags and wooden shoes, knitting woollen stockings for the Thénardier girls; Éponine and Azelma can be heard laughing in the next room; a cat-o'-nine-tails hangs on a nail by the chimney. From somewhere in the house comes the crying of a little boy, three years old, born to the Thénardiers one winter, whom his mother nursed but does not love and leaves to shriek in the dark."],["Thénardier has just turned fifty; his wife is nearing forty, which in a woman, Hugo says, equals fifty. She is tall, blond, red, fat and square, one of those colossal fairground women; she does all the housework, with Cosette as her only servant, a mouse in the service of an elephant. She has a beard, swears splendidly, can crack a nut with her fist and reads sentimental romances; to hear her speak is to think of a gendarme, to see her drink a carter, to see her handle Cosette the hangman.","Thénardier is small, thin, pale and feeble-looking and in fact perfectly healthy; he smiles habitually as a precaution and is almost polite to everyone, even the beggar he refuses. He has the glance of a polecat and the bearing of a man of letters, drops the names of Voltaire, Raynal and Saint Augustine, claims to have \\"a system,\\" and is a scientific thief. He likes to tell how, as a sergeant at Waterloo, he alone covered with his body and saved a dangerously wounded general under grapeshot, which gave his tavern its sign and its name, the Sergeant of Waterloo. He is a liberal and a Bonapartist and is said to have studied for the priesthood; Hugo thinks he studied in Holland for an innkeeper, some Fleming from Lille at home on both sides of the frontier. As for his Waterloo prowess, the reader already knows what it was and may notice that he exaggerates a trifle: in June 1815 he belonged to the marauding sutlers who follow armies in rickety carts with wife and children, attaching themselves to the winning side, and his little capital of purses, watches, rings and silver crosses gathered from the dead set him up at Montfermeil.","Everyone takes the wife for the master; in truth the frail husband rules the mastodon with a word or a sign, and she adores and fears him. She loves no one but her children, and her maternity, Hugo notes, stops at her daughters and does not extend to boys. He thinks only of getting rich and fails, ruining himself at Montfermeil; in 1823 he owes some fifteen hundred francs in petty debts, which worries him. He is an admirable poacher and a theorist of hospitality, whose creed is to sell the traveller everything, down to the flies his dog eats. Ruse and rage wedded, Hugo calls them, a hideous team.","Cosette is between them, ground in a mill and torn with pincers at once. The woman's method is blows; the man's is that she goes barefoot in winter. She runs up and down stairs, washes, sweeps, scrubs and carries heavy loads, weak as she is, with no mercy from either; the tavern is a spider's web in which she lies trembling. The child says nothing."],["Four new travellers have arrived. Cosette, though only eight, reflects with the gloomy air of an old woman; her eye is blackened from Madame Thénardier's fist, which the woman uses as a joke about how ugly she is. She thinks that it is very dark, that the pitchers upstairs must have been filled, and that there is no more water in the cistern; she is reassured only because no one in that house drinks water. When Madame Thénardier draws a glass from the cistern and finds only a trickle, the child stops breathing, but the woman decides it is enough.","Then a peddler lodging at the inn comes in and says harshly that his horse has not been watered. Madame Thénardier says it has; he insists it has not. Cosette crawls out from under the table and swears the horse drank a whole bucketful and that she talked to him; it is a lie, and the peddler, who knows how his horse blows when thirsty, calls her a little jade. Madame Thénardier agrees the beast must be watered, hunts out \\"that other beast\\" cowering among the drinkers' feet, and orders \\"Mademoiselle Dog-lack-name\\" to go and water the horse. Cosette says feebly that there is no water. The woman flings the street door open: then go and get some.","Cosette fetches the empty bucket from the chimney corner; it is bigger than she is and she could have sat in it. Grumbling that there is plenty at the spring and that she should have strained her onions, Madame Thénardier rummages in a drawer and hands \\"Mam'selle Toad\\" a fifteen-sou piece to buy a big loaf at the baker's on the way back. Cosette puts the coin in the little pocket of her apron and stands motionless before the open door, as if waiting for someone to rescue her. \\"Get along with you!\\" The door closes behind her."],["The line of booths from the church runs as far as the Thénardiers' inn and is all lit up for the crowds going to midnight mass, an effect the schoolmaster, drinking at the tavern, calls magical; there is not a star in the sky. The last stall, exactly opposite the tavern door, is a toyshop glittering with tinsel, glass and tin, and in front of it, on white napkins, sits an immense doll nearly two feet tall, in a pink crepe dress with gold wheat-ears in her real hair and enamel eyes. All day it has drawn every child under ten, but no mother in Montfermeil is rich or extravagant enough to buy it. Éponine and Azelma have spent hours gazing at it, and even Cosette has stolen a glance.","Coming out with her bucket, Cosette cannot help lifting her eyes to the doll, which she calls \\"the lady.\\" She has never seen it close. The shop is a palace to her, the doll a vision of joy, splendour and riches; she measures with a child's sad sagacity the abyss between herself and it, decides one must be a queen or at least a princess to own such a thing, and thinks how happy the doll must be. The other dolls behind seem fairies and genii, and the pacing merchant something like God the Father. In this adoration she forgets everything, even her errand, until Madame Thénardier, glancing into the street, shouts at the \\"silly jade\\" and \\"little monster\\" to get along. Cosette flees, dragging her pail, with the longest strides she can manage."],["Since the tavern is at the church end of the village, Cosette must go to the spring in the forest toward Chelles. She looks at no other stall; beyond the last lights she plunges into darkness, rattling the bucket handle for company. A woman who sees her mutters about a werewolf child, then recognizes her: \\"it's the Lark!\\" Cosette keeps a tolerable boldness while there are walls on either side, but at the last house she stops and scratches her head in a child's gesture of terror and indecision: before her lie the open fields, beasts walking in the grass, spectres in the trees. She turns back, resolving to say there was no water; a hundred paces on she stops again, seeing Madame Thénardier's hyena mouth before her, and it is from the Thénardier that she recoils. She runs out of the village and into the forest, no longer looking or listening.","The spring is only seven or eight minutes from the edge of the wood and she knows the way by day; instinct guides her and she does not get lost. It is a little clay basin about two feet deep, edged with moss and tall grasses, with a brook running from it. Holding a young oak that leans over the water, she bends and plunges the bucket in, so violently excited that her strength is trebled; she does not notice that her apron pocket empties itself into the spring and the fifteen-sou piece sinks. She drags out the bucket nearly full and collapses on the grass, exhausted.","Above her the sky is black cloud; Jupiter is setting through mist, swollen and ruddy like a luminous wound, and the unfamiliar star terrifies her. The forest is dark and wintry, the grasses undulating like eels, nettles twisting like clawed arms. Hugo meditates on the dread of night in a forest, which no stoutness of heart escapes and which is indescribably sinister for a child: forests are apocalypses. Cosette feels seized by the black enormity of nature, something worse than fear; she begins by instinct to count aloud to ten, over and over, and this brings her back to herself. Her wet hands are cold; she has one thought, to flee to the lighted windows; but such is her fear of the Thénardier that she dare not go without the water.","She can barely lift the bucket. She goes a dozen paces and must set it down, then a little further, bent double like an old woman, the iron handle freezing her hands, cold water splashing her bare legs, in the depths of a forest at night in winter, a child of eight seen by no one but God, and, Hugo adds, surely by her mother. She dares not even weep for fear of the Thénardier, whom she always imagines present. At an old chestnut tree she knows she takes a last long rest, lifts the bucket again, and cannot keep from crying, \\"O my God! my God!\\" At that moment the bucket weighs nothing: an enormous hand has taken the handle. A tall black form is walking beside her, a man who came up behind her unheard and has silently taken her burden. There are instincts for all the encounters of life. The child is not afraid."],["On the afternoon of Christmas Day 1823 a man walks the most deserted stretch of the Boulevard de l'Hôpital in the faubourg Saint-Marceau, apparently seeking a lodging, and the narrator says he did in fact hire a room in that isolated quarter. He is the type of the well-bred beggar: extreme poverty and extreme cleanliness, an old well-brushed round hat, a threadbare ochre-yellow coat, black breeches gray at the knee, buckled shoes. His white hair and worn face suggest more than sixty, his vigorous tread barely fifty; there is something severe and humble in the set of his lip and a melancholy serenity in his eyes. In his left hand he carries a small bundle in a handkerchief; in his right he leans on a hedge-cut cudgel dressed up with a red wax head to look like a cane.","At that period Louis XVIII drove nearly every day to Choisy-le-Roi, and his gilded carriage thundered along the boulevard at two o'clock, a clock for the poor women of the quarter; Hugo describes the fat, crafty-eyed, impotent king who could not walk and so loved to gallop, and the local verdict, \\"That big fellow yonder is the government.\\" The stranger, evidently not of the quarter, is surprised by the cavalcade and hides behind a wall, which does not prevent the Duc de Havré, sitting opposite the king, from remarking an evil-looking man. A police agent is ordered to follow him and loses him in the twilight streets, as a report that evening to the Prefect of Police records.","The man doubles his pace, often looking back, pauses at the Porte Saint-Martin theatre to read a poster for a play called The Two Convicts, and at the Pewter Platter takes the last seat, beside the driver, on the half-past-four coach for Lagny, paying his fare to Lagny after the coachman eyes his shabby clothes. He answers only in monosyllables. At six they reach Chelles, where he gets down with his bundle and cudgel and vanishes without entering the inn; the coachman observes that a man who pays to Lagny and stops at Chelles on a night when every house is shut must have dived through the earth.","He strides down the main street of Chelles, turns right before the church onto the Montfermeil crossroad like someone who knows the country, hides in a ditch when he hears passers-by on the Gagny–Lagny road, then leaves the road and enters the forest. There he slows, examining the trees as if following a secret path, loses his way for a moment, and reaches a clearing with a great heap of whitish stones. He inspects the stones, passes his hand over the warts on the bark of a nearby ash, stands on tiptoe to touch a band of zinc nailed to a peeling chestnut opposite, and treads the ground between tree and stones like a man making sure the soil has not been disturbed. Then he takes his bearings and walks on through the woods toward Montfermeil. It was this man who had just met Cosette: he had seen a tiny shadow groaning under an enormous bucket, setting it down and taking it up, and had come up and silently taken the handle."],["The man speaks in a grave, almost bass voice: what she carries is very heavy for her. He takes the bucket and walks beside her, muttering that it really is heavy. She is eight; she has come from the spring in the forest; the inn is a good quarter of an hour away. Abruptly he says, \\"So you have no mother.\\" She does not know; other people have mothers and she has none; she thinks she never had one. He sets down the bucket, puts both hands on her shoulders and tries to see her thin, sickly face in the dark, and asks her name. \\"Cosette.\\" He seems to receive an electric shock, looks at her again, picks up the bucket and walks on.","She lives at Montfermeil; Madame Thénardier, her mistress, who keeps the inn, sent her for water. In a voice he tries to keep indifferent but which trembles, he asks what Madame Thénardier does, then says he will lodge there tonight and she must show him the way. Cosette follows without fatigue, looking up at him from time to time with a confidence she cannot name; never taught to pray, she feels something like hope rising in her. There is no servant in the house; she is alone there, except for two little girls, Ponine and Zelma, Madame Thénardier's young ladies, who have beautiful dolls full of gold and play all day long. And she? She works, all day long, she says gently, a tear in her great eyes. When they let her she amuses herself as best she can; the girls will not lend her their dolls, and she has only a little lead sword, no longer than her finger, which cuts salad and the heads of flies.","They reach the village, pass the baker's without Cosette remembering the bread, and he falls into gloomy silence. Seeing the booths he asks whether there is a fair; no, it is Christmas. Near the tavern Cosette timidly touches his arm and asks whether she may take her bucket now: if Madame sees that someone carried it for her, she will beat her. He hands it to her, and a moment later they are at the door."],["Madame Thénardier greets Cosette with abuse, then switches to her innkeeper's smile for the gentleman wanting a lodging, and back to scowling when she sees his threadbare coat and battered hat and her husband signals \\"a regular beggar.\\" She has no room; he says to put him anywhere and he will pay as for a room; she names forty sous, double the usual price, since she does not lodge poor folks for less. He sits with a bottle of wine he barely touches and watches Cosette, back under the table at her knitting. Hugo describes the child in detail: thin and pale, looking six rather than eight, eyes sunk in shadow and almost put out with weeping, a mouth curved in habitual anguish, hands ruined with chilblains, a rag of linen for clothing and black-and-blue marks where the Thénardier has touched her; her whole body expresses one idea, fear. She has never prayed or set foot in a church.","The Thénardier remembers the bread. Cosette lies that the baker's was shut; the woman demands the fifteen-sou piece back, and Cosette, turning her pocket inside out, finds nothing and is petrified. The woman reaches for the cat-o'-nine-tails; the stranger, who has been fumbling in his fob, bends to the floor, says he saw something roll from the child's apron, and holds out a coin. It is a twenty-sou piece, not fifteen, but the Thénardier pockets it with a glare at Cosette. Éponine and Azelma come in, pretty, plump, warmly and prettily dressed, and settle in the chimney corner with a doll; to them Cosette is a dog. When the Thénardier catches Cosette watching them instead of knitting and threatens the whip, the stranger says timidly, \\"let her play,\\" and, learning that she is knitting stockings for the daughters worth thirty sous, buys them for five francs, lays the coin on the table and tells Cosette, \\"Now I own your work; play, my child.\\" Thénardier pockets the coin and tells his wife he has seen millionaires in coats like that. Cosette dresses her little lead sword in rags and sings it to sleep, while the sisters swaddle the cat. Hugo reflects on the doll as the first form of motherhood.","The Thénardier, now calling him Monsieur, sidles up and explains that the child is not theirs but a little beggar taken in from charity, a sort of imbecile with water on the brain, whose mother in her native place has not answered in six months and must be dead; her mother \\"didn't amount to much\\" and abandoned her. Cosette, under the table, half hears and rocks her sword-baby singing, \\"My mother is dead!\\" The man orders bread and cheese for supper; the woman decides he is a beggar after all. Cosette sees the sisters' discarded doll on the floor, creeps out when no one is watching and takes it back to her corner; only the traveller sees. A quarter of an hour of ecstasy ends when Azelma spots a pink foot in the firelight. Éponine runs to her mother; the Thénardier shrieks \\"Cosette!\\" twice; Cosette lays the doll down with veneration and despair, wrings her hands, and, for the first time in the whole terrible day, bursts into sobs. The traveller asks what the matter is; the woman says the beggar has touched the children's doll with her dirty hands. He walks out the street door, and the Thénardier kicks Cosette under the table. He returns carrying the fabulous doll from the stall opposite and stands it in front of her: \\"Here; this is for you.\\"","Cosette retreats under the table as if from the sun; the whole room falls silent. Thénardier, scenting money, mutters to his wife that the thing cost at least thirty francs and she must grovel; both urge Cosette in honeyed voices to take her doll. She asks permission of Madame, then of the stranger, whose eyes are full of tears, seizes the doll, and announces, \\"I shall call her Catherine.\\" She sets Catherine on a chair and sits on the floor gazing at her, and when told to play says, \\"Oh! I am playing.\\" The Thénardier, who hates the man more than anyone in the world at that moment, sends her daughters and then Cosette to bed; Cosette goes with Catherine in her arms. The wife rages in whispers about forty-franc dolls for a jade she would sell for forty sous; the husband answers that a traveller may do as he likes so long as he pays. The other customers now regard the stranger with awe. The mass ends, the inn empties, and the man sits unmoving until two in the morning, when the wife goes to bed, and three, when Thénardier, who has read his newspaper three times, asks whether Monsieur is going to his \\"repose,\\" a word that swells a bill, and leads him to the mahogany bridal chamber, whose wedding bonnet under glass he lies about, since he bought the whole room second-hand. In their own room his wife says she will turn Cosette out tomorrow; he says only, \\"How you do go on!\\"","Alone, the traveller takes off his shoes, takes a candle, and follows the sound of a child breathing to a triangular recess under the staircase, where among old papers and cobwebs Cosette sleeps fully dressed on a holed straw pallet with no sheets, clutching the doll whose open eyes glitter in the dark, one wooden shoe beside her. Through an open door he sees the two little white beds of Éponine and Azelma, and behind them the cradle of the little boy who cried all evening. On the cold hearth he finds two dainty children's shoes, set out on Christmas Eve for the good fairy, each already holding a bright new ten-sou piece from their mother; and in the darkest corner a frightful wooden sabot caked in ashes and mud, which Cosette, with the trust of a child who has never been anything but disappointed, has placed there too. There is nothing in it. He puts a louis d'or in Cosette's shoe and goes back to his room with the stealthy tread of a wolf."],["Two hours before daybreak Thénardier sits by a candle composing the bill while his wife watches in religious admiration and the Lark sweeps the stairs: supper three francs, room ten, candle five, fire four, service (spelled \\"servisse\\") one, total twenty-three francs. She fears it is too much and that he will not pay; Thénardier laughs coldly, says he will pay, reminds himself that he owes fifteen hundred francs, tells her to hand the bill to the man, and goes out. When she again declares she will turn Cosette out today, he only says, \\"How you do go on!\\"","The traveller comes down with his bundle and cudgel; Thénardier reappears silently behind him in the half-open door, visible only to his wife. She, unusually embarrassed, hands him the bill; he glances at it absently and asks instead whether business is good. She laments hard times, taxes, and above all the cost of \\"the little one, the Lark as she is called hereabouts,\\" who has more the air of a bat; she has daughters of her own to raise. In a voice he strives to keep indifferent he asks, \\"What if one were to rid you of her?\\" Her face brightens hideously: take her, keep her, carry her away, and the blessings of all the saints on him. Agreed; immediately; call the child. He looks at the bill, repeats \\"Twenty-three francs?\\" in a tone between exclamation and question, lays down five five-franc pieces, and tells her to fetch Cosette.","Now Thénardier advances: Monsieur owes twenty-six sous, twenty for the room and six for supper; as for the child, he must discuss that with the gentleman alone. His dazzled wife withdraws. Thénardier, all good-fellowship, declares that he adores the child, has brought her up since she was tiny, has spent four hundred francs on her medicines, cannot give away a child to a passer-by without knowing where she is going, and would need at least some scrap of a passport. The stranger answers gravely that one needs no passport to travel five leagues from Paris; if he takes Cosette he takes her, Thénardier will never know his name or residence or where she is, and she will never see him again. Yes or no? Thénardier, who has watched him all night like a cat and puzzled over who this man can be, grasps that he is dealing with someone very strong whose mystery is simple and unanswerable, and unmasks his batteries: \\"Sir, I am in need of fifteen hundred francs.\\" The man takes three banknotes from an old black pocketbook, lays his thumb on them, and says, \\"Go and fetch Cosette.\\"","Cosette, meanwhile, had run to her shoe on waking and found the gold piece, a new twenty-franc coin of the Restoration. She did not know what it was and hid it as if stolen, but she guessed where it came from; the doll and the coin frighten her, only the stranger reassures her, and for the first time in the five years she can remember she feels that she is not alone. Madame Thénardier fetches her from the stairs without a blow or an insult. The stranger unties his bundle: a little woollen gown, apron, bodice, kerchief, petticoat, stockings and shoes, a complete outfit for a girl of seven, all black, and tells her to dress quickly. As day breaks, Montfermeil sees a poorly dressed old man leading a little girl in mourning with a pink doll in her arms along the road toward Livry. Many do not recognize Cosette out of her rags. She does not know with whom she is going or where, only that she is leaving the Thénardier tavern; no one says goodbye. She walks gravely, looking at the sky and now and then at the louis in her new apron pocket, and feels as if she were beside the good God."],["A quarter of an hour after the departure Thénardier shows his wife the three notes, and for the first time in their marriage she criticizes him: \\"Is that all?\\" He agrees he is a fool, calls for his hat and runs out, first in the wrong direction. The man, he reasons, is \\"a million dressed in yellow\\" who gave twenty sous, then five francs, then fifty, then fifteen hundred with equal readiness and would have given fifteen thousand; and the mourning clothes ready in the bundle mean a mystery, and the secrets of the rich are sponges of gold to be squeezed. Told they went toward Gagny, he strikes through the forest, stops to curse himself for not bringing his gun, and goes on anyway. Hugo pauses on Thénardier as a double nature: in calm times the makings of a respectable tradesman, and given the right shock, every requisite of a blackguard, a shopkeeper with a taint of the monster.","Beyond the ponds, on the turf path above the old aqueduct of the Abbey of Chelles, he spots the man's hat over the brushwood and the head of the doll beside it; the stranger is letting Cosette rest. Thénardier walks round and presents himself, breathless and respectful, with the fifteen hundred francs: he is taking Cosette back. Cosette shudders and presses against the old man, who repeats slowly, \\"You are going to take back Co-sette?\\" Thénardier explains that he is an honest man, that the child belongs to her mother and he can give her up only to the mother, or, if the mother is dead, to someone bringing a paper signed by her naming the person to receive the child. The man reaches into his pocket; Thénardier shivers with joy, expecting a bribe. Instead the man takes out a single small paper and hands it to him: \\"You are right; read!\\" It is a note dated Montreuil-sur-Mer, 25 March 1823, addressed to Monsieur Thénardier, instructing him to deliver Cosette to the bearer and promising payment for all the little things, signed Fantine. Thénardier recognizes the signature, and is told he may keep the paper as his receipt. He mutters that the signature is fairly well imitated, and lets it go.","He tries a last effort: he must be paid for all those little things, a great deal is owing. The man rises, dusting his sleeve, and recites the account: in January the mother owed a hundred and twenty francs; in February Thénardier sent a bill for five hundred and received three hundred at the end of February and three hundred at the start of March; nine months since at the agreed fifteen francs a month make a hundred and thirty-five; so he had already been paid a hundred too much, thirty-five remained owing, and he has just received fifteen hundred. Thénardier feels the steel jaw of the trap, shakes himself like a wolf, drops all ceremony, and announces that he will take Cosette back unless he is given a thousand crowns. The stranger says tranquilly, \\"Come, Cosette,\\" takes her by the left hand and picks up his cudgel with the right. Thénardier notes the size of the cudgel and the solitude of the spot, and stands speechless as they walk into the forest; looking from the man's huge shoulders and fists to his own thin arms, he again regrets the gun, \\"since I was going hunting.\\"","He does not quite give up: he follows at a distance to learn where they are going, left with an irony, the paper signed Fantine, and a consolation, the fifteen hundred francs. The man leads Cosette toward Livry and Bondy, slowly, head bowed, turning now and then to look back, and when he sees Thénardier he plunges with the child into the brushwood. Thénardier closes in, but at the thickest point the man wheels round and looks at him uneasily, then goes on; two or three hundred paces later he turns again with so sombre a look that Thénardier judges it useless to go further and retraces his steps."],["Jean Valjean was not dead. When he fell, or rather threw himself, into the sea at Toulon he was not ironed; he swam underwater to an anchored vessel with a boat moored to it, hid in the boat until night, swam again to the shore near Cape Brun, and, having money, bought clothes at a country house near Balaguier that specialized in outfitting escaped convicts. Then came an obscure, undulating flight: Pradeaux near Beausset, Grand-Villard near Briançon in the Hautes-Alpes, traces later found in the Ain, in the Pyrenees at Accons, near Périgueux. He reached Paris, and the reader has just seen him at Montfermeil.","His first care in Paris was to buy mourning clothes for a girl of seven or eight and to find a lodging; then he went to Montfermeil, where, the narrator reminds us, he had already made a mysterious trip during his earlier escape, of which the law had caught an inkling. Being thought dead deepened the obscurity around him; when one of the Paris papers reporting his drowning fell into his hands he felt reassured and almost at peace, as if he really had died.","On the evening of the day he took Cosette from the Thénardiers he re-entered Paris at nightfall by the Barrière Monceaux, took a cab to the esplanade of the Observatoire, and from there led her on foot through the dark, deserted streets near the Ourcine and the Glacière toward the Boulevard de l'Hôpital. The day had been strange for Cosette: bread and cheese eaten behind hedges, carriages changed often, stretches on foot. She did not complain, but he felt her dragging more and more on his hand, so he took her on his back; still holding Catherine, she laid her head on his shoulder and fell asleep."],["The narrator leads the reader to a desolate quarter near the Salpêtrière and the Barrière d'Italie, a place that is neither city nor country, where grass grows in the ruts of the streets. At the corner of the Rue des Vignes-Saint-Marcel stands a low building that looks like a hovel from the road but is in fact enormous: only a door and one large window face the street. The door opens straight onto a steep, ladder-like staircase; the window, once handsome, is patched with paper and broken blinds. Upstairs, a long corridor gives onto a row of cheerless rooms more like stalls than chambers.","The postmen call it Number 50–52; the neighbourhood calls it the Gorbeau house. Hugo tells the anecdote behind the name: around 1770 two Châtelet attorneys named Corbeau (Raven) and Renard (Fox) were so mocked with La Fontaine parodies that they petitioned Louis XV to change their names. The king, in a jovial mood, let Corbeau become Gorbeau and Renard become Prenard. This Maître Gorbeau is said to have owned the building and installed its grand window.","The chapter closes on the gloomy surroundings in 1823: the city wall and its barrier close by, the road to Bicêtre along which the condemned returned to Paris, the elms of the Barrière Saint-Jacques hiding the scaffold, the Salpêtrière on one side and Bicêtre on the other. Hugo reflects on the oppressive symmetry of the place, its sinister aspect at night, and how the Orleans railway has since begun to transform the whole faubourg."],["It is before the Gorbeau house that Jean Valjean stops with Cosette in his arms. He lets himself in with a passkey, climbs the stairs, and opens a modestly furnished attic with a mattress, a table, chairs and a lit stove, with a small dressing-room and folding bed at the far end. He lays the child down without waking her, lights a candle and gazes at her face with a tenderness that is almost ecstasy. He kisses her hand, as nine months earlier he had kissed the hand of her sleeping mother, and kneels by her bed.","In broad daylight a passing cart shakes the building and Cosette starts awake crying 'Yes, madame, here I am!' and gropes for her broom. Then she sees Jean Valjean smiling at her and says, 'Ah, so it is true! Good morning, Monsieur.' She finds her doll Catherine at the foot of the bed and pours out questions: where is she, is Paris big, is Madame Thénardier far away, must she go back? She declares the frightful hole pretty, because she feels free. When she asks whether she must sweep, he tells her to play, and the day passes in happiness."],["Hugo pauses on what has happened inside Jean Valjean. In fifty-five years he has never been father, lover, husband or friend; his sister and her children are a faded memory. Now all the affection dammed up in him rushes toward this child, and he feels something like a mother's pangs without knowing what they are. After the Bishop, who brought the dawn of virtue, Cosette brings the dawn of love. Cosette, for her part, had a cold heart at eight because no one had let her love anything; from the first day her whole being attaches itself to this kind man, whom she finds handsome just as she finds the hovel pretty. The widower and the orphan, Hugo says, complete each other.","The refuge is well chosen. Their room has the only window in the house, so no neighbour can see in; the ground floor is a wagon-shed with no connection to the upper storey; the only other inhabitant is the old woman who calls herself the 'principal lodger' and acts as portress. Valjean had rented from her on Christmas Eve, presenting himself as a gentleman ruined by Spanish bonds who was coming to live with his little daughter, paying six months in advance and having her furnish the room.","Weeks pass. Cosette sings from daybreak; she is no longer in rags but in mourning. Valjean teaches her to read, remembering that he learned to read in prison with evil intentions, and that this skill now ends in teaching a child. He talks to her of her mother and makes her pray; she calls him 'father' and knows no other name for him. Hugo suggests that Valjean, embittered by Fantine's fate and Javert's authority and sent back to prison for doing right, may have needed this love to persevere in virtue: he is her support, and she is his prop."],["Jean Valjean never goes out by day. Each evening at twilight he walks for an hour or two, often with Cosette, keeping to deserted side alleys and visiting churches at nightfall, especially the nearby Saint-Médard. The old woman keeps house and does the marketing; they live soberly. Valjean still wears his yellow coat, black breeches and old hat, and in the street is taken for a poor man; kind women sometimes turn back to give him a sou, which he accepts with a bow. When he meets a beggar he looks about, slips him a coin, often silver, and hurries off. The neighbourhood begins to call him 'the beggar who gives alms.'","The old 'principal lodger,' a cross, slightly deaf and therefore talkative woman with two remaining teeth, watches him closely. She has questioned Cosette, who knows only that she came from Montfermeil. One morning she follows Valjean into an empty room and, through a crack in the door, sees him rip open the lining of his coat and take out a thousand-franc note. He then asks her to go and change it, calling it his quarterly income; she wonders where he could have received it, since he only goes out at six and the bank is shut by then. Her tale of the note sets the gossips of the Rue des Vignes-Saint-Marcel talking.","A few days later, while Valjean saws wood in the corridor, she examines the coat hanging on a nail. The lining has been sewn up, but she feels thicknesses of paper in the skirts and lapels, and the pockets hold needles and thread, a big pocketbook, a very large knife and several wigs of different colours. The chapter ends as winter draws to a close."],["Near Saint-Médard an old ex-beadle of seventy-five, who crouches by a condemned well mumbling prayers, is one of Valjean's regular beneficiaries; some say he belongs to the police. One evening, as Valjean gives him his alms, the beggar looks up sharply under the newly lit street lamp and drops his head again. In that flash Valjean thinks he has seen not the placid old beadle but a well-known and terrible face. He goes home shaken, hardly daring to admit that the face he thought he saw was Javert's. The next evening he goes back, speaks to the man, and finds the ordinary ex-beadle; he laughs at himself and thinks no more of it.","A few days afterwards, around eight in the evening, while Cosette spells aloud, Valjean hears the house door open and a heavy step come up the stairs, though the old woman always goes to bed at dark. He blows out the candle, sends Cosette softly to bed, and sits motionless. After a while he sees a light through the keyhole: someone with a candle is listening at his door. The light retreats without the sound of footsteps, as if the listener had removed his shoes. Valjean lies awake all night.","At daybreak a door creaks at the end of the corridor and the same step passes his room. Through the keyhole he sees only a tall man in a long frock-coat with a cudgel under his arm, but when the figure is silhouetted at the top of the stairs Valjean has a complete view of his back: the formidable neck and shoulders belong to Javert. When the old woman comes at seven she mentions that a new lodger, 'Dumont, or Daumont,' came in last night, 'a gentleman of property, like yourself.' Valjean rolls up a hundred francs; a five-franc piece slips from his hands and rolls noisily across the floor. At dark he scans the empty boulevard, goes back up, says 'Come' to Cosette, and the two go out."],["The author first begs leave to speak of the Paris of his youth in the present tense: he has been long absent, the city has been rebuilt, and he will describe streets and houses as he remembers them even if they no longer stand.","Jean Valjean leaves the boulevard at once and plunges into the side streets, doubling back on his track like a hunted stag. The moon is full; he keeps to the dark side of the streets and watches the light side. Cosette walks on without a question; her early sufferings have made her passive, and she feels safe with him. Valjean has no plan beyond never returning to the Gorbeau house. He is not even certain the man was Javert, or that Javert, if it was he, knows him under his disguise and believes him dead. He winds through the Rue Censier, the Rue Copeau and the lanes near Saint-Victor without entering any lodging house.","As eleven strikes he passes the police commissary's office at 14 Rue de Pontoise and, turning instinctively, sees three men pass under the commissary's lantern close behind him; one goes into the alley to the commissary's house. He hurries Cosette into the Passage des Patriarches and down to the Rue des Postes, where the streets form an open, moonlit square. He hides in a doorway to see whether he is followed. Within three minutes four tall men in long brown coats and round hats, with heavy cudgels, appear and stop to consult. Their leader points in the direction Valjean has taken; as he turns, the moon falls on his face, and Valjean recognises Javert."],["While the men hesitate, Valjean leaves his hiding place and goes down the Rue des Postes toward the Jardin des Plantes, carrying the tired Cosette. The street lamps are unlit because of the moon. He passes the Goblet potteries, the Rue de la Clef and the Fountain Saint-Victor, skirts the Jardin des Plantes and reaches the deserted quay. No one is behind him.","At the Pont d'Austerlitz, where tolls are still collected, he offers one sou and the old soldier in charge demands two, since the child he carries can walk. Vexed at having drawn attention, he pays and crosses in the shadow of a heavy cart going the same way. Halfway over Cosette, her feet numb, asks to walk, and he takes her hand. On the right bank he heads for some timber-yards, crossing an open, lit space without hesitation, believing himself hunted but no longer followed.","Before entering the narrow Rue du Chemin-Vert-Saint-Antoine between the timber-yards, he glances back and sees four shadows just stepping onto the bridge from the Jardin des Plantes side. He shudders like a recaptured animal. His one hope is that they did not see him cross the lit ground with Cosette; he commits himself to the dark little street."],["Three hundred paces on, the street forks like a Y. Valjean takes the right branch because it leads toward deserted ground rather than the inhabited suburb. He carries Cosette again, looking back from time to time. At last he sees something moving far behind him in the dark and hurries on, looking for a side street, until he reaches a wall bordering a cross-lane. To the right the lane ends in a blind alley closed by a high white wall; to the left it runs two hundred paces to an open street. As he turns toward the left he sees a man, like a black statue, posted at the corner, barring the way and waiting.","Hugo explains where Valjean is: between the Faubourg Saint-Antoine and La Râpée, a district since rebuilt out of recognition, which old Paris called Le Petit-Picpus. It barely existed as a quarter, more like a monkish Spanish town of walls, gardens, convents and timber-yards, and survives only on the plan of 1727. The Rue du Chemin-Vert-Saint-Antoine splits into the Rue Petit-Picpus on the left and the Rue Polonceau on the right, joined at the top by the Rue Droit-Mur; a dead-end stub of the Rue Droit-Mur is the Cul-de-Sac Genrot.","Valjean stands at the end of the Rue Polonceau. The moving thing he saw behind him must be Javert and his squad; Javert evidently knows this little labyrinth and has sent a man ahead to guard the one exit into the Rue Petit-Picpus. The blind alley cuts him off on one side, the sentinel on the other; to advance is to fall into that man's hands, to retreat is to walk into Javert's arms. Caught as in a slowly tightening net, he looks up at the sky in despair."],["Hugo lays out the geography exactly. On the left of the Rue Droit-Mur stands a severe building that rises toward the Rue Petit-Picpus and sinks to a bare wall at the Rue Polonceau corner, where a deep niche hides anyone inside it from observers in either street. The niche is filled by what looks like a colossal, wretched door of planks bound with iron, with an ordinary carriage gate beside it; a linden shows above the wall, and ivy covers it on the Polonceau side.","The building's silent, uninhabited look tempts Valjean. He sets Cosette against a stone post with orders to keep quiet and runs to the lead cistern pipes that branch across the front like a leafless vine, hoping to climb. But the pipe is rotten and barely attached, every window is barred even in the roof, the moon lights the whole façade in view of the sentinel, and in any case he could not haul Cosette up three storeys.","He crawls back to the niche, where he realises he cannot be seen, and tries the doors. The carriage gate is immovable inside and out. The great decrepit door with its three rusted bands looks easy to pierce, but it has no hinges, lock or crossbar: through the gaps he sees rough stone blocks cemented together. It is not a door at all but a wooden facing on a wall."],["A measured tread announces seven or eight soldiers with bayonets coming up the Rue Polonceau, Javert's tall figure at their head and his two agents in the ranks: a patrol he has met and requisitioned. They halt often to search every doorway; at that pace they will reach Valjean in a quarter of an hour. For the third time the galleys yawn before him, and now the galleys mean Cosette lost forever.","Hugo recalls that Valjean, thanks to his escapes from Toulon, is a master of climbing the inner angle of two walls without ladder or irons, using back, shoulders, hips and knees. The wall is about eighteen feet high, with a five-foot mass of masonry filling the corner, so only fourteen feet remain. But he cannot carry Cosette up, and he has no rope. His eye falls on the lantern post in the Cul-de-Sac Genrot: in those days the lanterns were raised and lowered on a rope kept in a locked iron box. In one bound he crosses, forces the box with his knife, and returns with the rope. Since the lanterns are unlit tonight, no one will notice it is gone.","Cosette, frightened by his strange movements and the approaching patrol, whispers that she is afraid and asks who is coming. 'Hush,' he says, 'it is Madame Thénardier,' and warns her that if she cries out the Thénardier will take her back. He passes his cravat under her arms, knots it to the rope, takes the other end in his teeth, throws his shoes and stockings over the wall, and climbs the angle as steadily as a ladder; in half a minute he kneels on top. He tells her to put her back to the wall, lifts her up, puts her on his back and crawls along the crest to where a low roof slopes down to the linden tree inside.","As he reaches the slope, the patrol arrives below with a roar and Javert's voice thunders: search the blind alley, the Rue Droit-Mur and Rue Petit-Picpus are guarded, he is certainly in the alley. The soldiers rush into the Genrot alley. Valjean slides down the roof, reaches the linden, and leaps to the ground with Cosette, who has not made a sound."],["The garden is oblong and strange: an alley of tall poplars at the far end, forest trees in the corners, a great solitary tree in the middle, gnarled fruit trees, vegetable beds, a melon patch whose glass frames glitter in the moonlight, an old well, and stone benches black with moss. Beside Valjean is the ruinous building whose roof brought him down, a pile of fagots, and a mutilated stone statue against the wall. The large building on the Rue Droit-Mur turns two grim façades toward the garden, every window grated, not a gleam of light anywhere. The place seems made for no one to walk in, even by day.","Valjean recovers his shoes and takes Cosette under the shed. She presses against him, trembling, her mind still on the Thénardier. They hear the patrol searching the alley and streets, gunstocks striking the stones, Javert calling to the spies he has posted. After a quarter of an hour the uproar grows distant; Valjean, his hand lightly over Cosette's mouth, holds his breath. Yet the calm within these walls is so profound that the din outside hardly troubles him.","Then a new sound rises out of the gloom: a hymn sung by women's voices, pure as virgins and innocent as children, coming from the dark edifice above the garden. As the noise of the pursuit recedes, it is as though a choir of angels approaches. Cosette and Jean Valjean, not knowing what it is or where they are, kneel. The building still seems deserted; it is a supernatural chant in an uninhabited house. While it lasts Valjean sees not the night but a blue sky. When the song dies away, street and garden are silent, and only dry weeds rustle on the wall."],["The night wind has risen; it is between one and two in the morning. Cosette's eyes are wide open and she is still trembling. She says she is very cold, then asks, 'Is she still there?' Valjean has forgotten the fiction he used to silence her, then understands and tells her Madame Thénardier is gone and she need fear nothing more. He takes off his coat and wraps it round her, tells her to wait, and sets out along the great building to find a warmer place.","The doors are locked and the ground-floor windows barred. Round the inner angle he comes to arched windows with a light inside and, on tiptoe, looks into a large flagged hall of pillars and arcades lit by a single taper. On the floor, something covered in a winding-sheet and shaped like a human form lies face down, arms stretched out in a cross, utterly still, with what seems a rope trailing from its neck like a serpent. Valjean, who would say afterward that he had never seen anything more bloodcurdling, presses his face to the glass and watches for a long time; the form never moves.","Seized by terror, he runs back to the shed without daring to look behind him, knees failing and sweat pouring. What is this house, with a street number, that calls souls with the voice of angels and then shows them the gates of the tomb? He touches the stones to be sure it is not a dream. Cold, anxiety and the night's emotions have given him a fever. He goes to Cosette: she is asleep."],["Sitting beside the sleeping child, Valjean grows calm and sees the truth that will govern his life henceforth: as long as she is with him he needs nothing and fears nothing except for her. He does not notice that he is cold without his coat.","A faint tinkling, like cattle bells in a night pasture, makes him turn. Someone resembling a man is walking among the bell-glasses of the melon beds, rising and stooping with regular movements as if spreading something on the ground, and he seems to limp. The sound of the bell follows every movement: it approaches when he approaches and ceases when he halts. Who is this man belled like a ram or an ox? Valjean's chimerical fears give way to real ones. Javert's men may still be watching the street; if this gardener finds him he may shout for help. He carries Cosette behind a heap of old furniture in the farthest corner of the shed.","Then he touches Cosette's hands and finds them icy. He calls her name; she does not open her eyes. He shakes her; she does not wake. He fears she is dead. She still breathes, but weakly, and he remembers that sleeping outdoors on a cold night can kill. Everything else vanishes from his mind. He rushes out of the ruin: Cosette must be in bed beside a fire in less than a quarter of an hour."],["Valjean walks straight up to the man with the roll of silver in his hand and cries, 'One hundred francs!' if he will give him shelter for the night. The man starts, looks at him in the moonlight, and exclaims, 'What! so it is you, Father Madeleine!' Valjean recoils; he expected anything but that. The speaker is a bent, lame old man dressed like a peasant, with a leather kneecap on his left knee from which hangs a bell. He pours out astonished questions: how did Father Madeleine get in here, without hat, cravat or coat? He says he is the person for whom Valjean got the place here, and that Valjean saved his life. When a ray of moonlight catches his profile, Valjean recognises old Fauchelevent.","Fauchelevent explains that he is covering his melons with straw mats against the frost, which accounts for the movements Valjean watched. The bell on his knee is so that he may be avoided: there are only women and young girls in this house, and when he comes they go. The house is the Petit-Picpus convent, the very one in the Saint-Antoine quarter where, two years before, Fauchelevent was admitted as gardener on the recommendation of the mayor of M. sur M. after his cart crushed him. Fauchelevent insists no man may enter here; Valjean replies that he must stay.","Valjean reminds him gravely: 'Father Fauchelevent, I saved your life.' The old man, trembling with joy, takes his hands and tells Monsieur le Maire to dispose of him. He has an isolated three-room hut behind the ruins of the old convent, in a corner no one looks into. Valjean asks two things: tell no one what you know about me, and do not try to learn more. Fauchelevent agrees, saying Valjean can do nothing dishonest. 'So there is a child?' he says when Valjean mentions her, and asks nothing further.","Within half an hour Cosette is asleep and rosy again in the gardener's bed before a good fire. Valjean has his coat and cravat back on and his hat has been found. The two men warm themselves over bread, cheese and wine, Fauchelevent chiding Father Madeleine for saving people's lives and then forgetting them."],["After Valjean escaped from the jail at Montreuil-sur-Mer, the police assumed he had gone to Paris and summoned Javert there to help; his zeal was noticed by M. Chabouillet, secretary of the Prefecture, who had him attached to the Paris police. In December 1823 Javert read in a newspaper that the convict Jean Valjean was dead and thought no more of it. Later a report from the Seine-et-Oise about a child named Cosette, daughter of a Fantine, stolen from an innkeeper at Montfermeil by a stranger, set him thinking: he remembered Valjean asking for three days to fetch that woman's child and being arrested as he boarded the Montfermeil coach. He went to Montfermeil, but Thénardier, wary of drawing the law's eye to his own affairs, had silenced his wife and now described a respectable 'grandfather,' a wealthy farmer called Guillaume Lambert with a passport. Javert returned calling himself a ninny.","In March 1824 he heard of a shy man of means in the parish of Saint-Médard who lived with an eight-year-old girl from Montfermeil, went out only at night, and wore an old yellow coat said to be wadded with banknotes. An old ex-beadle, a police spy who begged near the church, supplied details. To see the man close without alarming him, Javert borrowed the beadle's rags and place for an evening; when the 'suspected individual' gave him alms Javert looked up, and each man recognised, or thought he recognised, the other. But Valjean's death was official and Javert, scrupulous, would not arrest on a doubt. He followed him to the Gorbeau house, got the old woman talking about the thousand-franc note, hired a room and listened at Valjean's door; the next day the old woman, hearing the five-franc piece fall, warned him that the lodger might be leaving, and Javert waited behind the boulevard trees with two men.","Javert had told no one at the Prefecture whose arrest he hoped for: an indiscretion might alert his man, the old hands would take such a prize from a newcomer, and he liked to unveil his masterpieces suddenly. He never lost sight of Valjean, but did not arrest him because he was still in doubt: the fugitive's altered, aged walk, his émigré-tutor costume, Thénardier's grandfather, the official death, and the fear of the newspapers' outcry over an arrested white-haired grandfather all held him back. He also reasoned that if the man was not Valjean he might be some gang chief whose hideouts were worth discovering. Only in the Rue de Pontoise, by the light of a dram-shop, did he positively recognise him; then he asked for reinforcements, which cost him time at the Carrefour Rollin.","Guessing that Valjean would put the river between them, Javert went straight to the Pont d'Austerlitz, learned from the toll-keeper of a man with a little girl, saw him cross the lit ground, remembered the Cul-de-Sac Genrot and the single exit of the Rue Droit-Mur, posted an agent there, requisitioned a passing patrol, and, sure of his prey, took a pinch of snuff and savoured letting him run a little longer. When he closed the net the fly was gone; his sentinel had seen no one pass. Hugo catalogues his blunders: hesitating to recognise, not arresting at the Gorbeau house or in the Rue de Pontoise, consulting in the moonlight, and above all playing cat and mouse. The cut lantern rope led him to search the gardens off the blind alley all night; at daybreak he left two men on watch and returned to the Prefecture ashamed."],["From the street, Number 62 looks like any other carriage gate, usually ajar, showing a vine-hung courtyard and a lounging porter, with tall trees beyond. Yet the house behind the smiling threshold prays and weeps. Past the porter, who admits only those who know the formula, a narrow yellow-and-chocolate staircase and a turning corridor lead to an unfastened door and a small, clean, utterly empty room. In the facing wall is a square hole closed by a black iron grating reinforced with a pierced sheet of tin, with a letter-box slot and a bell-pull beside it.","Pull the tape and a gentle, mournful woman's voice very close by asks who is there. Without the second password the voice falls silent; with it, it says 'Enter on the right.' There, through a glass door, is a narrow box like a grated theatre box, with two old chairs and a black wooden ledge, closed by a monstrous iron lattice and, six inches beyond it, black shutters that are always shut. After a while a voice behind the shutters says, 'I am here. What do you wish with me?' Under rare conditions a slat opens, and behind grating and shutter appears a head of which only mouth and chin can be seen beneath a black veil and guimpe; it speaks but never looks at you or smiles. Behind her the visitor sees only night and emptiness: the interior of a cloister.","This is the Convent of the Bernardines of the Perpetual Adoration; the box is the parlour, the first voice the portress who sits motionless beside the grated opening. The parlour has a window on the world's side and none on the convent's, for profane eyes must see nothing of the sacred place. Yet there is life behind that shadow, and Hugo announces that he will take the reader inside and tell things that storytellers have never described."],["The convent, long established by 1824, belongs to the Bernardines of the obedience of Martin Verga, a Spanish congregation founded in 1425 and attached to Cîteaux, hence to Saint Benoît rather than Saint Bernard. Hugo distinguishes them from the Benedictine Ladies of the Holy Sacrament at the Temple and the Rue Neuve-Sainte-Geneviève, who also practise the Perpetual Adoration but wear a white guimpe and a silver-gilt Holy Sacrament; the Petit-Picpus nuns wear all black except a white band, novices all white.","Theirs is the harshest rule after the barefoot Carmelites: fasting all year and abstaining from meat, rising between one and three to chant matins, sleeping on straw between serge sheets, never bathing or lighting a fire, scourging themselves every Friday, keeping silence except in brief recreations, and wearing a fever-inducing drugget chemise from September 14 to Easter. The prioress is elected for three years and may serve at most nine. They never see the priest, hidden by a nine-foot serge curtain; only the archbishop may enter, and the old gardener, who wears a bell on his knee so that they may avoid him. Submission to the prioress is absolute; they may not read or write without leave.","Each in turn makes 'reparation': twelve hours kneeling on stone before the Holy Sacrament with hands clasped and a rope around her neck, lying prostrate in a cross when exhausted, praying for all the sins of the world. Because it is done before a post with a candle, they call it 'being at the post.' Another sister is always kneeling before the Holy Sacrament in hourly relays: the Perpetual Adoration. Mothers bear names such as Nativity or Passion. They never say 'my,' only 'our'; cells stay open; greetings and knocks are answered with 'Forever'; at every hour three bell strokes stop all speech for the formula praising the Holy Sacrament. They chant in full voice, and for the office of the dead in a tone almost too low for women.","They have a burial vault under the altar but the government forbids its use; they may be buried at a special hour in a corner of the old Vaugirard cemetery. A weekly chapter hears each sister confess her faults aloud; lesser offences bring the self-imposed coulpe, lying prostrate before the prioress until she taps her stall. Nuns summoned to the parlour drop their veils; only the prioress may communicate with strangers, others see family rarely and through the shutters, and men are always refused. These nuns are pale and grave; between 1825 and 1830 three of them went mad."],["A postulant waits two to four years, a novice four; final vows seldom come before twenty-three or twenty-four, and widows are not admitted. In their cells the nuns practise unspoken macerations. On the day of her profession the novice is dressed in her finest clothes and crowned with white roses, then prostrates herself; a great black veil is thrown over her and the office for the dead is sung. The nuns pass in two files, one lamenting 'Our sister is dead,' the other answering 'Our sister is alive in Jesus Christ.'","At the time of the story a boarding-school for girls of noble, mostly wealthy families is attached to the convent, with pupils such as the Mesdemoiselles de Saint-Aulaire and de Bélissen and an English Talbot. Reared between four walls, they grow up with a horror of the world; one told Hugo that the sight of street pavement made her shudder. They wear blue with a white cap and a Holy Spirit on the breast, and on great feasts such as Saint Martha's day they are allowed, as a supreme treat, to dress as nuns and keep the full offices for a day, an amusement the convent encourages.","The pupils follow all the practices except the austerities, and some keep the reflex of answering 'Forever' long after marriage. Like the nuns they see relatives only in the parlour; even mothers cannot embrace them. Hugo recounts a girl who wept to kiss her three-year-old sister and begged that the child's hand at least be passed through the bars, which was almost indignantly refused."],["At the recreation hour a door swings open and youth floods the garden cut by a cross-shaped path; after the psalms and knells the girls' voices burst out sweeter than bees, and the veiled nuns watch the laughter from a distance. Hugo says Homer would have come there to laugh with Perrault. The nuns are gentle with the children, severe only toward themselves.","He records sayings from those walls: a five-year-old delighted to learn she has 'only nine years and ten months longer to remain here'; a quarrel in which little Alix opened the history book at random and asked 'What happened after that?'; a remark on a well-bred parakeet that eats the top of its bread and butter like a person; a seven-year-old's written confession accusing herself of avarice, adultery and raising her eyes to the gentlemen; a six-year-old's tale of three little cocks eaten by a wolf; a rhyme about Punchinello and the cat; and a foundling raised by the convent out of charity who, hearing the others talk of their mothers, says that hers was not there when she was born.","The stout portress with her keys, Sister Agatha, is nicknamed Agathocles by the big girls. The dark, damp refectory is 'full of beasts,' and its four corners are named Spider, Caterpillar, Woodlouse and Cricket; each pupil belongs to the 'nation' of her corner, so that when the Archbishop visits and asks who a pretty girl is, he is told 'She is a spider, Monseigneur,' and his informant declares herself a woodlouse. Hugo adds a parallel from Écouen, where girls were ranked as 'virgins' and 'florists' for the procession, and a seven-year-old told a sixteen-year-old, 'You are a virgin, but I am not.'"],["Above the refectory door was painted the 'white Paternoster,' a folk prayer promising paradise to whoever recites it thrice at evening and morning; by 1827 it was painted over. The pupils ate in silence at black tables under white walls, a single dish of meat and vegetables or salt fish, while a big girl read lives of the saints from a little pulpit and the supervising mother snapped a wooden book at any noise. A child who broke silence 'made a cross with her tongue' by licking the floor. The pupils once got hold of the forbidden rule of Saint-Benoît and found it dull except for some unintelligible pages about the sins of young boys; they hid windfall apples and pears under their pillows and ate them in bed, as a letter from a former pupil, now a duchess, attests.","During an archiepiscopal visit Mademoiselle Bouchard, a Montmorency connection, bet that she would ask for a day's leave, an enormity there. She stepped from the ranks and asked; M. de Quélen smiled and granted three days, and the prioress could do nothing. Hugo also records, as a fact unconnected with his story, the mysterious Madame Albertine: not a nun, treated with great respect, said to be mad and to 'pass for dead' in the world, where her supposed death served some great marriage settlement. Pale, cold to the touch, gliding rather than walking, she alone heard the offices from the Œil-de-Bœuf gallery, from which the priest could be seen. When the young Duc de Rohan preached for the first time she half rose and cried, 'Ah! Auguste!' then sank back into immobility; the convent buzzed, since Auguste was indeed his name and she had used it as a pet name.","The schoolgirls, who could not see M. de Rohan, a former musketeer and now vicar-general, knew his sweet, shrill voice and dreamed of his well-dressed hair and elegant cassock. Two severe duchesses, Mesdames de Choiseul and de Sérent, terrified them when they visited. One year a flute began to play 'My Zétulbé, come reign o'er my soul' from the Rue Droit-Mur two or three times a day for months; the girls fell in love with the unknown musician, punishments rained, one waved a handkerchief through a grating, and two climbed onto a roof at the risk of their lives to see him. He was an old, blind, penniless émigré gentleman playing in his attic to pass the time."],["Within the Petit-Picpus are three distinct buildings. Besides the Great Convent of the nuns and the Boarding-school of the pupils stands the Little Convent, with its own garden, where old nuns of every order, relics of cloisters destroyed in the Revolution, live under the Empire's permission and a small pension, each keeping her own rule: a 'harlequin convent' of black, grey and white habits. The pupils sometimes visited them as a treat and remembered Mothers Sainte-Bazile, Sainte-Scolastique and Jacob. One refugee, the last nun of Sainte-Aure, whose order once occupied this very house, was too poor to wear her white robe and scarlet scapular and dressed a little manikin in it instead; the doll outlived the order.","Some old society women also retired there with the prioress's leave, like Madame Albertine, Madame Beaufort d'Hautpoul and the Marquise Dufresne, and one known to the pupils only as Madame Vacarmini for the noise she made blowing her nose. Around 1820 Madame de Genlis, then editing a little periodical, asked to enter as a lady resident on the Duc d'Orléans's recommendation; the vocal mothers fluttered because she had written novels, but she declared she detested them and got in. She left after six or eight months complaining that the garden had no shade, to the nuns' delight, leaving pasted in her cupboard five lines of sixth-century Latin verse about the two thieves of Calvary, which she believed kept robbers away.","The church separates the Great Convent from the Boarding-school like an entrenchment and is common to all three houses; the public may enter by a sort of lazaretto door on the street. But the choir is folded back into a dark hall at the priest's right, shut off by a seven-foot curtain, with the nuns on the left, the schoolgirls on the right and the lay-sisters and novices behind; the public knows they are present only by the clatter of the folding stall seats."],["From 1819 to 1825 the prioress was Mademoiselle de Blemeur, Mother Innocente in religion, of the family of the author of the Lives of the Saints of the Order of Saint-Benoît. About sixty, short and thick, 'singing like a cracked pot,' she was an excellent woman and the only merry one in the convent, and adored for it; learned in history, Latin, Greek and Hebrew, she was more Benedictine monk than Benedictine nun, and had been re-elected. The sub-prioress was Mother Cineres, an old Spanish nun almost blind.","Hugo lists the vocal mothers: Sainte-Honorine the treasurer, Sainte-Gertrude mistress of novices, Saint-Ange her assistant, Annonciation the sacristan, Saint-Augustin the nurse and the only malicious one, the young and beautiful-voiced Sainte-Mechtilde who drilled seven pupils of graduated ages into a 'living pan-pipe of angels,' and others with their worldly names, two of whom went mad; also the pretty twenty-three-year-old Mother Assumption from the Isle de Bourbon. The lay-sisters the pupils loved most were Euphrasie, Sainte-Marguerite, the doting Sainte-Marthe and long-nosed Sainte-Michel. All were gentle with the children, who alone had fire and better food, though a nun addressed by a child never replied.","Because speech was withdrawn from people, it passed to things: the church bell and the gardener's bell spoke, and a sonorous bell by the portress rang a code for every person and action, one-and-one for the prioress, six-five for lessons, four-four for Madame de Genlis, nine strokes for the opening of the iron 'door of seclusion' only when the archbishop came. Besides archbishop and gardener the schoolgirls saw two other men: the chaplain Abbé Banés, old and ugly, glimpsed through a grating, and the drawing-master M. Ansiaux, 'a frightful old hunchback.' All these men, Hugo notes, were carefully chosen."],["After the convent's moral face, its material configuration. The convent fills nearly the whole trapezium between four streets that ring it like a moat. Seen from above, the main building traces a gibbet laid flat: the long arm along the Rue Droit-Mur holds the cells of mothers, sisters and novices; the shorter arm, a grey grated façade on the Rue Petit-Picpus ending at the carriage entrance Number 62, holds kitchens, refectory, cloisters and church, with a low dusty arched door that is the public entrance to the church, opened only on Sundays and when a nun's coffin leaves. The elbow is the servants' hall the nuns call 'the buttery.' The school, invisible from the street, lies between Number 62 and the closed Aumarais lane.","The rest is the garden, sunk well below the Rue Polonceau so that the walls are far higher inside than out. From a conical fir on a central hillock run four grand alleys and eight small ones, like a cross on a wheel, bordered with currant bushes; an alley of tall poplars links the ruins of the old convent at the Rue Droit-Mur corner to the Little Convent at the Aumarais corner, with its 'little garden.' The whole stands on the site of a famous fourteenth-to-sixteenth-century tennis-ground, 'the tennis-ground of the eleven thousand devils.' The streets are older than Paris: Aumarais was once Maugout lane, and the Rue Droit-Mur the Rue des Églantiers, 'for God opened flowers before man cut stones.'"],["Hugo begs one more digression to show that the cloister has its original figures. In the Little Convent lived a centenarian from the Abbey of Fontevrault who had been in society before the Revolution and loved to drop the names of M. de Miromesnil, Keeper of the Seals, and a Presidentess Duplat. She spoke with a Picard accent that amused the pupils, said Fontevrault was like a city with streets, and each year, renewing her vows, recited a chain of saints who had passed the oath from one to another before she gave it to the priest, which set the girls laughing under their veils.","She claimed the Bernardine monks of her youth were as good as the musketeers, and described the old custom of the four wines, in which a great lord passing through Burgundy or Champagne was offered four goblets labelled monkey, lion, sheep and hog wine, the four stages of drunkenness. In a locked cupboard she kept a mysterious object she showed to no one, shutting herself up to contemplate it and falling silent whenever it was mentioned, which set the whole idle convent guessing at relics and holy books.","When she died they rushed to the cupboard and found, under a triple linen cloth, a Faenza platter painted with little Loves fleeing apothecary lads armed with enormous syringes, one Cupid already spitted: 'Love conquered by the colic.' The platter was still for sale on the Boulevard Beaumarchais in 1845. The old woman refused all visitors from outside because, she said, 'the parlour is too gloomy.'"],["The sepulchral parlour just described is a local trait. At the Temple convent, which belonged to another order, brown curtains replaced black shutters and the parlour was a polished salon with muslin curtains, a portrait of an unveiled Benedictine, painted bouquets and even the head of a Turk; its garden held the chestnut tree reputed 'father of all the chestnut trees of the realm.'","The Temple house belonged to the Benedictines of the Perpetual Adoration, an order barely two hundred years old. In 1649 the holy sacrament was profaned at Saint-Sulpice and Saint-Jean-en-Grève; a solemn procession did not satisfy two pious women, Madame Courtin, Marquise de Boucs, and the Comtesse de Châteauvieux, who in 1652 and 1653 endowed Mother Catherine de Bar to found a Benedictine monastery devoted to perpetual adoration, on condition that each entrant bring three hundred livres of income. The Abbé of Saint-Germain, then the king, then the Chamber of Accounts and Parliament in 1654 confirmed it; the first convent was in the Rue Cassette.","That order descends from the Abbé of Saint-Germain des Prés, as the Sacred Heart ladies from the Jesuits and the Sisters of Charity from the Lazarists, and is wholly different from the Cîteaux Bernardines of the Petit-Picpus, whom Pope Alexander VII authorised in 1657 to practise the Perpetual Adoration too. The two orders nonetheless remained distinct."],["At the start of the Restoration the Petit-Picpus was declining with the whole order, as all religious orders have declined since the eighteenth century. By 1840 the Little Convent and the school had vanished, the old women dead and the girls gone. The rule of Perpetual Adoration is so harsh that vocations recoil from it; in 1845 it still found a few lay-sisters but no professed nuns. Forty years earlier there were nearly a hundred nuns, fifteen years earlier twenty-eight; the burden stays the same for the few as for the many, and it crushes them. In Hugo's own Paris years two died, aged twenty-five and twenty-three. This decay is why the convent gave up educating girls.","Hugo says he could not pass this extraordinary house without entering it and bringing his readers in, for the profit, perhaps, of the melancholy history of Jean Valjean. He has described it in detail but with respect, understanding not everything and insulting nothing, standing between Joseph de Maistre's anointing of the executioner and Voltaire's sneer at the cross. The nineteenth century is unlearning things, and rightly, but there is no vacuum in the human heart and demolitions must be followed by reconstructions.","Meanwhile, he says, let us study what is no more, if only to avoid it: the past counterfeits itself and calls itself the future, wearing the face of superstition and the mask of hypocrisy. Convents pose a complex problem: civilisation condemns them, liberty protects them."],["Hugo states that this book is a drama whose leading personage is the Infinite, and man the second. A convent having happened to be on the road, it was his duty to enter it, because the convent, common to East and West, to antiquity and modern times, to paganism, Buddhism, Islam and Christianity, is one of the optical apparatuses man applies to the Infinite.","While keeping all his reserves, restrictions and even indignations, he confesses that whenever he meets man in the Infinite, well or ill understood, he is overcome with respect. Every place of worship has a hideous side he execrates and a sublime side he adores; the reverberation of God upon the human wall is endless food for thought."],["From the standpoint of history, reason and truth, Hugo condemns monasticism. Monasteries that abound in a nation clog its circulation like mistletoe on an oak; useful when the brutal needed taming by the spiritual, they are detestable in the nineteenth century, and their leprosy has gnawed Italy and Spain nearly to skeletons, from which only 1789 is letting them recover.","The ancient female convent, above all the Spanish one, is for him the point where horrors intersect: huge dark altars, bleeding ivory Christs hung with rubies and diamonds, veiled women bruised by hair shirts and scourges who neither think, will, love nor live, with an abbess who is a spectre. He likens the Spanish convent to an oriental seraglio of souls, the archbishop its keeper, the nun its odalisque, where a glance at the world was infidelity and the in pace replaced the drowning sack.","Defenders of the past now simply smile at such facts and call Rousseau, Diderot, Voltaire and even Tacitus 'declaimers.' Facts are obstinate: Hugo has seen with his own eyes, eight leagues from Brussels at the Abbey of Villers, the hole of the oubliettes and four half-submerged stone dungeons by the river Thil, the in pace, with a rusted iron collar riveted to one wall and a granite box too short to lie in and too low to stand in, closed with a stone lid, where a living being was shut up. This exists, he says; it can be touched. 'What declaimers!'"],["Monasticism of the Spanish or Tibetan kind is a phthisis of civilisation: it depopulates, castrates, forces vocations, feeds feudalism with younger sons, walls up brains and buries living souls. Yet the spirit of the cloister persists, and an ascetic revival is astonishing the civilised world. Hugo mocks the obstinacy of dead institutions with a chorus of the old garment, the stale fish, the faded perfume and the corpse that each plead 'I served you once,' to which the only answer is 'In former days.'","Theorists who would embalm the past relabel it social order, divine right, family, tradition and religion, like the ancient soothsayers who chalked a black heifer white. Hugo will respect and spare the past where it consents to be dead; if it insists on living he attacks it, for humanity is condemned to eternal war with phantoms. A convent in the Paris of '89, 1830 and 1848 is a college of owls facing the light, an anachronism; in ordinary times one need only make it spell out its date.","'Let us fight,' he says, but with distinction: truth never exaggerates, and some things must be destroyed while others need only be examined and elucidated. He opposes ascetic claustration everywhere, in Asia as in Europe, as a marsh and a plague. That said, the religious question remains, with its mysterious and formidable sides, and he asks leave to look at it fixedly."],["People unite in communities by the right of association, shut themselves in by the right every man has to open or close his door, and stay in by the right to come and go. Inside, they speak low, drop their eyes, work, renounce the world and own nothing: the rich man becomes poor, the lord equals the peasant, all take the same tonsure and frock, eat the same black bread, sleep on the same straw, bear only first names, dissolve the carnal family into a spiritual one, tend the poor and sick, and elect those they obey, calling each other brother.","To the objection that this is the ideal convent, Hugo answers that it is enough that it is the possible convent. That is why he spoke of the Petit-Picpus with respect. Setting aside the Middle Ages, Asia and the political question, and on condition that the monastery is absolutely voluntary and holds only consenting members, he will always regard a cloistered community with attentive and in some respects deferential gravity. Where there is a community there is a commune and therefore right; the monastery is Equality and Fraternity, and liberty would transform it into a republic.","But what do these men and women behind four walls do besides dressing alike and calling each other brothers? They gaze on the darkness, kneel, and clasp their hands. What does this signify?"],["They pray, to God. Hugo asks what the words mean. Is there an infinite beyond us, necessarily substantial and intelligent since it is infinite, the absolute of which we are only the relative? And is there not also an infinite within us, superposed on and mirroring the first, an abyss concentric with another abyss? If both infinities are intelligent each has a will and an I: the I below is the soul, the I above is God. To bring the infinity below into contact with the infinity above by means of thought is to pray.","He asks that nothing be taken from the human mind: suppression is bad, reform and transformation are the way. Thought, reverie and prayer are faculties turned toward the Unknown, that ocean whose compass is conscience; they are majestic radiations of the soul into the shadow, which is to say the light. Democracy's grandeur is to deny nothing of humanity, and beside the rights of man stands the right of the soul.","The law is to crush fanaticism and venerate the infinite. Beyond contemplating the starry tree of creation, we must labour over the human soul, defend mystery against miracle, adore the incomprehensible and reject the absurd, purify belief, strip superstition from religion, and 'clear God of caterpillars.'"],["Every sincere mode of prayer is good; 'turn your book upside down and be in the infinite.' A philosophy that denies the infinite is like a philosophy that denies the sun: blindness. Hugo mocks the mole's pity for those with their sun, yet salutes the great atheists as philosophers, saying that by their very force they prove God, while denouncing their philosophy. He dismisses a northern metaphysical school that replaces 'force' with 'will': if the plant wills and so has an I, then the universe wills and so has a God.","To deny the will of the infinite is to deny the infinite, which leads to nihilism, where everything becomes 'a mental conception' and no discussion is possible since the nihilist doubts even his interlocutor; yet by uttering the word 'mind' he admits all he denied. To 'No' the only reply is 'Yes.' There is no nothingness; man lives by affirmation even more than by bread.","Philosophy should be an energy that betters man: Socrates entering Adam to produce Marcus Aurelius, Eden turned into a Lyceum, science as a cordial, wisdom as holy communion, so that philosophy is promoted to religion. Hugo closes by naming the two motors of man's departure and progress's end, faith and love. Progress is the goal, the ideal is the type, and the ideal is God; ideal, absolute, perfection and infinity are identical words."],["History and philosophy have clear duties, to combat Caiaphas, Draco, Trimalchio and Tiberius, but the right to live apart, with all its abuses, insists on being stated and taken into account. Cenobitism is a human problem, and about convents, abodes of error and innocence, aberration and goodwill, torture and martyrdom, one must finally say yes or no.","A convent is a contradiction: its object salvation, its means sacrifice; supreme egoism resulting in supreme abnegation; abdication in order to reign. In the cloister one suffers in order to enjoy, draws a bill of exchange on death, accepts hell in advance as a post-obit on paradise. Taking the veil is a suicide paid for with eternity. Mockery is not permissible on such a subject; everything about it, good and bad, is serious. The just man frowns but never sneers: wrath is understandable, malice is not."],["A few words more. Hugo blames the church when it is full of intrigue and despises the spiritual when it is harsh to the temporal, but honours the thoughtful man everywhere and salutes the man who kneels. Faith is a necessity; woe to him who believes nothing. Contemplation is labour and thought is action: folded arms toil, clasped hands work, and Thales, motionless for four years, founded philosophy. A perpetual memory of the tomb is proper for the living, and on this the ascetic and the sage converge.","To those who ask what use these motionless figures are, he answers that there is probably no work more divine or more useful, and that there must be some who pray constantly for those who never pray at all; the whole question is how much thought is mingled with the prayer. Leibnitz praying is grand, Voltaire adoring is fine. He is for religion as against religions, believing in the wretchedness of orisons and the sublimity of prayer, and in an age of low brows and enjoyment-morality he venerates whoever exiles himself. Sacrifice wrongly directed is still sacrifice.","The female convent in particular has a certain majesty, since in this century it is woman who suffers most and her cloistered exile is a kind of protest. This austere existence is neither life, since it is not liberty, nor the tomb, since it is not plenitude, but a misty frontier between two abysses. Hugo, who does not believe what these women believe but lives by faith like them, has never been able to think without tender, religious terror and envious pity of these trembling, trusting souls who dwell on the brink of the mystery, kneeling, bewildered, half lifted by the deep breaths of eternity."],["Into this house Valjean had, as Fauchelevent put it, fallen from the sky. The hymn he heard was the nuns chanting matins; the hall was the chapel; the shrouded form was the sister making reparation; the bell was on Fauchelevent's knee. After their supper the two men lie down on straw, Cosette having the only bed, and neither sleeps. Valjean, knowing Javert is on his scent, sees that he and Cosette are lost if they return to Paris, and that this convent is both the most dangerous place, since a man found there is one step from prison, and the safest, since no one would look for him in an impossible place. His one thought is to remain.","Fauchelevent cudgels his brains: how did M. Madeleine come over such walls, with a child, and who is she? He has heard nothing of Montreuil-sur-Mer since he came here and guesses at bankruptcy or some political trouble, which pleases his old Bonapartism. He does not understand, but one thing is certain: M. Madeleine saved his life, and did not stop to deliberate before crawling under the cart. He resolves to save him whether he is a saint, a thief or an assassin. Hugo adds that Fauchelevent, a Picard peasant, had once been a notary, which added trickery to his cunning; failing in business he became a carter, but kept his grammar and his wit, and the convent's air had made a good deed necessary to him.","At daybreak Fauchelevent asks, 'Now that you are here, how are you going to contrive to enter?' First, neither Valjean nor the child must set foot outside the hut. They have come at an inauspicious moment: one of the ladies is dying, the forty hours' prayers are being said, and the community is absorbed; a bell strikes once, then again: the knell, which will sound once a minute for twenty-four hours. The hidden hut is no refuge, because of the little girls, who chase stray balls everywhere in recreation and would shriek 'a man!'; today there will be no recreation. Valjean notes that there are pupils and thinks that here is Cosette's education provided. 'This convent would be our salvation,' he murmurs; the difficulty is to remain. 'No,' says Fauchelevent, 'the difficulty is to get out,' for the nuns require one to enter by the door, and Valjean cannot possibly return to the street where the police are swarming: 'Say that I fell from the sky.'","Fauchelevent sketches a plan for Cosette: he will carry her out in his vintage basket on his back, past the porter, to a deaf old fruit-seller he knows in the Rue du Chemin-Vert who will keep her overnight as his niece. For Valjean he has no idea; Valjean asks for some way out 'in a basket, under cover, like Cosette.' Further peals mark the doctor of the dead being summoned and departing. Fauchelevent rambles about his duties: he nails up the nuns' coffins in the dead-room off the church, which no man but the doctor may enter, and the undertaker's men take them to the Vaugirard cemetery, where his friend Father Mestienne is gravedigger and the nuns have leave to be buried at nightfall. 'Mother Crucifixion is dead, and Father Madeleine—' 'Is buried,' says Valjean, smiling sadly. A fourth peal summons the gardener; he buckles on his bell, tells Valjean not to stir, and hurries off to the parlour where the prioress is waiting."],["The prioress, Mademoiselle de Blemeur, Mother Innocente, usually cheerful, wears a grave and agitated air. She calls him 'Father Fauvent,' the convent's abbreviation, and says she has sent for him; he answers, with a boldness that terrifies him inwardly, that he too has a request. Hugo explains Fauchelevent's standing: in more than two years of solitary gardening he has learned to read every peal and clothe the veiled shadows with flesh, and has set the porter and the gravedigger chattering so that he knows the nuns' life and their death. He knows all and conceals all; the convent thinks him stupid, which is a great merit in religion, and the vocal mothers make much of him.","With the assurance of a man who feels appreciated, he launches into a long rustic harangue about his age, his infirmities, the size of the garden and nights like the last spent covering melons, and concludes that he has a brother, no longer young, an excellent gardener, who would come and help him, failing which he himself must regretfully leave; and that the brother has a little daughter who might be reared for God in the house and might one day become a nun. The prioress makes a movement at the word 'brother' and a second, reassured one at 'no longer young.'","When he has finished she stops her rosary and asks whether he can procure a stout iron bar between now and evening. 'For what purpose?' 'To serve as a lever.' He says yes. Without another word she rises and goes into the adjoining chapter hall, where the vocal mothers are probably assembled, leaving Fauchelevent alone."],["After a quarter of an hour the prioress returns and the dialogue proceeds, which Hugo reports as if stenographically. There is a stone to be raised: the slab beside the altar that closes the vault, which swings on a pivot and has a ring. Fauchelevent will put his bar through it; Mother Ascension, 'as strong as a man,' and the four Mother Precentors will help him, and something must be lowered into the vault before it is closed. Then she tells him that a mother died at daybreak: Mother Crucifixion, a blessed woman who converted a Jansenist by being seen at prayer, who kept her consciousness to the last, spoke to the angels and gave her last commands. Fauchelevent, who pretended not to have heard the knell, keeps answering 'Amen' whenever Latin or piety flows, and an exchange about 'more often' tangles them both until the nine o'clock bell cuts it short.","'What the dead wish must be done.' Mother Crucifixion slept in her coffin for twenty years by permission of Pius VII and wishes to be buried in it, beneath the altar where she prayed, not in profane earth. Fauchelevent is to nail up that coffin, reject the undertaker's, and lower it into the vault between eleven and midnight in total secrecy, bell removed, with only the Precentors and Mother Ascension present; the sister at the post will not turn round. To each of his objections, the sanitary agent, the commissary, the inspector, she replies with a saint or a council: 'Forbidden by men, enjoined by God.' Fauchelevent, who has heard too much Latin, says 'Amen,' and she, whose silence has been dammed too long, pours out a torrent on Saint Bernard, Saint Benoît, the rights of the monastery over its dead, and the impiety of an age in which burial is a civil matter and God is subordinated to the commissary of police.","Fauchelevent agrees to everything, seizing every chance to mention his strong brother, 'a perfect Turk.' Then the prioress raises the last point: the empty coffin that the administration will send and the undertaker's men will carry to Vaugirard. The bearers will feel there is nothing in it. Fauchelevent nearly swears, catches himself under her sign of the cross, and improvises: he will put earth in it to give the effect of a corpse. 'Earth, that is the same thing as man,' she says, satisfied. As he reaches the door she adds gently that she is pleased with him, and that he is to bring his brother tomorrow, after the burial, and tell him to fetch his daughter."],["Fauchelevent returns to find Valjean telling Cosette that the good man will carry her out on his back in the basket, that she will wait at a lady's house and he will fetch her, and that she must obey and say nothing unless she wants Madame Thénardier to get her again. Cosette nods gravely. 'Everything is arranged, and nothing is,' says Fauchelevent: he has permission to bring Valjean in, but first he must be got out, and 'Get out as you came in' meets the same 'Impossible.' He is also troubled that earth in the coffin will shift and the bearers will notice.","He explains the bargain: the dead nun asked to be buried in her own coffin under the altar, the prioress and vocal mothers mean to do it despite the police regulations, and in return the brother becomes gardener and the niece a pupil; the brother is M. Madeleine and the niece is Cosette, to be brought tomorrow evening after the counterfeit interment. But he cannot bring Valjean in from outside if he is not outside, and the administration's empty coffin must go to the cemetery with something in it. 'Put something in it,' says Valjean. 'A corpse? I have none.' 'A living person.' 'What person?' 'Me!' Fauchelevent springs up as if a bomb had burst under his chair. Valjean smiles: Mother Crucifixion is dead, and Father Madeleine is buried; the basket he asked for will be of pine and the cover a cloth, white, since nuns are buried in white.","Valjean questions him precisely: the empty coffin stands on trestles under the pall in the ground-floor dead-room, which has a grated window on the garden and two doors, one to the convent, whose key Fauchelevent holds, and one to the public church, whose key the porter holds and opens only for the undertaker's men around three the next afternoon. Fauchelevent alone nails the coffin and spreads the pall. He can hide Valjean in the locked tool-closet opening off the dead-room overnight, bring him food, and nail him in at two o'clock, boring gimlet holes around his mouth and fixing the top plank loosely. A man making his escape, Valjean says, does not cough or sneeze. Hugo notes that such endurance in a box is one of Valjean's gloomy convict talents, and that according to the monk Austin Castillejo it was also the device of Charles V.","Fauchelevent, hesitating like a cat in a half-open door, yields to Valjean's coolness. Only the cemetery worries Valjean; that, says Fauchelevent, is the easy part. The gravedigger, Father Mestienne, is his friend and a drunkard. After the priest's prayers the two will be alone; if Mestienne is sober Fauchelevent will take him to drink at the Bon Coing, get him under the table, take his card to re-enter the cemetery and come back alone; if he is already drunk he will send him off and do his work. Then he will dig Valjean out. They shake hands: 'All will go well.' 'Provided nothing goes wrong,' thinks Fauchelevent."],["As the sun declines, passers-by on the Boulevard du Maine doff their hats to an old-fashioned hearse bearing a coffin under a white pall with a black cross, followed by a mourning-coach with a priest and choir boy, two undertaker's men, and a limping old labourer from whose pocket protrude a hammer, a cold chisel and pincers. Hugo describes the Vaugirard cemetery, a faded, old-fashioned enclosure soon to be suppressed, with its carriage gate and foot gate beside the porter's pavilion. Its gates close at sundown; a gravedigger caught inside after that can get out only by dropping his card into the porter's box, or by naming himself and paying a fifteen-franc fine. Next door stands the dram-shop Au Bon Coing.","The interment of Mother Crucifixion under the altar, the exit of Cosette and the installing of Valjean in the dead-room have all gone off without a hitch; Hugo remarks in passing that he finds the nuns' offence a venial one, a fault that resembles a duty. Fauchelevent follows in high contentment, sure of Mestienne, whom he has made drunk ten times in two years; he rubs his hands and says, 'Here's a fine farce!'","At the gate, while the permit is shown, a tall, thin, livid man with a mattock under his arm places himself beside him and, asked who he is, replies, 'The gravedigger.' Father Mestienne is the gravedigger, protests Fauchelevent. 'He was. He is dead.' 'After Napoleon, Louis XVIII. After Mestienne, Gribier.' Fauchelevent laughs it off and invites him to drink; Gribier announces that he has been a student, passed his fourth examination, and never drinks. As the hearse rolls up the alley Fauchelevent tries again and again: Gribier has seven small children who must eat, so he cannot drink; 'their hunger is the enemy of my thirst.' He is a public writer with a stall in the Rue de Sèvres who writes love letters in the morning and digs graves at night, and 'business first.' Fauchelevent thinks, 'I am lost.' The hearse turns into the thicket of the nuns' corner and halts with a wheel on a heap of earth beside an open grave. 'What a farce this is!' he repeats, in consternation."],["Who is in the coffin? Jean Valjean, who has arranged things so that he can almost breathe. Hugo observes how security of conscience gives security of everything else: like Fauchelevent, Valjean counts on Father Mestienne, and never was a situation more critical nor a composure more complete. Something of the repose of the dead seems to enter him between the four planks.","He has followed every phase: the nailing of the top plank, being carried out and driven off, the change from pavement to earth road, the dull sound of the Pont d'Austerlitz, the first halt at the cemetery gate and the second at the grave. He feels hands seize the coffin, the rasp of the rope, then a giddiness as the coffin tilts head-first before coming to rest horizontal at the bottom. A cold sensation follows. A glacial, solemn voice pronounces Latin over him, slowly enough for him to catch the words, and a child's voice answers; a patter like raindrops on the lid is the holy water. He thinks: soon the priest will leave, Fauchelevent will take Mestienne to drink, and within an hour he will be out.","He hears 'Requiescat in pace,' 'Amen,' and retreating footsteps. 'I am alone,' he thinks. Then a sound like a clap of thunder over his head: a shovelful of earth on the coffin. A second falls. One of the holes through which he breathes is stopped up. A third, a fourth. There are things too strong for the strongest man; Jean Valjean loses consciousness."],["When the priest and choir boy have gone, Gribier takes up his shovel. Fauchelevent plants himself between him and the grave and declares, 'I am the one to pay!' for the Argenteuil wine at the Bon Coing. 'Go to the devil,' says Gribier, and throws in a shovelful; the coffin gives back a hollow sound and Fauchelevent nearly falls into the grave. He clutches the man's arm, offering to help him work by night if they first go and drink, and wonders even then whether the man would get drunk. Gribier consents to drink 'after work, never before,' calls him a bell-ringer, and throws in a second and third shovelful, saying the corpse would shriek after them if planted without a coverlet.","As Gribier bends to load his shovel his waistcoat pocket gapes and Fauchelevent sees something white at the bottom. An idea flashes through the Picard peasant's eyes: he slips his hand in from behind and takes it. After the fourth shovelful he asks calmly, 'By the way, you new man, have you your card?' The sun is touching the dome of the Invalides and the gate will close in five minutes. Gribier searches every pocket in vain. 'Fifteen francs fine,' says Fauchelevent, and turns green with him, then gives friendly advice: run home to 87 Rue de Vaugirard, a quarter of an hour away, fetch the card, and the porter will let you back in to bury your corpse; 'I'll watch it for you, so that it shall not run away.' Gribier, overwhelmed with gratitude, runs off.","Fauchelevent leans over the grave and calls, 'Father Madeleine!' No reply. He tumbles in, pries up the lid with chisel and hammer, and sees Valjean's face pale, eyes closed. 'He is dead! And this is the way I save his life!' He sobs and rails at Mestienne for dying, at himself, and at the good man who put himself under the cart, wondering what will become of the little girl and the fruit-seller. The cemetery gate grates shut in the distance. He bends over again and recoils: Valjean's eyes are open and looking at him. 'I fell asleep,' says Valjean, sitting up. He had merely fainted, and the fresh air revived him. A mouthful of brandy from Fauchelevent's gourd finishes the cure. Valjean climbs out, helps nail the lid back on, and together they fill the grave over the empty coffin.","With the gravedigger's card Fauchelevent opens the gate, and shovel and pick serve as passports at the Vaugirard barrier. At No. 87 he climbs to the garret and finds Gribier's wretched household turned upside down by a furious search for the card, the wife in tears, the children probably beaten. He lays down the shovel and mattock and says that Gribier dropped his card, that he found it, buried the corpse, filled the grave and did his work, and that the porter will return the card in the morning, so there is no fine to pay. 'Thanks, villager!' cries Gribier, radiant. 'The next time I will pay for the drinks.'"],["In the dark of night two men and a child knock at No. 62: Fauchelevent, Jean Valjean and Cosette, whom the old men have fetched from the deaf fruit-seller's in the Rue du Chemin-Vert. Cosette has spent the twenty-four hours trembling and silent, neither eating nor sleeping, answering the fruit-seller's hundred questions with the same melancholy gaze; she has understood that she must 'be good' and say nothing, and no one keeps a secret like a child. When she sees Valjean again she gives a cry of joy that seems to issue from an abyss. Fauchelevent knows the passwords, so all the doors open, and the porter admits them through the little servants' door into the garden and on to the reserved parlour.","The prioress, rosary in hand, waits with a veiled vocal mother beside her, and examines Valjean with downcast eyes. 'You are the brother?' Fauchelevent answers every question for him: his name is Ultime Fauchelevent (a real dead brother's name), from Picquigny near Amiens, fifty years old, a gardener, a good Christian since 'everyone is in the family,' and the little girl's grandfather. 'He answers well,' whispers the vocal mother. Valjean has not said a word. The prioress looks attentively at Cosette and says half aloud, 'She will grow up ugly.' After a whispered consultation she tells Father Fauvent to get another kneecap with a bell, since two will now be required.","Next day two bells sound in the garden and the nuns cannot resist lifting the corner of their veils to watch two men digging side by side under the trees: 'He is an assistant gardener,' 'a brother of Father Fauvent.' Jean Valjean is officially installed under the name Ultime Fauchelevent. The decisive factor, Hugo says, was the prioress's verdict on Cosette, for plain girls are likelier to become nuns; she took a fancy to the child and gave her a place in the school as a charity pupil. Fauchelevent's triumph is triple: Valjean saved, Gribier spared a fine, and the convent able to keep Mother Crucifixion's coffin under its altar, so that there is a coffin with a body at the Petit-Picpus and one without at Vaugirard, and no one the wiser. The prioress confesses and boasts of the affair to the archbishop, who whispers it to M. de Latil, and the fame of the holy gardener 'Fauvent' reaches even a note of Pope Leo XII, though nothing of it reaches Fauchelevent in his hut, who goes on grafting and covering his melons."],["Cosette holds her tongue in the convent; she naturally thinks herself Valjean's daughter, and unhappiness has trained her to silence. She settles in quickly, regretting only Catherine: 'Father, if I had known, I would have brought her away with me.' Obliged to wear the pupils' dress, she gives up the little mourning suit Valjean had put on her when she left the Thénardiers' inn; he recovers it, locks it with her stockings and shoes in a small valise full of camphor and convent aromatics, keeps the key on him, and sets the valise by his bed. 'Father, what is there in that box which smells so good?' she asks one day.","Fauchelevent is happier, has half the work, and takes three times as much snuff, since M. Madeleine pays for it. The nuns do not use the name Ultime but call Valjean 'the other Fauvent.' Had they possessed Javert's glance they would have noticed that every outside errand falls to the old lame man and never to the other, but eyes fixed on God do not spy. It is as well, for Javert watches the quarter for more than a month. The convent becomes for Valjean an island surrounded by gulfs; those four walls are his world, with enough sky to keep his serenity and enough of Cosette to keep her happy. He takes the main room of the three-room hut over his own protests, under a Vendean banknote of '93 nailed up by the previous gardener, an old Chouan. Once a pruner, he gardens every day and grafts the wild orchard trees into bearing good fruit.","Cosette may spend an hour with him daily and flies to the hut at the appointed time; comparing the melancholy sisters with his kindness, she adores him, and he watches her at recreation and picks out her laugh, for Cosette laughs now. At night he rises to look at her dormitory windows. Hugo says the convent, like Cosette, completes the Bishop's work: Valjean had begun comparing himself with men in general instead of with the Bishop, and pride was springing up; the convent stops him. He sets this second place of captivity against the first. The convicts rose at dawn, slept on two-inch mattresses in rarely heated rooms, wore red blouses, lived by numbers under the cudgel; these women rise in the night to pray on cold stone, sleep on straw in unheated cells, wear black serge that gives them fever, lose their names and never eat meat. The men had stolen, violated and murdered; the women had done nothing. Both undergo expiation, but the convicts expiate for themselves and the nuns for others, 'the most divine of human generosities.'","He remembers that he had dared to murmur, and rises at night to hear these innocents sing while the justly punished blaspheme. It strikes him as a whisper from Providence that all his efforts to escape the one place of expiation were made to gain entrance to this one, a prison too, with gratings and bars to guard angels where he had seen them guard tigers, yet more austere than the galleys. His pride vanishes; he feels his pettiness and weeps often. Everything since Montfermeil has led him back to the Bishop's injunctions, Cosette through love, the convent through humility. At twilight he can be seen kneeling in the walk by the chapel, before the window through which he looked on the night of his arrival, turned toward the sister making reparation, as if he dared not kneel directly before God. The garden, the flowers, the children and the grave women permeate him, and he reflects that two houses of God have received him at the two crises of his life, one when all doors were closed to him, the other when society again hunted him. His heart melts in gratitude, and he loves more and more. Many years pass thus; Cosette is growing up."],["The narrative pauses to introduce a type rather than a person. Paris, Hugo says, has a child as the forest has a sparrow: the gamin. He is between seven and thirteen, lives in bands, sleeps in the open, wears a man's cast-off trousers and someone else's hat, swears, haunts wine-shops, knows thieves and sings dirty songs.","For all that, Hugo insists, there is no evil in him. He carries innocence in his heart like a pearl that mud cannot dissolve. Asked what this creature is, the great city would answer: it is my little one."],["The gamin sometimes has a shirt, but only one; sometimes shoes, but without soles; sometimes a lodging, which he loves because his mother is there, but he prefers the street for its liberty. His games are founded on hatred of the bourgeois. He earns coppers by calling cabs, letting down carriage steps and laying planks across flooded gutters, and the children trade in a currency of scrap copper they call loques.","He studies the small creatures of the pavements, prizes above all a fabulous monster called 'the deaf thing', and has as many sayings as Talleyrand, though more honest ones. Hugo gives two samples of his repartee, one aimed at a doctor following a funeral, the other at an indignant husband in a crowd."],["With a few sous the gamin gets into a theatre, where he is transformed into the titi, the creature of the topmost gallery, whose applause turns that stinking loft into Paradise. Hugo's formula: give a man the useless and deprive him of the necessary, and you have the gamin.","His literary taste is not classical; he nicknamed the actress Mademoiselle Mars 'Mademoiselle Muche'. He fishes in sewers, laughs at everything, mixes hymns with obscenities, and is, Hugo says, Rabelais in childhood. Nothing frightens or astonishes him; he would greet a sea-monster with a jeer."],["Paris begins with the lounger, who watches passively, and ends with the gamin, inexhaustible initiative; the whole monarchy is in the one and all anarchy in the other. The pale child of the faubourgs grows supple in suffering and watches social realities more closely than he seems to. Prejudice, abuse, tyranny and injustice should beware of him.","Of what clay is he made? The first mud to hand, Hugo answers; but a god has always passed over the gamin, and the spirit of Paris, which makes men of destiny out of children of chance, turns the jug into an amphora."],["The gamin loves the city but also solitude, and Hugo, confessing himself a lifelong prowler about the barriers of Paris, describes the strange amphibious country where fields meet roofs: waste ground, quarries, drum practice, tea-gardens by cemeteries, great walls cutting across sunlit land full of butterflies. He lists the familiar spots, from the Glacière to the Pierre-Plate de Châtillon with its rotting trapdoor over an old quarry.","In these limbos one finds groups of ragged children crowned with cornflowers, runaways from poor families, playing marbles round a hole, who, on seeing a stranger, remember they have a trade and offer to sell a stocking full of cockchafers or a bunch of lilacs. Sometimes thin, sunburnt girls are among them. For these children Paris and its suburbs are the universe; nothing exists two leagues beyond the barriers."],["At the time of the story there was no policeman on every corner and stray children abounded; the police picked up some two hundred and sixty homeless children a year under bridges and in building sites. All a man's crimes, Hugo says, begin in the vagabondage of the child. Yet he claims an exception for Paris: the vagabond child elsewhere is a lost man, but the Paris street boy stays almost intact inside, preserved by something in the air of the city like salt in the sea.","This does not lessen the anguish of meeting such a child, trailing the threads of a broken family, 'cast on the pavements of Paris'. The old monarchy did not discourage abandonment, since it hated schooling the people. Under Louis XIV, Colbert needed galley-slaves for the fleet and had street children of fifteen sent to the galleys; under Louis XV children vanished for purposes people only dared whisper about, and when fathers fought the guards who took them, it was the fathers who were hanged."],["The street boys of Paris nearly constitute a caste, which not everyone may join. The word gamin itself, Hugo notes, first reached print in 1834. Respect among them goes to the boy who has seen a man fall from Notre-Dame, who has stolen lead from statues, or who 'knows' a soldier; the great lament is never yet to have seen anyone fall from a fifth-floor window.","Irreverence in religion is a point of honour: a condemned man listening to his confessor in the cart is mocked for 'talking to his black cap'. Attending executions is a duty; the boys climb walls, lamps and chimneys to see, give the guillotine pet names, hoot at or admire the victims, and keep a tradition of what every famous criminal wore. A memorable accident, a bad cut 'to the bone', a strong fist, a squint or left-handedness all raise a boy's standing."],["In summer the gamin turns frog, diving into the Seine from coal barges and laundry boats in defiance of modesty and the police, and warning his fellows with a famous cry when the police arrive. He can always daub, if not read; from 1830 he scrawled pears on walls, and Hugo tells how Louis Philippe, meeting a tiny boy struggling to draw a giant pear on a gate pillar at Neuilly, finished the pear for him and gave him a louis.","He loves uproar and detests priests, though he will serve mass if asked. His two unattainable desires are to overthrow the government and to get his trousers mended. He knows every policeman in Paris by face and habit, with a private verdict on each."],["Hugo traces the gamin's spirit through French genius: Poquelin (Molière), the son of the fish-market, Beaumarchais, Voltaire, Camille Desmoulins, the general Championnet who as a boy played irreverent pranks in the churches of Paris. Mixed with good sense, gaminerie strengthens it as alcohol strengthens wine.","The gamin has bad teeth because he is ill-fed and fine eyes because he has wit. He plays in the gutter and stands up in a revolt; his effrontery persists under grapeshot; Barra the drummer-boy was a gamin of Paris. This child of the puddle is also the child of the ideal. In one word: he amuses himself because he is unhappy."],["The gamin is the infant populace with the wrinkle of the old world on his brow, a disease to be cured by light: by science, letters, art and education. Sooner or later France must choose between the children of France and the gamins of Paris.","Then Hugo turns to the city itself. Paris is the ceiling of the human race; everything that exists elsewhere exists there. In a long cascade of parallels he matches Parisian types, places and characters with those of ancient Athens and Rome, from fishwives and tightrope dancers to parasites and dandies, from Père-Lachaise to the Esquiline.","Paris accepts everything, laughs at everything, condones everything provided it is amused; it is all civilisations and all barbarisms in abridgement. And, Hugo adds grimly, it would regret not having a guillotine: a little of the Place de Grève seasons the eternal festival."],["There is no limit to Paris. It may be stupid when it chooses, and the universe is stupid with it; then it wakes and laughs in the face of the human race. Its gaiety is thunder and its farce holds a sceptre. Its 14th of July delivered the globe, its night of the 4th of August dissolved feudalism, and its light fills Washington, Bolívar, Garibaldi, John Brown and the patriots of a dozen nations; its books and thinkers are the manuals of mankind. Yet the same genius scrawls caricatures on the temple of Theseus and graffiti on the Pyramids.","Paris is a moral being, immense because it dares. To dare is the price of progress: Montesquieu may foresee, Diderot preach and Rousseau premeditate the Revolution, but Danton had to dare it. The cry 'Audacity!' is a fiat lux, and the same lightning runs from the torch of Prometheus to Cambronne's short pipe."],["To paint the child is to paint the city: the Parisian people, even grown, remains the street arab. The true Parisian race lives in the faubourgs, where it toils and suffers, among unknown beings from the porter of La Râpée to the knacker of Montfaucon. Cicero called them the dregs of the city and Burke the mob.","What does it matter that they go barefoot and cannot read? Hugo asks. Should they be abandoned for that? He calls on philosophers to teach, enlighten and sow enthusiasm, to spend their alphabets lavishly and proclaim rights. This crowd may be made sublime; the sand trodden underfoot, cast in the furnace, becomes the crystal through which Galileo and Newton discover stars."],["Eight or nine years after the second part of the story, a boy of eleven or twelve could be seen about the Boulevard du Temple and the Château-d'Eau who matched Hugo's ideal gamin in every point but one: behind the laugh of his age his heart was sombre and empty. He wore a man's trousers and a woman's chemise, given in charity, not by his parents. He had a father and mother, yet was an orphan, for his father did not think of him and his mother did not love him. The street was kinder to him than his mother's heart, and he was merry because he was free.","Every two or three months he said, 'I'll go and see mamma,' and walked down to the Salpêtrière quarter, to the house numbered 50–52 the reader already knows: the Gorbeau hovel. It was now, unusually, full of lodgers, all poor. The old principal tenant of Jean Valjean's day had died and been replaced by another, Madame Bourgon, remarkable only for her succession of three parakeets.","The most wretched household there was a family of four, father, mother and two grown daughters, in one attic room. The father had given his name as Jondrette, and had told the portress that anyone asking for a Pole, an Italian or a Spaniard meant him. This was the boy's family. When he came, he found a cold hearth and cold hearts, and was asked where he came from and where he was going; his answer was 'the street'. His mother loved his sisters, not him.","On the boulevard the boy was called Little Gavroche, probably, Hugo says, because his father's name was Jondrette: wretched families like to break the thread. The Jondrettes' room was the last along the corridor; the cell next to it was occupied by a very poor young man called M. Marius. Hugo promises to explain who this M. Marius was."],["In the streets near the Temple a few old inhabitants still remember M. Gillenormand, who was very much alive in 1831. He was over ninety, walked erect, talked loudly, ate, drank and snored, had all thirty-two of his teeth and wore spectacles only to read. The complete and rather haughty bourgeois of the eighteenth century, he claimed to have renounced women ten years before, not because he was too old but, he said, because he was too poor: he had about fifteen thousand francs a year and dreamed of an inheritance to spend on mistresses.","He was quick-tempered and beat people with his cane when contradicted, as men did in the great century. He had an unmarried daughter over fifty whom he scolded savagely and would gladly have whipped; to him she seemed eight years old. He boxed his servants' ears and was shaved daily by a barber who hated him out of jealousy over a pretty barberess. He prided himself on his penetration and loved the words 'the sensible man' and 'nature', by which he meant that civilisation gnaws and claws where savages devour."],["He owned and lived in No. 6 Rue des Filles-du-Calvaire in the Marais, in a vast first-floor apartment between street and garden hung with Gobelins and Beauvais pastorals, his bed enclosed in a nine-leaved Coromandel screen. A short staircase from his window led into the garden, which he climbed nimbly. He treasured a boudoir hung with flowered straw made by Louis XIV's convicts, inherited from a centenarian great-aunt.","He had had two wives. His manners lay between those of a courtier he had never been and a lawyer he might have been; he had been the kind of husband always deceived by his wives and never by his mistresses. He was a connoisseur of painting and owned a portrait by Jordaens of no one knows whom. He dressed like an Incroyable of the Directory, in swallowtail coat, knee-breeches and buckled shoes, and pronounced that the French Revolution was a heap of blackguards."],["At sixteen, ogled at the opera by two famous beauties at once, he had retreated towards an obscure little dancer his own age named Nahenry. He abounded in such memories, rhapsodised about the dancer Guimard's finery and a waistcoat of his youth, and had been called 'a charming fool' by Madame de Boufflers.","He read the newspapers only to laugh at the vulgar names of the men in power, imagining the farce of 'M. Gillenormand, minister'. He called everything by its name, decent or not, even before ladies, with an elegant lack of embarrassment that belonged to his century. His godfather had predicted genius for him and given him the names Luc-Esprit."],["Born at Moulins, he had won school prizes there and been crowned by the Duc de Nivernais, whom he called the Duc de Nevers and considered the great figure of the century; neither the Convention nor Napoleon had effaced the memory. He believed Catherine the Great had atoned for partitioning Poland by buying the secret of the 'elixir of gold', a cure for the catastrophes of love, and would have been furious to learn it was merely perchloride of iron.","He adored the Bourbons and had a horror of 1789, forever recounting how his gaiety and cleverness had saved his head during the Terror. Praise of the Republic in his presence made him purple with rage. Alluding to his ninety years he would say he hoped not to see ninety-three twice, hinting that he meant to live to a hundred."],["He held that a man fond of women and saddled with a disagreeable wife should hand her the purse-strings: she would busy herself with leases, lawsuits and notaries, feel herself sovereign, and console herself for his disdain by ruining him. He had applied the theory; his second wife had so managed his fortune that, when widowed, he had just enough to sink into an annuity of fifteen thousand francs, most of which would die with him. He did not mind, having no wish to leave property behind and no faith in the public debt.","He kept two servants, a man and a woman, and rebaptised each one who entered his service. Men got the name of their province; his last valet, a stout, short-winded fellow of fifty-five from Bayonne, became 'Basque'. Every maid was called Nicolette, including a certain Magnon of whom, Hugo says, more will be heard. When a haughty cook named Olympie asked thirty francs a month, he offered fifty on condition she be called Nicolette."],["Gillenormand turned sorrow into wrath and prided himself on remaining a gallant, which he called 'royal renown'. When he was eighty-four, a maid dismissed six months earlier sent him a yelling newborn boy in a basket and attributed it to him. Amid the household's uproar he was not angry at all; flattered, he cited aged fathers from history, declared the child none of his, and ordered it cared for. The woman, Magnon, sent a second boy the following year. He capitulated, returned both to their mother with eighty francs a month for their keep, on condition she stop, and insisted on visiting them from time to time, which he did.","He had had a brother, a priest and rector of the Academy of Poitiers for thirty-three years, a peaceable miser who gave alms only in bad or demonetised sous. Gillenormand himself gave gladly and nobly and liked everything about him done grandly, even his own fleecing; cheated in an inheritance, he complained only that he had been badly robbed.","By his first wife he had a daughter who remained unmarried; by his second, a daughter who died at about thirty, having married for love a soldier of fortune who had served the Republic and the Empire, won the cross at Austerlitz and been made colonel at Waterloo. 'He is the disgrace of my family,' said the old bourgeois. He took much snuff and believed very little in God."],["Such was Luc-Esprit Gillenormand, grey-haired rather than white, venerable in spite of everything, with something of the eighteenth century about him: frivolous and great.","In 1814 and the early Restoration, when he was a mere seventy-four, he lived in the Rue Servandoni near Saint-Sulpice, and only withdrew to the Marais when he quit society after eighty. There he walled himself in his habits. The chief was to keep his door closed all day and receive no one, not even the king, before evening: he dined at five, and after that his door was open. The day, he said, is vulgar and deserves a closed shutter."],["The two daughters were born ten years apart and were nothing alike. The younger had a charming soul turned toward flowers, verses and music, wedded in imagination to a vague heroic figure; the elder dreamed of a rich contractor or prefect for a husband and the glories of being 'Madame la Préfète'. One had wings like an angel, the other like a goose. The younger married the man of her dreams, and died. The elder never married.","When she enters the story she is an antique virtue and incombustible prude with a sharp nose and a dull mind; no one outside the family knows her first name, and she is simply 'Mademoiselle Gillenormand the elder'. Her modesty is carried to excess (she cherishes the frightful memory of a man once seeing her garter), yet she allows her grand-nephew Théodule, an officer of Lancers, to kiss her. She is also a bigot, devoted to a rococo chapel, with a chapel friend, Mademoiselle Vaubois, who is even stupider than she is. Age has softened her; she has never been malicious, and there breathes from her the stupor of a life that is finished without ever having begun.","She keeps house for her father, as the Bishop's sister kept house for him. Between the old man and the old maid there is also a child, a little boy, always trembling and mute before M. Gillenormand, who never addresses him except in a severe voice, sometimes with raised cane, calling him rascal and scoundrel. He idolises him. This is his grandson."],["While living in the Rue Servandoni, Gillenormand frequented aristocratic Royalist salons, where, bourgeois though he was, he was an oracle. About 1817 he spent two afternoons a week at the house of the Baronne de T., widow of a former ambassador to Berlin who had died bankrupt leaving ten volumes of memoirs on Mesmer. Her poor, proud salon was purely Royalist: they sipped tea, groaned at the century, the charter and the Bonapartists, sang fishwives' songs calling Napoleon 'Nicolas', made feeble puns on the ministry and sang their own Ça ira against the Bonapartists. In the Fualdès affair they sided with the accused because the victim was a Bonapartist. The salon's other cock was the Comte de Lamothe-Valois, respected despite, or because of, his part in the affair of the necklace.","Gillenormand's standing was first-rate; his great age and his sallies gave him dignity. He said that all kings who were not King of France were provincial kings, and, seeing Talleyrand pass at a Te Deum, 'There goes his Excellency the Evil One.'","He was always accompanied by his tall daughter, over forty and looking fifty, and by a handsome, rosy little boy of seven with trusting eyes, around whom voices murmured, 'How handsome he is! What a pity! Poor child!' He was called 'poor child' because his father was 'a brigand of the Loire'. This brigand of the Loire was Gillenormand's son-in-law, 'the disgrace of his family'."],["Around 1817 a passer-by on the bridge at Vernon might have seen, in the smallest of the walled gardens along the Seine, a bowed man of about fifty in a leather cap and wooden shoes, with a great scar down his face and nearly white hair, hoeing among celebrated tulips and dahlias. He lived alone with a servant woman, saw only the poor and his curé, the Abbé Mabeuf, and opened his door smiling to anyone who came to see his flowers. He was the 'brigand of the Loire'.","He was Georges Pontmercy. Hugo recounts his career: a soldier of the Rhine and Italian armies, wounded at Mont-Palissel, with Joubert at the Col de Tende, at Lodi, at Austerlitz where the Emperor gave him the cross, one of three survivors of the cemetery at Eylau, at Friedland, Moscow, the Berezina, Leipzig and the campaign of France, twenty-seven splinters taken from one arm. He followed Napoleon to Elba. At Waterloo, as chief of a squadron of cuirassiers, he captured the standard of the Lunenburg battalion, took a sabre-cut across the face, and was made colonel, baron and officer of the Legion of Honour on the field; an hour later he fell in the ravine of Ohain, from which, as the reader will remember, he was pulled out.","The Restoration put him on half-pay and sent him to live under surveillance at Vernon, refusing to recognise his rank, title or decoration. He signed himself 'Colonel Baron Pontmercy' regardless, wore his rosette in defiance of threatened prosecution, returned unopened letters addressed to 'Commandant Pontmercy', and asked the crown attorney whether he was permitted to wear his scar. Under the Empire he had married Mademoiselle Gillenormand; she died in 1815 leaving a child, and the grandfather claimed the boy, threatening to disinherit him otherwise. The father yielded for the child's sake and transferred his love to flowers; he was forbidden ever to see or speak to his son.","The boy, Marius, knew he had a father and nothing more, and from the whispers of his grandfather's society came to think of him with shame. Every two or three months the colonel slipped into Paris and hid behind a pillar at Saint-Sulpice to watch his son at mass, afraid of the aunt. The churchwarden there noticed the scarred man weeping, recognised him later at Vernon when visiting his brother the curé, and the two came to know the whole story and venerate him. Twice a year Marius wrote his father duty letters dictated by his aunt; the colonel's tender replies went unread into the grandfather's pocket."],["The salon was Marius's one window on life, and it made the once joyful child melancholy and grave. He took its ancient ladies with their biblical-sounding names for patriarchs and phantoms. Hugo catalogues the habitués: marquises and viscounts, the Chevalier de Port-de-Guy who had been chained in the galleys at Toulon in 1793 gathering guillotined bodies, a bailiff who had humbled the dancer Guimard, abbés and preachers, the Pope's Nuncio, two cardinals (one a merry billiards player), five peers of France including the Duc de Valentinois, Prince of Monaco. Over this feudal salon a bourgeois, Gillenormand, reigned.","There taste was haughty, speech barely a breath, and the vocabulary consisted of 'conserve' and 'conservation'; the masters were embalmed, the servants stuffed with straw. They spoke of the king in the third person because 'Your Majesty' had been soiled by the usurper, and held that the time since Coblentz had not existed. What did they do there? They were ultra: to be ultra is to attack the sceptre in the name of the throne, to find the Pope not papish enough and the king not royal enough, to be so strongly for as to be against.","Hugo sketches the six years from 1814 to 1820, when returned émigrés scorned the nobility of the Empire and called Bonaparte Scapin, a world now vanished beneath two revolutions; from 1818 the doctrinarians appeared, Royalists ashamed of being so, who argued that France should accept the whole of its history. He adds that he recalls this world without bitterness, since it touches the memory of his mother.","Marius was handed from his prudish aunt to a pedantic classical tutor, went through college and entered the law school. He was a fanatical and severe Royalist, did not much love his cynical grandfather, and was gloomy about his father: a cold and ardent, noble, proud, religious lad, dignified to harshness and pure to shyness."],["When Marius finished his classical studies Gillenormand left society for the Rue des Filles-du-Calvaire, with Nicolette and Basque for servants. In 1827, when Marius was seventeen, his grandfather told him he would leave for Vernon the next day to see his father, who was ill and asked for him. Marius was shaken; he had thought of everything except this, and, convinced the father who had abandoned him did not love him, he did not love him. Neither thought to take the night coach that would have got him there by morning.","At twilight the next day Marius asked in Vernon for 'M. Pontmercy's house', refusing him his titles as the Restoration did. A weeping woman opened the door and told him his father no longer expected him. In a candlelit room on the ground floor lay the colonel in his shirt, dead on the floor, with a doctor and the praying curé beside him. Struck by brain fever three days before, he had written for his son; that very evening, delirious, he had risen crying, 'My son is not coming! I shall go to meet him!', run into the antechamber and fallen dead. A large tear still stood on his cheek: his son's delay.","Marius looked for the first time at that scarred, heroic face and felt only the chill he would have felt before any dead stranger. Ashamed of his own coldness amid the others' grief, he let his hat fall to feign being overcome, and despised himself for it. The colonel left nothing; the sale of his furniture barely paid for his burial. The servant handed Marius a paper in the colonel's hand: the Emperor had made him a baron at Waterloo, and since the Restoration disputed the title, his son should take and bear it and would be worthy of it. Below, the colonel added that a sergeant named Thénardier had saved his life at Waterloo and now kept an inn near Paris, at Chelles or Montfermeil; if his son met him, he should do him all the good he could.","Marius kept the paper out of a vague respect for death. Gillenormand sold the colonel's sword and uniform to an old-clothes dealer; neighbours pillaged the rare flowers and the garden went to nettles. Marius stayed forty-eight hours, returned to his law studies and put on crape; in two days the colonel was buried and in three forgotten."],["One Sunday at Saint-Sulpice, Marius absent-mindedly knelt on a chair bearing the name 'Monsieur Mabeuf, warden'. An old man claimed it, and after mass apologised and explained his attachment to the spot. From that chair he had watched, for ten years, a poor brave father who came every two or three months to see his child at mass, the only way left him since the family forbade it; he hid behind a pillar, gazed at the boy and wept. A father-in-law and a rich aunt had threatened to disinherit the child if the father saw him; the man had sacrificed himself for his son's future over political opinions. He had been one of Bonaparte's colonels, lived at Vernon where the warden's brother was curé, and was called something like Pontmarie or Montpercy.","'Pontmercy,' said Marius, turning pale: 'he was my father.' The old warden clasped his hands and told him he could say he had had a father who loved him dearly. Marius saw him home.","Next day Marius asked his grandfather's leave for a three-day hunting party with friends. 'Four!' said Gillenormand, and whispered to his daughter with a wink that it was some love affair."],["Where Marius went on his absence Hugo defers. On his return he went straight to the law-school library for the Moniteur, then read every history, memoir and bulletin of the Republic and Empire. Finding his father's name in the bulletins of the grand army gave him a week's fever. He visited generals under whom Georges Pontmercy had served and went back to the warden Mabeuf, who told him of the colonel's flowers and solitude at Vernon. Marius came to know the rare, sweet, sublime man his father had been, and was on the high road to adoring him, while his grandfather, seeing him vanish from meals, chuckled that it was an affair of passion.","His ideas changed with his heart. The Republic and Empire, which had been a guillotine and a sword in the dark, now appeared as stars and a rising sun; he judged that all this had been good, and understood that he had known neither his country nor his father. He was tormented by remorse that his love could now be spoken only to a tomb. From rehabilitating his father he passed, with more labour, to rehabilitating Napoleon, whom the party of 1814 had taught him to see as the Corsican Ogre; one night, reading the bulletins at his open window under the stars, hearing in imagination the drums and cavalry of the Empire, he leapt up, stretched his arms into the dark and cried, 'Long live the Emperor!' Napoleon became for him the incarnation of France, the man-people. Hugo notes that, like all converts, he went too far, admitting force along with genius.","Having shed his Bourbon and ultra skin and become a democrat and republican, he ordered a hundred cards engraved 'Le Baron Marius Pontmercy', and, knowing no one to leave them with, kept them in his pocket. As he drew nearer his father he receded from his grandfather, whose frivolity had always jarred on him and who, he now reflected, had torn him from the colonel; pity for the father brought him near to aversion for the grandfather, though outwardly he only grew colder and rarer at home. His aunt wondered where he went. On one brief trip he went to Montfermeil to find the sergeant of Waterloo, the innkeeper Thénardier, but the inn had failed and closed and no one knew where he had gone. The family thought they saw something hanging from his neck on a black ribbon."],["Lieutenant Théodule Gillenormand, a great-grand-nephew on the paternal side, was a fine lancer officer with a lady's waist who rarely visited Paris; Marius had never met him. He was the aunt's favourite because she never saw him. One morning, puzzled and faintly scandalised that Marius had again asked leave to go away that evening, Mademoiselle Gillenormand was embroidering cartwheels when Théodule walked in, passing through Paris as his regiment moved from Melun to Gaillon. She gave him ten louis and begged him to stay; he had to leave that night.","Théodule mentioned that, booking his seat in the diligence, he had seen the name Marius Pontmercy on the card of a traveller in the imperial, bound for Andelys by way of Vernon. The aunt seized on it: Marius absents himself, travels, sleeps out, and nobody knows why. 'Some petticoat or other,' said Théodule; 'a lass.' Since the cousins did not know each other, she asked him to follow Marius, get a sight of the girl and write to amuse the grandfather. Touched by the ten louis, he agreed, telling himself he had become a duenna.","He slept all night in the coupé and woke at Vernon, where he was to change coaches, laughing at the commission. Then Marius climbed down from the imperial, bought the finest flowers from a peasant girl's basket and walked, seeing nothing, towards the church. Now curious on his own account, Théodule followed, expecting a rendezvous spiced with mass. Marius skirted the apse and vanished round a corner; Théodule crept after him and stopped in amazement. Marius was kneeling in the grass on a grave, his bouquet strewn on it, sobbing, before a black wooden cross lettered in white: Colonel Baron Pontmercy."],["The grave was where Marius had gone each time his grandfather said he was 'sleeping out'. Théodule, abashed before the tomb and the colonel, retreated with something like a military salute and decided not to write to his aunt. Nothing might have come of it had the scene not had an immediate echo in Paris. Returning on the third morning, Marius ran up to his room, threw off his greatcoat and the black ribbon from his neck and went straight out to the swimming-school. Gillenormand, who had hurried upstairs to embrace and question him, found only the coat and ribbon on the bed and said, 'I like this better.'","He carried his trophies triumphantly to the salon where his daughter sat embroidering: here was the romance, the portrait worn on the heart. The black shagreen case held only a folded paper, which he took for a love-letter, and the aunt put on her spectacles. They read the colonel's words: the Emperor had made him a baron at Waterloo, and his son should take the title and be worthy of it. Father and daughter felt chilled as by a death's-head. 'It is the slasher's handwriting,' Gillenormand murmured. Then a packet of blue paper fell from the coat pocket: Marius's hundred cards, 'Le Baron Marius Pontmercy'. Gillenormand rang for Nicolette, flung coat, ribbon and case on the floor and told her to carry the duds away. An hour of silence followed.","When Marius came in, his grandfather, holding one of the cards, mockingly congratulated him on being a baron. 'It means that I am the son of my father,' Marius replied. 'I am your father,' said Gillenormand harshly. Marius answered that his father was a humble and heroic man who had served the Republic and France gloriously for twenty-five years, had taken two flags and twenty wounds, and had died forgotten, having made only one mistake, to love two ingrates too well, his country and his son. At the word 'republic' the old man sprang up and exploded: he did not know and would not know what Marius's father was, but all who served B-u-o-naparte were brigands, traitors and cowards, and Marius was no more a baron than his slipper.","Torn between a sacred grave and hoary locks, unable to insult his grandfather or leave his father unavenged, Marius stood staggering, then cried in a voice of thunder, 'Down with the Bourbons, and that great hog of a Louis XVIII!' (dead these four years). Gillenormand turned white, bowed solemnly to a bust of the Duc de Berry, paced the room twice in silence, told his daughter with an almost calm smile that a baron like this gentleman and a bourgeois like himself could not stay under one roof, then pointed at Marius and shouted, 'Be off!' Marius left. Next day Gillenormand ordered his daughter to send 'that blood-drinker' sixty pistoles every six months and never to speak his name, and addressed her as 'you' for three months. Nicolette had dropped the medallion on the dark attic stairs; it was never found, and Marius, who knew the lines by heart, was convinced 'Monsieur Gillenormand' had burned his father's testament. He went off in a hackney-coach toward the Latin quarter with thirty francs, his watch and a few clothes."],["A revolutionary quiver was in the air of that time; Royalists were becoming liberals and liberals democrats, and people adored both Napoleon and liberty. Among the small secret groups forming in Paris was the society of the Friends of the A.B.C., ostensibly for the education of children, really for the elevation of man: a pun on the Abaissé, the debased, the people. They met near the markets in a wine-shop called Corinthe and, more often, in a back room of the Café Musain near the Panthéon, reached by a long corridor, with a private stair to the Rue des Grès and a map of Republican France on the wall. They were mostly students from the South, on good terms with working men.","Enjolras, an only son and wealthy, is angelically handsome and capable of being terrible, a priest and soldier of the ideal, twenty-two looking seventeen, with one passion, the right, and no eye for women; he would have been Saint-Just in the Convention. Combeferre represents the philosophy of the Revolution beside Enjolras's logic: broader, gentler, a Polytechnic graduate who reads everything, believes in railways and balloons, and prefers the dawn to the conflagration; Enjolras is a chief, Combeferre a guide. Jean Prouvaire, who spells his name Jehan, is softer still: a shy, badly dressed, erudite poet in love, who studies social questions by day and planets by night, and is intrepid. Feuilly is a fan-maker, an orphan earning three francs a day, self-taught, who has adopted the oppressed peoples of the world and is inexhaustible on the partition of Poland.","Courfeyrac has dropped the particle from his father's name; he has the sparkling youth of Tholomyès in 1817, but with a paladin where Tholomyès had a district attorney; he is the centre of the group, its warmth. Bahorel, a cheerful brawler in his eleventh year as a law student, spends his peasant parents' money doing nothing, loves a quarrel less than an uprising and an uprising less than a revolution, and links the group to others. Lesgle or Laigle of Meaux, the one bald member, signs himself Légle (de Meaux) and is nicknamed Bossuet: a gay and spectacularly unlucky fellow who has lost his inheritance and lodges wherever he can, most often with Joly. Joly is a medical student and hypochondriac who sleeps with his head to the south and feels his pulse in thunderstorms, otherwise the gayest of all.","All share one religion, Progress, and are the direct sons of '89. Among them sits one sceptic, Grantaire, who signs himself with the rebus R: a drinker, boxer and connoisseur of every café in Paris, inordinately ugly, who believes in nothing, mocks every devotion and hums drinking-songs. His one fanaticism is a man, Enjolras, whom he admires, loves and venerates as his opposite; his shapeless ideas lean on that firmness like a spine. Enjolras disdains the drunkard and treats him harshly; Grantaire, rebuffed and always returning, says of him, 'What fine marble!'"],["On the afternoon that followed Marius's expulsion, Laigle de Meaux leaned against the doorpost of the Café Musain, musing on a misadventure at the law school two days earlier. A cab passed at a walk carrying a young man and a bag labelled Marius Pontmercy. Laigle hailed him. Marius, fresh from his grandfather's and in no mood for jokes, did not know him; Laigle did not know Marius either, but had been looking for him.","He told his story. Entering the lecture by chance, he had heard the professor Blondeau, who delighted in scenting out absentees, call 'Marius Pontmercy' three times; at the third call, when the name would have been erased and sixty francs lost, Laigle, out of compassion for an honourable idler who did not attend, answered 'Present!' Blondeau then skipped to the letter L and called 'Laigle'; Laigle answered 'Present!' again, and Blondeau, observing that if he was Pontmercy he was not Laigle, crossed him off instead. Laigle delivered a mock funeral oration on Blondeau and declared himself delighted: the erasure saved him from becoming a lawyer.","Asked where he lived so that Laigle might call to thank him, Marius answered, 'In this cab,' which he had hired by the hour and did not know how to get rid of. Courfeyrac came out of the café at that moment and said, 'Come to my place.' Laigle claimed priority but admitted he had no home; Courfeyrac told 'Bossuet' to hold his tongue, and Laigle explained the metaphor to a puzzled Marius. That evening Marius was installed beside Courfeyrac in a room at the hôtel de la Porte-Saint-Jacques."],["Within days Marius and Courfeyrac were friends; Courfeyrac asked no questions, since at that age faces say everything. One morning he asked Marius his political opinions. 'A democrat-Bonapartist,' said Marius. 'The grey hue of a reassured rat,' said Courfeyrac, and next day took him to the Café Musain to give him, as he whispered, his entry to the revolution, presenting him to the Friends of the A.B.C. with the single word 'A pupil'.","Marius had fallen into a wasps' nest of wits. Solitary and used to soliloquy, he was buffeted by these free minds talking of philosophy, art, history and religion in unexpected ways, and, having thought himself fixed since exchanging his grandfather's opinions for his father's, he uneasily suspected he was not. Nothing seemed sacred to them. When Bahorel cried down classical tragedy, Combeferre defended its right to exist as the duck exists beside the bird. In the Rue Jean-Jacques Rousseau, Courfeyrac joked about Rousseau and Thérèse and their children sent to the foundlings, and Enjolras cut him short: Rousseau denied his children, perhaps, but he adopted the people.","Not one of them said 'the Emperor'. Jean Prouvaire alone sometimes said Napoleon; the others said Bonaparte, and Enjolras pronounced it Buonaparte. Marius was vaguely surprised: the beginning of wisdom."],["Nearly all the Friends of the A.B.C. were in the back room that evening, talking loudly and at random while Enjolras and Marius kept silent. Only Louison the dishwasher ever passed through. Grantaire, thoroughly drunk, held one corner, demanding drink and declaring life a hideous invention, everything vanity dressed in big words, every virtue bordering on a vice, all history plagiarism, victory stupid and glory a matter of convincing. He ran through the nations one by one, Greece, England, America, Germany, Italy, Russia, Asia, finding each absurd, concluded that Paris carried the day since even its ragpickers were sybarites, and ended by catching at Louison as his Cleopatra. When Bossuet tried to quiet him he grew worse, pronouncing man a failure and woman a rhyme for infamous.","Bossuet, meanwhile, was deep in a point of Norman customary law; two heads bent over paper were inventing names for a vaudeville; a man of thirty was warning a youth of eighteen that his duelling adversary was a fine left-handed swordsman. Joly and Bahorel played dominoes and talked of love: Bahorel on the dangers of a mistress who laughs, Joly sighing over his sulking Musichetta and being advised to buy elegant trousers. In another corner Jean Prouvaire, roused from his timidity, championed Olympus against Christian mythology, insisting Jupiter might not be dead and Pan might still breathe into the willows.","In the last corner they talked politics. Combeferre weakly defended the Charter; Courfeyrac attacked it, brandishing a copy of the Touquet Charter. He wanted no kings, if only because they are expensive, and no charter granted from above, which is a mask with a lie beneath it and an Article 14 to snatch back what it gives. It was winter and a fire was burning; he crumpled the Charter and flung it in. 'The charter metamorphosed into flame,' said Combeferre, watching it burn."],["In the midst of the crossfire of jokes, Bossuet ended some remark to Combeferre with the date '18 June 1815, Waterloo'. Marius, elbows on a table beside a glass of water, looked up. Courfeyrac observed that 18 was Bonaparte's fatal number, Brumaire at the beginning, June at the end. Enjolras, silent till then, called it the crime and the expiation. That word was more than Marius could bear. He went to the map of France on the wall, laid his finger on the inset of Corsica and said: a little island which has made France very great.","A chill fell on the room. Enjolras, gazing at nothing, replied that France needed no Corsica to be great; France is great because she is France. Marius turned on him and poured out his confession of faith: he heard them say Buonaparte like Royalists, and his grandfather did worse; where was their enthusiasm, and whom did they admire if not the Emperor? He had everything: codes like Justinian, dictation like Caesar, bulletins that were Iliads; he taught emperors majesty at Tilsit and answered Laplace at the Academy, and then Europe trembled, the armies moved, and he rose on the horizon with a blazing brand, the archangel of war. What a destiny for a nation to be the Empire of such an Emperor, to make kings of grenadiers, to hurl into history words like Marengo, Arcola, Austerlitz, to conquer the world twice, by conquest and by dazzlement; what greater thing is there?","'To be free,' said Combeferre. The cold, simple words went through Marius's epic like a blade and he lowered his head; when he looked up, Combeferre had gone and all the others but Enjolras had followed him. As Marius gathered his thoughts to argue, they heard Combeferre on the stairs singing a song about a man who, offered glory and war by Caesar at the price of leaving his mother, would hand back the sceptre and chariot: 'I love my mother better.' Marius repeated mechanically, 'My mother?' and felt Enjolras's hand on his shoulder. 'Citizen,' said Enjolras, 'my mother is the Republic.'"],["The evening left Marius profoundly shaken, like earth torn open for the seed. He had barely acquired a faith and already doubted it; standing between two religions, one not yet left and one not yet entered, he feared that each step toward his friends was a step away from his father. Daring to his grandfather, behind the times to his friends, he felt doubly isolated and ceased to go to the Café Musain.","Reality elbowed him. One morning the hotel-keeper, who had let him stay on Courfeyrac's word, demanded his money. Marius told Courfeyrac what he had never mentioned: that he was alone in the world without relatives. Courfeyrac questioned him: fifteen francs, no debts wanted, a frock-coat and trousers to sell, a gold watch, no English or German, which might have earned him translating work for a publisher friend. 'I will learn English and German,' said Marius, and live meanwhile on his clothes and watch. The clothes fetched twenty francs, the watch forty-five; with his fifteen that made eighty. The hotel bill was seventy, leaving ten francs, as Courfeyrac drily computed, five for English and five for German.","Aunt Gillenormand, good-hearted at bottom, had found out where he lived and sent sixty pistoles, six hundred francs in gold, in a sealed box. Marius sent the money back with a respectful letter saying he could supply all his needs; at that moment he had three francs. She did not tell her father of the refusal, for fear of his rage. Marius left the hotel rather than run into debt."],["Life became hard. Marius ate what the French call the mad cow: days without bread, nights without sleep, evenings without a candle, a door locked for unpaid rent, the sneers of porters and cook-shop men. At the age when a young man most needs his pride he was jeered at for his clothes and ridiculed for his poverty. Hugo calls it the crucible from which the feeble emerge base and the strong sublime; there are obscure heroes in the petty battles of destitution.","There came a time when Marius swept his own landing, bought a sou's worth of Brie and slipped into the baker's at dusk as if stealing his loaf. An awkward young man with books under his arm would bow to the butcher's wife, buy a mutton cutlet for six or seven sous, and live on it for three days: meat the first day, fat the second, the bone the third. His aunt sent the sixty pistoles again and again; he returned them every time. Still in mourning for his father, he found his black clothes wearing out; Courfeyrac gave him an old coat which Marius had turned for thirty sous, but it was green, so he went out only after nightfall, which made it black: he clothed himself with the night.","In spite of everything he was admitted to practise as a lawyer, nominally living in Courfeyrac's room, whose law books served as the required library. He informed his grandfather in a cold, submissive letter. Gillenormand read it trembling, tore it in four and threw it in the wastebasket; days later his daughter overheard him telling himself that a man cannot be a baron and a lawyer at the same time."],["Wretchedness ends by becoming bearable. Through Courfeyrac's publisher friend Marius, having learned English and German, earned about seven hundred francs a year drawing up prospectuses, translating newspapers and compiling biographies. He rented for thirty francs a year a fireless room called a cabinet in the Gorbeau house, paid the old principal tenant three francs a month to sweep it and bring hot water, an egg and a roll each morning, and dined at six at Rousseau's in the Rue Saint-Jacques, 'Rousseau the Aquatic', on a six-sou plate of meat, vegetables, dessert and bread for sixteen sous, drinking water and winning a smile from Madame Rousseau. Food, rent, service, clothes, linen and washing came to about six hundred and fifty francs; he was rich, and sometimes lent ten francs to a friend. He had two black suits, one old and one new, and three shirts, always ragged, so he buttoned his coat to the chin.","It had taken hard years to reach this flourishing state, and he had never once contracted a debt, which he regarded as the beginning of slavery; rather than borrow he went without food. Fearing that lowered fortunes might lower his soul, he kept a jealous watch on his pride and was timid to the point of rudeness. A secret inner force upheld him: the soul is the only bird that bears up its own cage.","Beside his father's name another was graven in his heart, Thénardier, the intrepid sergeant who had carried the colonel out of the bullets at Waterloo. Marius worshipped him on a lesser altar beside his father, the more tenderly because he had learned at Montfermeil of the innkeeper's ruin and bankruptcy. For three years he had scoured Chelles, Bondy, Gournay, Nogent and Lagny with his small savings, finding no trace; Thénardier was thought to have gone abroad, and even his creditors could not find him. It was the one debt his father had left him, and his sweetest dream was to find Thénardier, rescue him from misery and say, 'You do not know me; well, I know you! Dispose of me!'"],["Marius was twenty and had been gone three years; neither side had sought the other. He was mistaken about his grandfather's heart. There is no grandfather who does not adore his grandson, and Gillenormand, who idolised Marius with boxes on the ear, felt a black void when he left. He had expected the 'Jacobin' to return; years passed and he did not. Asked whether he would turn him out again, his pride said yes and his old head sadly shook no. He never inquired about Marius but thought of him incessantly, grew more retired, and said sometimes, 'Oh! if he only would return, what a good box on the ear I would give him!' To busybodies he answered with a sigh that Monsieur le Baron de Pontmercy was pettifogging in some corner. The aunt thought too little to love much and cared more for her cat or parakeet.","Marius, meanwhile, applauded himself. Misfortune had removed his bitterness; he thought of Gillenormand kindly but would accept nothing from the man who had been unkind to his father. He was happy to suffer: it was an expiation for his own impious indifference, the only way to approach and resemble his father, to be brave before indigence as the colonel had been before the enemy, and surely what 'He will be worthy of it' had meant. He had left a child and was now a man. Hugo praises the poverty of youth that turns the will to effort and the soul to aspiration, giving the poor young man work, which makes him free, and thought, which makes him dignified.","Yet Marius inclined too far toward contemplation; having secured his living he worked as little as possible and gave whole days to reverie, not seeing that this was becoming a form of idleness. Though a lawyer, he did not plead. A publisher, Magimel, offered to lodge him and pay fifteen hundred francs a year for regular work; he refused rather than lose his liberty. He had not entered decisively into Enjolras's group, though they remained friends. He had two friends, one young, Courfeyrac, and one old, M. Mabeuf, to whom he owed the revolution in himself and the knowledge of his father: 'He operated on me for a cataract.' Mabeuf had been the candle, not the hand that carried it, and understood nothing of Marius's politics."],["When Mabeuf told Marius he approved of political opinions, he meant it: he approved them all, provided they left him in peace. He was neither Royalist nor Bonapartist but a bouquinist, a collector of old books, and could not see why men hated each other over charters and republics when there were mosses, shrubs and folios to look at. He had bred seedling pears and perhaps originated the October Mirabelle; what the colonel did for flowers he did for fruits. He went to mass from gentleness, loving men's faces but hating their noise, and had chosen the career of warden to be something in the State. He had never loved a woman as much as a tulip bulb; asked if he had ever married, he said he had forgotten. He had published a Flora of the Environs of Cauteretz with coloured plates which sold well and brought in about two thousand francs a year, nearly his whole fortune, and had patiently formed a precious collection of rare books. The sight of a gun chilled his blood. His only friend was an old bookseller of the Porte-Saint-Jacques named Royal, and his dream was to naturalise indigo in France.","His housekeeper, an innocent old spinster with a moustache and a cat called Sultan, whom he nicknamed Mother Plutarque, could read and spent Sundays counting her linen. Mabeuf took to Marius because the young man's gentleness warmed his age without startling his timidity, and when Marius came saturated with his father's battles, Mabeuf talked of the hero from the point of view of flowers.","About 1830 his brother the curé died, a notary's failure swallowed the ten thousand francs that was all he and his brother had, and the July Revolution brought a crisis in publishing; the first thing not to sell in hard times is a Flora, and purchasers stopped coming. He gave up the Rue Mésières and his wardenship, sold his prints rather than his books, tried a house in the Rue Montparnasse which was too dear and too near a shooting-gallery, and settled for fifty crowns a year in a thatched cottage with a garden and well in the village of Austerlitz near the Salpêtrière, a name he found disagreeably warlike. He hung his herbariums himself, dug all day and told the melancholy Mother Plutarque, 'We have the indigo!' Only Royal and Marius were admitted there.","Absorbed minds, Hugo observes, are slow to notice their own ruin; Mabeuf remained serene as his hopes went out one by one, like a clock that keeps going after the key is lost. His pleasures were cheap and unexpected: hearing Mother Plutarque read aloud a romance in which 'the beauty pouted, and the dragoon', he drifted into a reverie about Buddha converting the dragon and pronounced it a beautiful book."],["Marius liked the candid old man who was sliding toward indigence without becoming melancholy, and saw him, like Courfeyrac, rarely, twice a month at most. His pleasure was to walk alone on the outer boulevards, the Champ-de-Mars or the quieter alleys of the Luxembourg, spending half a day watching a market garden or a horse at a waterwheel; passers-by found his look suspicious. On one such stroll he had found the Gorbeau house and taken a room there for its isolation and cheapness; there he was known only as M. Marius. Some of his father's old generals and comrades, Comte Pajol, General Bellavesne, General Fririon, invited him to their evenings; he went, in his new coat, only on freezing days, since he could not afford a carriage and would not arrive with boots other than mirrors. A drawing-room, he said, forgives everything but your shoes.","Reverie had dissolved his political fevers, and the Revolution of 1830 had calmed them. He held the same opinions, tempered; more exactly, he had no longer opinions but sympathies. His party was humanity; within humanity France, within the nation the people, within the people woman. He now preferred an idea to a deed and Job to Marengo, and from gazing at the sky over the boulevards had come to look at little else, while multiplying plans and scaffoldings for the future. Hugo adds that a man is better judged by his dreams than by his thoughts, and Marius's soul, seen within, would have dazzled by its purity.","Towards the middle of 1831 the old woman who served him told him that his neighbours, the wretched Jondrette family, were being turned out for owing two quarters' rent, twenty francs. Marius, who spent his days outside and hardly knew he had neighbours, had thirty francs saved in a drawer. He gave her twenty-five: pay for the poor people, give them five francs, and do not tell them it was I."],["Théodule's regiment came to Paris, and the aunt, having once used him to spy on Marius, now planned to have him replace Marius in the old man's affections: for Marius, read Théodule. She announced that his grandnephew would call. 'Who's Théodule?' said Gillenormand, and returned to his Royalist paper, which reported that the law and medical students would assemble at midday on the Place du Panthéon to deliberate on the National Guard's artillery. He thought at once of Marius, who was a student and would surely go.","Théodule entered in plain clothes, calculating that the old druid had not sunk all his money in an annuity, and was told by his aunt in a whisper to approve of everything. He stammered 'Good day, uncle'; Gillenormand told him to sit down and forgot him, pacing with his hands in his fobs and talking aloud. Brats scarcely weaned were to deliberate in the open air; the Republicans and the galley-slaves were one nose and one handkerchief. He turned to the absent Marius: why had he left his house to become a Republican, a Carbonaro? The people wanted no republic; the nineteenth century was poison; the young let their beards grow, called themselves romantics, ran to Hernani, dressed like stablemen, were afraid of women and reconstructed Europe while ogling laundresses' ankles. 'That is true,' 'You are right, uncle,' 'That is evident,' said Théodule at intervals, and once ventured that there should be no newspaper but the Moniteur and no book but the Army List.","Gillenormand went on, cursing newspapers, the Odéon arcades where students read them, Sieyès the regicide turned senator, and the whole dream of progress, humanity and revolution, which he denounced as madness, crime and monster. 'Parbleu!' cried the lieutenant, 'that is wonderfully true.' The old man stopped in mid-gesture, wheeled round, stared him in the eyes and said: 'You are a fool.'"],["Marius was now a handsome young man with thick black hair, a lofty brow and a charming smile that softened a severe face, reserved and cold in manner. In his worst poverty he had thought girls turned to laugh at his clothes, when they turned because they dreamed of him, and the misunderstanding had made him shy; he fled them all. Courfeyrac, who called him Monsieur l'Abbé, advised him to read fewer books and look at the lasses, which only made Marius avoid women, and Courfeyrac, for a week.","Two women he did not flee, because he never thought of them as women: the bearded old woman who swept his room, and a sort of little girl he saw constantly and never looked at. For more than a year, in the Luxembourg walk along the Pépinière, at its loneliest end on the Rue de l'Ouest side, he had found a man and a very young girl almost always on the same bench. The man seemed about sixty, sad, serious, with the robust and weary look of a retired officer, a kindly but unapproachable air, very white hair, and a blue coat, a broad-brimmed hat and a coarse but dazzling white shirt. The girl, when she first came, was thirteen or fourteen, thin almost to plainness, awkward, with a promise of fine eyes raised with a displeasing assurance, in a badly cut black merino gown like a convent schoolgirl's. They looked like father and daughter and talked together peacefully, she chattering, he saying little and gazing at her with ineffable paternity.","Marius had the mechanical habit of walking the alley's length five or six times, passing the bench each time, five or six days a week, without a greeting on either side. The couple had caught the attention of the few students who used that walk; Courfeyrac, finding the girl homely, had fled, naming the daughter Mademoiselle Lanoire for her dress and the father Monsieur Leblanc for his hair, and the nickname stuck for want of any other. Hugo adopts it too. Through the first year Marius saw them nearly every day and found the man to his taste and the girl insipid."],["In the second year, at the point the story has now reached, Marius's habit of the Luxembourg lapsed for nearly six months without his quite knowing why. One serene summer morning, in joyous mood, he went straight to his alley and saw the familiar couple on their bench. The man was the same; the girl, it seemed to him, was not. She was now a tall and beautiful creature at the exact moment when a woman's lines still carry a child's grace, 'fifteen years': brown hair threaded with gold, a marble brow, rose-leaf cheeks, an exquisite mouth, and a nose that was not handsome but pretty, the Parisian nose. Her eyes were lowered; he saw only long chestnut lashes.","He thought for a moment she must be a sister of the earlier girl, but on his second pass recognised her: in six months the little girl had become a young maiden, as trees flower in three April days. She had grown and been idealised, and taste had come with beauty. Gone were the felt hat, merino gown and red hands; she wore black damask with a cape of the same, a white crape bonnet, white gloves, a parasol with a carved Chinese ivory handle and silk shoes, and exhaled a youthful perfume. The man was as usual.","The second time Marius approached, she raised eyes of deep celestial blue that held as yet only a child's glance, looked at him as indifferently as at a brat under the sycamores or a marble vase, and he walked on thinking of something else. In the following days he found 'the father and daughter' there as usual and paid them no more attention now that she was beautiful than when she was plain."],["One day the air was warm, the Luxembourg flooded with light and shade, sparrows twittering in the chestnuts, and Marius, his soul open to nature and thinking of nothing, passed the bench; the young girl raised her eyes to him and the two glances met. What was in hers he could not have said: nothing and everything, a strange flash. She dropped her eyes and he walked on.","It was no longer the simple eye of a child but a mysterious gulf half opened and abruptly closed. Hugo dwells on that first gaze of a soul that does not yet know itself, like dawn in the sky, innocent and fatal, a snare the maiden sets without knowing it, from which the flower called love springs in the depths of whoever meets it.","That evening in his garret Marius looked over his clothes and perceived for the first time that he had been going to the Luxembourg in his everyday things: a battered hat, coarse carter's boots, black trousers white at the knees and a coat pale at the elbows. He judged this slovenly, indecorous and inconceivably stupid."],["Next day Marius put on his new coat, trousers, hat and boots, and gloves, a tremendous luxury, and set out, pretending not to see Courfeyrac on the way; Courfeyrac told his friends he had met Marius's new hat and coat with Marius inside them, looking utterly stupid, as if going to an examination. At the Luxembourg Marius circled the basin, stared at the swans and a mouldy statue, listened to a fat bourgeois instructing his small son to shun excess, and only then turned slowly toward his alley, as if both forced and forbidden to go.","Seeing M. Leblanc and the girl on their bench at the far end, he buttoned his coat to the top, smoothed it, admired the gleam of his trousers and marched on the bench as on a fortress, all the while thinking about the stupidity of the Manuel du Baccalauréat and with a whistling in his ears. His pace slackened; well short of the bench he turned back without knowing why, holding himself erect in case anyone was looking. On the next pass he got within three trees and hesitated, then with a violent effort rushed past, red to the ears, hand thrust into his coat like a statesman, not daring to look, and heard an ineffable voice that must be hers; he consoled himself that she would esteem him if she knew he was the real author of a dissertation M. François de Neufchâteau had passed off as his own. He passed again, very pale, and fancied she was gazing after him, which made him stumble.","He went no nearer, but sat down in the middle of the walk, a thing he never did, reflecting that it was hard that a white bonnet and black gown should be insensible to splendid trousers and a new coat. After a quarter of an hour he rose, stood motionless, and for the first time in fifteen months considered that the gentleman who sat there daily with his daughter had probably noticed him and found his assiduity singular; for the first time, too, he felt some irreverence in calling the stranger M. Leblanc even in thought. He traced figures in the sand with his cane, turned his back on the bench and went home. He forgot to dine, ate a bit of bread at eight, and brushed and folded his coat with great care before bed."],["Next day Ma'am Bougon, as Courfeyrac styled the old portress and principal tenant of the Gorbeau hovel (her name was really Madame Burgon), observed with stupefaction that M. Marius was going out again in his new coat. He went to the Luxembourg but no further than his bench midway along the alley, and sat there until the gates closed, making out from afar the white bonnet, the black dress and above all that blue light; he did not see M. Leblanc and his daughter leave, and weeks later could not recall where he had dined.","On the third day Ma'am Bougon was thunderstruck, 'Three days in succession!', and tried to follow him, a hippopotamus pursuing a chamois; she lost him in two minutes and came back choking with asthma and furious. At the Luxembourg the girl was there with M. Leblanc; Marius approached as near as he dared pretending to read, retreated to his bench and spent four hours watching sparrows who seemed to mock him.","So a fortnight passed. Marius came no longer to stroll but to sit always in the same place without knowing why, putting on his new coat every morning for the purpose of not showing himself. She was decidedly a marvellous beauty; the only possible criticism was that the contrast between her melancholy gaze and her merry smile gave her face a rather wild effect, strange without ceasing to be charming."],["Near the end of the second week Marius sat with an open book he had not turned a page of for two hours when he started: Leblanc and his daughter had risen and, arm in arm, were advancing toward the middle of the alley where he sat. He closed and opened his book and trembled; the aureole was coming straight at him and he would not have time to strike an attitude. He imagined M. Leblanc darting angry looks at him and feared the gentleman might speak to him. He dropped his head; when he raised it they were close. The girl passed and glanced at him, a steady gaze of pensive sweetness that thrilled him from head to foot, as if reproaching him for not having come to her and saying, 'I am coming myself.' He was dazzled, and at the same time horribly vexed by the dust on his boots, which he was sure she had looked at.","He followed her with his eyes until she vanished, then paced the Luxembourg like a madman, laughing and talking to himself, so dreamy near the nursemaids that each thought him in love with her. Leaving the garden in hopes of meeting her in the street, he ran into Courfeyrac under the Odéon arcades and took him to dine at Rousseau's, where he ate like an ogre, spent six francs, tipped six sous and praised a speech by Audry de Puyraveau. He treated Courfeyrac to Frédérick in L'Auberge des Adrets at the Porte-Saint-Martin and was enormously amused, yet his shyness redoubled: he refused to look at a modiste's garter and was almost horrified when Courfeyrac wished to add her to his collection.","At breakfast at the Café Voltaire next morning he ate more still, was thoughtful and merry, embraced a stranger from the provinces and, amid a discussion of Guicherat's grammars, exclaimed that it was very agreeable to have the cross. 'That's queer,' whispered Courfeyrac to Jean Prouvaire. 'No,' said Prouvaire, 'that's serious.' Marius had reached the first violent and charming hour of a grand passion; a glance had done it all. Hugo compares a woman's glance to quiet machinery past which one walks safely for years until the day it catches hold, after which one falls from gearing to gearing and emerges either disfigured by shame or transfigured by passion."],["Marius's solitude, pride, chastity and life of reverie had prepared him for passion; his worship of his father had retreated to the depths of his soul and something was needed in the foreground. Love came. For a full month he went to the Luxembourg every day; 'He is on duty,' said Courfeyrac. It was certain the girl looked at him. Grown bolder, he approached the bench but no longer passed in front of it, so as not to attract the father's attention, and posted himself behind trees and the pedestals of Leonidas or Spartacus with a book, over which his eyes sought her, while she, chatting naturally with the white-haired man, turned her profile toward him with a vague smile and bent on him the reveries of a passionate eye: her mouth answered one, her glance another.","M. Leblanc evidently noticed something. Often when Marius arrived he rose and walked about; he abandoned their usual place for the bench by the Gladiator at the other end, as if to see whether Marius would follow. Marius did not understand and followed, an error. The father grew irregular and no longer brought his daughter every day; sometimes he came alone, and then Marius did not stay, another blunder. He had passed from timidity to blindness and paid no heed.","One evening at dusk he found on the bench they had just left a plain white handkerchief, fine and seemingly perfumed, marked with the letters U. F. He knew nothing of her, neither family name, first name nor address; these two letters were the first thing of hers he possessed, and he built on them at once. U was the Christian name: 'Ursule! what a delicious name!' He kissed the handkerchief, wore it on his heart by day and slept with it at his lips by night, declaring her whole soul lay in it. The handkerchief belonged to the old gentleman, who had let it fall from his pocket. In the following days Marius showed himself at the Luxembourg only kissing it and pressing it to his heart; the beautiful child, understanding nothing, signified as much by imperceptible signs, and Marius said, 'O modesty!'"],["Once, Hugo admits, 'his Ursule' caused Marius serious grief. One May day she had persuaded M. Leblanc to leave the bench and stroll; father and daughter passed Marius, who stood watching them from behind. A merry gust from the nursery lifted her dress almost to the garter and a leg of exquisite shape appeared. Marius saw it, and was exasperated and furious. She hurriedly pushed her dress down with a divinely troubled gesture, but he remained angry: the alley was empty, but someone might have been there, and what she had done was horrible. The poor child had done nothing; the only culprit was the wind, but Marius, in whom the jealous Bartholo stirred within the youthful Cherubin, was determined to be vexed. The sight itself gave him no pleasure; a stranger's white stocking would have pleased him more.","When she came back past his bench he darted a sullen, ferocious glance at her; she gave the slight backward straightening and lift of the eyelids that means 'Well, what is the matter?' It was their first quarrel. At that moment a bent, wrinkled old soldier crossed the walk in a Louis XV uniform with the cross of Saint-Louis, one empty sleeve, a silver chin and a wooden leg, looking extremely well satisfied, and seemed to give Marius a fraternal, merry wink as if they had shared some good luck. Marius reached a paroxysm of jealousy: perhaps the veteran had been there, perhaps he had seen; he wanted to exterminate him.","Time blunts all points. Marius's wrath against 'Ursule', just as he thought it, passed off; he pardoned her, with great effort, after sulking for three days. And in spite of all this, and because of it, his passion grew to madness."],["Knowing her name was Ursule was a great deal and very little; in three or four weeks Marius had used up that bliss and wanted to know where she lived. After the blunders of the Gladiator bench and of leaving when M. Leblanc came alone, he committed a third and immense one: he followed 'Ursule'. She lived in the Rue de l'Ouest, in its most unfrequented part, in a new, modest three-storey house, and from then on he added to the Luxembourg the happiness of following her home.","Wanting to know who she was, he one evening went in after them and asked the porter whether the gentleman who had just come in lived on the first floor. The third, said the porter; a gentleman of property, a very kind man who did good to the unfortunate though not rich himself. Asked his name, the porter raised his head: 'Are you a police spy, sir?' Marius went off abashed but delighted, having learned that Ursule was the daughter of a man of independent means living on the third floor in the Rue de l'Ouest.","Next day the couple left the Luxembourg early; Marius followed as usual, and at the carriage gate M. Leblanc let his daughter pass in, paused, and stared intently at Marius. The day after they did not come. At nightfall Marius went to the Rue de l'Ouest, saw a light on the third floor and walked beneath it until it went out. For a week he waited all day at the Luxembourg in vain and stood sentinel under the windows at night, feeding on fever; sometimes a shadow crossed the red light and his heart beat. On the eighth night there was no light; he waited until one in the morning and no one came or lit a lamp. The next evening the shades were drawn and the third floor dark. He knocked and asked the porter for the gentleman on the third floor. 'Has moved away.' Yesterday. Where to? The porter knew nothing and no address had been left. Then he looked up, recognised Marius, and said: 'So it's you! But you are decidedly a spy then?'"],["Every society has what the theatre calls a third lower floor: a subsoil mined in every direction, sometimes for good and sometimes for evil. The Encyclopaedia was a mine almost open to the sky; the catacombs where the first mass was said were the vaults of the world. Beneath the social structure run religious, philosophical, economic and revolutionary galleries where utopias travel, meet, fraternise and fight, and what emerges from them is the future.","The deeper one goes the more mysterious the toilers. Hugo sets them on a descending ladder, Huss, Luther, Descartes, Voltaire, Condorcet, Robespierre, Marat, Babeuf, with Saint-Simon, Owen and Fourier in side galleries, and below them dim men who perhaps do not yet exist. Different as they are, all these pioneers share one mark, disinterestedness, the starry eye that seeks the absolute; even Marat forgets himself like Jesus. The other sign is the shadowy eye, with which evil begins: fear the man who has no glance at all.","Far below all these mines, lower than Marat and Babeuf and unconnected with them, lies the last mine, the troisième dessous: the grave of shadows, the cellar of the blind, which communicates with the abyss."],["In this gulf the 'I' howls and gnaws; the spectres who roam there, almost beasts, know neither the idea nor the word of progress and think only of satisfying their desires. Their two stepmothers are ignorance and misery, their guide necessity, their satisfaction appetite. Ferocious as tigers rather than tyrants, they pass from suffering to crime; hunger is the point of departure and Satan the point reached. From that vault Lacenaire emerges.","Book IV showed a compartment of the upper mine, the political and philosophical excavation, where even error is heroic and the work is called Progress. Now Hugo turns to hideous depths: the great cavern of evil beneath society, which will exist until ignorance is dissipated. It is hatred without exception, knows no philosophers, has never turned a page, and desires the destruction of everything, including the upper mines it execrates; its name is theft, prostitution, murder, and its vault is ignorance.","Everything above it aims to suppress it: destroy the cavern Ignorance and you destroy the lair Crime. The only social peril is darkness. All men are of the same clay, but ignorance mixed into that clay blackens it and turns to evil within."],["Gueulemer, a Hercules of no fixed position, lairs in the sewer of the Arche-Marion: six feet tall, with marble pectorals and a bird's head, under forty but already lined, a great idle force whose muscles want work and whose stupidity refuses it. He is an assassin through coolness, thought to be a creole, possibly mixed up in the death of Marshal Brune while a porter at Avignon in 1815.","Babet is Gueulemer's contrast: thin, learned, transparent but impenetrable, a talker who underlines his smiles. He has acted in vaudeville, sold plaster busts of the head of State, pulled teeth in a fairground booth under a grandiloquent poster, had and lost a wife and children as one loses a handkerchief, and, rare in his world, reads the newspapers; he once regretted that his wife had not given him a child with a calf's muzzle, which would have made a fortune. Later he gave up everything to 'undertake Paris'.","Claquesous is night. He appears only after dark and returns to a hole no one knows before dawn, speaks to his accomplices with his back turned in total darkness, puts on a mask if a candle is brought, and is a ventriloquist; Babet calls him a nocturne for two voices. No one is sure he has a name, a voice or a face. Montparnasse is a child under twenty with a handsome face, cherry lips and spring in his eyes, who has every vice and aspires to every crime: a street boy turned pickpocket turned garrotter, genteel, effeminate, ferocious, with a curled hat-brim, a threadbare coat of the best cut, a flower in his buttonhole and a bludgeon in his pocket. The first grisette who called him handsome made a Cain of this Abel; wanting elegance, hence idleness, hence crime, he had numerous corpses behind him at eighteen. A dandy of the sepulchre."],["The four were a Proteus, slipping among the police under different names, lending each other aliases and hiding-places, now shrinking to a single man, now multiplying into a throng: not four men but one mysterious robber with four heads, the monstrous polyp of evil in the crypt of society. Through their network they held the general enterprise of ambushes in the Seine department; men of nocturnal imagination brought them ideas, and they supplied the scenery and the troupe, underletting their accomplices to any crime that needed muscle and paid well. They met at nightfall, the hour they woke, on the plains beside the Salpêtrière and planned their twelve black hours.","The underworld called the association Patron-Minette, an old popular term for daybreak, probably from the hour at which their work ended and phantoms vanish. When the presiding judge asked Lacenaire who had committed a crime he denied, he answered, 'Perhaps it was Patron-Minette.' Hugo gives the troupe's roll of names: Panchaud alias Printanier alias Bigrenaille, Brujon (of a whole dynasty of Brujons), Boulatruelle the road-mender already met, Laveuve, Finistère, Homère-Hogu, Mardisoir, Dépêche, Fauntleroy alias the Flower Girl, Glorieux a discharged convict, Barrecarrosse called Monsieur Dupont, L'Esplanade-du-Sud, Poussagrive, Carmagnolet, Kruideniers called Bizarro, Mangedentelle, Les-pieds-en-l'Air, Demi-Liard called Deux-Milliards, and others; each name is a species of fungus from the underside of civilisation.","These men were not seen by day; worn out by their nights they slept in limekilns, abandoned quarries at Montmartre or Montrouge, or sewers. They still exist, Hugo says, and always have; individuals are extirpated but the tribe subsists, reborn from the social ooze under new names. They scent purses and watches, patiently stalk bourgeois with a 'stealable' air, and, met at midnight on a deserted boulevard, seem not men but forms of living mist detached from the night. What makes such spectres vanish? Light, in floods; no bat resists the dawn. Light up society from below."],["Summer, autumn and winter pass, and neither M. Leblanc nor the girl returns to the Luxembourg. Marius has one thought left: to see her face again. He looks everywhere and finds nothing. The hopeful, planning young man becomes, in the narrator's phrase, a lost dog. Work disgusts him; he reproaches himself endlessly for having followed her and spoiled everything. Courfeyrac, who is told nothing and guesses a good deal, first congratulates him on being in love and then, seeing the gloom, tells him he has been an animal and drags him off to the Chaumière.","Once, trusting a fine September sun, Marius lets Courfeyrac, Bossuet and Grantaire take him to the ball at Sceaux in the wild hope of finding her there. He does not, and walks home alone through the night past the carts of singing revellers, while Grantaire grumbles that this is where all lost women turn up. Marius grows more and more solitary, going and coming in his pain like a wolf in a trap.","On another day, in the narrow streets near the Boulevard des Invalides, he passes a man dressed as a workingman, in a long-visored cap that shows locks of very white hair. The man walks slowly, as if absorbed in painful thought, and Marius believes he recognises M. Leblanc: the same hair, the same profile, the same bearing, only sadder. Why the workman's clothes? By the time Marius decides to follow, the man has turned into some side street and is gone. The encounter occupies him for three days and then fades; he tells himself it was probably only a resemblance."],["Marius still lives in the Gorbeau house and pays no attention to anyone in it. By now the only other inhabitants are the Jondrettes, the family whose rent he once paid without ever speaking to them; the rest have moved away, died or been turned out. It is the 2nd of February, Candlemas, with a treacherous bit of afternoon sun that the almanac-makers say heralds six weeks of cold. Marius leaves his room at nightfall to go and dine, passing Ma'am Bougon sweeping and lamenting that nothing in the world is cheap except trouble.","Walking up the boulevard towards the Rue Saint-Jacques with his head down, he is elbowed in the dusk by two girls in rags, one tall and slim, the other a little shorter, running breathless and terrified. The taller mutters in slang that the police nearly caught her at the half-circle; the other answers that she bolted. They vanish among the trees. Marius then notices a little greyish packet at his feet, an envelope that seems to hold papers. He assumes the girls dropped it, calls after them without result, and puts it in his pocket.","On the way to dinner he passes a child's coffin on three chairs in an alley of the Rue Mouffetard, and thinks of the two girls: sadder than seeing one's children die is seeing them lead an evil life. Then the shadows pass and he returns to his habitual thoughts of his six months of happiness under the trees of the Luxembourg. Young girls still appear to him, he reflects, but where they used to be angels, now they are ghouls."],["Undressing for bed, Marius finds the forgotten packet in his coat and opens it, hoping it will tell him where to return it. Inside are four unsealed letters, all reeking of tobacco. One, to a Marquise de Grucheray, is from \\"Don Alvarès\\", a Spanish royalist cavalry captain stranded in France. One, to a Comtesse de Montvernet, is from \\"Mistress Balizard\\", an abandoned mother of six. One, to a stocking merchant named Pabourgeot, is from \\"Genflot, man of letters\\", who has sent a drama to the Théâtre-Français and will accept as little as forty sous. The fourth, to \\"the benevolent gentleman of the church of Saint-Jacques-du-Haut-Pas\\", is from \\"P. Fabantou, dramatic artist\\", who promises to show his certificates if the gentleman will follow his daughter home.","None gives an address, and though they claim four writers, all four are in the same handwriting, on the same coarse yellow paper, with the same spelling mistakes reproduced with great tranquillity. Marius concludes they come from one person, finds the petty mystery not worth solving, and tosses the envelope into a corner. Nothing proves it even belonged to the two girls.","About seven the next morning, as he settles to work, there is a gentle knock. Marius, who never locks his door since he owns nothing worth stealing (Ma'am Bougon once triumphed when a pair of old boots went missing), calls \\"Come in\\" without looking up, assuming it is she. A hoarse, broken voice, roughened with drink and sounding like an old man's, says \\"Excuse me, sir\\". He turns and sees a young girl."],["In the half-open door stands a girl of about sixteen in nothing but a chemise and petticoat, barefoot, emaciated, with missing teeth and bold dull eyes: a face that must once have been pretty and now shows fifty years mixed with fifteen. She knows his name, calls him Monsieur Marius, and hands him a letter whose wafer is still moist. It is from his neighbour Jondrette: the family has had no bread for two days, four persons and a sick wife, and he hopes the young man who paid his rent six months ago will help again. The writing, spelling, paper and tobacco smell are those of the four letters. Don Alvarès, Mistress Balizard, Genflot and Fabantou are all Jondrette, if Jondrette is really his name.","Marius now understands his neighbour's trade: writing under false names to people he thinks rich and charitable, and sending his daughters out to deliver the letters at their own peril. The two girls he jostled on the boulevard were these two; he had never really looked at them. While he reflects, the girl wanders the garret with the boldness of a ghost, rummaging, handling his clothes, delighted to find a mirror. She seizes an open book and reads a line about the attack on Hougomont, declares that her father was at Waterloo and that they are good Bonapartists, then takes a pen and writes \\"The bobbies are here\\" to prove she can spell. \\"We have not always been as we are now,\\" she begins, and breaks off with a laugh. She hums a street song about hunger and cold, chatters about the theatre and a little brother who gets her tickets, and tells Marius he is a handsome fellow and that she often sees him going to Father Mabeuf's.","Marius retreats and hands her the envelope of four letters. She is overjoyed: she and her sister had searched everywhere, and rather than be beaten had told their father the letters were delivered and refused. She picks out the one for \\"the old fellow who goes to mass\\" at Saint-Jacques and says she will carry it to him now; it is his hour. In a wandering monologue she describes sleeping under bridges last winter, thinking of drowning herself but finding the water too cold, and the hallucinations that come when one has not eaten.","Marius searches his pockets and finds five francs sixteen sous, all he owns. He keeps the sixteen sous and gives her the five francs. She erupts in slang about wine and meat and a royal feast, bows, snatches a mouldy crust from his commode and bites into it on her way out."],["Marius has known five years of poverty, but he realises he has never seen true misery until this girl passed before him. The narrator reflects that a man at his last extremity drags the weak around him, women and children, down with him into shame, and that despair is walled in by fragile partitions that open on vice or crime. The girl was a messenger from that realm of shadows.","Marius blames himself. Only a wall separated him from these abandoned beings; he heard them live and groan beside him every day and never listened, his mind given up to dreams and loves in the air. A less dreamy, more attentive neighbour would have noticed their distress and had them rescued. However corrupt and vile they seem, few fall without being degraded, and there is a point where the unfortunate and the infamous meet in one word, \\"the miserable\\"; charity should be deeper the deeper the fall.","Staring at the wall as if his pity could warm the people behind it, he studies its bare laths and plaster and suddenly notices, near the ceiling, a triangular gap between three laths where the plaster is missing. From the top of his commode one can see into the Jondrettes' attic. Commiseration, the narrator says, has its curiosity: one may look at misfortune like a traitor in order to help it. Marius climbs up and puts his eye to the hole."],["Cities, like forests, have caverns where their most wicked creatures hide, but the city's are mean and ugly where the forest's are grand. What Marius sees is a hovel: a straw chair, a rickety table, broken crockery, two indescribable pallets, and a four-paned dormer draped in cobwebs. The walls are leprous and damp, scrawled with obscene charcoal drawings; the floor is bare blackened plaster. The one fireplace, which lets the room for forty francs a year, holds a brazier, a pot, boards, rags, a birdcage, ashes and two smouldering brands. The garret is large, full of black nooks. On the wall hangs a coloured print called The Dream, with Napoleon on a column lettered with misspelt battle names; beneath it a wooden panel leans with its face to the wall.","At the table sits a man of about sixty, small, thin, livid, with a cunning, cruel, uneasy look, a long grey beard, and a woman's chemise over muddy trousers and burst boots. He smokes a pipe (there is no bread, but there is tobacco) and writes, probably more letters like the four. A tattered novel of 1814 lies on the table. He talks aloud as he writes, raging that even in death there is no equality, since the rich lie up on the paved alley of Père-Lachaise and the poor are put in the mud below to rot faster. He strikes the table: he could eat the whole world.","A big woman of forty or a hundred squats on bare heels by the fire, in chemise and patched petticoat, with reddish-blond hair going grey and huge shining hands; a giant beside her husband, with another volume of the same romance open on the floor. On one pallet sits a tall, pale, half-naked girl with dangling feet who seems not to listen, see or live: the younger sister, who looks eleven or twelve and is really fourteen, the one who said \\"I bolted\\" on the boulevard. There is no sign of work anywhere, only some dubious ironmongery in a corner.","The man goes on grumbling that everybody is canaille. The woman, with the flattened affection of long misery, tells her \\"little friend\\" to calm himself; he is too good to write to all those people. He keeps writing."],["As Marius is about to climb down, the door of the garret flies open and the elder girl appears in men's shoes caked with mud and a tattered mantle, breathless and triumphant: \\"He is coming!\\" The philanthropist from the church of Saint-Jacques read the letter, asked her address, said his daughter had purchases to make and that he would take a carriage and arrive as soon as she did. He seemed surprised and hesitated when she named the house, then said never mind. She saw the father and daughter get into a fiacre after mass, and has just seen it turn into the Rue Petit-Banquier; she even noted its number, 440. \\"He is Rothschild,\\" says her father, and praises her as a clever girl. She retorts that she will not wear these squelching shoes again; he answers that the poor must have shoes to enter churches, one cannot go barefoot to God.","Then he springs into action like a general before battle. He tells his wife to put out the fire, and when she does not move, douses the brands himself with a jug of water. He kicks his leg through the straw seat of the only chair. Learning it is snowing, he orders the younger girl off the bed to break a pane; the bewildered child obeys with her fist and the glass clatters down. He orders his wife into bed, and she obeys. A sob comes from the corner: the little girl has cut her wrist. The mother protests; the father says so much the better, he foresaw it, and tears a strip from the chemise he is wearing to bind the wound, pleased that the torn chemise too looks well.","Icy wind and mist pour through the broken pane; the snow promised by the Candlemas sun has come. Jondrette spreads ashes over the wet brands to hide them, surveys the room, and leans against the chimney-piece: now they can receive the philanthropist."],["The elder girl puts her cold hand in her father's; he says he is colder still, and when his wife remarks that he always has something better than everyone else, even bad things, he silences her with a look. The mother kisses and soothes the sobbing younger child; the father tells her to keep sobbing, that's right. He frets that the man may not come after all, and that he will have put out his fire, wrecked his chair, torn his shirt and broken his pane for nothing; \\"and wounded the child,\\" the mother murmurs.","He works himself into a fury against the rich who go to mass, give to priests, and come to humiliate the poor with old duds not worth four sous and bread, when what he wants is money, which they refuse because they say the poor would drink it. They are thieves themselves, he says; society ought to be grasped by the four corners and tossed into the air so that no one has anything.","A light tap at the door. Jondrette rushes to open it, bowing and smiling, and bids the respected benefactor and his charming young lady enter. A man of ripe age and a young girl step onto the threshold. Marius, at his post, is overwhelmed: it is She, the face he has sought for six months, a little pale, in a violet velvet bonnet and black satin pelisse, reappearing in this horror. She is still accompanied by M. Leblanc. She sets a bulky parcel on the table. Behind the door the elder Jondrette girl stares with sombre eyes at the velvet bonnet, the silk mantle and the happy face."],["Coming from daylight, the visitors can barely see in the dark den while its inhabitants see them clearly. M. Leblanc, with his sad kindly look, presents the package of new clothes, woollen stockings and blankets. Jondrette bows to the ground, then whispers to his daughter, \\"Duds, no money, they are all alike,\\" and asks how the letter was signed. \\"Fabantou,\\" she says, just as Leblanc gropes for the name. Jondrette supplies it and launches into his act: a pupil of Talma, once favoured by fortune, now without bread or fire, his chair seatless, his pane broken, his wife ill in bed, his child wounded. He pinches the little girl's sore hand to make her shriek, and the young lady hurries to comfort her. The wrist, he says, was hurt at a machine earning six sous a day and the arm may have to come off.","All the while he studies the benefactor strangely, as if trying to remember something. Passing his wife's bed, he whispers, \\"Take a look at that man!\\" Then he resumes his lamentations: his only garment is his wife's chemise; if he had a coat he would go to see Mademoiselle Mars, who played with him in the provinces; his wife has suffocating fits; he has brought up his daughters religiously and kept them from the stage. And tomorrow, the fourth of February, is his landlord's last day of grace: if the rent is not paid tonight, all four will be turned into the snow. He owes four quarters, sixty francs. The narrator notes the lie: four quarters would be forty, and he cannot owe four since Marius paid two less than six months ago.","M. Leblanc tosses five francs on the table (Jondrette mutters that it will not even pay for his chair and pane), takes off his big brown greatcoat and lays it over the chair, and says these five francs are all he has with him; he will take his daughter home and return this evening, when the rent is due. Jondrette's face lights up strangely. Six o'clock, says Leblanc, with the sixty francs. \\"Take a good look at him, wife!\\" Jondrette whispers again. As the visitors turn to go, the elder girl points out the forgotten coat and gets an annihilating look from her father; Leblanc smiles that he is leaving it, and tells Jondrette to put it on if he comes out, since it is very cold. Jondrette dons it and sees them down to their carriage."],["Marius has seen the whole scene and yet seen nothing but her. He watched her unpack the clothes and question the mother and the hurt child kindly, straining to hear her voice, which he has never clearly heard, but Jondrette's trumpeting drowned everything. A hummingbird among toads, he thinks. When she leaves he has one idea: to follow and learn where she lives. He hesitates, fearing that if M. Leblanc catches sight of him on the stairs he will take alarm and vanish for good, then risks it. The corridor and stairs are empty, and from the boulevard he sees the fiacre turning into the Rue du Petit-Banquier, then far down the Rue Mouffetard.","An empty cab passes. Marius hails it: \\"By the hour?\\" The driver eyes the young man with no cravat, a buttonless working-coat and a torn shirt, and rubs his thumb and finger: pay in advance, forty sous. Marius has sixteen. \\"I will pay on my return.\\" The driver whistles and whips up his horse. For want of twenty-four sous Marius has lost his joy; he reflects bitterly on the five francs he gave the wretched girl that morning. He does not seem to register that Leblanc has promised to come back at six.","About to climb the stairs, he notices across the boulevard, by the deserted wall of the Rue de la Barrière-des-Gobelins, Jondrette in the philanthropist's greatcoat deep in talk with one of the \\"prowlers of the barriers\\", men of suspicious face who sleep by day. Marius barely attends, yet thinks the man resembles a certain Panchaud, alias Printanier, alias Bigrenaille, whom Courfeyrac once pointed out as a very dangerous night roamer. The narrator adds that this Panchaud, already watched by the police in 1832, went on to figure in many criminal trials and carved his name in a sewer of La Force during an escape attempt."],["Climbing the stairs, Marius finds the elder Jondrette girl following him. He finds the sight of her odious: she has his five francs, and it is useless to ask her where the visitors live since the letter was addressed only to \\"the benevolent gentleman of Saint-Jacques-du-Haut-Pas\\". He pushes his door, but her hand holds it open. She has lost her morning's assurance and does not look at him. \\"Monsieur Marius, you look sad. What is the matter with you?\\" He denies it and tells her to leave him alone; she holds on. He was kind this morning, she says; let him be kind again and tell her what ails him. She can be useful: she carries letters, goes from door to door, finds addresses, follows people, for her father.","An idea strikes Marius; what branch does a falling man disdain? He draws near and says \\"Listen\\", and she begs him to call her \\"thou\\". She brought the old gentleman and his daughter here; does she know their address? No. \\"Find it for me.\\" Her joyful eyes go dark. Does he know them? No. \\"That is to say, you do not know her, but you wish to know her.\\" She promises him \\"the beautiful lady's\\" address, a phrase that troubles him so that he adds, never mind, the address of father and daughter. \\"What will you give me?\\" \\"Anything you like.\\" She drops her head and pulls the door shut behind her.","Marius sinks into a chair, dizzy with the day: the angel's appearance and disappearance, this creature's offer, a gleam of hope in a vast despair. Then Jondrette's shrill voice through the wall rouses him: \\"I tell you that I am sure of it, and that I recognized him.\\" Whom? M. Leblanc? Does Jondrette know him? Is Marius about to learn at last who it is that he loves and who her father is? He bounds onto the commode and puts his eye to the hole."],["The wife and daughters have put on the new stockings and jackets and the blankets lie on the beds. Jondrette has just come in from outside and paces with extraordinary eyes. \\"Sure! Eight years have passed! But I recognize him!\\" Only older, better dressed, the same voice. He sends the girls out, keeping the elder back to order both of them to be there at five o'clock precisely; he will need them. Alone with his wife, he says: \\"The young lady—\\" and whispers in her ear, then concludes aloud, \\"It is she!\\" The mother's fury is monstrous: it cannot be, when her own daughters go barefoot; a satin pelisse, a velvet bonnet, two hundred francs of clothes; the other one was hideous and this one is not bad-looking. \\"That horrible, beautiful young lady, who gazed at my daughters with an air of pity—she is that beggar brat!\\" She would like to kick her stomach in. Marius finds her more frightening than her husband.","Then Jondrette announces, \\"My fortune is made.\\" He has had enough of misery; he wants to eat and drink and sleep and be a bit of a millionaire, like other people. Lowering his voice (but not enough, and the snow deadens the traffic outside), he explains: the Croesus is as good as caught. He has seen some people; everything is arranged. The man will come at six to bring sixty francs, fooled by the tale of the landlord and the fourth of February; six is when the neighbour dines, Mother Bougon is out washing dishes in town, and the neighbour never returns before eleven. The children will stand watch, the wife will help. \\"He will give in.\\" And if not? A sinister gesture: \\"We'll fix him,\\" and a cold, sweet laugh that makes Marius shudder.","He puts on an old cap to go and see \\"some more people, good ones\\", congratulates himself that the man did not recognise him in return, thanks to his romantic beard, and that the greatcoat lets him go out at all. He comes back to add that a brazier of charcoal, two good bushels, must be ready, and throws his wife the five-franc piece, warning her not to spend it all: he needs about three francs for something at the ironmonger's in the Rue Mouffetard. Eating is not the point today. As his step dies away on the stairs, one o'clock strikes from Saint-Médard."],["Marius, kindly as a brahmin and severe as a judge, pities a toad but crushes a viper, and he has just looked into a nest of vipers. None of his enigmas is solved; he knows only that Jondrette is acquainted with M. Leblanc and the girl, that an ambush is being prepared, and that both are in danger, the father certainly, the daughter probably. They must be saved. He feels a secret joy at the chance of serving the one he loves. But how to warn them? He has no address. Waiting at the door at six would only get him seized by Jondrette's men and doom the victim. It is one o'clock; he has five hours. He puts on his decent coat and a silk handkerchief and creeps out while the Jondrette woman rummages in her heap of old iron.","Halfway along the Rue du Petit-Banquier, by a low wall bordering waste ground, he hears voices in the empty street. Looking over the wall he sees two strangers sitting in the snow with their backs to it: a bearded man in a blouse and fez, and a long-haired, bareheaded man in rags with snow in his hair. The long-haired one says that with Patron-Minette's help it cannot fail; it is worth five hundred francs a head and at worst five, six, ten years; \\"Father What's-his-name's\\" team will already be harnessed. The bearded one shivers and agrees you cannot go against such things. Then they discuss a melodrama they saw at the Gaîté. Marius walks on, sure this must be \\"the affair\\".","He asks at a shop in the Faubourg Saint-Marceau for the commissary of police and is directed to No. 14, Rue de Pontoise. On the way he buys a two-sou roll, knowing he will not dine, and gives Providence its due: had he not given his five francs to the Jondrette girl, he would have followed the fiacre, known nothing, and M. Leblanc and his daughter would have walked into the trap."],["The commissary is absent; an inspector takes his place. Behind a grating stands a tall man in a vast three-collared topcoat, with a square face, thin mouth, grey whiskers and a look that turns your pockets inside out, no less terrible to meet than Jondrette: the dog, the narrator says, can be as alarming as the wolf. He asks \\"What do you want?\\" without a \\"monsieur\\". Marius, giving his name and calling himself a lawyer, tells everything: a person he knows only by sight is to be drawn into a trap that evening; he heard the plot through the partition of the neighbouring room; the planner is one Jondrette; accomplices will include prowlers of the barriers such as Panchaud, alias Bigrenaille; the daughters will keep watch; the victim's name is unknown; the hour is six, at No. 50–52 on the Boulevard de l'Hôpital.","At the number the inspector looks up: the room at the end of the corridor? Apparently he knows the house, the former property of Gorbeau. He mutters that Patron-Minette must have a hand in it, and when Marius repeats the talk behind the wall, identifies the long-haired man as Brujon and the bearded one as Demi-Liard, alias Deux-Milliards, and thinks he recognises \\"Father What's-his-name\\" too. He asks whether Marius saw a little dandy, a big lump like an elephant, a scamp like an old red-tail; as for the fourth, no one sees him. He will not say who they are. Hiding police inside the house would only make the \\"artists\\" call off the performance; he wants to hear them sing and make them dance.","\\"Are you afraid?\\" \\"No more than yourself!\\" Marius retorts, stung at still not being called monsieur. The inspector approves: courage does not fear crime, honesty does not fear authority. He asks for Marius's passkey to the house and pockets it, and when Marius advises him to come in force, pulls two small steel pistols from his pockets, \\"knock-me-downs\\", each with two balls. Marius is to go home, hide in his room as if out, watch through the hole, let the men proceed, and when the crime is about to be committed fire a shot into the ceiling; above all, not too soon. The rest concerns him. He makes Marius move the pistols to his trousers pockets, checks the time (half past two) and, as Marius reaches the door, adds that if he needs anything before then he should ask for Inspector Javert."],["About three o'clock Courfeyrac and Bossuet, walking in the thickening snow (\\"a plague of white butterflies in heaven,\\" says Bossuet), catch sight of Marius coming up the street with a peculiar air. Courfeyrac says not to speak to him: he is busy, following someone, in love. But there is no woman in the street; he is following a man in a grey cap and grey beard, wearing a brand-new greatcoat too large for him over frightful ragged trousers. \\"He's a poet,\\" Courfeyrac decides, and refuses to follow a man who is following another man.","Marius has indeed seen Jondrette in the Rue Mouffetard and is spying on him. Jondrette enters one of the worst hovels of the Rue Gracieuse for a quarter of an hour, returns, stops at an ironmonger's at the corner of the Rue Pierre-Lombard and comes out with a huge white-handled cold chisel, which he hides under his coat, then hurries to the Rue du Petit-Banquier. Marius wisely waits at the corner: Jondrette looks round to make sure he is not followed, then springs over the low wall where Marius heard the two men talking. The waste ground beyond adjoins the back yard of a failed livery-stable keeper of bad repute who still keeps a few old carriages in his sheds.","Night is falling and a red moon rises behind the Salpêtrière. Marius has given his key to the inspector, and Ma'am Bougon locks the house at dusk when she goes to wash dishes in town, so he hurries back. The door is still open. Creeping along the corridor, where all the empty attics stand open, he thinks he sees in one of them the motionless heads of four men in the last of the daylight. He does not try to look, reaches his room unseen, and a moment later hears Ma'am Bougon leave and lock the house behind her."],["At half past five Marius sits on his bed hearing his own pulse, thinking of crime advancing on one side and justice on the other. The snow has stopped and moonlight gives the room a twilight glow; the hole in the wall shines with a reddish light that seems bloody, though the Jondrette den is utterly silent. He takes off his boots. Then the street door turns, a heavy step mounts, and Jondrette comes in to the yelps of his family. All is first-rate, he says, though his feet are frozen; he approves of their having dressed up, since they must inspire confidence. He lays something heavy on the table, probably the chisel. The mother has cooked three potatoes on the fire; tomorrow, he promises, they will dine like Charles the Tenth. \\"The mousetrap is open. The cats are there.\\" He tells her to put something in the fire; Marius hears charcoal being handled. The hinges have been greased.","It is nearly six. Jondrette asks whether old Bougon has left and whether anyone is in the neighbour's room; the mother says he has been out all day and this is his dinner hour, but Jondrette sends the elder girl with a candle to make sure. Marius crawls under his bed. The girl reports from the door that the key is in the lock so he must be out; her father makes her go in anyway. She walks straight to the mirror nailed by the bed, smooths her hair, smiles at herself, and sings a sentimental song about a love that lasted a week, while iron is moved about next door. She looks out of the window (\\"How ugly Paris is when it has put on a white chemise!\\") and preens again, answering her father's shout that she is looking under the bed and furniture and there is no one. She sings another verse and leaves.","Marius hears the girls' bare feet in the corridor and Jondrette's orders: one at the barrier, one at the corner of the Rue du Petit-Banquier; watch the door and run up at the first sign of anything. The elder grumbles at standing watch barefoot in the snow; tomorrow, says her father, she shall have dainty green silk boots. The outer door bangs. Now only Marius, the Jondrettes and the mysterious men in the empty attic remain in the house."],["The den is lit less by its candle than by the red glow of a large sheet-iron brazier in the fireplace, full of burning charcoal, with the chisel from the Rue Pierre-Lombard thrust in to heat; the heat makes the candle droop. By the door lie two heaps, one of old iron and one of rope. A copper dark-lantern stands on the chimney-piece. The brazier's fumes go up the chimney and the broken pane airs the room, so nothing betrays it. The narrator recalls that this is the most retired room in the most isolated house on the most deserted boulevard in Paris; its only window gives on walled waste ground. If ambushes had not existed, they would have been invented here.","Jondrette smokes on the seatless chair while his wife, absurd in a plumed black bonnet, an immense tartan shawl and the men's shoes her daughter scorned, talks low. Jondrette still wears M. Leblanc's too-large coat. He instructs her: the man will come in a carriage; she is to wait behind the street door with the lantern, open at once, light him up the stairs and corridor, then go down, pay the coachman and send the fiacre away. For the fare he hands her the five francs the neighbour gave them that morning. Then he says two chairs will be needed, \\"to sit on\\", a mild answer that chills Marius, and she goes to fetch the neighbour's. Marius has no time to hide. She enters by moonlight, which leaves the wall he leans on in deep shadow, takes his only two chairs, and leaves without seeing him.","Jondrette sets the chairs on either side of the table, turns the chisel in the brazier, masks the fire with an old screen, and bends over the rope pile, which Marius now sees is a well-made rope ladder with wooden rungs and two hooks. The heavy tools mixed with the old iron were not there in the morning; Marius takes them for an edge-tool maker's, though a more knowing eye would recognise burglars' instruments for forcing locks and cutting. The candle is now the only light. Jondrette, his pipe gone out, pulls open the table drawer, takes out a long kitchen knife, tries its edge on his nail and puts it back. Marius draws and cocks his pistol; at the click Jondrette starts and listens, then laughs that it is only the partition cracking. Marius keeps the pistol in his hand."],["The clock of Saint-Médard strikes six; Jondrette nods at each stroke, snuffs the candle with his fingers, paces, and listens at the corridor, muttering \\"Provided only that he comes!\\" The door opens: his wife stands in the corridor with the dark-lantern lighting her grimace from below. \\"Enter, sir.\\" M. Leblanc appears, with an air of serenity that makes him singularly venerable, and lays four louis on the table for Monsieur Fabantou's rent and most pressing needs; the rest, he says, they will attend to hereafter. Jondrette whispers to his wife to dismiss the carriage; she slips out and returns: it is done. The snow is so deep that the fiacre's coming and going are inaudible.","Leblanc takes one chair, Jondrette the other, facing him. The narrator asks the reader to picture the scene: a cold night, the snowbound solitudes of the Salpêtrière white as shrouds in the moonlight, the red street lanterns and black elms of the deserted boulevard, the Gorbeau hovel at its pitch of silence and darkness, and in it the vast garret lit by a single candle, two men at a table, Leblanc tranquil, Jondrette smiling and alarming, the she-wolf in a corner, and behind the partition Marius, erect and invisible, missing nothing, pistol in hand.","Marius feels horror but no fear. He grips the pistol: he can stop that wretch whenever he pleases. He is sure the police are somewhere in ambush waiting for the agreed signal, and he hopes this encounter will throw light on everything he longs to know."],["M. Leblanc asks after the wounded little girl; her sister, says Jondrette, has taken her to the Bourbe hospital and they will be back shortly. The wife, in her eccentric finery, has placed herself between him and the door in an attitude of menace. When Leblanc remarks that Madame Fabantou seems better, Jondrette says she is dying but has the courage of an ox; flattered, she calls him Monsieur Jondrette, and he hastily explains the slip as an artistic sobriquet, Fabantou alias Jondrette. He rambles on about their happy marriage, their willingness to work, his wish to have his girls taught paper-box making, and the one relic of his prosperity, a picture he is willing to sell.","Marius notices a man who has entered without a sound and sat on the nearest bed behind Jondrette: in a ragged knitted vest, wooden shoes, no shirt, bare tattooed arms, and a face smeared with black. Leblanc turns at the same moment and cannot hide his surprise. Jondrette pretends to think he is admiring the coat, then calls the man a neighbour not worth attention. A second blackened man glides in and sits beside the first. Jondrette says they belong in the house and turns the panel against the wall round: a coarse daub with a central figure in crude colours, which he calls a painting by a master, as dear to him as his two daughters.","Leblanc's eye returns to the back of the room, where there are now four men, three on the bed and one by the doorpost, all bare-armed, motionless and black-faced; one, old, with white hair, leans against the wall with closed eyes as if asleep, and of the two younger one is bearded and the other long-haired. Jondrette calls them friends and neighbours, chimney-builders black from charcoal, and presses him to buy. Looking him full in the eye, Leblanc says it is some tavern signboard worth about three francs. Jondrette sweetly asks whether he has his pocketbook: he would be satisfied with a thousand crowns. Leblanc springs up with his back to the wall, Jondrette on his left by the window, the woman and the four men on his right by the door.","Jondrette resumes his whine about the paper-box trade, four sous a day for fourteen hours' work, and his thoughts of drowning himself from the steps by the bridge of Austerlitz, his eye all the while on the door, so that Leblanc seems to wonder whether the man has gone mad. Then his dull eyes flash, he draws himself up and takes a step towards Leblanc, crying in a voice of thunder that none of this is the point: \\"Do you know me?\\""],["Three men in blue blouses and black paper masks enter: a thin one with an iron-tipped cudgel, a colossus with a butcher's poleaxe, and a thickset man with an enormous prison key. Jondrette checks with the thin man that all is ready, that the carriage with two good horses is waiting where ordered, and that Montparnasse has stopped to chat with his eldest girl. M. Leblanc, very pale but without fear, has made a rampart of the table and suddenly looks less like a kindly old man than an athlete. Marius feels proud of the unknown man and raises his pistol towards the ceiling. Three of the \\"chimney-builders\\" arm themselves with shears, tongs and a hammer and block the door. Jondrette repeats his question, leans across the candle, and says: his name is not Fabantou, nor Jondrette, but Thénardier, the innkeeper of Montfermeil. Leblanc replies with perfect calm that he knows him no more than before.","Marius does not hear the answer. The name Thénardier is the one written in his father's will, \\"a certain Thénardier saved my life; if my son encounters him, he will do him all the good that lies in his power\\": the name he has carried for four years and sought in vain. His father's saviour is a ruffian about to commit something like murder, and against the father of the girl he loves. If he fires, Leblanc is saved and Thénardier lost; if not, Leblanc is sacrificed. Remorse waits either way; his knees give way. Thénardier, whom the narrator says he will henceforth call by no other name, paces in wild triumph and pours out his grievance: it was Leblanc who came to his inn on Christmas eve 1823 in a yellow greatcoat, carried off \\"that Fantine's child\\", the Lark, for fifteen hundred francs, a girl who belonged to rich people and would have kept him all his life, and who threatened him with a cudgel in the woods; now Thénardier holds the trumps. He laughs at how easily his tale of the landlord and the fourth of February was swallowed. Leblanc answers quietly that he is mistaken for someone else, a very poor man, not a millionaire. When Thénardier presses, Leblanc says politely: \\"I see that you are a villain!\\" Thénardier rages that the rich warm their feet and eat asparagus in January while the poor are the thermometers of the cold; he has been a licensed tradesman and an elector, an old soldier who at Waterloo saved a general whose name he never caught, only \\"Merci\\"; the picture, he says, painted by David, shows him carrying that general through the grapeshot. He wants money, a great deal of money. Marius, listening, has no doubt left: this is the Thénardier of the will, and the daub is the sign of the Montfermeil tavern, a battle, smoke, one man carrying another. His father seems to rise from the panel and look at him.","The man with the axe pulls off his mask for fun and cracks a joke from the corridor; while Thénardier scolds him with his back turned, Leblanc kicks over the chair and table and is half out of the window before six fists drag him back and the Thénardier woman winds her hands in his hair. The other ruffians rush in; the old drunk on the bed reels up with a stone-breaker's hammer. One blackened face Marius recognises as Panchaud, alias Bigrenaille, who raises a leaded bludgeon over Leblanc's head. Marius thinks \\"My father, forgive me!\\" and his finger finds the trigger, but Thénardier shouts \\"Don't harm him!\\" and the moment passes; the adroit man in him has replaced the ferocious one. Leblanc fells the old man, throws off two assailants and pins them under his knees, but four others hold him and he vanishes under the pile like a boar under hounds. They throw him on the bed by the window and search him: a purse with six francs and a handkerchief, no pocketbook, no watch. Thénardier pockets the handkerchief, has him tied upright to the leg of the bed, orders the drunk, whom he calls Boulatruelle, swept into a corner, and reproaches Babet, the man with the cudgel, for bringing so many; they all wanted to be in it, says Babet, business is slow.","Thénardier's face turns from violence to polished sweetness, a tiger become a lawyer. He points out that the prisoner has not cried out once, even while struggling: a man who does not want the police must have something to hide, as they do, so they can come to an understanding. He unmasks the brazier so the white-hot chisel can be seen, and names his price, two hundred thousand francs, not to be paid now, only to be written for. He has Bigrenaille untie the right arm and dictates a letter: \\"My dear daughter, come immediately, I am in absolute need of thee...\\", striking out \\"come with confidence\\" as likely to arouse distrust. Does he address \\"the little one, the Lark\\" as thou? Leblanc says he does not know what he means. Thénardier carefully avoids naming the girl before his accomplices. Asked his name, the prisoner says Urbain Fabre; the handkerchief is marked U. F. He addresses the letter to Mademoiselle Fabre, at M. Urbain Fabre's, No. 17 Rue Saint-Dominique-d'Enfer. Thénardier sends his wife off in the carriage with the letter and the axe-man riding behind, shouting after her not to lose the two hundred thousand francs she carries. He then explains the plan to \\"Monsieur Fabre\\": his wife, dressed to inspire confidence, will fetch the young lady; outside the barrier a trap with two good horses waits; his comrade will carry her to a quiet place and return her when the money is paid, and if Thénardier is arrested, the comrade will \\"give a turn of his thumb to the Lark\\". Marius, who now sees that the two letters meant Urbain Fabre and that \\"Ursule\\" was never her name, is held fast by the threat as much as by the will.","After half an hour the wife bursts in: false address, nobody at No. 17 but a carriage gate, no Monsieur Fabre; the old fellow has duped him, and she would have chopped the beast in quarters. Marius breathes: the girl is safe. \\"A false address? What did you expect to gain by that?\\" \\"To gain time!\\" cries the prisoner, shaking off his cut bonds; he is held only by one leg. He snatches the glowing chisel from the brazier and holds the gang at bay. The narrator explains that the later investigation found under the bed a hollowed sou piece of the kind made in the galleys, with a watch-spring saw inside, which he must have hidden in his hand when searched and used to cut his ropes. Declaring that his life is not worth defending and that they cannot make him write or speak what he does not choose, he bares his left arm and lays the red-hot iron on his own flesh; the room fills with the smell of burning, the ruffians shudder, and his face shows no hatred, only serene majesty. \\"Have no more fear of me than I have for you!\\" He hurls the chisel out of the window into the snow, says they may do what they please with him, and is seized again. Below the hole Marius hears husband and wife agree in a whisper to cut his throat. Thénardier takes the knife from the drawer. Marius, torn for an hour between his father's testament and the cry to save the prisoner, sees in a shaft of moonlight on his table the sheet on which the elder girl wrote that morning, \\"The bobbies are here\\", wraps it round a bit of plaster and tosses it through the hole into the den. The woman picks it up; Thénardier reads it and recognises Éponine's hand, concludes that since \\"Ponine\\" threw it through the window that side is not watched, and orders the ladder: leave the bacon in the mousetrap and decamp, no time to cut the man's throat. The ventriloquist-voiced man gives a silent signal, the ladder is hooked to the sill, and Thénardier rushes to go first, but Bigrenaille hauls him back by the collar, the ruffians insist on drawing lots, and Thénardier is shouting that they are mad, with names in a hat, when a voice from the threshold offers its hat. It is Javert, holding it out with a smile."],["The narrator backtracks: at nightfall Javert posted his men among the trees across the boulevard and began by pocketing the two girls set to watch the approaches, but he caged only Azelma; Éponine was not at her post and could not be found. Agitated by the comings and goings of fiacres and having recognised many of the ruffians who entered, he grew impatient and went up with Marius's passkey without waiting for the pistol shot, arriving in the nick of time.","The seven men snatch up their weapons; Thénardier grips his knife and the Thénardier woman a huge paving-stone that served her daughters as a stool. Javert, hat on, arms folded, cane under his arm, tells them they will go out by the door, not the window: there are seven of them and fifteen of him, no need to collar one another like men of Auvergne. Bigrenaille slips a pistol to Thénardier, not daring to fire at Javert himself; Thénardier aims at three paces, Javert tells him calmly that it will miss fire, and it does. Bigrenaille throws down his bludgeon and surrenders, asking only not to be denied tobacco in prison; the rest follow. Javert calls in his squad with swords and bludgeons. The Thénardier woman, shielding her husband behind her discarded shawl, raises the stone like a giantess and roars at the police to keep off; Javert advances, remarking that she has a beard like a man and he has claws like a woman, ducks the stone, and lays one hand on her shoulder and the other on her husband's head. Handcuffed, she weeps for her daughters. \\"They are in the jug,\\" says Javert. The drunk wakes asking Jondrette whether it is all over; Javert answers yes.","Telling the masked men to keep their masks on, Javert reviews them like Frederick II at a parade: good day Bigrenaille, Brujon, Deuxmilliards, to the three chimney-builders; good day Gueulemer to the man with the axe, Babet to the man with the cudgel, and \\"your health, Claquesous\\" to the ventriloquist. He orders the gentleman untied and no one let out, sits down at the table to write his report, and after the first formulas asks the gentleman the others bound to step forward. He is gone. The door was guarded but the window was not; while the police were busy he slipped down the rope ladder, which is still swaying. \\"The devil!\\" mutters Javert; he must have been the most valuable of the lot."],["On the evening after the ambush a pale, thin boy in rags and linen trousers comes singing up the boulevard from the direction of the bridge of Austerlitz. At the corner of the Rue du Petit-Banquier he jostles a bent old woman picking over a heap of refuse by a street lamp and mocks her for an enormous dog; she straightens in fury, her wrinkled face lit red by the lantern, and he tells her that madame does not possess the style of beauty which pleases him.","He reaches No. 50–52, finds the door locked, and batters it with heroic kicks that betray the man's shoes he is wearing rather than the child's feet inside them. The old woman hurries after him shrieking that he is knocking the house down, then recognises him: it is that imp. He greets her as Bougonmuche and says he has come to see his ancestors. There is no one here, she says. Where is his father? At La Force. His mother? At Saint-Lazare. His sisters? At the Madelonettes.","The boy scratches his head, says \\"Ah!\\", spins on his heel, and a moment later Ma'am Bougon hears his clear young voice singing his song about King Coupdesabot hunting crows on stilts as he plunges off under the black elms in the winter wind."],["The narrator sets 1831 and 1832 apart as two mountain years of revolutionary grandeur and says he will try to grasp their principal lines. After the Empire, a weary nation wanted only rest; at the same time the accomplished facts of revolution and war demanded guarantees, as England demanded them of the Stuarts and France of the Bourbons. Princes \\"grant\\" such guarantees, but the force of things gives them. The returning Bourbons believed they had bestowed the charter and could take it back, that right divine was theirs and France possessed nothing. They looked glum at every development of the century and did not see that the hand which removed Napoleon had brought them in too. France had done without them for twenty-two years; the nation's roots were everywhere except under the throne.","Believing itself strong, the Restoration one morning contested the sovereignty of the nation and the liberty of the citizen in the ordinances of July, and fell, justly. Yet Hugo credits it: under Louis XVIII and Charles X, after the Revolution's word and Bonaparte's cannon, intelligence had its turn; for fifteen years equality before the law, liberty of conscience, speech and press were seen at work in peace. The Bourbons were an instrument of civilisation that broke in the hands of Providence. Their fall had grandeur on the nation's side rather than theirs: Charles X, sailing from Cherbourg, fussed over a round table being cut square. The people, attacked by a royal insurrection, defended itself, set the old king gently on the ground and halted, carrying away respect but not regret.","The Revolution of July had friends and enemies at once across Europe; the princes, owls of this dawn, shut their eyes and opened them only to threaten, offended that the revolution was formidable and yet gentle, shedding no royal blood. Hugo defines it as the triumph of right overthrowing fact, hence both its brilliance and its mildness. Right is the just and the true and remains eternally pure; fact, however necessary, becomes deformed and monstrous over time, as Machiavelli shows, who is simply the fact of the sixteenth century. To reconcile right and fact is the task of sages."],["As soon as a revolution has made the coast, Hugo says, the skilful hasten to prepare the shipwreck. The skilful of this century call themselves statesmen, a word that has become almost slang; where there is nothing but skill there is pettiness. They treat a revolution like a severed artery needing a prompt ligature: right proclaimed, now power must be strengthened. And after a revolution a people on a monarchical continent must, they say, procure itself a dynasty, which hides the scaffolding and covers the ambulance. A man of genius or of fortune can be made a king, Napoleon or Iturbide; but a dynasty needs the wrinkle of centuries, and must be national, revolutionary at a distance by ideas accepted rather than acts committed, historic and sympathetic. Royal houses are like Indian fig-trees whose branches root and become trees, on condition that they bend down to the people.","The great art is to make success sound like catastrophe, to muffle right, to wrap the giant people in flannel and put it to bed early, to dilute the event with the expedient. 1830 practised this theory, as 1688 had in England: a revolution stopped midway, half progress, quasi-right, and logic knows no \\"almost\\". Who stops revolutions halfway? The bourgeoisie, which Hugo defines not as a class but as the contented portion of the people, the man who now has time to sit down; a chair is not a caste. Its state after 1830 was not inertia but the halt, repose armed and on guard, the partition between 1830 and 1848.","The bourgeoisie and the statesmen needed a man who should express the word Halt, an Although-Because, signifying both revolution and stability. He was already found: Louis Philippe d'Orléans. The 221 made him king and Lafayette undertook the coronation, calling it \\"the best of republics\\"; the town hall of Paris replaced the cathedral of Rheims. This half-throne for a whole throne was \\"the work of 1830\\". When the skilful had finished, the vice of their solution appeared: all had been done outside absolute right, which cried \\"I protest!\\" and retired into the darkness."],["Revolutions, even debased like that of 1830, usually keep enough providential lucidity not to fall amiss, and 1830 had luck: the king amounted to more than royalty. Hugo piles up the traits of Louis Philippe in one immense sentence: son of a father more blamed than he deserved esteem; careful of his health, fortune and affairs; sober, serene, patient; a good man and a good prince; an admirable representative of the middle class and greater than it; a bourgeois from the day he became king; reputed but not proved a miser; a fascinating talker and an undeceived statesman, inwardly cold, governing at the shortest range, incapable of rancour and of gratitude; fond of his country but preferring his family; a general at Valmy and a soldier at Jemappes, attacked eight times by regicides and always smiling; a prodigious memory for deeds, names and dates but blind to tendencies and the hidden currents of souls; something of Charlemagne and something of an attorney. He would rank among history's most illustrious rulers had he loved glory a little, and had he felt what is great as keenly as what is useful.","He had been handsome and remained graceful, pleased the masses if not always the nation, wore no crown and no white hair, kept the old pronunciation and spelling in the service of modern opinions, went out with his umbrella under his arm, dabbled in masonry, gardening and medicine, and bled a fallen postilion. Hugo sorts the grievances into three columns: democratic right confiscated, progress made secondary, the street repressed, the Rue Transnonain and the councils of war belong to royalty; Belgium refused, Algeria too harshly conquered, Blaye, Deutz bought and Pritchard paid belong to the reign; a policy more domestic than national belongs to the king. His great fault was to be modest in the name of France, too much the paternal king incubating a dynasty and afraid of being disturbed, though his family deserved his tenderness.","Louis Philippe was 1830 made man, Prince Equality, carrying in himself the contradiction of Restoration and Revolution, and exile was his great recommendation to the throne: proscribed and poor, he had sold a horse for bread in Switzerland and taught mathematics at Reichenau while his sister Adelaide sewed. He had demolished the iron cage of Mont-Saint-Michel with his own hands, had been Dumouriez's companion and Lafayette's friend, had known Mirabeau and Danton, and at twenty-four had watched the trial of Louis XVI from a box, which left in him a respectful fear of the immense justices of the people. He could recite from memory the whole letter A of the Constituent Assembly's roll. He was a king of broad daylight: under him press, tribune, conscience and speech were free.","History has not yet pronounced on him; Louis Blanc has softened his first verdict. Elected by two \\"almosts\\", the 221 and 1830, he can be judged here only with reservations in the name of absolute democratic right, but taken in himself he will remain one of the best princes who ever sat on a throne. Take away the king and the man is good, at times admirable: after days of wrestling with the diplomacy of Europe he sat up at night revising death sentences, fighting the guillotine foot by foot against his crown attorneys (\\"I won seven last night\\"); in his early years the death penalty was as good as abolished, and after Fieschi's machine he regretted not being wounded so that he might pardon. Hugo closes by saying that a man who is himself a phantom today and knew that king may testify for him before history; an epitaph penned by a dead man is sincere, and one shade may console another."],["Because the story is about to enter one of the tragic clouds at the beginning of Louis Philippe's reign, the narrator wants no equivocation about the king. He came to the throne without violence or personal initiative, born a prince and believing himself elected king, convinced, wrongly but sincerely, that the offer accorded with right and its acceptance with duty. Since both the king and the democracy acted in good faith, the terror of the social conflict weighs on neither: a clash of principles is like a clash of elements, and those in error are sincerely so; a blind man is no more a criminal than a Vendean is a ruffian. These collisions are to be imputed to the fatality of things.","The government of 1830 had to fight the day after it was born. God writes his will in events as an obscure text, and men make hasty translations of it: each translation a party, each misreading a faction. The old legitimist parties attacked the revolution in its royalty (\\"Revolution, why this king?\\"), illogically, since in such revolutions it is the king who revolts and not the people, revolution being the contrary of revolt; the republicans raised the same cry logically, because 1830 had bankrupted the people. Abroad, having become a monarchy, it had to keep the peace with Europe, hence armed peace; pushed on by progress in France, it pushed on the loitering monarchies. Within, pauperism, wages, education, prisons, prostitution, the fate of women, capital and labour multiplied above society like a terrible slope.","Outside the parties, thinkers, whom Hugo gathers under the name socialists, tranquilly mined into the volcano, raising the question of happiness rather than of rights and adding to the rights of man those of woman and child. Their problems reduce to two: to produce wealth and to share it, employment of forces and distribution of enjoyment, public power and individual happiness. England solves only the first and so breeds monstrous opulence beside monstrous wretchedness; communism and agrarian law think they solve the second and kill production, a partition made by the butcher. Both must be solved together: encourage the wealthy, protect the poor, suppress misery, adjust wages to labour, make education free and compulsory, make property universal rather than abolish it; then one is worthy to be called France. These doctrines, the need to reckon with philosophers, a new politics to be invented, the use of Lafayette to defend Polignac, all weighed on Louis Philippe, who felt under his feet a formidable disaggregation that was nevertheless not dust.","Twenty months after July, 1832 opened menacingly: distress and laborers without bread, the last Prince de Condé dead in the shadows, Belgium offering herself to a French prince and given to an English one, Nicolas's hatred, Ferdinand in Spain and Miguel in Portugal, Italy quaking, Poland nailed in her coffin, England a suspected ally, the peerage refusing four heads to the law, the fleurs-de-lys erased from the king's carriage and the cross torn from Notre-Dame, Lafayette lessened, Laffitte ruined, Benjamin Constant dead in indigence, Casimir Périer dead exhausted; civil war in Paris, servile war at Lyons; the Duchesse de Berry in the Vendée, plots, risings and cholera, adding the roar of events to the roar of ideas."],["By late April everything had come to the boil. Since 1830 small revolts had kept bursting out, signs of a vast conflagration beneath; France watched Paris and Paris watched the Faubourg Saint-Antoine. In the grave and stormy wine-shops of the Rue de Charonne workmen swore to take to the street at the first alarm; upstairs rooms held almost masonic initiations; \\"subversive\\" pamphlets were read aloud. Hugo quotes the talk: nobody knows the leaders and the day will be known only two hours before; three hundred men at ten sous each will buy powder and shot; one man does not sleep because he makes cartridges all night; \\"We have no arms!\\" \\"The soldiers have!\\" Well-dressed men came for ten minutes, shook hands with the most important and left. A man would drink and tell the wine-merchant the revolution would pay. A fencing master's trophy of foils one day lost its buttons.","The brewing became oddly notorious: women sweeping doorsteps spoke of cartridge-making; proclamations to the provincial National Guard were signed by a wine-merchant; a bearded man with an Italian accent read a manifesto from a stone post in the Marché Lenoir to applause. A man who shouted \\"I am a Babouvist!\\" and denounced the republicans was silenced as a police spy. A workman was warned near the canal by a stranger from \\"the committee\\" that an eye was fixed on him. Two ragged passers-by: \\"Who governs us?\\" \\"M. Philippe.\\" \\"No, it is the bourgeoisie.\\" A carpenter in the Rue de Reuilly found torn papers: a committee note about recruiting and a cache of five or six thousand guns, a list headed Q C D E (quinturions, centurions, decurions, éclaireurs) with names, arms and a coded date, April 15th 1832, and a third paper mixing the sections of the Society of the Rights of Man with Kosciusko, Washington, Hoche, Marseillaise and the sovereignty of the people. Searches turned up gray paper folded into cartridges with a powder recipe, a mason's package of printed dialogues and a tin of cartridges, children's finds of a bullet-mould and melted lead, a man caught at five in the morning making cartridges; two hundred thousand cartridges were distributed in the faubourg one week and thirty thousand the next, and the police seized none. Shopkeepers asked workmen how the rising was coming along as they would ask after a wife.","The network of societies spread like a membrane: from the Friends of the People sprang the Society of the Rights of Man, with sections named Pikes, Tocsin, Phrygian Cap, January 21, Robespierre, Ça Ira, which begot the Society of Action; alongside them the Gallic Society, societies for the press and against indirect taxes, the Equal Workingmen split into levellers, communists and reformers, and the Army of the Bastilles in which no more than five men knew one another. The Parisian societies had branches in Lyons, Nantes, Lille and Marseilles, each with its Cougourde. The schools stirred as much as the faubourgs: the Friends of the A.B.C., affiliated with the Mutualists of Angers and the Cougourde of Aix, met at the Café Musain and at the wine-shop Corinthe in the Rue Mondétour. The army too was mined, and liberty trees went up in Burgundy and the south.","Hugo closes on the Faubourg Saint-Antoine itself, laborious, angry as a hive, suffering under attic roofs and full of rare ardent minds, where misery and intelligence meet; a reservoir of people whose cabarets, like the taverns on the Sibyl's cave, intoxicate on words more than wine. In '93 it sent forth now savage legions, now heroic bands: savages, yes, but the savages of civilisation, demanding light with the mask of night, whom he prefers to the civilised men of barbarism who politely glorify the sword, the stake and the scaffold. But a third choice remains: neither despotism nor terrorism; progress with a gentle slope, which is God's whole policy."],["About this time Enjolras, foreseeing a possible catastrophe, takes a kind of mysterious census. In the back room of the Café Musain he tells the friends they must know where they stand and whom they can count on, and that it must be done today: Courfeyrac to the polytechnic students on their day out, Feuilly to the men of the Glacière, Combeferre to Picpus, Bahorel to the Estrapade, Prouvaire to the masons' lodge in the Rue de Grenelle-Saint-Honoré, Joly to Dupuytren's clinical lecture to feel the pulse of the medical school, Bossuet to the young law licentiates in the courts; he will take the Cougourde himself. One important group remains: the marble-workers, painters and sculptors' journeymen at the Barrière du Maine, enthusiasts liable to cool, who have taken to playing dominoes at Richefeu's and need someone to talk to them with firmness. He had counted on \\"that abstracted Marius\\", who no longer comes.","Grantaire offers himself. Enjolras is incredulous: Grantaire, who believes in nothing, to warm cold hearts in the name of principle? \\"I believe in you,\\" Grantaire answers. Told to sleep off his absinthe and not meddle, he protests that he is terrible when he sets about it, knows the Social Contract and the constitution of the Year Two by heart, keeps an old Republican bank-bill in his drawer, and can talk superb twaddle for six hours by the clock. Enjolras gravely consents to try him. Grantaire goes home and returns in five minutes wearing a red Robespierre waistcoat, lays his hand on its scarlet points, whispers \\"Be easy\\" in Enjolras's ear, and sets out.","A quarter of an hour later the back room is empty, each friend gone to his task. Walking towards the abandoned quarries at Issy where the Cougourde of Aix meets, Enjolras reviews the situation: the people are again taking possession of right, the furnace is being heated, and he has a powder train of friends laid across Paris, Combeferre's eloquence, Feuilly's enthusiasm, Courfeyrac's dash, Bahorel's smile, Prouvaire's melancholy, Joly's science, Bossuet's sarcasm. This makes him think of Grantaire, and since the Barrière du Maine is not far out of his way he decides to look in at Richefeu's.","One o'clock strikes from Vaugirard as he pushes open the smoky door and stands with folded arms. Through the haze he hears Grantaire's voice, hammering a marble table strewn with bran and dominoes and arguing with an adversary: double-six, fours, blank, domino, plague take it. Grantaire is playing dominoes."],["As soon as Javert has carried off his prisoners in three cabs, Marius slips out too and goes to sleep on a mattress on the floor at Courfeyrac's, who now lives in the Rue de la Verrerie \\"for political reasons\\". At seven next morning he returns, pays Ma'am Bougon the quarter's rent, loads his books, bed, table, commode and two chairs on a handcart and leaves no address, so that when Javert comes back to question him he hears only \\"Moved away!\\" Ma'am Bougon is convinced the girlish young man was in league with the robbers. Marius has two reasons: horror of the house where he saw the wicked poor man, a deformity perhaps worse than the wicked rich man, and an unwillingness to appear in the lawsuit and testify against Thénardier. Javert supposes the young lawyer, whose name he has forgotten, has taken fright, and does not find him.","Months pass. Learning from a law student that Thénardier is in close confinement, Marius has five francs handed in to the clerk's office of La Force for him every Monday; having no money, he borrows them from Courfeyrac, the first loan of his life, a riddle to the lender and to the receiver alike. Marius is heartbroken. He had seen the girl and the old man who seemed her father at close quarters, and a gust swept them away without one spark of certainty. She was not Ursule; the Lark is only a nickname; the old man, in hiding from the police, disguised as a workman near the Invalides, heroic and equivocal, may or may not be her father, may or may not be the man Thénardier recognised. His whole life is now absolute uncertainty within an impenetrable fog; he still longs to see her but no longer expects to.","Poverty returns. He had long since stopped working, and the narrator warns that reverie, wholesome as a narcotic in small doses, drowns when it replaces thought: the generous poor man who does not work is lost, and that slope ends in suicide or crime. Marius descends it slowly with his eyes on the girl he cannot see, feeling his coats, shirts, hat and boots wearing out and saying, \\"If I could but see her once again before I die!\\" One sweet thought remains, that she loved him and perhaps thinks of him still; at dusk he pours his purest reveries into a notebook, which he calls \\"writing to her\\". His reason is not deranged; stripped of hope, his judgment grows clearer than ever, and the soul that loves and suffers is in a state of sublimity.","Beyond the Rue de la Santé and the Glacière, near the little river of the Gobelins, lies a green meadow strung with drying linen, with an old market-gardener's house of Louis XIII's time, a little water among poplars, and the Panthéon, the Val-de-Grâce and the towers of Notre-Dame on the horizon, the one spot on the boulevards where Ruysdael would sit down, and therefore deserted. Marius's strolls lead him there. One day he asks a rare passer-by its name: \\"It is the Lark's meadow,\\" where Ulbach killed the shepherdess of Ivry. After the word \\"Lark\\" Marius hears nothing more. It has replaced Ursule in his melancholy. \\"This is her meadow. I shall know where she lives now.\\" Absurd, but irresistible: he returns to the meadow of the Lark every day."],["Javert's victory in the Gorbeau hovel had its gaps. His chief anxiety was that he had not taken the prisoner: the assassinated man who flees is more suspicious than the assassin, and would have been a fine prize for the authorities. Montparnasse had escaped too, having met Éponine on watch under the trees and led her off, preferring to play the lover with the daughter rather than the brigand with the father. Éponine was later seized and joined Azelma at the Madelonettes. And between the Gorbeau house and La Force, Claquesous had been lost, melted out of the handcuffs and the cab like a snowflake in water. Was there connivance? Javert bristles at such compromises, but his squad includes inspectors more initiated than he, and Claquesous was villain enough to make a very good agent. Such double-edged rascals exist. As for the booby of a lawyer whose name he has forgotten, Javert cares little; a lawyer can be hunted up any time.","The magistrate leaves one of the Patron-Minette band, Brujon, the long-haired man of the Rue du Petit-Banquier, out of close confinement in the Charlemagne yard in the hope that he will chatter. A \\"Brujon, 1811\\" carved on a wall of the Lions' Ditch at La Force was his father's. This Brujon is a cunning young spark with a plaintive air who seems stupefied by prison, staring for hours at the sutler's price list and complaining of fever. Robbers, Hugo notes, do not interrupt their profession because they are in the hands of justice; they are artists with one picture in the salon and another on the easel. Late in February 1832 it comes out that he has paid the prison errand-men fifty sous for three commissions under comrades' names, to the Panthéon, the Val-de-Grâce and the Barrière de Grenelle, the homes of three redoubtable prowlers, Kruideniers alias Bizarro, Glorieux and Barre-Carosse, who are promptly arrested. A week later a watchman catches Brujon writing at night; he gets a month in solitary, but the paper is not found.","Next morning a \\"postilion\\", a pellet of bread with a note inside, is thrown from the Charlemagne yard over the five-storey building into the Lions' Ditch. It reaches Babet, one of the four heads of Patron-Minette, in solitary, and reads: \\"Babet. There is an affair in the Rue Plumet. A gate on a garden.\\" Babet passes it to a woman friend in the Salpêtrière, who passes it to Magnon, a woman suspected by the police but not yet arrested, who has relations with the Thénardiers (to be described later) and can bridge the Salpêtrière and the Madelonettes. Just then, for lack of proof against them, Éponine and Azelma are released, and Magnon, waiting at the gate, gives Éponine the note and tells her to look into it. Éponine goes to the Rue Plumet, finds the gate and garden, watches the house for days, and brings Magnon back a biscuit, which in prison symbolism means: nothing to be done. When Brujon and Babet cross in the prison yard, \\"Well? the Rue P.?\\" \\"Biscuit.\\" So Brujon's crime miscarried, though, the narrator adds, the miscarriage had consequences of its own, quite distinct from his programme: when we think we are knotting one thread we are often tying another."],["Marius sees no one now, but sometimes meets Father Mabeuf by chance; both are descending the cellar stairs of misery. The Flora of Cauteretz no longer sells at all; the indigo trials in the shady garden at Austerlitz have failed, and Mabeuf has pawned his copper plates to get a sunny corner in the Jardin des Plantes to try again at his own expense. He breakfasts on two eggs and leaves one for his old servant, unpaid for fifteen months; often that is his only meal. Grown morose, he no longer receives visitors, and when the old man and the young one pass on the Boulevard de l'Hôpital they only exchange a melancholy nod; misery has loosened their bond. Royol the bookseller is dead. Mabeuf, nearly eighty, dreams that his balls of blueing will make him rich enough to redeem his plates and buy a 1655 Art de Naviguer.","One evening, with Mother Plutarque ill in bed, he dines on a bone and a crust and sits on an overturned stone post in his garden reading two books on demons and on the goblins of the Bièvre, which interest him because his garden was once haunted. Four rainless days have left his beloved rhododendron drooping. He rises to draw water but has not even the strength to unhook the well-chain, and looks up at a sky filling with stars: not a cloud, not a drop. \\"A tear of dew! A little pity!\\" He tries the chain again and fails.","A voice asks, \\"Father Mabeuf, would you like to have me water your garden for you?\\" Something like a wild animal passes through the hedge and a tall, slender girl, barefoot in a tattered petticoat, stands boldly before him, a form blossomed out of the twilight. Before the timid old man can speak she has unhooked the chain, filled the watering-pot, and is running among the beds distributing life, black and angular as a bat. She draws bucket after bucket and waters the whole garden. Mabeuf, in tears, lays his hand on her brow and calls her an angel who takes care of flowers. \\"No, I am the devil, but that's all the same to me,\\" she says. He laments that he is too poor to do anything for her. \\"You can do something. Tell me where M. Marius lives.\\"","He searches his memory: Monsieur Marius, the Baron Marius Pontmercy. He lived, or rather no longer lives, he does not know where; then, bending to train a branch, he recalls that Marius often passes along the boulevard towards the Glacière and the Rue Croulebarbe, to the Lark's meadow; it is not hard to meet him there. When he straightens up the girl is gone. Decidedly terrified, he reflects that if his garden had not been watered he would take her for a spirit, and falling asleep an hour later wonders whether, as Rubaudière describes, it could have been a goblin."],["One Monday morning, the day he borrows a hundred-sou piece from Courfeyrac for Thénardier, Marius takes a stroll before carrying it to the prison, hoping it will help him work on his return; it never does. He is supposed to be translating the Gans and Savigny controversy, reads four lines, sees a star between himself and the paper, and goes out to the Lark's meadow, where he sees the star more than ever. He lives there more than at Courfeyrac's; his real address is the seventh tree from the Rue Croulebarbe on the Boulevard de la Santé. That morning he sits on the parapet of the Gobelins river in the spring sun, dreaming of \\"Her\\" and reproaching himself for his paralysis of soul, hearing the laundresses beating linen below and birds in the elms above, the sound of toil and the sound of liberty.","A familiar voice says, \\"Come! Here he is!\\" It is the wretched girl who came to his room one morning, the elder of the Thénardier daughters, Éponine; he knows her name now. She has grown poorer and prettier at once, barefoot and in rags two months older, with straw in her hair from sleeping in a stable loft and the terrified, lamentable look that prison adds to wretchedness, and in spite of it all she is beautiful. She has hunted for him for six weeks: she was in the jug a fortnight and released since there was nothing against her and she is two months short of the age of discretion; Father Mabeuf was right that he could be found on this boulevard. He has moved, she understands, because of \\"that affair\\". She chatters about his old hat, the hole in his shirt she must mend, and Mabeuf calling him Baron; Marius barely answers. \\"You don't seem glad to see me.\\" He is silent. Then: if she chooses, she can force him to look glad. He asks what she means. \\"Ah! you used to call me thou.\\" \\"Well, then, what dost thou mean?\\"","She hesitates, struggling, then decides: so much the worse. She wants him to smile, and reminds him he promised to give her anything she liked. \\"I have the address.\\" Marius turns pale. The address he asked her to get, \\"of that young lady\\", she says, and sighs deeply. He leaps from the parapet and seizes her hand: lead him there, ask anything. She does not know the street or number well but knows the house, in quite another direction; she will take him. \\"Oh! how glad you are!\\" she says in a tone that would have rent any observer's heart but does not graze his.","A cloud crosses his brow. He grips her arm: swear not to give this address to her father. She stares, stupefied: how does he know that her name is Éponine? \\"That's nice! You have called me Éponine!\\" She does not seem to hear his demand until he shakes her, then laughs: her father is in close confinement, and what does she care for him; yes, she swears, she will not tell him, nor anyone. They set off at once, she walking ahead so that a nice young man will not be seen with \\"a woman like me\\", a word no tongue can express in that child's mouth. After a dozen paces she reminds him sideways that he promised her something. All he owns is the five francs intended for Thénardier the father; he lays them in her hand. She opens her fingers, lets the coin fall to the ground, and looks at him gloomily: \\"I don't want your money.\\""],["The chapter opens with the history of a \\"little house\\" in the Rue Blomet, now the Rue Plumet, built about the middle of the previous century by a chief justice of the Parliament of Paris to hide a mistress. A pavilion fronts a large overgrown garden; behind it lies a courtyard and a low back building, and from that a walled, roofless corridor winds for a quarter of a league between neighbours' gardens to a second secret door at the far end of the Rue de Babylone. After the Revolution the house stood empty, furnished but unlet, until October 1829, when a man of a certain age quietly took it under the name of M. Fauchelevent, repaired the secret doors, and moved in with a young girl and an elderly maidservant.","The narrator then names them outright: the tenant is Jean Valjean, the girl is Cosette, and the servant is Toussaint, a stammering provincial woman whom Valjean rescued from the hospital. He adds that the reader will have recognised Valjean as promptly as Thénardier did. Nothing drove him from the Petit-Picpus: he left because his happiness there alarmed his conscience. Cosette was being gently steered towards taking the veil, and he decided that to let her renounce the world before she had seen it would be to steal her life for his own comfort. After five hidden years he judged the danger to himself small, and that in any case his own past gave him no right to condemn her to the cloister.","When old Fauchelevent died, Valjean told the prioress he had inherited from his brother, offered the community five thousand francs for Cosette's five years of schooling, and took her away. He carried out himself the small locked valise that smells of embalming, which Cosette teases him about and calls his \\"inseparable\\". He also rented two shabby lodgings in distant quarters, in the Rue de l'Ouest and the Rue de l'Homme Armé, where he passes a month or six weeks at a time without Toussaint, so that he can vanish at the first alarm and never again be caught unprepared as on the night he fled Javert."],["Cosette and Toussaint occupy the pavilion, with its painted mirrors, gilded boudoir and tapestried drawing-room; Valjean has filled Cosette's room with a canopied bed, a Persian rug, gilt-edged books and a worktable of mother-of-pearl, and keeps the whole house heated all winter. He himself sleeps in a porter's lodge at the end of the back courtyard, with a folding bed, two straw chairs, a few old books, his valise and never a fire. He dines with Cosette and keeps a loaf of black bread for himself. To Toussaint he explained that the young lady is the mistress of the house; he is \\"a much better thing than the master\\", the father.","Cosette, taught housekeeping at the convent, runs their modest budget. Every day Valjean takes her for a walk, to the quietest alley of the Luxembourg, and every Sunday to mass at the distant church of Saint-Jacques-du-Haut-Pas, where his lavish almsgiving to the poor earned him Thénardier's letter addressed to \\"the benevolent gentleman\\" of that church. He takes Cosette to visit the poor and the sick. No stranger enters the house; Valjean fetches water himself from a fountain on the boulevard, and wood and wine are kept in the old grotto by the Rue de Babylone. The letterbox on that door receives only tax notices and summonses to guard duty.","The census of 1831 reached even the convent, so M. Fauchelevent, independent gentleman, belongs to the National Guard and three or four times a year puts on the uniform and mounts guard, glad of a disguise that makes him look like any other taxpayer. He has just turned sixty, the age of exemption, but looks under fifty and claims nothing, since he has no legal existence to claim with. With Cosette he dresses like a retired officer; alone, usually at night, he goes out in a workman's blouse and a cap that hides his face. Toussaint, told by her butcher that he is \\"a queer fish\\", answers that he is a saint. The three never use the Rue Plumet gate, which stays locked, and Valjean leaves the garden wild so as not to draw attention. In this, the narrator remarks, he may have made a mistake."],["Left to itself for more than fifty years, the garden has become extraordinary. Passers-by of forty years ago stopped to peer through the padlocked gate, with its two moss-covered pillars and an undecipherable pediment. Inside there is a stone bench, a couple of mouldering statues, rotting trellises and no paths or lawn, only grass and weeds everywhere. Trees have bent down to the nettles, climbers have climbed, and every growth has mingled with every other so that the enclosure is at once as impenetrable as a forest, as peopled as a city, as fragrant as a bouquet and as solitary as a tomb.","The narrator follows it through the seasons: in spring the thicket drinks the April sap and sheds flowers and dew over the statues, the crumbling steps and even the pavement of the empty street; at midday a thousand white butterflies drift through it like summer snow; at evening honeysuckle and convolvulus pour out their scent while woodpeckers and wagtails settle in the branches. In winter it is black and dripping, and the house shows through. Though the mansions of the Rue de Varennes, the dome of the Invalides and the Chamber of Deputies are a few steps away, the Rue Plumet is a desert, and nature has reclaimed this Parisian corner with the force of a virgin forest.","From the garden Hugo rises to a wider thought: nothing is small. The perfume of the hawthorn may matter to the constellations; a bit of mould is a constellation of flowers and a nebula an anthill of stars; where the telescope ends the microscope begins. All beings and things are bound together in an immense mechanism whose first wheel is the gnat and whose last is the zodiac, and in which the material and the moral worlds flow into one another."],["The old pleasure-garden has been remade by neglect into a place fit for chaste mysteries, and in it waits a heart ready for love. Cosette left the convent at a little over fourteen, at the awkward age, homely rather than pretty apart from her eyes, timid and bold at once. Her education consisted of religion, devotion, convent \\"history\\", geography, grammar, a little music and drawing, and otherwise complete ignorance. Hugo reflects that a girl needs a mother's instinct to be led gently into knowledge of life, and that nothing prepares a girl for passion like the convent, which drives the heart inward. Cosette has had no mother, only many mothers in the plural, and Valjean, all tenderness, knows nothing of such things.","The Rue Plumet, the narrator says, is the sweetest and most dangerous place she could have come to: solitude with a beginning of liberty, a closed garden whose gate opens on the street. Valjean hands the wild garden over to her, and as a child she hunts insects in it while waiting to dream in it. She loves her father with her whole soul; he has gone on reading since his days as M. Madeleine and talks well, and on their walks explains everything to her from his reading and his suffering. She follows him everywhere, preferring his bare lodge and the paved back courtyard to her tapestried drawing-room. When she scolds him for having no carpet or stove he answers that many better men have no roof; when she asks why he eats such horrible bread he says only \\"Because, my daughter\\", and when she threatens to eat it too, he switches to white bread.","Of her childhood Cosette keeps only confused memories: two hideous figures, the Thénardiers, as in a dream, and a night journey to fetch water in a forest. She feels she began life in an abyss from which Valjean pulled her. She prays morning and evening for the mother she never knew, and sometimes, leaning her cheek on his white hair, thinks that her mother's soul may have passed into this good man. She does not even know her mother's name. When she asks, Valjean is silent, or smiles, and once the smile ended in a tear. The narrator asks whether prudence, respect, or some fear of handing Fantine's name to another memory seals his lips, and suggests that the dead woman's modesty may itself be keeping her in her grave. When Cosette dreams her mother had wings and must have been a saint, he answers: through martyrdom. And still Valjean is happy, thanking God for being loved by this innocent creature."],["One day Cosette glances at herself and thinks she might almost be pretty. She sleeps badly, decides next morning that she is ugly after all, and for a fortnight does her hair with her back to the glass. Then, in the garden, she overhears Toussaint ask her father whether he has noticed how pretty Cosette is growing. She runs to her mirror, which she has not looked into for three months, and cries out: she has dazzled herself. Her figure has formed, her skin has whitened, her blue eyes have caught fire. She goes back down to the garden feeling like a queen.","Valjean has watched this beauty growing for some time with terror. A man still bleeding from fate, whom the law could seize at any moment, he asks nothing of God or the world except that Cosette should love him; that love heals and crowns him, and anything that might touch it makes him shudder. He does not know what a woman's beauty means, but he senses that it is something terrible, and asks himself what is to become of him. What a mother would have seen with joy, he sees with anguish.","The symptoms follow at once. Remembering a passer-by's remark that she was pretty but badly dressed, Cosette conceives a horror of her merino gown and plush hat and, with a father who refuses her nothing, masters within a month the whole science of bonnets, gowns and mantles. A mother would have told her that a young girl does not wear damask; nobody does. When she first goes out in black damask and a white crape bonnet and asks how he likes her, Valjean answers \\"Charming!\\" in a voice like an envious man's, and that evening asks whether she will not put on the old gown again. She laughs at \\"that disguise\\". From now on she always wants to go out, prefers the front garden to the back courtyard, and strolls by the railings, while the shy Valjean keeps to his yard like a dog. It is at this point, after a gap of six months, that Marius sees her once more at the Luxembourg."],["Cosette, like Marius, was ready to take fire, and Hugo defends the unfashionable truth that people fall in love through a look. She had long since noticed the young man and his fine hair, eyes and voice, though he paid her no attention and so meant nothing to her. On the day their eyes met she went home thoughtfully to the Rue de l'Ouest, where Valjean was spending six weeks, and woke the next morning vexed at the disdainful stranger who now seemed to notice her, and childishly pleased to think she would have her revenge: knowing she was beautiful, she knew she had a weapon.","Marius, as the reader remembers, stayed on his bench and dared not approach. So Cosette went to him, asking her father to stroll in that direction; the first symptom of love in a young man is timidity, in a young girl boldness. That day her glance drove him beside himself and his set her trembling, and from then on they adored each other.","Her first feeling is a deep melancholy, as if her soul had gone dark. She does not know what love is: in the convent even the word amour in songbooks was replaced by tambour or pandour. Not knowing the name of her illness, she loves the more passionately, and the love that has come suits her exactly: an admiration from afar, the deification of a stranger, a vision rather than a lover. She smiles at him with complete frankness, waits impatiently each day for the walk, and tells Valjean in all sincerity what a delicious garden the Luxembourg is. The two never speak, never greet each other, do not know each other's names; they live by gazing at one another."],["Some instinct warns Valjean of Marius's presence. He sees a young man who now sits far off pretending to read, wears a new coat every day, gloves, perhaps curled hair, and he cordially detests him. Cosette gives nothing away; without knowing what is the matter with her, she knows it must be hidden. Once Valjean cannot help remarking what a pedantic air that young man has, and Cosette answers with perfect calm, \\"That young man!\\", as if seeing him for the first time, so that Valjean reproaches himself for pointing him out.","Valjean opens an undeclared war: he changes the hour, changes the bench, forgets his handkerchief, comes alone, and Marius blunders into every snare, while Cosette stays so imperturbable that Valjean concludes the ninny is mad about her and she does not know he exists. Only once does she slip, saying \\"What, already?\\" when he rises to leave. Watching Marius, Valjean feels the old wrath of his soul opening again, and rages inwardly at a great booby who comes lounging at the Luxembourg to prowl about his happiness after he has spent sixty years on his knees earning it. He looks at Marius as a dog looks at a thief.","The reader knows the rest: Marius follows Cosette to the Rue de l'Ouest, speaks to the porter, the porter speaks to Valjean, and a week later Valjean has gone, swearing never to set foot in the Luxembourg or the Rue de l'Ouest again. He returns to the Rue Plumet. Cosette asks no questions, afraid of betraying herself, and he, with no experience of these miseries, does not understand her silence. Three months on he offers the Luxembourg; her face lights up, they go, and Marius is not there. The next day she declines, sadly and gently. Valjean sits up whole nights asking what is in Cosette's mind, and looks back with despairing longing to the convent he left of his own free will. Cosette, for her part, languishes, no longer noticing seasons or weather, and shows him nothing but her pallor; each asks the other what is wrong and each answers, nothing."],["Valjean, who sometimes becomes childish in his grief, envies a general in full uniform and dreams that gold lace might dazzle Cosette back to him. The two have kept one habit from happier days: walks to see the sunrise in the poor fields near the barriers, where Cosette becomes a little girl again and weaves poppy garlands. One morning in October 1831, at daybreak near the Barrière du Maine, Valjean sits on some planks at a timber-yard gate, sunk in thought, when Cosette says someone is coming.","A noise turns into a convoy of seven wagons. The first six are long ladders on wheels, each drawn by four horses and carrying twenty-four men seated back to back with their legs dangling, an iron collar on every neck and a single chain running behind them all; the seventh is an open rack wagon piled with pots and chains among which sick men lie bound. A double file of ragged guards in three-cornered hats, with sabres, muskets and cudgels, marches alongside, and mounted gendarmes ride at head and rear. The prisoners, livid with cold, in wooden shoes and a harlequin's rags, say nothing while the escort curses and cudgels fall; children run laughing behind. Then the sun rises and the whole mass erupts into grins, oaths and songs, one wagon striking up a popular potpourri, while a crowd of market-gardeners and bourgeois gathers to stare. Dante, the narrator says, would have seen his seven circles on the march.","Valjean's eyes become those of a man seeing a vision; he tries to rise and flee and cannot move. Then he remembers: this is the usual route, the detour taken to spare the King at Fontainebleau the sight, and thirty-five years ago he passed through this barrier himself. Cosette, terrified in another way, asks what those men are. Convicts, he says; going to the galleys. As the cudgelling redoubles she asks whether they are still men. \\"Sometimes,\\" he answers. The gang, the narrator adds, had left Bicêtre before dawn and would go round by Le Mans, lengthening the journey by days.","Valjean goes home overwhelmed and does not notice Cosette's further questions, but hears her murmur that evening that she would die if she met one of those men close by. Next day there happen to be public festivities, a review in the Champ de Mars and fireworks at the Étoile, and Valjean, putting on his National Guard uniform as if taking shelter, takes her to them and believes the vision has been erased. Some days later, standing in her morning wrapper on the garden steps and pulling a daisy to pieces, Cosette turns her head and asks: \\"Father, what are the galleys like?\\""],["One diversion survives their sadness: carrying bread and clothing to the hungry and cold, visits on which they recover something of their old ease, and Cosette is sometimes merry in the evening afterwards. It was in this period that they went to the Jondrette den. The next morning Valjean appears in the pavilion as calm as ever but with a large inflamed wound like a burn on his left arm, which he explains away. It keeps him indoors with fever for a month, and he refuses a doctor: \\"Call the dog-doctor,\\" he tells Cosette.","Cosette dresses the wound morning and evening with such happiness at being useful that Valjean's fears dissolve. She deserts the pavilion for his little lodge, spends her days beside him and reads him books of travel; she sings him the melancholy songs she learned from Sister Sainte-Mechtilde at the convent. The Luxembourg, the prowling young stranger and Cosette's coldness all fade, and he tells himself he imagined it all. Even the discovery of the Thénardiers in the Jondrette hovel slides over him: he escaped, his trace is lost, and he thinks of them only to pity a family in such distress, now in prison and harmless. Cosette never mentions the Barrière du Maine again.","When spring comes he urges her to walk in the garden, and to please him she does, usually alone, since he hardly ever goes there himself, probably for fear of being seen through the railings. As March lengthens the days and April arrives, the blackness leaves her spirit without her noticing, and when she coaxes her convalescent father into the sunlight for a quarter of an hour, supporting his arm, she laughs every moment. Valjean whispers again what a good wound it was and feels grateful to the Thénardiers. Once healed, he resumes his solitary twilight walks, and the narrator warns that no one strolls alone through the empty regions of Paris without some adventure."],["Gavroche has eaten nothing for two days. Out beyond the Salpêtrière, in the straggling village of Austerlitz, he remembers a garden with an apple-tree and a badly fastened fruit-house. As he is about to climb the hedge at dusk he hears voices: on a stone bench sits the old man of the garden, and his housekeeper is listing their debts. She calls him Monsieur Mabeuf; he calls her Mother Plutarque, and Gavroche thinks both names a farce. They owe three quarters' rent, the greengrocer will send no more wood, the butcher and baker refuse credit. To each complaint the old man answers mildly: there is the sun; he does not digest meat; there are apples in the apple-room; he has no money. She goes in, and Gavroche, moved, curls up under the hedge behind the bench instead of robbing it, and dozes with one eye open.","Two figures appear in the pale lane: an elderly, plainly dressed bourgeois strolling slowly, and behind him a slim, elegant young man with a rose in his mouth whom Gavroche knows at once as Montparnasse. Gavroche pities the old man but knows the two of them together would be two mouthfuls for such a ruffian. Montparnasse springs; a moment later it is Montparnasse on the ground with a knee on his chest. The old man makes him stand, holds both his arms in one hand, and questions him: nineteen years old, strong, healthy; why not work? It bores him. His trade? An idler. What would he like to be? A thief.","Then, in a gentle voice, the old man delivers a long harangue which Gavroche hears every word of. Idleness, he says, is the most laborious of lives. The idler who will not be a workman becomes a slave: he will tear his sheets into ropes, climb chimneys and sewers, hollow out a sou to hide a watch-spring saw, and his reward will be the dungeon. He will enter at twenty rosy and white-toothed and come out at fifty bent and broken; the hardest of all work is thieving, and it is less disagreeable to be honest. He ends by asking what the young man wanted of him, his purse? Here it is. He puts it in Montparnasse's hand and walks calmly away. The narrator asks who this goodman was and says the reader has surely guessed.","Montparnasse stands amazed, and for the first time in his life thinking. Gavroche creeps up behind him, slips his hand into the back pocket of the fine black coat, lifts the purse, crawls back to the hedge, flings it over onto Father Mabeuf's foot and runs. Mabeuf wakes, opens it, and finds small change in one compartment and six napoleons in the other. Mother Plutarque pronounces that it has fallen from heaven."],["Four or five months on, nature, spring and youth have healed Cosette's grief without her knowing it. One day she catches herself thinking that she no longer thinks of Marius. That same week a handsome officer of lancers passes the gate: wasp-waisted, blue-eyed, fair, with waxed moustaches, a cigar and a glazed schapka, vain, insolent and good-looking, the reverse of Marius in every way. She supposes he belongs to the regiment barracked in the Rue de Babylone. The next day he passes again and she notes the hour; from then on she sees him nearly every day.","His comrades notice the pretty creature who is always in the untidy garden behind the rococo railings when the lieutenant goes by, and tell him so. The officer, whom the reader already knows, is Théodule Gillenormand, and he replies that he has no time to look at all the girls who look at him. The narrator sets this against Marius at the same moment, descending towards agony and longing only to see her once before he dies; had he seen her gazing at the lancer he would have died of grief. Whose fault? No one's: Marius is a temperament that buries itself in sorrow, Cosette one that plunges in and comes out again.","Hugo adds that Cosette is passing through the dangerous phase in which a girl's lonely heart, like a vine tendril, clings to whatever is nearest, a marble column or a tavern post, and that real misalliance is a misalliance of souls. What does her soul hold? Passion lulled, something limpid at the surface with the officer's image reflected in it, and perhaps a memory lingering at the bottom. Cosette herself does not know. Then a singular incident occurs."],["In the first fortnight of April Valjean goes away for a day or two, as he does from time to time, usually when money runs short; where he goes no one knows, though Cosette once rode with him as far as the Impasse de la Planchette. He says he will be back in three days. That evening Cosette, alone in the drawing-room at ten o'clock, sings the huntsmen's chorus from Weber's Euryanthe at her piano-organ, and afterwards seems to hear a man's soft tread in the garden. From her window upstairs she sees nothing in the full moonlight and puts it down to the music.","The next evening, towards nightfall, she thinks she hears the sound again among the trees and dismisses it as branches rubbing. Coming out of the thicket onto the little lawn, with the moon behind her, she sees her own shadow on the grass and beside it another, clearly outlined, wearing a round hat: the shadow of a man standing a few paces behind her. For a moment she cannot speak or move. When she turns there is no one, and a bold search of the thicket as far as the gate finds nothing. Two hallucinations in two days she cannot believe, and phantoms do not wear round hats.","Valjean returns and she tells him, hoping to be called a little goose. Instead he grows anxious, says it cannot be anything, and goes out to examine the gate closely. That night she wakes, hears someone by the steps, opens her shutter and sees a man in the garden with a big club; the moon shows her it is her father. He spends that night and the next two in the garden. On the third night he calls her to the window, laughing, and shows her on the turf a shadow with a round hat, cast by a hooded sheet-iron chimney-pipe on a neighbouring roof. She laughs with him and her fears vanish. The narrator notes what neither of them asks: whether the chimney really stood in that direction, whether the moon had been in the same place, and why a chimney-pot's shadow should take fright and disappear when someone turns to look at it. A few days later a fresh incident occurs."],["Near the street railings stands a stone bench screened by yoke-elms but reachable by an arm thrust through the gate. One evening that April, with Valjean out, Cosette sits there after sundown in an objectless twilight sadness; the narrator wonders whether Fantine is in that shadow. She takes a slow turn round the dewy garden, and when she comes back there is a fair-sized stone on the seat she just left. The thought that an arm reached through the railings to put it there frightens her properly this time. She runs into the house and bolts the garden door.","She asks Toussaint whether her father is back (the narrator notes here that Toussaint stammers and asks leave not to transcribe it any further) and whether the garden shutters are barred every night. Toussaint assures her she fastens them up like a prison, then frightens her thoroughly with a speech about lone women, men coming in at night to cut one's throat and blunt knives. Cosette dares not even mention the stone for fear of having the gate opened. She makes Toussaint go over the house from garret to cellar, locks herself in, looks under her bed and sleeps badly, dreaming of a stone as big as a mountain.","At sunrise she laughs at her night terrors, lumps the stone with the footsteps and the round-hatted shadow as things she dreamed, and goes down to the bench; the stone is there, and she breaks into a cold sweat. Daylight turns terror to curiosity. She lifts the stone and finds a white envelope with no address and no seal, containing a small notebook of numbered pages covered in a fine, rather pretty handwriting. There is no name. It must be for her, since a hand left it on her bench; from whom? Trying and failing to look away, she begins to read."],["The pages are short aphorisms and sighs set down at different times. Love, they say, is to shrink the universe to a single being and expand a single being to God; it is the greeting of angels to stars; to love someone is to make them transparent to God behind everything. Certain thoughts are prayers, and there are moments when the soul is on its knees whatever the body is doing. Parted lovers who cannot write send each other birdsong, the scent of flowers, children's smiles and starlight; spring itself is a letter the writer sends to her.","The writer claims that God can add nothing to lovers' happiness but endless duration, that a woman is a sweeter radiance and a greater mystery than any star, and that when a woman passes emitting light as she walks, one is lost, and nothing remains but to think of her until she is forced to think of you. Love is composed of the infinitely great and the infinitely little, in despair over a lost glove, enchanted by a found handkerchief. Then a fragment of dialogue: does she still come to the Luxembourg? No. Is this the church where she hears mass? She no longer comes. Does she still live in this house? She has moved; she left no address. What a melancholy thing not to know the address of one's soul.","The notebook grows darker: the writer dwells in night since a being went away and carried off his sky; he would be content to lie in the same grave hand in hand, touching a finger now and then. To die of love is to live in it. He describes meeting in the street a very poor young man in love, with holes in his elbows, water in his shoes and stars in his soul, and closes with the thought that if there were no one who loved, the sun would go out. Nothing in the pages is signed, dated or addressed."],["As Cosette lifts her eyes from the last page the handsome officer passes the gate at his hour, and she thinks him hideous. The fifteen pages are in one hand but in inks of different blackness, written on different days, without order or plan: a mind unfolding sigh by sigh. Her convent education spoke always of the soul and never of love, as one might speak of the firebrand and never of the flame; these lines reveal to her all at once love, sorrow, destiny and eternity. A letter without name, address, date or signature, a love-letter from a phantom to a shade, written with one foot in the grave and one finger in heaven.","From whom? She does not hesitate for an instant: one man only, him. It was he who wrote, he who thrust his arm through the railings; while she was forgetting him he found her again. But had she forgotten him? Never, she tells herself; the fire was only smothered, and now it blazes through her whole being. She reads the pages a third time, recognising in them what she had already read in his eyes, and when Lieutenant Théodule rattles his spurs past the gate and feels obliged to smile at her, she finds him insipid, foppish, impertinent and extremely ugly, and would gladly throw something at his head.","She shuts herself in her room, learns the manuscript by heart, kisses it and puts it in her bosom. All day she is in a daze, hoping for vague things, feeling the paper's corners against her skin, certain that it comes from him and is for her, and telling herself that an intervention of angels, a celestial chance, has given him back to her. The narrator closes on the real shape of that celestial chance: a pellet of bread tossed by one thief to another, from the Charlemagne courtyard to the Lion's Ditch, over the roofs of La Force."],["That evening Valjean goes out and Cosette, without knowing why, dresses her hair carefully and puts on a gown cut a trifle low. At dusk, while Toussaint is busy in the kitchen on the back yard, she goes down through the low branches to the bench. The stone is still there, and she lays her hand on it as if to thank it. Then she feels someone behind her, turns and rises. It is he: bareheaded, thin and pale, in black clothes the twilight barely shows, with something about him of death and night. He has thrown his hat into the thicket. She retreats until a tree stops her, and would have fallen without it.","In a voice she has never heard, barely above the rustle of the leaves, he begs her pardon for coming: his heart was full and he could not go on living as he was. Has she read what he left on the bench? Does she recognise him at all? He reminds her of the day she looked at him near the Gladiator and the days she passed before him, the 16th of June and the 2nd of July, nearly a year ago; he asked the woman who let the chairs, he knew she lived on the third floor in the Rue de l'Ouest, he followed her, then she vanished. At night he has been coming to look at her windows, walking softly so as not to frighten her; the other evening he was behind her and fled when she turned; once he heard her singing and was happy. Let him come sometimes; he thinks he is dying; he adores her.","\\"Oh! my mother!\\" she says, and sinks down as if dying. He catches her and holds her, tottering himself, beside himself with love and yet, the narrator insists, without a trace of passion for the body in his arms. She lays his hand on her heart, where he feels the paper, and stammers: you love me, then? Hush, she breathes, thou knowest it. They fall onto the bench, and their lips meet, a kiss and nothing more. They sit in the cool night without feeling the cold stone or wet grass, their hands clasped, and little by little begin to talk, telling each other everything: their dreams, how they adored each other from afar, their despair at losing each other. In a quarter of an hour each has the other's soul. She never asks how he got into the garden; it seems so simple that he should be there. When they have told each other everything she lays her head on his shoulder and asks his name. Marius, he says; and hers? Cosette."],["Since 1823, as the Montfermeil inn sank into debt, the Thénardiers had two more children, both boys, making five in all. Madame Thénardier, a mother to her daughters only, detested Gavroche and cursed the two youngest, and got rid of them while they were still small, at a profit. The woman Magnon, who had made old M. Gillenormand support the two children she said were his, lost both boys to the croup epidemic on the same day; with them went eighty francs a month, paid punctually in M. Gillenormand's name by his rent-collector, M. Barge, of the Rue du Roi-de-Sicile. In the freemasonry of evil she found two children of the same age and sex: the little Thénardiers became little Magnons, and Magnon moved to the Rue Clocheperce to break the trail. The registry office noticed nothing. Thénardier demanded ten francs a month for the loan of his sons and received it. M. Gillenormand went on visiting the children every six months and saw no change, Magnon telling him how much they resembled him.","Thénardier used the occasion to become Jondrette, and his daughters and Gavroche hardly had time to learn that they had two small brothers. On the evening she gave them up the Thénardier felt, or seemed to feel, a scruple, which her husband cauterised with the remark that Jean-Jacques Rousseau had done even better, and when she worried about the police he assured her that no one looks closely after children who have not a sou. Magnon, a kind of fashionable woman of the criminal world, shared her lodgings with a gallicised English thief known as Mamselle Miss. The two boys, recommended by the eighty francs, were well fed and dressed and treated almost like little gentlemen, better by their false mother than by their real one. Thénardier even remarked once that the father ought to give them some education.","Then the wholesale arrest in the Jondrette garret spread its catastrophe through the underworld. Shortly after Magnon had passed Éponine the note about the Rue Plumet, the police raided the Rue Clocheperce and seized Magnon, Mamselle Miss and everyone in the house. The two little boys were playing in the back yard and saw nothing; when they tried to go in, the door was locked and the house empty. A cobbler across the street gave them a paper their mother had left, with the address of M. Barge, rent-collector, 8 Rue du Roi-de-Sicile, and told them to go there. The elder led the younger, holding the paper in numb fingers; at the corner a gust of wind tore it away, and as night fell he could not find it. They began to wander the streets."],["The spring of 1832, the year the cholera reached Europe, blows harsh north winds and frequent thunderstorms. One such evening Gavroche, in a woman's woollen shawl, stands outside a wig-maker's near the Orme-Saint-Gervais pretending to admire a wax bride while planning to pinch a cake of soap. Two neatly dressed little boys of about seven and five come in weeping to beg and are shoved out by the barber. Gavroche runs after them, learns they do not know where to sleep, and with lordly tenderness tells them to come along. On the way up the Rue Saint-Antoine he insults the barber, a portress and a passer-by, gives his shawl to a shivering beggar-girl, and learns that the boys have a papa and mamma but do not know where they are, and that mamma is a lady who lives with Mamselle Miss. The reader, unlike Gavroche, knows who they are from the previous chapter. He finds a sou in his rags, marches them into a baker's, insists on white bread, and divides it into three, keeping the smallest piece.","At the corner of the Rue des Ballets, within sight of the wicket of La Force, a man in blue spectacles greets him: Montparnasse in disguise. Under a porte-cochère Montparnasse tells him that Babet, moved to the Conciergerie that morning, has escaped by turning left instead of right; Gavroche draws a dagger from the cane Montparnasse carries and asks whether he means to fight the police tonight. Montparnasse says only \\"Things\\", and mentions a bourgeois who the other day gave him a sermon and a purse that vanished from his pocket a minute later. Asked where he is putting the infants to bed, Gavroche answers: in the elephant. Montparnasse stuffs quills up his nostrils to change his face, and when a police sergeant passes he warns Gavroche in a coded phrase full of the syllable \\"dig\\". Gavroche tells him where to find him: ask for Monsieur Gavroche.","Hugo describes the elephant of the Bastille: a forty-foot model in timber and plaster of a monument Napoleon planned, crumbling and forgotten in the south-west corner of the square beside the boarded-up column of July, black with weather, with grass between its legs, melancholy by day and formidable by night. Gavroche gets the boys through the fence, props a workmen's ladder against a foreleg, and coaxes the elder up to a hole in the belly, then carries the younger up and kicks the ladder away. Inside, by the light of a resin \\"cellar rat\\", the children see a skeleton of beams and plaster stalactites, with black spots scurrying in the corners. Gavroche's bed is a straw mat and a warm blanket under a pyramid of poles covered with brass aviary netting weighted down with stones, all of it, he explains, borrowed from the monkeys, the giraffe and the Jardin des Plantes. He tucks the little one in like a mother, corrects the boys' vocabulary (crib, darkmans, nut), promises them summers at the Glacière, the theatre, the Opera claque and a look at the executioner, and puts out the light. The rats swarm onto the netting; the five-year-old learns that the rats ate Gavroche's cat and asks whether they would eat them too, and Gavroche gives him his hand across his brother until all three sleep.","Towards the end of the hour before dawn a man soaked through runs from the Rue Saint-Antoine to the elephant and twice gives a parakeet-like cry. Gavroche answers, slides down the leg, and finds Montparnasse, who says they need him and asks him to lend a hand. The boy asks no questions, and the two set off through the file of market-gardeners' carts towards the Rue Saint-Antoine."],["Babet, Brujon, Gueulemer and Thénardier had planned an escape, with Montparnasse to help from outside; Babet got himself away first, as Montparnasse told Gavroche. Brujon, a month in the punishment cell, came out with a woven rope and a plan, and in the decrepit New Building found Gueulemer and a nail. Roofers were relaying the slates, so ladders and scaffolding linked the courtyards. Thénardier, held in close confinement since the night of 3 February in one of the iron cages of the top storey called the Fine-Air, had somehow obtained a bottle of drugged wine and kept, to spike his bread against the rats, an iron bolt.","On the night Gavroche took in the two children, Brujon and Gueulemer pierce the chimney against which their beds stand, the storm covering the noise, climb the flue, force the grating and reach the roof. They fasten Brujon's rope to the wrenched bars, swing across a six-foot gap eighty feet deep to the outer wall, slide down onto the bathhouse roof and let themselves out into the street in under three-quarters of an hour, breaking their rope and leaving a piece on the chimney. Thénardier, warned no one knows how, sees two shadows pass his dormer and recognises Brujon. At two o'clock a young conscript relieves the old soldier on guard; at four the conscript is found drugged asleep beside an empty cage, with a hole in the ceiling, a hole in the roof, a bed-plank gone and his bayonet missing.","Thénardier has found Brujon's rope too short to descend the outer wall. How he reached the top of the ruined back wall of a demolished house at the corner of the Rue des Ballets and the Rue du Roi-de-Sicile, three storeys above the street, no one has ever explained. There he lies in the rain, flayed and exhausted, as four o'clock strikes and the uproar of a discovered escape breaks out behind him with torches running along the roofs. Four men slip into the fenced recess beneath him to confer. From their slang he knows them: Brujon's barrier argot, Babet's old slang of the Temple, Montparnasse who speaks plain French, and Gueulemer by his shoulders. Brujon and Babet argue that the innkeeper has bungled it and been retaken, and that they should go and drink a bottle; Montparnasse grumbles that one does not desert a friend. Thénardier dare not call out; he drops the end of rope into the enclosure. They look up and see him.","He is too numb to tie a knotted rope to the wall himself, and the old plaster stove-flue running up the ruin is too narrow for a grown man; a brat is needed. Montparnasse runs to the Bastille and returns in eight minutes with Gavroche, who tells the ruffians that a young 'un like him is a man and men like them are babes. He takes off his shoes, is lifted onto the shanty roof, and as he is about to climb sees the face above him in the first light and recognises his father, remarking that it won't hinder. He climbs the flue with the rope in his teeth, straddles the wall and knots the rope to the window crossbar; a moment later Thénardier is in the street, cold and fear forgotten, and his first words are to ask whom they are to eat. Brujon raises the Rue Plumet job: an isolated house, a rotten gate, lone women. Babet says Thénardier's girl Éponine looked into it and sent Magnon word there was nothing to be made there; Thénardier says the girl is no fool but it must be seen to. Nobody looks at Gavroche, who waits on a fence-post for his father to turn towards him, then puts his shoes on and goes off to get his brats out of bed. Babet asks Thénardier whether he took a good look at the boy who carried him the rope; it struck him it was his son. \\"Bah!\\" says Thénardier. \\"Do you think so?\\""],["The book begins with a Latin word, pigritia, idleness, which Hugo makes the mother of a son, theft, and a daughter, hunger, and of a whole world and dialect, slang. He recalls the outcry when, thirty-four years earlier, he put a thief talking argot into an earlier work, and the same objections raised since against Balzac and Eugène Sue. Slang is horrible, he agrees; but when has horror excluded study? A naturalist does not refuse to examine the viper, nor a surgeon the ulcer. Argot is both a literary phenomenon and a social fact, and properly speaking it is the language of misery.","He anticipates the objection that everyone speaks slang: merchants, stockbrokers, gamblers, soldiers, printers, phrenologists, duchesses, diplomats with their ciphers, classical academicians who call the sea Neptune, and sailors with the heroic slang of the sea. He grants it and sets it aside, reserving the word for the crafty, venomous tongue of combat invented by the last misery when it decides to make war on the whole order of fortunate facts and reigning rights. Preserving even a fragment of such a language, as Plautus preserved Phoenician and Molière the Levantine dialects, is a service to civilisation, and the tongue spoken for four centuries by every form of human misery deserves study more than any provincial dialect.","The historian of manners and ideas, he argues, has a mission no less austere than the historian of events: one records crowns, battles and revolutions in daylight, the other the toiling, suffering, oppressed life underground, descending as brother and judge among those who bleed and those who strike. The two histories interpenetrate; man is an ellipse with two foci, facts and ideas. Slang is the dressing-room where language disguises itself in word-masks and metaphor-rags to perform evil; overheard at the portals of society it sounds like hydras talking. He closes by asking for compassion on the chastised, reflecting that the earth itself resembles a jail and that no one is truly fortunate; the real division is between the luminous and the shady, and the remedy is education, though even those who learn to know and love will still suffer."],["Slang is the tongue of those who sit in darkness; every syllable seems branded. Yet it has its syntax and poetry and belongs in literature: Villon spoke it, and the famous line about the snows of yesteryear uses a slang word, antan. Studied as a geologist studies strata, it is an alluvial deposit in which lie old popular French, Provençal, Spanish, Italian, the Levantine of the ports, English, German, Latin, Basque and Celtic, each accursed race having dropped its layer. Hugo gives examples in each language, from boffete and gat to bichot (from bishop), caleur (from Kellner), cadene (from catena) and the Celtic guedouze for death.","Beyond borrowing, slang has roots in the mind itself. First, direct creation: barbarous, solitary words with no etymology, like taule for the executioner or rabouin for the devil, which both mask and reveal. Second, metaphor, the refuge of the thief plotting a stroke and the prisoner planning an escape: to unscrew the nut is to wring a neck, it lansquines means it rains like a hedge of lancers' pikes. Third, expedient: deforming ordinary words and tacking on terminations in aille, orgue, iergue or uche to throw listeners off. Because it is the dialect of corruption and always seeking concealment, slang decomposes and recomposes faster than any language, so that Cartouche's talk would have been Hebrew to Lacenaire; the Temple preserved the seventeenth-century slang, Bicêtre the slang of Thunes.","Every metaphor holds a lesson. For these men night is la sorgue and man l'orgue, man a derivative of night; a prisoner is a sick man and a condemned man a dead one; the saw that cuts his fetters is a dance-hall, because his first thought on being free is to dance; the head that plots is the Sorbonne and the head that pays la tronche; prison is the college. Hugo then describes the cellar of the Grand-Châtelet, eight feet below the Seine, where men bound for Toulon were chained by the neck to a beam too short to let them lie down, standing in mud for months, and where nearly all the galley songs were born, because song lingers where hope has gone. To inform is \\"to eat the bit\\"; a slap is a camoufle, from which Voltaire's camouflet. Slang is language turned convict, and the chapter ends asking whether no deliverer will ever come to the human soul chained like Andromeda in that darkness."],["The old slang of the Court of Miracles, like the cards its vagrants painted with hares roasting a huntsman, breathed a powerless, dejected sadness; its songs were humble enough to draw tears, the thief always the hare in hiding who lies flat and begs, conscious of his guilt. Towards the middle of the eighteenth century the tone changed: prison songs became strident and jovial, sung in a cellar or a wood while a throat was being cut; the rabble began to mock the grand meg and the grand dab and call the King of France the Marquis de Pantin. Hugo reads this gaiety as a grave symptom: the darkened classes were losing the sense of their criminality and feeling some unspoken support among thinkers, as theft began to filter into doctrines and sophisms.","He is careful whom he accuses: not the eighteenth century or philosophy, whose encyclopaedists, physiocrats, philosophers and utopians, Diderot, Turgot, Voltaire and Rousseau, were four sacred vanguards of humanity. Beside them grew sophists, forgotten writers licensed by the King, chief among them Restif de la Bretonne, whose disorganising works were devoured by the unfortunate. In Germany, summed up by Schiller's Robbers, theft rose up against property and labour dressed as theory. When suffering breeds wrath and the prosperous sleep, the result is the jacquerie, the revolt of discomfort against comfort, an earthquake of the people beside which political agitation is child's play.","This peril, imminent at the end of the eighteenth century, the French Revolution cut short: the idea armed with a sword, it closed the door of evil and opened the door of good, ended torture, and gave man a second soul, the right. Hence, Hugo insists, the social catastrophe is now impossible; revolution is the vaccine against jacquerie. Since 1789 the whole people has grown into a sublime individual: he who is free is scrupulous, he who votes reigns, and on days of deliverance there is no longer any populace. His proof is 1848, when the ragpickers of the Faubourg Saint-Antoine, barefoot, guarded the wagons carrying the crown jewels of France. The red spectre, he concludes, no longer frightens anyone."],["Society need no longer fear the apoplexy of a jacquerie, but it may die of consumption, and social consumption is called misery. The first fraternal obligation and the first political necessity is to think first of the disinherited: to air, enlighten and love them, to lavish education on them, to offer the example of labour and never of idleness, to limit poverty without limiting wealth, to open workshops for every arm, schools for every aptitude and laboratories for every intelligence, and to proportion enjoyment to effort. And all this is only the beginning: labour cannot be a law without being a right. If nature calls itself Providence, society should call itself foresight; a soul starving for light is more heartbreaking than a body starving for bread.","Progress, Hugo affirms, tends towards solution; as humanity rises, the deep layers will emerge naturally from the zone of distress and misery will be abolished by a simple raising of the level. The past is strong at this moment, walking like a conquering corpse with superstition, despotism and ignorance for its legions, but ideas do not flow backwards any more than rivers, and those who say no to the future condemn only themselves. The people sketched by the eighteenth century will be finished by the nineteenth; a force compounded of earth and heaven governs human affairs and conducts them towards equity.","Social philosophy is science and peace, dissolving wrath by studying antagonisms. Civilisations have foundered before, Babylon, Nineveh, Thebes, Rome, for reasons no one knows; but ours lies in the light where its faults can be probed and the remedy found, and it will be saved. Hugo calls these pages an austere interlude in a mournful drama and wishes to end on encouragement, yet confesses that those who follow the social clinics sometimes shake their heads: between the swollen selfishness of the prosperous and the covetous misery of the wretched, the ideal can look like a lost point of light surrounded by monstrous black menaces, though no more in danger than a star in the maw of the clouds."],["The narrator fills in what the reader has guessed: Éponine, having recognised through the gate who lived in the Rue Plumet, first kept the ruffians away and then led Marius there, and after days of ecstasy outside the railing he forced one of the rusty bars and entered the garden as Romeo entered Juliet's. Since the street is always empty and he comes only at night, no one sees him. From the evening of their first kiss he is there every night.","Hugo insists on the purity of what follows. Through the whole month of May the two sit in the neglected, fragrant garden and adore each other without crossing any line they are even aware of; Marius kisses her hand or a lock of hair and no more, and once turns his eyes away when her bodice slips. They have begun to call each other thou. Cosette confides that her real name is Euphrasie and that Cosette was a childhood nickname, then decides she prefers Cosette after all because he does. Marius confesses he once thought her name was Ursule, which makes them laugh all evening; he starts to mention the day he nearly fought a veteran at the Luxembourg and stops, since that would mean speaking of her garter.","Marius imagines life as nothing more than this: coming every evening, moving the bar, sitting elbow to elbow on the bench, calling her thou, smelling the same flower. His compliments are lyrical and shy; she answers that she has loved him a little more every hour since morning. Cosette, fresh from the convent, talks with surprising good sense; both weep over a crushed ladybird and laugh like two boys. The chapter closes on the two lovers whispering in the dark while the planets wheel overhead."],["Marius and Cosette do not notice the cholera decimating Paris that very month. They have confided in each other, but only as far as names and outlines. Marius has said he is an orphan named Marius Pontmercy, a lawyer who lives by writing for publishers, the son of a colonel who was a hero, on bad terms with a rich grandfather; he hinted at being a baron, a word Cosette does not understand. She has told him she was brought up at the Petit-Picpus convent, that her mother is dead like his, that her father is M. Fauchelevent, very good, generous to the poor and poor himself.","Strangely, the recent past has gone so hazy for Marius that this satisfies him entirely. It never occurs to him to tell Cosette about the night in the Jondrette garret, about Thénardier, the burn, or the odd behaviour and flight of her father. In the evening he hardly remembers the morning. The two live only in the hours they are together, somnambulists who have told each other everything except everything.","Hugo describes them suspended between earth and heaven, hardly flesh and blood, with no yesterday or tomorrow. Marius sometimes closes his eyes in her presence to see her soul better. Neither of them asks where this is to lead; they consider that they have already arrived."],["Cosette's gaiety is enough to keep Jean Valjean content, and the small habitual precautions of lovers keep him blind. She agrees to every proposal he makes; since he goes to bed at ten, Marius comes only after that hour, when he hears Cosette open the glass door onto the veranda. Valjean has stopped thinking that Marius exists, though one morning he remarks that Cosette has whitewash on her back, from being pushed against the wall the night before. Toussaint, who retires early, knows nothing either. Marius never enters the house; the two sit in a recess by the steps, and he always sets the bar of the gate back so no displacement shows.","He returns to Courfeyrac's lodgings around midnight. Courfeyrac, a practical man, is impatient with this invisible paradise, tells Marius he is getting irregular in his habits, and demands the girl's name, but nothing will make Marius talk. Hugo lists the delights of that May: quarrelling over you and thou, discussing people they care nothing about, Cosette talking finery, Marius talking politics, listening knee to knee to carriages in the Rue de Babylone, keeping silence together.","Complications approach. One evening, as Marius comes along the Boulevard des Invalides with his head down, a voice says 'Good evening, Monsieur Marius.' It is Éponine. He has not thought of her once since the day she led him to the Rue Plumet, and though he owes her his happiness the meeting embarrasses him; absorbed in Cosette he has not even registered that this is Éponine Thénardier, bearer of the name in his father's will. He answers with 'you' rather than 'thou', and when she asks whether she has done anything to him, says no. She tries to speak, cannot smile, pauses twice with downcast eyes, then says good evening abruptly and goes."],["The next evening, 3 June, Marius sees Éponine coming through the trees of the boulevard and turns aside to reach the Rue Plumet by the Rue Monsieur. She follows him for the first time, sees him move the bar and slip in, finds the bar herself, mutters 'None of that, Lisette!' and sits on the stone footing of the railing in a dark nook where the gate meets the wall. A belated passer-by hears a threatening voice from the dark say she is no longer surprised he comes every evening, and hurries on.","Minutes later six men arrive separately along the wall and gather at the railing with putty for the window-pane, a drugged ball for the dog, and saws for the old bars. The sixth man, testing the bars, reaches the loose one, and a hand seizes his arm with the words 'There's a dog.' He recoils and asks what jade this is; she answers 'Your daughter.' It is Thénardier. The other five, Claquesous, Gueulemer, Babet, Brujon and Montparnasse, close in with their tools. Éponine throws herself on her father's neck, asks how he got out and how her mother is, and insists there is nothing to be had in the house: she was the one charged to investigate, and the people have moved. Babet points to a light moving in the attic, Toussaint spreading linen to dry. Hugo remarks that Éponine no longer speaks slang since she has known Marius.","When Thénardier pushes past to enter, she sets her back to the gate and tells the six armed men they shall not go in. If they touch the gate she will scream, beat on the door, rouse the street and call the police, beginning with her father. She laughs that she is the daughter of a wolf, that she will be hungry this summer and cold this winter, and that she does not care whether she is found dead on this pavement tomorrow or in the nets at Saint-Cloud a year hence; a dry cough racks her narrow chest. To her father's plea that they must eat she answers 'Burst!' and sits humming on the stone with her chin in her hand.","The six retreat under the lantern, humiliated. Babet guesses she is in love with the master of the house; Montparnasse offers to stay with her, knife open, while the others do the job; Thénardier says nothing. Brujon, the gang's oracle and the man who put up the job, says he saw two sparrows fighting that morning and jostled a quarrelling woman that evening, that it is all bad, and that they should quit. They go, agreeing to sleep 'under Pantin', Thénardier having the key to the gate. Éponine creeps after them to the boulevard and watches them melt into the dark."],["After the ruffians leave, the street resumes its tranquil night aspect. Hugo observes that what has just happened would not have astonished a forest, where what is below man glimpses through the mists what is beyond man, and the forces of the gloom know and balance one another.","Teeth and claws, he says, fear what they cannot grasp; blood-drinking bestiality is uneasy before an impassive spectral form standing in its shroud. A black figure barring the way stops the wild beast short. That which comes from the cemetery intimidates that which comes from the cave: the ferocious fear the sinister, and wolves recoil when they meet a ghoul."],["While Éponine guards the gate, Marius is with Cosette under the most beautiful sky of that spring, and finds her weeping. Her father told her that morning to hold herself in readiness, to pack a large trunk for her and a small one for him, that he has business and must travel, that all is to be ready in a week and that they may go to England. Marius, who for six weeks has possessed Cosette as spirits possess, hears the words 'we are going away' as 'Cosette is not yours' and re-enters reality with a shock.","He asks coldly whether she will go; she answers 'If my father goes', and when he says he will then go elsewhere, she turns pale and understands him. She proposes that he follow wherever they go; he bursts out that he has no money, owes Courfeyrac more than ten louis, has a hat worth three francs and boots that let in water, and could not pay for a passport. He presses his brow against a tree for a long time while Cosette sobs beside him, then kneels and kisses the tip of her foot.","He gives her his word of honour, which he has never given anyone, feeling his father beside him: if she goes away, he will die. The solemnity of it stops her tears. He tells her not to expect him tomorrow but the day after, and mutters aside that 'he' is a man who never changes his habits and receives only in the evening, which Cosette does not understand. Saying she ought to know his address in case anything happens, he scratches '16 Rue de la Verrerie' into the plaster of the wall. Cosette promises to pray and to sing the music from Euryanthe he once listened to outside her shutters, and fixes nine o'clock the day after tomorrow. They kiss without noticing it, eyes on the stars. When Marius leaves, the street is empty: it is the moment Éponine is following the ruffians away. An idea he himself judges senseless has crossed his mind, and he has come to a desperate decision."],["M. Gillenormand, past ninety-one, still lives with his daughter in the Rue des Filles-du-Calvaire, outwardly erect but inwardly sinking. For four years he has waited for Marius to ring his bell; now the idea that he may never see him again chills him. He keeps a portrait of his dead daughter, Madame Pontmercy, opposite his bed and remarks that it resembles 'him' too. When Mademoiselle Gillenormand ventures to ask whether he is still angry with poor Marius, he calls Marius a heartless ingrate and turns away to hide a tear, then forbids his name to be mentioned. Théodule, the lancer, has failed as a substitute: a vulgar chatterbox in a tricoloured cockade who only made the old man miss Marius more.","One evening, 24 June, as he sits before his fire thinking of Marius bitterly and lovingly, Basque announces 'M. Marius'. Marius enters, shabby, calm and sad. The grandfather, dazzled with joy, finds only harshness on his lips: 'What have you come here for?' Marius asks him to have pity on him; the old man retorts that youth is asking pity of a man of ninety-one who has lost his teeth and is losing his sight, and demands to know what he wants. Marius says he has come to ask permission to marry. Gillenormand summons his daughter to say good day and dismisses her, then interrogates him: no profession, nothing earned, only the twelve hundred livres allowance, a girl with no dowry and no expectations, a father he knows nothing of, named Mademoiselle Fauchelevent. He laughs and says 'Never, sir, never!'","Marius turns to go, and the single word 'Father' transforms the old man: he hauls Marius back into an armchair, tells him to jabber about his love affairs, gives him a hundred louis to buy a hat. Marius pours out the story: the Luxembourg, the garden with the iron fence in the Rue Plumet, the father who means to take her to England. At 'Rue Plumet' the grandfather drops his snuff: Théodule has told him of a girl behind a railing in the Rue Plumet near the barracks, a 'Pamela' the lancer has been courting. He treats the matter as an old man's gallantry, says one does not marry such a girl, and ends: 'Booby! make her your mistress.' The words enter Marius like a sword. He bows and says: 'Five years ago you insulted my father; today you have insulted my wife. I ask nothing more of you, sir. Farewell.' Gillenormand runs to the window shouting 'Marius!' four times, but he is already round the corner, and the old man falls back into his chair with nothing left in his eyes."],["On 4 June, towards four in the afternoon, Jean Valjean sits in his workman's waistcoat and long-visored cap on a solitary slope of the Champ-de-Mars. He rarely goes out with Cosette now. For a week or two he has repeatedly caught sight of Thénardier on the boulevard, and though his own disguise saved him from recognition he is sure Thénardier is prowling nearby. Paris is unquiet, and police hunting political men like Pépin or Morey might easily stumble on a man like Jean Valjean. He has decided to leave Paris and France for England before the end of the week, and has warned Cosette.","An inexplicable thing has added to his alarm. That morning, alone in the garden before Cosette's shutters opened, he found a line freshly engraved on the wall, probably with a nail: '16 Rue de la Verrerie'. The grooves in the old black mortar were white and plaster dust powdered the nettles below. A signal to others, or a warning to him? In any case the garden has been violated. He recalls the earlier alarms of the household and says nothing to Cosette.","As he sits, a shadow tells him someone has stopped on the crest behind him, and a paper folded in four falls on his knees as if dropped over his head. In large pencilled letters it says: 'Move away from your house.' He springs up; no one is on the slope, but he sees a creature larger than a child and smaller than a man, in a grey blouse and dust-coloured velvet trousers, jump the parapet into the moat of the Champ-de-Mars. He goes home very thoughtful."],["Marius has left M. Gillenormand with immense despair, though the grandfather's talk of Cousin Théodule has left no trace in his mind: at his age one suspects nothing, and he could sooner commit any crime than suspect Cosette. He wanders the streets till two in the morning, flings himself dressed on his mattress at Courfeyrac's, and wakes to find Courfeyrac, Enjolras, Feuilly and Combeferre standing hatted and ready to go out. 'Are you coming to General Lamarque's funeral?' Courfeyrac asks; it sounds like Chinese to him.","He goes out later and puts in his pocket the two pistols Javert gave him for the ambush of 3 February, still loaded, without knowing why. All day he prowls in intermittent rain, buys a penny roll and forgets it, apparently bathes in the Seine without noticing. He no longer hopes for anything; his one clear thought is that at nine he will see Cosette, and after that, gloom. From the deserted boulevards he hears strange noises and asks himself whether there is fighting.","At nine precisely he moves the bar and rushes into the garden. Cosette is not at her usual place nor in the recess by the steps; the shutters are closed; the garden is deserted. He knocks on the shutters, risking her father's face at the window, and calls 'Cosette!' twice. No reply. He sits on the steps, blesses his love, and tells himself that since Cosette is gone, all that remains is to die.","Then a voice from the street calls through the trees: 'Mr. Marius, are you there? Your friends are waiting for you at the barricade of the Rue de la Chanvrerie.' The voice is not wholly unfamiliar; it resembles the hoarse, rough voice of Éponine. He hurries to the gate, puts his head through, and sees someone who looks like a young man running off into the dark."],["The purse Gavroche flung over the hedge did M. Mabeuf no good: unable to believe a star could coin itself into louis, he took it to the police commissioner as a lost article, and no one claimed it. Meanwhile his indigo experiments have failed at the Jardin des Plantes as in his garden, the pawnshop has sold the copper plates of his Flora to a coppersmith for stewpans, and he has sold the text as waste paper. He eats bread and potatoes, has sold his furniture, bedding, clothes, herbariums and prints, and keeps only his rarest books in a glass-fronted case: a Bible of 1560, a Tibullus printed by the Aldines, a unique Diogenes Laertius of 1644 among them. He burns no fire and goes to bed at sundown to save candles; people avoid him.","One day Mother Plutarque says she has no money for dinner and no one will give credit. Mabeuf looks over his books like a father choosing which child to sacrifice, goes out with one under his arm and comes back with thirty sous. From that day a veil falls over his face that never lifts. Day after day a book goes out and a coin comes back, dealers paying twenty sous for what cost him twenty francs. He says at times 'But I am eighty', as though hoping to reach the end of his days before the end of his books. Once he sells a Robert Estienne for thirty-five sous and buys an Aldus for forty, and goes without dinner, beaming. The Horticultural Society's president gets him invited to dine with the Minister; he sells an Elzevir for the cab fare, no one speaks to him all evening, the Minister's wife asks who that old gentleman is, and he walks home at midnight in a rainstorm.","His only pleasure is a few pages of the Diogenes Laertius each night. Then Mother Plutarque falls ill; the doctor orders an expensive potion and a nurse. The bookcase is empty except for that one volume. On 4 June 1832 he carries it to the Porte Saint-Jacques and returns with a hundred francs, which he lays in five-franc pieces on the old servant's nightstand without a word. Next morning he sits all morning on the overturned post in his garden, head drooping, in intermittent rain. In the afternoon shots and shouting break out over Paris. A passing gardener tells him it is the riots, that they are fighting near the Arsenal. Mabeuf takes his hat, mechanically feels for a book to put under his arm, finds none, says 'Ah! truly!' and goes off with a bewildered air."],["Revolt, Hugo says, is composed of nothing and of everything: a wandering force that meets heads that speak, brains that dream, souls that suffer, and bears them away at random against the state, the laws and the insolence of others. Its elements range from embittered conviction and youthful courage to vanity, the taste for change and the rabble at the bottom, that mud which catches fire. Like a waterspout it carries off great natures and small, breaking one against the other, and gives whoever it seizes an extraordinary power, making a cannonball of a stone and a general of a porter. Some crafty politicians hold that a little revolt is hygienic for a government, since it tests the army and strengthens the police.","He then gives the floor to the school of the 'golden mean', the lukewarm-water party of demi-science. In its view the riots after 1830 spoiled the purity of the July Revolution, turning deliverance into catastrophe; every uprising closes shops, depresses the funds and shakes credit, at a cost reckoned at twenty million francs the first day, forty the second, sixty the third, the equivalent of a lost fleet. Uprisings have their beauty, this school concedes, the war of the pavements being as grand as the war of the thickets, lighting up Parisian generosity and bravery, but at the price of bloodshed, progress compromised, honest liberals in despair, foreign absolutism rejoicing, and the massacres of order grown ferocious over liberty gone mad. In sum: disastrous.","Thus speaks the bourgeoisie's approximation to wisdom. Hugo refuses the word 'uprisings' as too large and convenient, distinguishes one popular movement from another, and declines to weigh a revolt against a battle in francs: if July cost a hundred and twenty millions, the installation of Philip V in Spain cost two milliards, and he would still prefer the 14th of July. All the doctrinaire objection concerns effects; he will look for the cause, and promises to be explicit."],["There is uprising and there is insurrection, two phases of wrath, one wrong and one right. In democratic states, when a fraction usurps, the whole may rise to reclaim its rights, even by arms: that is insurrection. The same cannon is wrong against the people on 10 August and right on 14 Vendémiaire. Machine-breaking, Ramus killed by students, Rousseau stoned in Switzerland, the salt smugglers who turned into Chouans, the Saint-Bartholomew assassins, the September cutthroats, the Vendée: these are revolt. Paris against the Bastille is insurrection. 'There is no insurrection except in a forward direction'; every violent step backward is a revolt against the human race. Danton against Louis XIV is insurrection; Hébert against Danton is revolt. Insurrection can be resurrection.","Before universal suffrage, each epoch protested as it could: under the Caesars there was no insurrection but there was Juvenal, and Tacitus, whose concentrated prose Hugo credits to the tyrant's compression of history, and whom God spared from meeting Caesar. Riot proceeds from a material fact, insurrection is a moral phenomenon; riot is Masaniello, insurrection Spartacus. A bread riot like Buzançais may start from a just grievance and still be a riot because it strikes blindly at the innocent. Every armed protest, even 14 July, begins as a torrent before becoming a river; some insurrections are lost in a swamp like the Rhine. Universal suffrage dissolves riot at birth by giving insurrection the vote, and whatever today may be, tomorrow will be peace. The bourgeois knows none of these shades: for him all is sedition, the dog biting its master, until the dog's head is suddenly outlined as a lion's and he shouts 'Long live the people!'","What then was the movement of June 1832, revolt or insurrection? Hugo will sometimes say revolt for the surface and keep insurrection for the foundation. Even those who call it an uprising speak of it with respect, as a relic of 1830, a last undulation of the mountain sinking into the plain. He closes by promising to relate facts the historian neglects, the foliage of great events, from the two days of 5 and 6 June, showing only one side and one episode, the least known, altering a few names, and able to say of what he tells: 'We have seen this.'"],["In spring 1832, with the cholera three months old, Paris is long ripe for commotion; the spark is Lamarque's death. Lamarque had shown the bravery of the battlefield under the Empire and of the tribune under the Restoration, hated Wellington, kept the sadness of Waterloo for seventeen years, and died clasping a sword given him by the officers of the Hundred Days. His expected death was dreaded by the people as a loss and by the government as an occasion. On the eve and morning of 5 June the Faubourg Saint-Antoine arms itself: joiners take door-weights, a man sharpens a stocking-weaver's hook into a dagger, artisans are sent to Filspierre's for cartridges, drinkers in wineshops ask each other whether their pistols are under blouse or shirt, and leaders run from house to house.","On the 5th, a day of rain and sun, the procession crosses Paris with official pomp: two battalions with draped drums, ten thousand National Guards, the hearse drawn by young men, officers of the Invalides with laurel, then an innumerable crowd of students, refugees, strikers and printers under Spanish, Italian, German and Polish flags, brandishing sticks and sabres. Windows and roofs are full of anxious faces. The government has carabineers in the Place Louis XV, dragoons at the Célestins, artillery in the Louvre courtyard, twenty-four thousand soldiers in the city and thirty thousand outside. Rumours run through the cortège of Legitimist tricks, of the Duc de Reichstadt, of a factory of arms to be thrown open; among the noble emotions are also criminal faces saying 'Let us plunder!' Incidents mark the route: the coffin carried round the Vendôme column, stones at the Duc de Fitz-James on his balcony, an officer of the 12th Light declaring himself a Republican, the Polytechnic School arriving against orders. At the Bastille the Faubourg Saint-Antoine joins the throng and it begins to seethe; a man with a red beard is pointed out as the one who will give the word to fire.","At the esplanade of the Austerlitz bridge the hearse halts and Lafayette bids Lamarque farewell, all heads uncovered. Suddenly a man in black on horseback appears with a red flag, or a pike topped with a red cap; Lafayette turns away, Exelmans leaves the procession. Shouts go up of 'Lamarque to the Pantheon! Lafayette to the Town-hall!' and young men drag the hearse across the bridge and Lafayette's carriage along the Quai Morland. Dragoons emerge from the Célestins and advance at a walk; the crowd and the cavalry touch; no one knows what happened in that minute, a trumpet or a child's dagger-blow, but three shots are fired, killing the squadron chief Cholet and an old deaf woman closing her window. A woman screams 'They are beginning too soon!', a fresh squadron charges with bared swords, stones rain, a fusillade breaks out, the timber-yards of the Isle Louviers bristle with combatants and a barricade is begun. The cry 'To arms!' flies to all four quarters of Paris."],["Nothing is more extraordinary than the first outbreak of a riot: foreseen but not prepared, coming from the pavements and from the clouds, plot here and improvisation there. Within fifteen minutes it is happening everywhere. Twenty bearded young men carry a crape-draped tricolour out of a dram-shop; a well-dressed bourgeois hands out cartridges in the street; a black flag reading 'Republic or Death!' is paraded in the Rue Saint-Pierre-Montmartre; section flags appear. A small-arms factory on the Boulevard Saint-Martin and three armourers' shops are stripped of two hundred and thirty guns, sixty-four swords and eighty-three pistols. Young men install themselves in women's houses to fire from the windows; a curiosity shop yields yataghans; a mason lies dead in the Rue de la Perle.","All over Paris men break lanterns, unharness carriages, tear up paving-stones and trees, roll out hogsheads and build barricades, forcing the bourgeois to help, requisitioning absent husbands' guns and chalking receipts on doors, disarming sentinels and tearing epaulets off officers. Students pour out of the Quartier Saint-Jacques and arms are distributed from the cafés. In one place only, the Rue Sainte-Avoye, the inhabitants tear down a barricade themselves; in one place only, the Rue du Temple, the insurgents abandon one. In less than an hour twenty-seven barricades spring up in the Halles quarter, centred on house No. 50, the fortress of Jeanne and his six hundred, with barricades at Saint-Merry and the Rue Maubuée, and countless others in the Marais, at Mont-Sainte-Geneviève, in the Rue Ménilmontant, and near the Prefecture of Police. Money is handed out at some barricades, passwords carried, sentinels posted; the tangled streets of the Halles are admirably chosen, and a dead man in the Rue du Ponceau carries a plan of Paris.","Within three hours the insurgents hold the Arsenal, the Marais, the Popincourt arms factory, the Château-d'Eau, the Veterans' barracks, Sainte-Pélagie, the Place Maubert and all the barriers; by five o'clock the Bastille, the Lingerie and the Blancs-Manteaux, with scouts menacing the Bank and the Post Office. A third of Paris is theirs, and the fighting has passed from stones to gunshots. At six the Passage du Saumon becomes a battlefield between the two fires, where the author of this book, gone to see the volcano close, sheltered half an hour behind a half-column. Drummers beating the call to arms are stabbed; three officers fall in the Rue Michel-le-Comte. The centre of Paris has become a colossal citadel where, tellingly, no fighting has yet begun. Some regiments waver, remembering the 53rd's neutrality in July 1830. Marshal Lobau and General Bugeaud command; huge patrols reconnoitre; the government, army in hand, hesitates as night comes and the Saint-Merry tocsin begins. Marshal Soult, who saw Austerlitz, looks on gloomily; reinforcements converge from Saint-Denis, Courbevoie and Vincennes, and around the Tuileries solitude forms. Louis Philippe is perfectly serene."],["Ordinarily nothing is calmer than Paris outside the rebellious quarters. The shopkeeper hears the drum and says there seems to be a squabble in the Rue Saint-Martin or the Faubourg Saint-Antoine, 'or somewhere in that direction'; when platoon firing becomes audible he remarks that it is getting hot, shuts his shop and dons his uniform. Men fire in a square and grapeshot riddles house-fronts while a few streets away billiard-balls click in the cafés, theatres play vaudevilles and people go out to dinner. In 1831 a fusillade was stopped to let a wedding pass; in 1839 an old man wheeled a handcart of cocoa from barricade to troops and back, serving both sides. Only the size and gaiety of Paris, the city of Voltaire and Napoleon, make such a thing possible.","On this occasion, however, the city feels something stronger than itself: it is afraid. Doors and shutters close even in distant quarters; the courageous take arms and the cowards hide. Alarming news is hawked: that the insurgents hold the Bank, that six hundred are entrenched in the church of Saint-Merry, that the line cannot be relied on, that Marshal Clausel told Armand Carrel to get a regiment first, that Lafayette, though ill, said he would follow wherever there was room for a chair, that pillagers would roam the deserted corners at night, that Lobau and Bugeaud would march four columns on the centre at midnight or dawn, or that the troops might evacuate Paris altogether. People wonder why Soult does not attack; the old lion seems to scent an unknown monster in the gloom.","Evening comes; the theatres do not open; patrols search passers-by and arrest suspects, more than eight hundred by nine o'clock, filling the Prefecture, the Conciergerie and La Force. In the Conciergerie's long vault prisoners lie heaped on straw, harangued by Lagrange of Lyons. People barricade themselves at home, wives and mothers ask why he has not come home, and everyone listens on the threshold to cavalry, caissons, trumpets, firing, and above all to the lamentable alarm peal of Saint-Merry, waiting for the first cannon-shot and asking how it will all end."],["When the clash at the Arsenal sends a frightful ebb back through the crowd on the boulevards, a ragged child coming down the Rue Ménilmontant with a sprig of laburnum spots an old holster-pistol in a bric-a-brac window, tells the shopwoman he is borrowing her machine, and runs off with it. Fleeing bourgeois in the Rue Amelot meet him brandishing it and singing a couplet about pointed hats. It is little Gavroche on his way to the wars. On the boulevard he notices the pistol has no trigger.","Hugo wonders who wrote the songs Gavroche sings; perhaps Gavroche himself. He knows every popular tune and mixes in his own chirpings, the repertory of the birds with the repertory of the workshops; he knows thieves, was apprenticed three months to a printer, and once ran an errand for an Academician: a gamin of letters. He has no idea that the two brats he housed in his elephant that rainy night were his own brothers. Having spent that night with his brothers and the dawn with his father, he fetched the two from the elephant at daybreak, shared some invented breakfast, entrusted them to the street, and told them to come back in the evening if they did not find papa and mamma. They never returned, picked up by a policeman, stolen by a mountebank, or simply lost in the Chinese puzzle of Paris; ten or twelve weeks later he still scratches his head and asks where the devil his two children are.","In the Rue du Pont-aux-Choux the only shop open is a pastrycook's; he searches his pockets, finds not a sou, and shouts 'Help!' at missing the last cake. Passing through the Rue du Parc-Royal he consoles himself by tearing down theatre posters in broad daylight, and spits philosophical bile at a group of fat, comfortable proprietors who, he says, eat their money."],["Waving his pistol in the open street, Gavroche feels his fervour grow. Between scraps of the Marseillaise he harangues the bourgeois, declares the police spies dogs, announces that the boulevard is coming to the boil and that he gives his days to his country and will never see his concubine Nini again. When a National Guard lancer's horse falls he lays down the pistol, helps the man up and the horse too, then resumes his march. The Rue de Thorigny is all peace and silence, in the Marais's usual apathy.","Four old women gossip in a doorway, three portresses and a ragpicker with her basket, standing at the four corners of old age. The ragpicker, humble and grateful in the hierarchy of refuse heaps, listens as they talk of cats and dogs, the King of Rome's sheep-carriage, the Duc de Bordeaux, Louis XVIII, and the dreadful price of meat; then boasts of how she sorts her haul at home, rags in a basket, bones under the bed. Gavroche, listening behind her, asks what they mean by talking politics, and is met with a quadruple howl: another rascal, with a pistol in his paddle, the sort who is never easy unless overturning the authorities. He thumbs his nose.","Mother Patagon the portress foresees evil doings, says the errand-boy next door now carries a gun instead of a girl in a pink bonnet, that there was a revolution at Pontoise, that the Célestins are full of pistols, that tobacco will get dearer, and that she will go to see him guillotined. Gavroche tells her to blow her promontory and passes on, reflecting in the Rue Pavée that the ragpicker is wrong to insult revolutionists since his pistol is in her interest. Patagon follows to shout 'You're nothing but a bastard' after him; he does not care a brass farthing. Passing the Hotel Lamoignon he cries 'Forward march to the battle!', reproaches his pistol that he is going off but it will not, pities a gaunt poodle whose hoops all show, and turns towards the Orme-Saint-Gervais."],["The barber near the Orme-Saint-Gervais is shaving a veteran of the legion who served under the Empire. From the riot and Lamarque they pass to the Emperor: how he rode (badly, but he never fell because he did not know how to fall), his white racing mare with a black star, his one wound, in the heel at Ratisbon. The veteran lists his own wounds, two sabre-blows at Marengo, a bullet at Austerlitz, another at Jena, a bayonet at Friedland, seven or eight lance-thrusts at the Moskowa, a shell splinter at Lutzen, a biscaïen ball in the thigh at Waterloo, 'that's all'. The hairdresser declares grandly that he would rather die of a cannonball in the belly than slowly in bed with drugs and syringes; the soldier remarks that he is not over fastidious.","A fearful crash shatters the show-window. The wig-maker turns pale and cries that it is a cannonball. The soldier picks up what is rolling on the floor: a pebble. At the broken window the hairdresser sees Gavroche fleeing at full speed towards the Marché Saint-Jean. Passing the shop with the two brats still in his mind, Gavroche had been unable to resist saying good day with a stone. The hairdresser, from white turned blue, shrieks that the fellow does mischief for the pure pleasure of it: 'What has anyone done to that gamin?'"],["In the Marché Saint-Jean, where the post has already been disarmed, Gavroche 'effects a junction' with a band led by Enjolras with a double-barrelled hunting-gun, Combeferre with a National Guard's musket and two pistols, Jean Prouvaire with an old cavalry musket, Bahorel with a rifle, Courfeyrac brandishing a sword-cane, and Feuilly at their head with a naked sword shouting 'Long live Poland!' Cravatless, hatless, soaked, with lightning in their eyes, they reach the Quai Morland; Gavroche asks where they are going and Courfeyrac tells him to come along.","Bahorel, like a fish in water in a riot, wears a scarlet waistcoat that makes a passer-by cry 'Here are the reds!'; he retorts that he does not tremble before a poppy and leaves fear of red to horned cattle. Spotting the Archbishop of Paris's Lenten permission to eat eggs addressed to his 'flock', he calls flock a polite word for geese and tears it down, which conquers Gavroche. Enjolras tells him he is wrong: the Archbishop is not the enemy, and one does not fire out of the ranks with the soul any more than with a gun. Bahorel answers that each has his own fashion, that he wants to eat eggs without permission, and that he is only whetting his appetite, 'Hercle!' Gavroche asks what Hercle means and is told it is Latin for cursed name of a dog; when Bahorel shouts 'para bellum' for cartridges to a bearded young man at a window, Gavroche, now understanding Latin, approves.","A tumultuous retinue of students, artists, members of the Cougourde of Aix, artisans and longshoremen follows with clubs and bayonets. Among them walks an extremely old man, unarmed, hurrying so as not to be left behind, with a thoughtful air. Gavroche asks Courfeyrac 'Keksekça?' and is told he is an old duffer. It is M. Mabeuf."],["Enjolras and his friends were on the Boulevard Bourdon when the dragoons charged, and fled into the Rue Bassompierre shouting 'To the barricades!' In the Rue Lesdiguières they met an old man walking in a zigzag as if drunk, hat in hand though it was raining hard. Courfeyrac recognised Father Mabeuf, whom he knew from having often walked Marius to his door, and, amazed to see the timid old beadle-book-collector strolling among bullets, told him to go home. 'Why?' 'There's going to be a row.' 'That's well.' Sword-thrusts and firing, cannon: 'That is good.' Where are you going? 'We are going to fling the government to the earth.' 'That is good.'","He has followed them ever since without a word, his step suddenly firm, refusing the arms artisans offer him, marching near the front rank with the gait of a man walking and the face of a man asleep. The students mutter what a fierce old fellow he is, and the rumour spreads that he is a former member of the Convention, an old regicide. The mob turns through the Rue de la Verrerie.","Little Gavroche marches ahead like a trumpet, singing a nonsense ballad of Charlot and Charlotte and two drunken sparrows, with refrains for Chatou, Passy, Meudon and Pantin. They head for Saint-Merry."],["The band grows every moment. Near the Rue des Billettes a man of lofty stature with greying hair and a bold, daring mien joins them; Courfeyrac, Enjolras and Combeferre notice him but none of them knows him. Gavroche, busy singing, whistling and pounding shutters with the butt of his triggerless pistol, pays him no attention.","In the Rue de la Verrerie they pass Courfeyrac's door. Saying he has forgotten his purse and lost his hat, he runs upstairs and seizes an old hat, his purse, and a large square coffer the size of a valise hidden under his soiled linen. Coming down, the portress hails him as 'Monsieur de Courfeyrac', which he threatens to repay by calling her Mother de Veuvain, and tells him someone has been waiting over an hour in her lodge. Out comes a pale, thin, small, freckled artisan in a tattered blouse and patched velvet trousers, with rather the air of a girl dressed as a man but a voice not at all like a woman's, who asks for Monsieur Marius.","Courfeyrac says Marius is not there, that he does not know whether he will return, and that he himself will not come back because he is going to the barricades. The youth asks why, where, offers to carry the coffer, and asks to come along; Courfeyrac says the street is free and the pavements belong to everyone, and runs to rejoin his friends, giving the coffer to one of them. A quarter of an hour later he notices the young man has actually followed. A mob does not go precisely where it intends: they overshoot Saint-Merry and find themselves, without quite knowing how, in the Rue Saint-Denis."],["Parisians who today see a basket-maker's sign of Napoleon in willow opposite the Rue Mondétour have no idea what happened there thirty years ago. That is where the Rue de la Chanvrerie and the wine-shop Corinthe stood, site of a barricade eclipsed by Saint-Merry's, on which Hugo means to shed light. As with Waterloo, he gives a letter: imagine an N with its top on the Rue Saint-Denis and its base on the Halles, the uprights the Rue de la Grande-Truanderie and the Rue de la Chanvrerie, the crossbar the Rue de la Petite-Truanderie, with the Rue Mondétour cutting all three at crooked angles: seven islands of eight-storey houses shored up with beams, wet pavements, cellar-like stalls and century-old gratings, all since devoured by the Rue Rambuteau. Entering the Rue de la Chanvrerie from the Rue Saint-Denis one found it narrowing like a funnel into what looked like a blind alley, with two dark cuts of the Rue Mondétour left and right, and at the bottom a two-storey house forming a cape in the street.","There a wine-shop had stood three hundred years, on the spot old Theophilus wrote of a poor lover's skeleton swinging. In Régnier's day it was the Pot-aux-Roses, with a rose-painted post for a rebus sign; in the last century the painter Natoire, grateful for his drinking bouts there, painted a bunch of Corinth grapes on the post, the landlord added 'Au Raisin de Corinthe', and the ellipsis of drunkards made it Corinthe. The last of the dynasty, Father Hucheloup, painted the post blue. Taproom and kitchen below, billiard-room above reached by a spiral stair, the family's lodging on the second floor up a ladder, servants in the mansards, a trapdoor to the cellar. Hucheloup, cook rather than chemist, invented stuffed carps, carpes au gras, and advertised them on his wall in his own spelling, 'Carpes ho gras'; rain washed out an S and a G, leaving 'Carpe ho ras', so that knowing no French he had equalled Horace, and the advice also meant 'Enter my wine-shop'. All of it vanished when the Mondétour labyrinth was opened up in 1847.","Corinthe was the meeting-place of Courfeyrac and his friends; Grantaire discovered it, entering for the Carpe horas and returning for the carpes au gras. They ate, drank, shouted, paid badly or not at all, and were always welcome. Hucheloup, a moustached former fencing-master, growled at customers and then burst out laughing, a snuffbox shaped like a pistol. He died about 1830, taking the secret of the carps with him; his inconsolable widow, bearded and homely, kept on, the cooking became execrable and the bad wine worse, and the friends kept coming 'out of pity', as Bossuet said. Mother Hucheloup, breathless and misshapen, mangled her rustic reminiscences with her pronunciation. The first-floor room, lit by one narrow window and a lamp always burning, had three-legged furniture and a charcoal quatrain on the wall mocking the wart on her nose, before which she passed all day untroubled. Two maids, Matelote, large, plump, red-haired and noisy, the late Hucheloup's favourite, and Gibelotte, tall, pale, languid, first up and last to bed, waiting silently on everyone with a sleepy smile, served the poor wine and broth. On the door Courfeyrac had chalked: 'Régale si tu peux et mange si tu l'oses.'"],["Laigle de Meaux lives with Joly as a bird lives on a branch, the two sharing everything, even Musichetta to some extent. At nine on the morning of 5 June, Joly stuffed up with a cold, they climb to Corinthe's first floor and order oysters, cheese and ham from Matelote and Gibelotte. Grantaire's head appears at the hatchway, drawn, he says, by the smell of Brie; Gibelotte sets two bottles before him. He drinks while the others eat, teases Laigle about his old coat, and when Laigle marvels at how quiet the street is with Paris turned upside down and recalls the convents that once swarmed there, Grantaire launches a long harangue: a bad oyster has brought back his hypochondria; a pretty grisette he knows has just become a banker's mistress and looks no worse for it; right is Brennus taking Rome and crying 'Vae victis'; there is but one reality, drink. As for revolution, it proves only that God is in a quandary, patching events like a tradesman with an empty till giving a ball; creation is bankrupt; he should have been born a Turk. It is folly to massacre each other in June when they might be out in the hay.","Joly remarks that it is aberrant that Marius is in love, and Grantaire imagines the pair, souls who forget to kiss and lie among the stars. A ragged boy under ten climbs in and asks for Monsieur Bossuet: a tall blonde fellow on the boulevard paid him ten sous to say 'A.B.C.' Laigle borrows ten sous from each friend and gives the child twenty; he gives his name as Navet, Gavroche's friend, declines breakfast because he must shout 'Down with Polignac!' in the procession, and scrapes a bow. Laigle decodes A.B.C. as the burial of Lamarque and Grantaire names the blonde as Enjolras sending a warning. Joly will not go through water, Grantaire prefers a breakfast to a hearse, and Laigle concludes they stay: they may miss the funeral without missing the riot. Grantaire mutters that Enjolras disdains him and sent to Bossuet, not to him.","By two in the afternoon the table is covered with empty bottles and two candles burn in the gloom of the storm-dark room. Grantaire has passed from wine to the beer-glass, the abyss, mixing brandy, stout and absinthe; still gay, he proclaims that everyone shall be an Academician with the right to embrace Madame Hucheloup, and asks who has unhooked the stars to put them on the table as candles. Joly tells the maids to give him no more; he has drunk two francs ninety-five since morning. Bossuet, very drunk but composed, sits on the sill of the open window with his back in the rain and hears a tumult: in the Rue Saint-Denis at the end of the street pass Enjolras with his gun, Gavroche with his pistol, Feuilly and Courfeyrac with swords, Jean Prouvaire, Combeferre, Bahorel and the whole armed rabble. He makes a trumpet of his hands and bellows for Courfeyrac, who comes a few paces up the street and says they are going to make a barricade. 'Well, here! This is a good place! Make it here!' Courfeyrac agrees, and at his signal the mob pours into the Rue de la Chanvrerie."],["The spot is admirably suited: the street widens at its mouth and narrows into a pocket, Corinthe blocks the end, the Rue Mondétour is easily barricaded on either side, and attack is possible only from the Rue Saint-Denis, in front and in full view. Terror seizes the street; every shutter slams from ground floor to roof, and an old woman hangs a mattress before her window against musketry. Only the wine-shop stays open, because the mob has rushed into it; Mame Hucheloup sighs 'Ah my God!' Joly leans out to tell Courfeyrac he should have brought an umbrella.","In minutes twenty iron bars are wrenched from the wine-shop's grating and ten fathoms of street unpaved; Gavroche and Bahorel seize and overturn the dray of a lime-dealer named Anceau with its three barrels of lime; Enjolras raises the cellar trap and the widow's empty casks flank the lime; Feuilly, whose fingers paint fan-sticks, backs it all with heaps of rough stone, and beams torn from house-fronts are laid on the casks. Half the street is barred higher than a man before Bossuet and Courfeyrac turn round. Matelote and Gibelotte carry rubbish, Gibelotte serving the barricade as sleepily as she serves wine. An omnibus with two white horses passes the end of the street; Bossuet runs out, makes the passengers alight, hands down 'the ladies', dismisses the conductor and leads it back by the bridle, declaring in Latin that not everyone may go to Corinth. Unharnessed, the horses wander off down the Rue Mondétour, and the omnibus on its side completes the bar.","Mame Hucheloup takes refuge upstairs muttering that the end of the world has come; Joly kisses her fat red neck and tells Grantaire a woman's neck is an infinitely delicate thing. Grantaire, at the height of dithyramb, seizes Matelote round the waist and proclaims from the window that she is a chimaera born of a Gothic Pygmalion's love for a gargoyle, that she and the moustached Mother Hucheloup will fight like hussars and terrify the suburbs, that he understands only love and liberty, and that if kind hearts had fat purses there would be no more poor: he pictures Jesus Christ with Rothschild's fortune. Courfeyrac tells him to hold his tongue, 'you cask'. Enjolras, standing on the crest of the barricade gun in hand, raises his austere face and shouts at Grantaire to go and sleep off his wine elsewhere: this is the place for enthusiasm, not drunkenness, and he must not disgrace the barricade.","The rebuke works like cold water in the face. Suddenly sober, Grantaire sits at a table by the window, looks at Enjolras with indescribable gentleness and says 'Let me sleep here.' Enjolras tells him to sleep elsewhere. Grantaire, his tender troubled eyes still on him: 'Let me sleep here—until I die.' Enjolras, disdainful: 'Grantaire, you are incapable of believing, of thinking, of willing, of living, and of dying.' Grantaire gravely answers 'You will see', stammers a few words, drops his head on the table and is asleep within the instant."],["Bahorel exults that the street looks well in its low-necked dress. Courfeyrac, demolishing the wine-shop, reminds Mother Hucheloup of the hundred-franc fine she paid when Gibelotte shook a counterpane out of the window and a flowerpot fell into the street, and tells her they are avenging her, a consolation she finds as useful as the Arab father who, asked to avenge a slap on his daughter's left cheek, slapped her right one and sent her to tell her husband the score was even. The rain stops. Recruits bring a barrel of powder under their blouses, bottles of vitriol, carnival torches and fire-pots left over from the King's festival of 1 May, said to come from a grocer named Pépin in the Faubourg Saint-Antoine; they smash every street-lantern in the Rue de la Chanvrerie and the streets around.","Enjolras, Combeferre and Courfeyrac direct everything. Two barricades rise at right angles resting on the Corinthe house, the large one across the Rue de la Chanvrerie, a narrow one of casks and paving-stones across the Rue Mondétour towards the Rue du Cygne. About fifty men work, thirty of them armed from a wholesale loan on an armourer's shop: one in a round-jacket with a cavalry sabre and holster-pistols, one in shirtsleeves with a powder-horn, one in a breastplate of nine sheets of grey paper armed with a saddler's awl, one vowing to die at the point of a bayonet he does not possess, one wearing a National Guard's cartridge-box lettered 'Public Order'. All ages and faces, pale young men and bronzed longshoremen, brothers who do not know each other's names, discuss their chances: succour at three in the morning, a regiment that is sure, Paris rising. In the kitchen they melt the establishment's pewter mugs, spoons and forks into bullets; in the billiard-room Mame Hucheloup, Matelote and Gibelotte, variously stupefied, breathless and roused by terror, tear dishcloths into lint with three bushy-bearded insurgents.","The tall man noticed at the Rue des Billettes is at work on the smaller barricade, making himself useful. Gavroche works on the larger one. The young man who had waited for Courfeyrac and asked for Marius disappeared about the time the omnibus was overturned. Gavroche is a whirlwind, climbing, whistling, pricking a student here and biting an artisan there, a fly on the revolutionary coach, shouting for more paving-stones and casks and insisting that a glass door is excellent in a barricade because it cuts the corns of the National Guard. Furious at his triggerless pistol, he goes from man to man demanding a gun; he had one in 1830, he says, when they had a dispute with Charles X. Enjolras shrugs: when there are enough for the men they will give some to the children. Gavroche answers haughtily that if Enjolras is killed before him he will take his, and the two exchange 'Gamin!' and 'Greenhorn!' A lost dandy at the end of the street flees when Gavroche invites him to do something for his country."],["The newspapers that called the Rue de la Chanvrerie barricade a nearly impregnable structure reaching the first floor were wrong: it averaged six or seven feet, built so that defenders could hide behind it or mount its crest by a quadruple row of paving-stone steps inside. Outside, paving-stones and casks bound with beams and planks tangled in the wheels of Anceau's dray and the overturned omnibus gave it a bristling look. A gap wide enough for a man was left between the houses and the end furthest from the wine-shop; the omnibus pole stood upright roped in place, a red flag floating from it. The little Mondétour barricade, hidden behind the wine-shop, made the whole a redoubt. Enjolras and Courfeyrac deliberately left the other fragment of the Rue Mondétour, opening through the Rue des Prêcheurs into the Halles, unbarricaded, to keep a line to the outside, fearing no attack from that difficult street. Inside, an irregular square with twenty paces between the barrier and the tall, inhabited but tightly shut houses at the back.","All this is done in under an hour without a single bearskin or bayonet appearing; bourgeois who still venture into the Rue Saint-Denis glance down the street and hurry on. With the barricades finished and the flag up, a table is dragged out, Courfeyrac mounts it, Enjolras brings the square coffer and Courfeyrac opens it: cartridges. A tremor and a silence pass through the bravest. Courfeyrac distributes them with a smile, thirty to each man; many have powder and make more with the bullets they have cast. The barrel of powder is set on a table by the door in reserve.","The alarm beating through Paris has become a monotonous noise they no longer heed, receding and returning in melancholy undulations. They load guns and carbines together, without haste, with solemn gravity. Enjolras posts three sentinels outside: in the Rue de la Chanvrerie, in the Rue des Prêcheurs, and at the corner of the Rue de la Petite-Truanderie. Then they wait, alone in streets no one passes any more, among dumb houses that seem dead, as the twilight deepens and something can be felt advancing through the silence."],["Hugo asks what they did during those hours of waiting, and answers that he must tell, since it is history. While men mould bullets over a brazier and women make lint, while sentinels watch and Enjolras keeps an eye on the sentinels, Combeferre, Courfeyrac, Jean Prouvaire, Feuilly, Bossuet, Joly, Bahorel and some others seek each other out and sit together in a corner of the wine-shop turned casemate, loaded carbines leaning on their chairs, as in the most peaceful days of their student conversations, and begin to recite love verses.","The poem, murmured in a low voice in the dusk by Jean Prouvaire, the gentle poet, asks a woman whether she remembers their sweet life when they were both so young that their ages together did not make forty, when he was a briefless lawyer taking her to dine at the Prado and the roses seemed to turn to look at her; the garret in the Latin Quarter where the Sorbonne was their pastoral spot, the Place Maubert and the Place Dauphine, a flower she gave him that proved celestial goodness better than Malebranche or Lamennais, her burnt muff and lost boa, the portrait of Shakespeare sold one evening for supper, a Dante in folio for a table to eat a hundred chestnuts, the first kiss that left him pale and believing in God, and all the sighs from hearts full of shadow flown into the deep heavens. The hour, the place, the stars beginning to show, the funereal calm of the deserted streets and the imminence of the inexorable adventure give the verses a pathetic charm.","Meanwhile a lamp is lit in the small barricade, and in the large one a wax torch of the kind seen on Shrove Tuesday before carts of masks bound for la Courtille, brought from the Faubourg Saint-Antoine. It is set in a cage of paving-stones closed on three sides against the wind and arranged so that all its light falls on the flag; street and barricade stay sunk in gloom, and nothing is visible but the red flag, lit as if by an enormous dark-lantern, its scarlet deepened to a terrible purple."],["Night is fully come and only distant, scattered fusillades are heard; the government is taking its time. Fifty men are waiting for sixty thousand. Enjolras, impatient, goes to find Gavroche, who is making cartridges in the taproom by two candles on the counter. Gavroche is preoccupied, but not with his cartridges: the man of the Rue des Billettes has just come in with the large musket that fell to his share and sat down at the least lighted table, and the gamin, following him with his eyes, has sprung to his feet. The man had been observing everything in the barricade with singular attention until he entered this room, where he has fallen into a brown study. Gavroche circles him on tiptoe, his face running through every grimace of disbelief, uncertainty and conviction; a great event has plainly happened in his life.","Enjolras tells him he is small and will not be seen: slip out, skirt the houses, skirmish about the streets and come back to report. Gavroche agrees, says the little chaps are good for something, then lowers his voice and nods at the tall man: 'He's a police spy.' Asked whether he is sure, he says it is not two weeks since the man pulled him off the cornice of the Pont Royal by the ear. Enjolras whispers to a longshoreman, who returns with three more broad-shouldered porters; they take their places behind the stranger's table. Enjolras asks him who he is. The man plunges his gaze into Enjolras's clear eyes, smiles with disdainful resolution and says 'I see what it is. Well, yes!' 'You are a police spy?' 'I am an agent of the authorities.' 'And your name?' 'Javert.'","At a sign the four porters collar him, throw him down, pinion and search him before he can turn round. They find a small round card between two pieces of glass bearing the arms of France, the motto 'Supervision and vigilance', the note 'Javert, inspector of police, aged fifty-two' and the signature of the Prefect Gisquet, plus his watch and purse of gold, which they leave him, and under the watch an envelope in the Prefect's own hand ordering Inspector Javert, once his political mission is done, to verify by special supervision whether malefactors have intrigues on the right bank near the Jena bridge. They bind his arms behind him and tie him to the famous post in the middle of the room that once gave the wine-shop its name. Gavroche steps up: 'It's the mouse who has caught the cat.' Javert has not uttered a cry.","Courfeyrac, Bossuet, Joly, Combeferre and the men from both barricades come running at the sight. Javert, roped to the post, raises his head with the serenity of a man who has never lied. Enjolras tells him he will be shot ten minutes before the barricade is taken. 'Why not at once?' 'We are saving our powder.' 'Then finish the business with a blow from a knife.' 'Spy, we are judges and not assassins.' Enjolras sends Gavroche about his errand; the gamin asks for the spy's gun as he goes, saying he leaves them the musician but wants the clarinet, gives a military salute, and passes out through the gap in the large barricade."],["Hugo will not omit an incident of epic and savage horror that occurred just after Gavroche left. Among the passers-by who joined the mob was a man in a threadbare porter's jacket, drunk or feigning it, called Le Cabuc, a stranger even to those who claimed to know him. Drinking at a table dragged outside the wine-shop, he eyes the five-storey house at the end of the barricade commanding the street towards the Rue Saint-Denis and declares they must fire from there. The door is shut; he knocks three times with the massive knocker, shouts, then batters it with a gun-butt, but it is an old oak alley door lined with iron, a prison postern. At last a small window opens on the third floor and a grey-haired old porter with a candle asks what the gentlemen want. 'Open!' 'That cannot be, gentlemen.' Le Cabuc levels his gun in the dark; the porter, who cannot see him, says no again, and the shot takes him under the chin and out at the nape. The candle falls, and a motionless head lies on the sill with a little white smoke drifting up.","'There!' says Le Cabuc, grounding his gun; and a hand like an eagle's talon falls on his shoulder with the words 'On your knees.' It is Enjolras, pistol in hand, pale, bare-necked, with his woman's face and the look of antique Themis. The frail young man of twenty bends the sturdy porter like a reed to his knees in the mire. The whole barricade gathers in a circle, speechless. Enjolras releases him, takes out his watch, and says 'Collect yourself. Think or pray. You have one minute.' Le Cabuc murmurs 'Mercy!' and stammers oaths. The minute passes; Enjolras pockets his watch, grasps him by the hair as he shrieks and coils at his knees, puts the muzzle to his ear, and fires. Many turn their heads away. He spurns the corpse with his foot: 'Throw that outside.' Three men fling the body, still twitching, over the little barricade into the Rue Mondétour.","Enjolras tells the citizens that what that man did was frightful and what he has done is horrible: he killed, therefore Enjolras killed him; insurrection must have its discipline, assassination is more of a crime here than anywhere since they are the priests of the Republic under the eyes of the Revolution, so he tried the man and condemned him, and has judged himself also, and they will soon see to what he has condemned himself. Combeferre cries 'We will share thy fate.' Enjolras adds that necessity is a monster of the old world named Fatality, that the law of progress is for Fatality to vanish before Fraternity, that he pronounces and glorifies the word love even at this bad moment, that he uses death but hates it, and that the day will come when the earth beams and the human race loves: 'it is in order that it may come that we are about to die.' He stands in marble immobility; Jean Prouvaire and Combeferre press hands and watch him with admiration mixed with compassion, executioner and priest, crystal and rock.","Hugo adds that when the bodies were searched at the morgue after the action a police agent's card was found on Le Cabuc, and that he himself held the 1832 report on it in 1848. A strange but probably well-founded police tradition says Le Cabuc was Claquesous; from Le Cabuc's death there was no more question of Claquesous, who left no trace of his disappearance, his life all shadows and his end night. While the barricade is still under the emotion of this swift trial, Courfeyrac again notices the small young man who had asked him for Marius that morning, who has come by night to join the insurgents with a bold and reckless air."],["The voice through the twilight has the effect of the voice of destiny: Marius wished to die, and a hand in the darkness offers the key to the tomb. Mad with grief after two months of youth and love, he says 'I will go', sets out at a rapid pace, and finds himself opportunely armed with Javert's pistols. The young man he thought he glimpsed has vanished.","He takes the boulevard, the Esplanade and bridge of the Invalides, the Champs-Élysées and the Place Louis XV to the Rue de Rivoli, where shops are open, gas burns under the arcades, women shop and people eat ices, and only a few post-chaises gallop from the hotels. Through the Passage Delorme into the Rue Saint-Honoré the shops are shut but merchants chat at their doors and windows are lit; cavalry stands on the Place du Palais-Royal. Further on the lights thin, the crowd thickens, and a dull deep murmur rises from people who do not speak. Near the Arbre-Sec fountain stand motionless gloomy groups; at the Rue des Prouvaires the crowd no longer walks but forms a solid block of blouses and caps whispering in the dark, and beyond it no window is lit, only lanterns hanging like red stars casting spider shadows, and the gleam of bayonets and bivouacking troops. There the rabble ends and the army begins.","Marius wills with the will of a man who hopes no more. He threads the throng, passes the bivouac, shuns patrols and sentinels, circles by the Rue de Béthisy towards the Halles; at the Rue des Bourdonnais the lanterns stop. Beyond the troops he enters something startling: no passer-by, no soldier, no light, a chill, each street like a cellar. Someone runs past him unseen. In what he judges the Rue de la Poterie he stumbles on an overturned wagon and scattered paving-stones, a barricade begun and abandoned, and climbs over. Something white ahead becomes two white horses, the omnibus horses Bossuet unharnessed that morning, straying all day with the patience of brutes. Near the Rue du Contrat-Social a shot from nowhere whistles past and pierces a brass shaving-dish hanging over a hairdresser's shop, a dish still to be seen there in 1848. After that he meets nothing; the whole route is like a descent of black steps, and he presses on."],["A being hovering over Paris that night with an owl's wing would have seen the whole old quarter of the Halles, a city within a city threaded by the Rues Saint-Denis and Saint-Martin, as an enormous dark cavity in the centre of Paris. Lanterns broken and windows shut, the insurrection's invisible police keep order, that is, night: at dusk every window with a candle received a shot, so that nothing stirs and the houses hold only fright, mourning and stupor. Here and there indistinct gleams betray a barricade; above the lake of funereal obscurity rise the tower of Saint-Jacques, the church of Saint-Merry and a few more giants that night makes phantoms. All round, where street lamps still burn, the observer would see the metallic gleam of swords and bayonets, the rumble of artillery and silent battalions swelling minute by minute: a formidable girdle slowly tightening round the insurrection.","The invested quarter is a monstrous cavern full of traps, where those who enter shiver before those who wait and those who wait shudder before those who come. Nothing more is to be hoped for but the lightning of guns and the abrupt apparition of death, where and when no one knows. Government and insurrection, National Guard and popular societies, bourgeois and uprising are groping towards contact; for one side to advance is death and no one thinks of retreat, for the other to remain is death and no one thinks of flight. All must be ended tomorrow: the insurrection must prove itself a revolution or a skirmish, and every bourgeois feels it. The only sound is the tocsin of Saint-Merry, as heartrending as a death rattle and menacing as a curse.","Nature accords with men: the stars have gone, heavy clouds fill the horizon, and a black sky rests on the dead streets like a winding-sheet over a tomb. While a battle still wholly political prepares, youth and the schools in the name of principles against the middle classes in the name of interests, far outside the fatal quarter, in the depths of wretched old Paris beneath the splendour of happy Paris, the sombre voice of the people gives a dull roar, a voice composed of the roar of the brute and the word of God, from below like the lion and from on high like the thunder."],["The Halles are calmer, darker and more motionless than the surrounding streets, as if the peace of the sepulchre had spread from earth to sky. A red glow on the tall roofs at the Saint-Eustache end of the Rue de la Chanvrerie is the torch of the Corinthe barricade. Marius follows it to the Marché-aux-Poirées, enters the dark mouth of the Rue des Prêcheurs unseen by the insurgents' sentinel at the far end, and on tiptoe reaches the elbow of the short fragment of the Rue Mondétour that Enjolras left as his only link to the outside. From the corner of the last house he sees light on the pavement, a bit of the wine-shop, a flickering lamp within a shapeless wall, and men crouching with guns on their knees, ten fathoms off: the interior of the barricade. The houses on his right hide the rest.","He has one step more to take. Instead he sits on a post, folds his arms, and thinks of his father: Colonel Pontmercy, who guarded France's frontier under the Republic and touched Asia's under Napoleon, who saw Genoa, Madrid, Vienna, Moscow, grew grey in discipline, and came home after twenty years scarred, smiling and pure as a child, having done everything for France and nothing against her. Marius tells himself his own hour has struck, that he too will run to meet bullets and seek death, and that his field of battle is the street and his war is civil war. He shudders. He thinks of his father's sword, sold by his grandfather to a dealer, and decides it did well to flee: coming from Marengo and Friedland it would not go to the Rue de la Chanvrerie, would have scorched his hands rather than wound its country's side; his grandfather was its true guardian. He weeps bitterly.","Yet what can he do? He cannot live without Cosette; he gave her his word he would die; she left without a word or letter though she knew his address, so she no longer loves him. Should he slink away after peeping in, abandon the friends expecting him, a handful against an army, and make patriotism the pretext for cowardice? His father's ghost would beat him with the flat of his sword and shout 'March on, you poltroon!' Then a splendid rectification comes over his mind, the clear sight that nearness to death gives. Why should his father be indignant? There are cases where insurrection rises to the dignity of duty. This is no longer sacred territory but a holy idea; France bleeds but liberty smiles. All war between men is war between brothers; there is only just and unjust war, and war is a disgrace only when it assassinates right and progress. Washington's sword need not disown Camille Desmoulins's pike. Street war was the war of Ambiorix and Pelagius against the foreigner, and the monarchy is a foreigner, divine right a stranger: driving out the tyrant or the English is the same reconquest of one's own territory. After philosophy, action; after Diderot, Danton. Louis Philippe is a good king, like Louis XVI, but principles are not parcelled out, and when the master falls in France he falls everywhere. To conquer at Austerlitz is grand; to take the Bastille is immense.","Hugo notes the soul's strange aptitude for cold reasoning in the most violent extremity, the thread of the syllogism floating unbroken in the storm. Dejected but resolute, still shuddering, Marius lets his glance stray into the barricade, where the insurgents talk low in the quasi-silence of the last stage of expectation. Overhead, at a small third-floor window, he sees a spectator who seems singularly attentive: the porter killed by Le Cabuc, his livid astonished face, bristling hair, fixed eyes and gaping mouth bent over the street as if the dead were surveying those about to die, a long trail of blood descending in reddish threads from the window to the first floor."],["Nothing has come yet when ten strikes from Saint-Merry. Enjolras and Combeferre sit with their carbines near the outlet of the large barricade, listening. Out of the dismal calm a clear, gay young voice from the direction of the Rue Saint-Denis sings a mocking verse about his friend Bugeaud's gendarmes and the suburbs in their blue capotes, to the tune of 'By the Light of the Moon', ending in a cock's crow. 'That is Gavroche,' says Enjolras; 'He is warning us,' says Combeferre. A hasty rush, and Gavroche climbs over the omnibus into the barricade, breathless: 'My gun! Here they are!' Enjolras offers his carbine; Gavroche wants a big gun, and seizes Javert's. Two sentinels fall back with him; the vidette of the Rue des Prêcheurs stays at his post, so nothing is coming from the Halles side.","Each man takes his position. Forty-three insurgents, among them Enjolras, Combeferre, Courfeyrac, Bossuet, Joly, Bahorel and Gavroche, kneel inside the large barricade with heads level with its crest and gun-barrels laid on the stones as in loopholes; six under Feuilly man the windows of the two storeys of Corinthe. Then a sound of footsteps, measured, heavy and numerous, comes from the direction of Saint-Leu, approaching without halt with a tranquil and terrible continuity, like the stone tread of the Commander's statue multiplied into a legion. It stops. Many men seem to breathe at the end of the street, and in the dense dark a multitude of fine metallic threads moves like the phosphorescence under closed eyelids: bayonets and gun-barrels catching the torch's distant reflection.","A pause, as if both sides waited. Then a sinister voice, the gloom itself speaking, shouts 'Who goes there?' and guns click into position. Enjolras answers in a haughty, vibrating tone: 'The French Revolution!' 'Fire!' A flash empurples the house-fronts like a furnace door flung open and shut; a fearful detonation bursts on the barricade. The red flag falls, the staff cut at the very tip of the omnibus pole; bullets ricochet from cornices into the barricade and wound several men. The impression is freezing: they have an entire regiment at least to deal with. Courfeyrac shouts not to waste powder but to wait till they are in the street; Enjolras says above all they must raise the flag, and picks it up where it fell at his feet. Outside, ramrods clatter as the troops reload. Enjolras asks who has a bold heart to plant the flag on the barricade again. No one answers: to climb it now is simply death, and even Enjolras feels a thrill. He repeats: 'Does no one volunteer?'"],["No one has paid attention to Father Mabeuf since they reached Corinthe. He has sat behind the taproom counter, retreated into himself, not hearing Courfeyrac and the others who begged him to withdraw, his lips moving as if answering someone only when no one spoke to him. For hours he has kept one attitude, fists on knees, head thrust forward as if gazing over a precipice. When the men took their posts, only he and a single insurgent guarding Javert with a drawn sword remained in the taproom. The shock of the volley wakes him; he crosses the room, and at the instant Enjolras repeats 'Does no one volunteer?' the old man appears on the threshold. A shout goes up: 'It is the voter! It is the member of the Convention! It is the representative of the people!' He probably does not hear.","He walks straight to Enjolras, who recoils in amazement as the old man tears the flag from him, and since no one dares stop or help him, this man of eighty with shaking head and firm foot slowly climbs the paving-stone steps of the barricade. All around cry 'Off with your hats!' At every step his white hair, decrepit face, bald wrinkled brow and open mouth, his aged arm upholding the red banner, rise through the gloom enlarged in the bloody light of the torch, and the bystanders think they see the spectre of '93 rising from the earth with the flag of terror. On the last step, erect on the pile before twelve hundred invisible guns, he seems more powerful than death, and the whole barricade takes on a supernatural, colossal form. In the silence he waves the flag and shouts: 'Long live the Revolution! Long live the Republic! Fraternity! Equality! and Death!'","A low rapid whisper is heard from the far end of the street, probably the commissary of police making the legal summons; then the piercing voice shouts 'Retire!' Mabeuf, pale and haggard, eyes lit with the mournful flame of aberration, raises the flag above his head and repeats 'Long live the Republic!' 'Fire!' A second discharge rains on the barricade. The old man falls on his knees, rises, drops the flag and falls backwards full length on the pavement with outstretched arms, rivulets of blood beneath him, his pale sad face gazing at the sky. The insurgents, seized by an emotion that makes them forget to defend themselves, approach the body with awe.","'What men these regicides were!' says Enjolras. Courfeyrac whispers in his ear that this man was anything rather than a regicide: he knew him, his name was Father Mabeuf, he does not know what was the matter with him today, but he was a brave blockhead; just look at his head. 'The head of a blockhead and the heart of a Brutus,' Enjolras answers, and raises his voice: this is the example the old give to the young; they hesitated and he came, they drew back and he advanced; he has had a long life and a magnificent death, and each must defend this dead old man as he would his living father, so that his presence renders the barricade impregnable. A murmur of gloomy assent follows. Enjolras lifts the old man's head, kisses his brow, removes his coat with tender care, shows everyone the bloody holes in it, and says: 'This is our flag now.'"],["They throw Widow Hucheloup's long black shawl over Father Mabeuf, make a litter of six guns, and carry him bareheaded with solemn slowness to the big table in the taproom, wholly absorbed in the sacred task and forgetting their peril. As the corpse passes Javert, still impassive at his post, Enjolras says to the spy: 'It will be your turn presently!'","Little Gavroche alone has not left his post. He thinks he sees men stealthily approaching, and shouts 'Look out!' Courfeyrac, Enjolras, Jean Prouvaire, Combeferre, Joly, Bahorel, Bossuet and the rest run tumultuously out of the wine-shop, almost too late: a glistening density of bayonets undulates above the barricade, and tall Municipal Guards are coming in, some striding over the omnibus, others through the cut, driving the urchin before them, who retreats but does not flee. It is the moment when the flood reaches the top of the levee; a second more and the barricade is taken.","Bahorel dashes at the first guard to enter and kills him with a blow of his gun; the second kills Bahorel with his bayonet. Another has already thrown down Courfeyrac, who is shouting 'Follow me!' The largest of all, a colossus, marches on Gavroche with fixed bayonet. The child levels Javert's huge gun resolutely at the giant and pulls the trigger: nothing. Javert's gun was not loaded. The guardsman laughs and raises his bayonet over the child. Before it touches him the gun slips from the soldier's grasp and he falls on his back with a bullet in the centre of his forehead; a second bullet strikes the guard who assaulted Courfeyrac full in the breast and lays him on the pavement. This is the work of Marius, who has just entered the barricade."],["Concealed in the turn of the Rue Mondétour, Marius has watched the first phase shuddering and irresolute, but cannot long resist the call of the abyss: the imminent peril, the death of M. Mabeuf, that melancholy enigma, Bahorel killed, Courfeyrac shouting 'Follow me!', the threatened child, friends to succour or avenge. He flings himself in with his two pistols, saving Gavroche with the first shot and delivering Courfeyrac with the second. Amid the shots the assailants, Municipal Guards, soldiers of the line and suburban National Guards, climb the entrenchment and cover two-thirds of it, but hesitate to leap into the dark enclosure as into a lion's den, the torch lighting only their bayonets, bearskins and angry faces.","Marius, weaponless, has flung away his discharged pistols, but has seen the barrel of powder by the taproom door. As he half turns towards it a soldier takes aim at him. A hand is laid over the muzzle: someone has darted forward, the young workman in velvet trousers. The shot goes through the hand and perhaps through the workman too, who falls, but the ball misses Marius, who entering the taproom barely registers the levelled gun, the hand that blocked it and the report; in such moments everything vacillates and one pauses for nothing.","The insurgents rally. Enjolras shouts not to fire at random; most climb to the first-floor windows and attics commanding the assailants, while the most determined, Enjolras, Courfeyrac, Jean Prouvaire and Combeferre, set their backs to the rear houses, unsheltered, facing the soldiers crowning the barricade, so close they could talk without raising their voices. An officer in a gorget extends his sword: 'Lay down your arms!' 'Fire!' answers Enjolras. Both volleys go off at once and all vanishes in acrid smoke; when it clears both sides are thinned but in place, reloading in silence. Then a thundering voice: 'Be off with you, or I'll blow up the barricade!'","Marius has seized the barrel of powder, slipped through the smoke to the cage of paving-stones holding the torch, torn out the torch, set the barrel in its place and thrust the stones under it, staving it in, all in the time it takes to stoop and rise. Now he stands with one foot on the stones and the torch in his hand, his face lit by fatal resolution, lowering the flame towards the broken barrel, while guards, officers and soldiers huddled at the far end of the barricade stare at him: Marius on that barricade after the octogenarian, the young revolution after the apparition of the old. A sergeant says: 'Blow up the barricade, and yourself with it!' 'And myself also,' Marius answers, and drops the torch towards the powder. But the barrier is already empty: the assailants have abandoned their dead and wounded and fled pell-mell into the night. The barricade is free."],["All flock round Marius; Courfeyrac flings himself on his neck and says that but for him he would be dead, Gavroche that he would have been gobbled up. Marius asks where the chief is. 'You are he!' says Enjolras. The furnace in Marius's brain has become a whirlwind: his two luminous months, Cosette lost, the barricade, M. Mabeuf killed for the Republic, himself the leader of the insurgents, all seem a nightmare he must make an effort to believe real. In this mist he does not recognise Javert, bound to his post, who has not moved his head during the attack and watches the revolt with the resignation of a martyr and the majesty of a judge; Marius has not even seen him.","The assailants do not stir, swarming at the end of the street but not venturing in, awaiting orders or reinforcements. Sentinels are posted; medical students among the insurgents tend the wounded. All the tables except the two for lint and cartridges and the one bearing Father Mabeuf are thrown onto the barricade and replaced by mattresses from the beds of the widow Hucheloup and her servants, on which the wounded are laid. The three women of Corinthe, whom no one could find, turn up hidden in the cellar.","A poignant emotion clouds the joy of the cleared barricade. The roll is called: one man is missing, one of the dearest and most valiant, Jean Prouvaire. He is not among the wounded nor the dead; he is evidently a prisoner. Combeferre says to Enjolras, near Javert's post: they have our friend, we have their agent; are you set on the death of that spy? 'Yes, but less so than on the life of Jean Prouvaire.' Combeferre proposes to tie his handkerchief to his cane and go as a flag of truce to offer the exchange. Enjolras lays a hand on his arm: 'Listen.' At the end of the street there is a clash of arms, and a manly voice shouts 'Vive la France! Long live France! Long live the future!' They recognise Prouvaire's voice. A flash, a report, silence. 'They have killed him,' cries Combeferre. Enjolras looks at Javert and says: 'Your friends have just shot you.'"],["Since barricades are almost always attacked from the front, all attention is on the grand barricade; but Marius thinks of the little one and finds it deserted, guarded only by the fire-pot trembling between the stones, the Mondétour lane and its branches profoundly calm. As he withdraws he hears his name pronounced feebly in the dark, in the voice that called him two hours earlier through the gate in the Rue Plumet, now no more than a breath. 'At your feet,' it says. He sees a form dragging itself along the pavement: a blouse, torn velvet trousers, bare feet, what looks like a pool of blood, and a pale lifted head asking whether he does not recognise her. 'Éponine.' It is that unhappy child, dressed as a man.","She says she is dying. He cries that he will carry her inside to be tended and tries to lift her; she gives a feeble cry, and shows him a black hole in the middle of her hand. 'Did you see a gun aimed at you?' 'Yes, and a hand stopping it.' 'It was mine.' Marius calls it madness and says one does not die of a pierced hand; she answers that the bullet went through her hand and out through her back, that moving her is useless, and that he can care for her better than any surgeon by sitting beside her on the stone. He obeys; she lays her head on his knees and says she no longer suffers. Without looking at him she admits it puzzled her that he entered that garden, stupidly, since she herself showed him the house; then, with a heartrending smile: 'You thought me ugly, didn't you?'","She tells him he is lost, that no one can get out of the barricade now, that it was she who led him here and she counts on his dying, and yet when she saw them aiming at him she put her hand on the muzzle, because she wanted to die before him. After the bullet she dragged herself here unseen and waited for him, biting her blouse with the pain. She recalls the day she looked in his mirror and the day on the boulevard by the washerwomen when the birds sang and she refused his hundred sous, and hopes he picked up the coin. 'Oh! How happy I am! Everyone is going to die.' Blood spurts from a second hole in her breast under her pressed hand. Gavroche's cock-crow rings out as he loads his gun on a table singing a song about Lafayette and the gendarme; she listens and murmurs 'It is he. My brother is here. He must not see me. He would scold me.' Marius, who has been thinking bitterly of the duties to the Thénardiers his father bequeathed him, asks who her brother is: that little fellow, the one singing.","She says she does not wish to play him a trick: she has a letter for him in her pocket, which she was told to post and kept, not wanting it to reach him, but perhaps he would be angry with her when they meet again presently. She puts his hand in her blouse pocket; he takes the letter, and she makes a sign of contentment. For her trouble she asks a promise: to give her a kiss on her brow when she is dead; she will feel it. Her head drops on his knees and her eyes close; he thinks her gone. Then she opens them, with the sombre depth of death in them, and says in a voice that already seems to come from another world: 'And by the way, Monsieur Marius, I believe that I was a little bit in love with you.' She tries to smile once more and expires."],["Marius keeps his promise and kisses the livid, sweat-beaded brow, a pensive farewell to an unhappy soul and no infidelity to Cosette. He feels at once that the letter is an event of weight, lays Éponine gently down, and reads it by a taproom candle. It is a small note sealed with a woman's care, addressed to Monsieur Marius Pontmercy at M. Courfeyrac's, 16 Rue de la Verrerie: her father insists on leaving immediately; this evening they will be at No. 7 Rue de l'Homme Armé; in a week they will be in England. Signed Cosette, 4 June. Such was the innocence of their love that Marius did not even know her handwriting.","Hugo explains in a few words. Since the night of 3 June Éponine had two aims: to defeat her father and the ruffians at the Rue Plumet, and to separate Marius and Cosette. She swapped rags with a young scamp who found it amusing to dress as a woman, and in man's clothes conveyed to Jean Valjean on the Champ-de-Mars the warning 'Leave your house.' Valjean came home and told Cosette they would leave that evening for the Rue de l'Homme Armé with Toussaint and be in London next week. Cosette, overwhelmed, wrote a couple of lines to Marius, but could not post them herself and dared not trust Toussaint, who would show them to M. Fauchelevent; through the fence she saw the 'young workman' prowling, called to him, and gave him five francs and the letter to carry at once. Éponine pocketed it. On the 5th she went to Courfeyrac's asking for Marius, not to deliver it but 'to see'; when Courfeyrac said they were going to the barricades, the idea flashed on her to fling herself into that death and thrust Marius into it too. She followed, made sure of the place, then went to the Rue Plumet, certain he would come at dusk since he had had no warning, and sent him the summons in his friends' name, counting on his despair at finding Cosette gone. She died with the tragic joy of jealous hearts who say 'No one shall have him!'","Marius covers Cosette's letter with kisses: so she loves him. For a moment he thinks he ought not to die now; then he reflects that she is going to England and his grandfather refuses the marriage, so nothing in their fates is changed, and despair returns. Two duties remain: to tell Cosette of his death with a last farewell, and to save from the coming catastrophe that poor child, Éponine's brother and Thénardier's son. He tears a leaf from his pocketbook and writes in pencil that their marriage was impossible, his grandfather refused, neither of them has a fortune, he hastened to her and she was gone, he keeps his promise and dies, he loves her, and when she reads this his soul will be near her and she will smile; he folds it unsealed and addresses it to Mademoiselle Cosette Fauchelevent, 7 Rue de l'Homme Armé. On the first page of the pocketbook he writes: 'My name is Marius Pontmercy. Carry my body to my grandfather, M. Gillenormand, Rue des Filles-du-Calvaire, No. 6, in the Marais.'","He calls Gavroche, who runs up devotedly, saying that but for Marius he would have been done for, and asks him to take the letter, leave the barricade instantly, and deliver it tomorrow morning to Mademoiselle Cosette at M. Fauchelevent's. Gavroche scratches his ear: meanwhile the barricade will be taken and he will not be there. Marius says it will not be attacked before daybreak nor taken before noon, and that tomorrow will be too late because the streets will be blockaded. Gavroche stands undecided, then snatches the letter with a birdlike movement, says 'All right', and runs off through the Mondétour lane. His unspoken idea: it is barely midnight, the Rue de l'Homme Armé is not far, he will deliver the letter at once and be back in time."],["Hugo turns from the convulsions of the city to the insurrection of a soul: Jean Valjean, at that very moment, is on the brink of his own obscure revolution. On the evening before 5 June he installed himself with Cosette and Toussaint in the Rue de l'Homme Armé. Cosette had resisted for the first time in their life together, objections on one side and inflexibility on the other; the stranger's 'Leave your house' had made him peremptory, convinced he was traced. They arrived without a word, he too uneasy to notice her sadness, she too sad to notice his unease. He brought Toussaint, who is not curious ('I do my work; the rest is no affair of mine'), and carried away only the little embalmed valise Cosette calls 'the inseparable', since full trunks need porters and porters are witnesses; Cosette brought only her portfolio and blotting-book. They left at dusk, which gave Cosette time to write her note, and went to bed in silence in a second-floor back apartment of two bedrooms, a dining-room and kitchen, with a garret for Toussaint.","Confidence returns as foolishly as it goes. The narrow, silent old street, barred to carriages by a beam, soothes him; he sleeps well with the inseparable beside him and wakes almost gay, finding the hideous dining-room charming. Cosette keeps to her room all day with broth and a headache, glances at a cold chicken at five out of deference, and shuts herself up again. Valjean eats a wing with appetite, barely hears Toussaint stammer that they are fighting in Paris, and paces the room growing serene, arranging in his mind a few months in London with Cosette, who is his nation and suffices for his happiness; the idea that he might not suffice for hers does not even present itself. Then his glance meets, in the inclined mirror over the sideboard, four lines: 'My dearest, alas! my father insists on our setting out immediately. We shall be this evening in the Rue de l'Homme Armé, No. 7. In a week we shall be in England. Cosette. June 4th.' Cosette had left her blotting-book open on the sideboard at the page where she dried the note she gave the young workman; the mirror rights the reversed imprint.","He reads it again and does not believe it; examines the scrawl on the blotter, finds no sense in it, and breathes with foolish relief; then looks at the mirror again and understands. He totters into the old armchair, head drooping, telling himself the light of the world is eclipsed forever and Cosette has written that to someone, and hears his soul give a dull roar in the gloom. Chance has brought Marius's letter to him before Marius has received Cosette's. Hugo says that of all his trials this is the most terrible, the loss of the beloved being; that Valjean loves Cosette as a father, but a father forged from grandfather, son, brother and husband, with even a mother included, and that into this paternity the widowhood of his life has let in every shade of love. Seeing her slip from him like a cloud, seeing that another is the goal of her heart and he is nothing but her father, the 'I' in this man's abyss howls, and he feels the immense reawakening of egotism. He recognises the precipice he feared last summer: he is no longer on its brink but at its bottom, and had fallen without perceiving it.","His instinct does not hesitate: putting together dates, blushes and pallors, he says 'It is he', and strikes on the unknown prowler of the Luxembourg, that idler of romance, that coward who made eyes at a girl with a loving father beside her. And he, the regenerated man who laboured so long to turn all misery into love, looks into his breast and sees a spectre: Hate. Toussaint enters; he asks in what quarter the fighting is, and she says towards Saint-Merry. Five minutes later, by a movement he is hardly conscious of, he is in the street, sitting bareheaded on the stone post at his door, seeming to listen. Night has come."],["How long he sits there, whether he can still regain his footing in his conscience, he could not have said. The street is deserted; a few bourgeois hurry home; the lamplighter lights the lantern opposite No. 7 and goes. Valjean sits motionless as ice while alarm bells and uproar sound in the distance and the clock of Saint-Paul strikes eleven, for the tocsin is man and the hour is God. Then a brusque report from the direction of the Halles, and a second more violent, probably the attack on the Rue de la Chanvrerie barricade repulsed by Marius; he starts up, turns towards the noise, falls back on the post with folded arms and sinking head, and resumes his gloomy dialogue with himself.","Footsteps: by the lantern-light he sees a young, livid, beaming face. Gavroche has arrived in the Rue de l'Homme Armé, staring up at the house-fronts and feeling the bolted doors and windows of five or six barricaded houses, then shrugging: 'Pardi!' Valjean, who a moment ago would have spoken to no one, feels impelled to ask what is the matter with him. 'The matter with me is that I am hungry. Little fellow yourself.' Valjean draws out a five-franc piece, but Gavroche has picked up a stone and noticed the lantern: they still have their lanterns here, which is disorderly, and he flings the stone and puts out the light, to cries from behind curtains that 'Ninety-three' has come again. He asks what that gigantic monument at the end of the street is, the Archives, and talks of crumbling its pillars into a barricade.","Valjean murmurs 'Poor creature, he is hungry' and lays the hundred-sou piece in his hand. Gavroche, who knows five-franc pieces only by hearsay, contemplates 'the tiger' in ecstasy, then holds it out again: he prefers to smash lanterns and cannot be bribed. 'Have you a mother?' 'More than you have, perhaps.' 'Well, keep the money for your mother!' Gavroche is touched, notices the man has no hat, which inspires confidence, pockets the coin, calls him a fine man, and asks whether he belongs in this street and where No. 7 is. Then he checks himself, fearing he has said too much. An idea flashes on Valjean: 'Are you the person who is bringing a letter that I am expecting?' 'You? You are not a woman.' 'The letter is for Mademoiselle Cosette, is it not?' 'Cosette. Yes, I believe that is the queer name.' Valjean says he is the person to whom it is to be delivered; Gavroche warns that then he must know it was sent from the barricade. 'Of course.'","Gavroche draws a paper folded in four from another pocket, salutes ('Respect for despatches. It comes from the Provisional Government'), holds it above his head and declares it is not a love letter but for the people, since men fight and respect the fair sex, decides the man has the air of an honest one, and hands it over, adding that he should make haste because Mamselle Cosette is waiting. Valjean asks whether the answer goes to Saint-Merry; Gavroche says he is making brioches, the letter comes from the barricade of the Rue de la Chanvrerie and he is going back there, and flutters away like an escaped bird into the dark. A few minutes later the crash of another lantern in the Rue du Chaume wakes the indignant bourgeois again."],["Valjean goes into the house with Marius's letter, gropes up the stairs as pleased with the dark as an owl gripping its prey, shuts his door softly, listens to be sure Cosette and Toussaint are asleep, and plunges three or four matches into the lighter before his trembling hand gets a spark. What he has just done smacks of theft. He unfolds the paper. In violent emotion one does not read, one seizes on one point; in Marius's note to Cosette he sees only: 'I die. When thou readest this, my soul will be near thee.'","Before those two lines he is horribly dazzled, crushed by the change taking place within him, staring at the note with intoxicated amazement at that splendour, the death of a hated individual. He utters a frightful cry of inward joy. So it is all over; the catastrophe has come sooner than he dared hope; the being who obstructed his destiny is disappearing freely, willingly, through no fault of his. His fever calculates: perhaps he is dead already; no, the letter was meant to be read tomorrow morning, nothing has happened since the two discharges between eleven and midnight, the barricade will not be seriously attacked before daybreak; but it makes no difference, from the moment 'that man' is in this war he is caught in the gearing and cannot escape. Valjean feels delivered: he will be alone with Cosette again, the rivalry will cease, the future begin anew. He need only keep the note in his pocket; Cosette will never know what became of that man. 'What good fortune!'","Having said all this to himself, he becomes gloomy. Then he goes downstairs and wakes the porter. About an hour later Jean Valjean goes out in the complete costume of a National Guard, the uniform glimpsed earlier in Toussaint's packages, with a loaded gun and a cartridge-box full of cartridges which the porter has easily found in the neighbourhood, and strides off in the direction of the markets."],["After stoning the lantern in the Rue du Chaume, Gavroche enters the deserted Rue des Vieilles-Haudriettes and strikes up a long ditty with the refrain 'Où vont les belles filles, lon la', listing Atala, Mila, Agnès, Paméla, Suzette, Zéila, Lola, Jeanne and Stella, accompanied by grimaces that no one sees in the dark. He stops short at an 'ensemble' in a carriage doorway: a handcart with its shafts on the pavement and an Auvergnat errand-man asleep drunk inside it. 'We'll take the cart for the Republic, and leave the Auvergnat for the Monarchy.' Picturing how bully the cart would look on the barricade, he tugs the cart from behind and the man by the feet until the Auvergnat lies flat on the pavement still snoring, writes with a carpenter's red pencil a receipt, 'French Republic. Received thy cart. Gavroche', tucks it in the sleeper's waistcoat pocket, and sets off towards the Halles pushing the cart at a hard gallop with a glorious uproar.","This is perilous: there is a post of suburban National Guards at the Royal Printing Establishment. For an hour the boy's broken lanterns and bawled song have been the uproar of a fly in a bottle to that peaceable quarter, and the prudent sergeant has been listening; the mad rattle of the cart decides him to reconnoitre, muttering that there must be a whole band. Turning into the Rue des Vieilles-Haudriettes Gavroche comes face to face with a uniform, shako, plume and gun. 'Hullo, it's him. Good day, public order.' To the sergeant's repeated 'Where are you going, you rascal, rogue, villain, bandit, wretch?' Gavroche answers that he has not yet called him bourgeois so why insult him, that he must have been a man of wit yesterday but has degenerated this morning, that he should sell his hairs at a hundred francs apiece for five hundred francs, and finally, as the bayonet lowers, that he is going to fetch a doctor for his wife who is in labour. 'To arms!' shouts the sergeant.","The cart that betrayed him must now protect him: Gavroche launches it with all his might, it strikes the sergeant full in the stomach and tumbles him into the gutter as his gun goes off in the air. The post rushes out, the shot sets off a general random discharge, and the blind-man's-buff musketry lasts a quarter of an hour and kills several panes of glass. Gavroche, having retraced his steps at full speed, sits panting on the corner post of the Enfants-Rouges five or six streets away, thumbs his nose three times at the fusillade, and worries that he is losing his way and must take a roundabout route: 'If I only reach the barricade in season!' He runs on, picks up his ditty where he left it, with new verses about remaining bastilles and Charles X coming unstuck, and his voice dies away in the gloom. The cart is impounded and the drunken Auvergnat later harassed before the councils of war as an accomplice; the affair lingers in the Marais as 'The nocturnal attack by the post of the Royal Printing Establishment'."],["Hugo begins Volume V with a digression he insists is not one. The two most memorable barricades he knows belong not to 1832 but to June 1848, when, in the greatest street war in history, the rabble rose against the people and the republic itself had to be fought in the name of the republic. He pronounces the words beggars, canaille and populace with pain and respect: the populace saved Rome, the rabble followed Christ, and when the suffering crowd attacks the very principles that are its life, the honest man must resist it with a sad conscience.","The Saint-Antoine barricade was three storeys high and seven hundred feet wide, built of demolished houses, carts, an omnibus hoisted to the summit, doors, gratings and rags, with nineteen more barricades ranged behind it. It converted everything into a weapon, firing coat-buttons and bits of crockery, crowned with a red flag and a tumult of shouts and songs: a heap of filth and a Sinai. A quarter of a league away the barricade of the Faubourg du Temple was its opposite, a straight wall of paving-stones laid out by rule and line, pierced with invisible loopholes, in a street where nothing moved and every passer-by fell to an unseen marksman. Eighty men held it for three days against ten thousand; when it was taken through the houses and over the roofs, all died but their leader.","The first was a dragon, the second a sphinx. Hugo names their builders, Cournet, a huge, stormy former naval officer with something of Danton in him, and Barthélemy, a thin, pale street urchin who had killed a policeman at seventeen and come out of the galleys to build this wall. He adds their epilogue: years later in London Barthélemy killed Cournet in a duel and was afterwards hanged, a man of real intelligence whom destitution carried from the French galleys to the English gallows."],["Compared with the giants of 1848, the barricade of the Rue de la Chanvrerie was an embryo, but it was formidable for its day. Under Enjolras, since Marius no longer attended to anything, the insurgents used the night well: the wall was raised two feet and bristled with iron bars, the paving-stone staircase rebuilt, the taproom cleared, the kitchen turned into an ambulance, bullets cast and cartridges made. The dead were heaped in the Mondétour lane; among them were four suburban National Guardsmen, and Enjolras had their uniforms set aside. Feuilly spent the two hours of ordered rest carving \\"Long live the peoples!\\" on the wall with a nail. The three women of the house had slipped away to a neighbouring house.","Five badly wounded men, two of them municipal guards, lay on mattresses in the kitchen, the guards attended to first. In the taproom only Mabeuf under his black cloth and Javert bound to his post remained; Enjolras called it the hall of the dead, and the upright prisoner and the prone corpse made a kind of cross in the candlelight. Enjolras fastened the old man's bullet-riddled coat to the broken omnibus pole as the flag. There was nothing to eat; Enjolras forbade wine and rationed the brandy, and fifteen sealed bottles found in the cellar, Father Hucheloup's old stock, he placed under Mabeuf's table with a veto on them, Bossuet remarking how lucky it was that Grantaire was asleep. At two in the morning they counted thirty-seven men.","Dawn came pale over the roofs, birds crying in the sky and the breeze stirring the hair of the dead man in the third-storey window. Courfeyrac was glad the trembling torch was out. Joly drew a theory of creation from a cat on a gutter; Combeferre spoke of the dead, of Jean Prouvaire, Bahorel, Mabeuf and even Le Cabuc, and of how even a liberating killing leaves more remorse than joy, then drifted through translators of the Georgics to Caesar and Brutus, judging Caesar's fall just but finding the spitting on Christ a greater outrage than Caesar's twenty-three wounds. Bossuet, on his heap of stones with a rifle, cried out for Homer in Greek."],["The insurgents were full of hope. Having beaten off the night attack, they almost disdained the one to come, and they mapped out the day: a regiment would go over to them at six, all Paris would rise at noon, revolution by sunset. The alarm bell of Saint-Merry, ringing without pause, proved that the other great barricade still held. Their hopes hummed from group to group like a hive.","Enjolras, who had slipped out along the Mondétour lane to look, came back and listened for a moment with folded arms. Then he told them plainly: the whole army of Paris was to strike, a third of it was bearing down on this barricade, with the National Guard besides; he had seen the shakos of the fifth of the line and the standards of the sixth legion; they would be attacked within the hour; the populace that had seethed yesterday was not stirring today. Nothing to expect, nothing to hope for. \\"You are abandoned.\\" A silence fell in which death might have been heard passing.","It was brief. A voice from the darkest part of the groups answered: so be it, raise the barricade to twenty feet and all stay in it; let them offer the protest of corpses and show that if the people abandon the republicans, the republicans do not abandon the people. The words freed every man from his private fears and were hailed with enthusiasm. No one ever knew who spoke; he was some unknown man in a blouse, the anonymous hero who in a crisis says the decisive word and vanishes. At almost the same hour, Hugo notes, the insurgents of Saint-Merry were shouting the same resolve to die to the last man."],["The cry went up: long live death, let us all stay. Enjolras objected that thirty men were enough to hold the position and that the republic was not rich enough in men to waste forty. The murmurs grew; someone pointed out that the barricade was hemmed in anyway, and that a man in a blouse smelling of powder would be shot the moment he reached the Halles. Enjolras and Combeferre fetched the four National Guard uniforms with their belts and shakos and threw them on the ground: dressed in these, four men could mingle with the troops and escape. No one moved.","Combeferre then made a long appeal. It was a question of women and children: of mothers watching at candlelit windows, of daughters who, left without bread, would be driven to the street and the police, of small children left to starve, like the one he had seen as a house-surgeon at the Necker Hospital, dead with ashes in his teeth and mud in his stomach. To die was fine; to make others die was murder. Heads dropped. Hugo notes the contradiction: Combeferre, who was not an orphan, remembered other men's mothers and forgot his own.","Marius, fevered and fasting, sunk in the stupor of a man who has accepted death, was roused by this one thing: he could save someone else while destroying himself. He added his entreaty to Enjolras's order, and the men began denouncing one another as fathers and breadwinners. Enjolras had them vote; five were chosen unanimously. But there were only four uniforms, and the five began a generous quarrel over who should stay. They asked Marius to choose, and he counted them with horror, each crying \\"Me!\\"","At that moment a fifth uniform fell on the pile. Marius looked up and recognised M. Fauchelevent. Jean Valjean had come in by the Mondétour lane, admitted by the sentinel as a lone National Guardsman, and had silently taken off his coat. Bossuet asked who he was; Combeferre said, a man who saves others; Marius said, \\"I know him.\\" Enjolras welcomed him as a citizen and told him they were about to die. Without replying, Valjean helped the fifth man into his uniform."],["Hugo pauses on Enjolras's state of mind. He carried the whole revolution in him, with too much of Saint-Just and not enough of Anacharsis Cloots, but Combeferre's influence had widened him towards the idea of a human republic; in means, though, he remained violent, of the school of '93. Leaning on his gun on the stairway of stones, he seemed to quiver with prophecy, then threw back his blond head and spoke.","He pictured the future: cities flooded with light, nations as sisters, the workshop and the school as brothers, work and right and peace for all, no more bloodshed or war. Man had conquered the hydra in the locomotive and was about to conquer the griffin in the balloon; science would become government, Europe and then the globe would have their amphictyons, a parliament of intelligence. He saluted Feuilly, the orphan artisan who had adopted humanity for his mother. Whatever happened, defeat or victory, they were making a revolution, the revolution of the True. He defined the sovereignty of each man over himself as liberty, the equal concession each makes to all as equality, the protection of all over each as fraternity, and named equality's organ: free and compulsory instruction, the right to the alphabet. The nineteenth century is great, he said, but the twentieth will be happy, with no more conquest, famine, prostitution or scaffold.","Then he turned to where they stood. The hour was gloomy, but revolutions are a toll paid for the future. This barricade was not made of stones and joists but of two heaps, a heap of ideas and a heap of woes; here misery met the ideal, the day embraced the night and promised it rebirth. Whoever died here died in the radiance of the future; they were entering a tomb all flooded with dawn. He paused rather than stopped, his lips still moving, and there was no applause, only a long whispering like the rustling of leaves."],["Marius saw everything now as a man already beyond the wall of the tomb. He did not ask himself how or why M. Fauchelevent had come; in his despair it seemed natural that the whole world should come there to die, though he thought of Cosette with a pang. Fauchelevent did not speak to him or look at him, even when Marius vouched for him, and this suited Marius, who had always found it impossible to address that enigmatic and imposing man.","The five men chosen to live left by the Mondétour lane looking exactly like National Guardsmen; one wept, and they embraced those who stayed. Then Enjolras remembered the man condemned to death. Javert, still bound to the post, asked only when they meant to kill him; Enjolras answered that they needed every cartridge for the present, and when Javert asked for a drink, held a glass of water to his lips himself. Javert complained that they might at least lay him on a table like \\"that other man,\\" nodding towards Mabeuf's body.","Four insurgents untied him while a fifth held a bayonet to his chest. They left his arms bound behind him, hobbled his feet with a cord that allowed steps of fifteen inches, walked him to the long table where cartridges had been made and laid him down bound about the middle, adding a rope from his neck forking over the stomach to his hands, the bond prisons call a martingale. As they worked, a man standing in the doorway watched with singular attention; his shadow made Javert turn his head. He looked up and recognised Jean Valjean. He did not start; he lowered his eyelids proudly and said only, \\"It is perfectly simple.\\""],["The dawn brought no awakening: not a window opened, the Rue Saint-Denis lay silent as an avenue of sphinxes, and all the sentinels came in. On the advice of the man who had scouted towards the Halles, Enjolras, fearing a surprise from the rear, had the Mondétour lane barricaded too, tearing up more pavement. The redoubt now had three fronts and no exit; \\"a fortress but a rat hole too,\\" laughed Courfeyrac. Some thirty spare paving-stones were piled by the wine-shop door, brandy was doled out, and each man picked his place as at the theatre; peril, Hugo observes, produces order. When Enjolras took his double-barrelled rifle to his embrasure, the only sound was the cocking of guns.","From the Saint-Leu quarter came a clank of chains and iron: a cannon appeared, pushed by its gunners, the lint-stock already smoking. Enjolras ordered fire and the whole barricade let off a volley, but when the smoke cleared not a gunner had been hit, and the captain of the piece was levelling his gun with the gravity of an astronomer. Bossuet cried bravo for the cannoneers, and the barricade applauded. Courfeyrac welcomed the brutal part; Combeferre discoursed on the brass eight-pounder's tendency to burst, on hooping and rifling, and concluded that a cannonball travels six hundred leagues an hour while light travels seventy thousand a second, the superiority of Christ over Napoleon.","The shot came. At the same instant a joyous voice shouted \\"Present!\\" and Gavroche flung himself over the auxiliary barricade from the Rue du Cygne as the ball struck. He made a greater sensation than the cannonball, which buried itself in the heap, breaking an omnibus wheel and finishing the old Anceau cart. The barricade burst out laughing and Bossuet shouted to the gunners to go on."],["Marius drew Gavroche aside and asked sternly who had told him to come back and whether he had delivered the letter. Gavroche, uneasy at having handed it to a bareheaded stranger whose face he could not see, lied: he had given it to the porter, and the lady would have it when she woke. Marius had meant the letter to bid Cosette farewell and to save Gavroche; he had to be content with half. It occurred to him that the letter and M. Fauchelevent's arrival might be connected, but Gavroche, who had only ever seen the man at night, said he did not know him, and Marius's uneasy conjectures dissolved: perhaps Fauchelevent was simply a republican.","Gavroche got his gun back from Courfeyrac and told his comrades the barricade was blocked: a line battalion in the Rue de la Petite Truanderie watched the Rue du Cygne, the municipal guard held the Rue des Prêcheurs, the bulk of the army was in front. He authorised them to hit the enemy a tremendous whack. Meanwhile infantry occupied the end of the street behind the gun and built a low wall of paving-stones, and Enjolras heard grapeshot being drawn from the caissons and saw the muzzle shifted slightly to the left. He shouted to everyone to kneel along the wall, but the men who had left their posts to crowd round Gavroche were too slow: the charge struck the cut in the redoubt, rebounded off the wall and left two dead and three wounded. If this went on the barricade was untenable.","Enjolras resolved to prevent the second discharge and took aim at the captain of the gun, a very young, blond sergeant with a gentle, intelligent face. Combeferre, beside him, begged him to look at the man: he had a family, he was probably in love, he might be your brother. \\"He is,\\" said Enjolras; \\"he is mine too,\\" said Combeferre, \\"let us not kill him.\\" \\"Let me alone. It must be done.\\" A tear ran down Enjolras's marble cheek as he fired. The sergeant turned twice with arms outstretched and fell dead across his gun, shot through the breast. Several minutes were gained while he was replaced."],["The gun would fire again within minutes and the barricade could not survive a quarter of an hour of grapeshot. Enjolras ordered a mattress placed in the cut; Combeferre answered that the wounded were lying on all of them. Jean Valjean, who had sat apart on a stone post by the tavern with his gun across his knees, deaf to remarks about a gun that was doing nothing, rose at the order.","He remembered the old woman who, foreseeing bullets, had hung her mattress across her attic window on the roof of a six-storey house a little beyond the barricade; it hung from two ropes that looked like threads against the sky. He asked for a double-barrelled rifle, and Enjolras handed him his own. Valjean fired and cut one rope; he fired again and the second rope whipped the panes, and the mattress slid between its poles and fell into the street. The barricade applauded, but it had landed between besiegers and besieged, and the soldiers, enraged by the sergeant's death and lying behind their stone wall, were pouring musketry into the street while the gun was re-crewed.","Valjean stepped out of the cut, walked through the storm of bullets, hoisted the mattress on his back, returned and fixed it in the breach with his own hands, hidden from the gunners. The next discharge of grapeshot roared against it and did not rebound. The barricade was saved. \\"Citizen,\\" said Enjolras, \\"the Republic thanks you.\\" Bossuet laughed at the immorality of a mattress with so much power: glory to the thing that yields, which annuls a cannon."],["Cosette woke with the sun in her eyes in her narrow room over the back courtyard. She had not been out the evening before and had gone to bed before Toussaint mentioned \\"a row\\"; she knew nothing of what was happening in Paris. She had dreamed of Marius and woke smiling, filled, like Jean Valjean a few hours earlier, with a reaction of the soul that refuses unhappiness. It was three days since she had seen Marius, but he must have her letter, he knew where she was, and he was clever enough to find a way to her, surely today, perhaps this very morning. She was vexed that she could not remember what he had told her about an absence that was to last only a day.","Hugo declines to describe a young girl's rising: one may show a nuptial chamber but not a virginal one, and here contemplation would be profanation. Cosette dressed hastily and opened her window hoping for a scrap of street to watch for Marius, but the court was walled in and looked only onto gardens, which for the first time in her life she found ugly. She decided to watch the sky instead, as if he might come from there. Suddenly she burst into tears, with a confused sense of something horrible in the air, then calm and hope returned.","The house still slept; the porter's lodge was shut, Toussaint had not risen, and Cosette supposed her father asleep too. She told herself he had been unkind, but she counted on Marius. Now and then she heard sharp shocks in the distance and thought it odd that people were opening and shutting their carriage gates so early: it was the cannon battering the barricade. A few feet below her window a martin's nest sat in the black cornice, the mother spread over her brood, the father flying to and fro with food; Cosette, her hair in the sun, leaned over and gazed at that small family with the profound trouble a nest produces in a virgin."],["Musketry and grapeshot alternated without doing much harm except to the top of the Corinthe façade, whose first-floor window and attic were slowly losing their shape, forcing the men posted there to withdraw. Hugo explains the tactic: fire for a long time to make the insurgents waste their powder, then assault when their fire slackens. Enjolras did not fall into the trap, and the barricade stayed silent. Gavroche puffed out his cheek in disdain at each volley and asked the soldiers to rip up more cloth, since they wanted lint; Courfeyrac told the cannon it was growing diffuse.","The silence seemed to worry the besiegers, who needed to see what was going on behind that wall. A fireman's helmet glittered on a neighbouring roof, its wearer standing against a chimney with his glance falling straight into the barricade. \\"There's an embarrassing watcher,\\" said Enjolras. Jean Valjean, who had returned Enjolras's rifle but had his own gun, took aim without a word, and a second later the helmet, smashed by a bullet, rattled into the street; the terrified man vanished. An officer took his place, and Valjean, reloading, sent his casque after the fireman's. No one appeared on that roof again.","Bossuet asked Valjean why he had not killed the man. Jean Valjean made no reply."],["Bossuet muttered to Combeferre that the man had not answered his question; Combeferre replied that he was a man who does good by gunshots. Hugo then turns to the suburban National Guard, valiant against insurrection in June 1832: dram-shop keepers who became lions over a closed dance-hall, bankers who sang the Marseillaise as their piles of crowns shrank, shopkeepers defending the shop with Spartan enthusiasm. It was serious underneath, social elements in strife, but it mixed order with a lack of discipline. Colonels beat capricious drum calls, captains went into action by inspiration, and platoons set themselves up as councils of war to judge and shoot a captured insurgent in five minutes; such an improvisation had killed Jean Prouvaire, and a young poet was nearly killed for carrying the memoirs of the Duc de Saint-Simon.","On the 6th of June Captain Fannicot, a bold, impatient bourgeois and fanatical governmentalist, could not resist firing early. Exasperated by the red flag and by Mabeuf's old coat, which he took for a black flag, he blamed the generals who were letting the insurrection fry in its own fat and launched his company of \\"raging fellows\\" at the barricade alone. Two-thirds of the way up the street a general discharge met them; four of the boldest were mown down at the foot of the redoubt, the company wavered and fell back leaving fifteen corpses, a second volley struck it before it reached shelter, and then the cannon, which had received no order to stop, caught it between two fires. Fannicot himself died of that grapeshot, killed by order.","Enjolras was angry: the fools were getting their own men killed and using up the barricade's ammunition for nothing. He spoke, Hugo says, like the true general of insurrection he was, for insurrection has a fixed number of shots and men while repression has the army and the arsenal of Vincennes; the struggle is one against a hundred and always ends in the crushing of the barricade, unless revolution itself rises and the army, that lion, finds France, that prophet, standing before it."],["Among the feelings that defend a barricade there are intermittences of hope, and one crossed the Rue de la Chanvrerie when it was least expected. Enjolras, still on watch, cried out that Paris seemed to be waking. It was true that on the morning of the 6th the insurrection broke out again for an hour or two. The obstinate bell of Saint-Merry revived some fancies; barricades were begun in the Rue du Poirier and the Rue des Gravilliers; at the Porte Saint-Martin a young man knelt on the open boulevard, shot the commander of a cavalry squadron and was put to the sword; a woman fired from behind a blind in the Rue Saint-Denis; a boy of fourteen was arrested with his pockets full of cartridges; cuirassiers were fired on in the Rue Bertin-Poirée, and in the Rue Planche-Mibray crockery rained from the roofs, which made Marshal Soult think of Suchet's saying at Saragossa about old women and their chamber-pots.","These symptoms disturbed the military chiefs, who hurried to put out the sparks before dealing with the barricades of Maubuée, the Chanvrerie and Saint-Merry, so as to finish them at one blow. Columns swept the streets, broke in the doors of houses that had fired, and cavalry dispersed the groups on the boulevards. This was the commotion Enjolras had caught between cannonades; he had also seen wounded men carried past the end of the street and told Courfeyrac that those wounded did not come from them.","The hope did not last half an hour: a flash of lightning without thunder. The general movement miscarried, and the war minister and the generals could now concentrate on the three or four barricades still standing. The sun climbed. An insurgent asked Enjolras whether they were really going to die like this without anything to eat; Enjolras, elbows on his embrasure, nodded without taking his eyes from the end of the street."],["Courfeyrac, seated on a paving-stone beside Enjolras, went on insulting the cannon each time its grapeshot passed overhead: that was not thunder, only a cough. He and Bossuet poured out gaiety in place of wine. Bossuet admired Enjolras's impassive daring and theorised that all heroism comes from women, a man without a woman being a pistol without a trigger; Enjolras had no mistress and yet managed to be as cold as ice and as bold as fire. Enjolras seemed not to listen, but anyone near him would have heard him murmur, \\"Patria.\\"","Courfeyrac announced news in the tone of an usher: \\"My name is Eight-Pounder.\\" A second gun had arrived and was placed in line with the first; this outlined the catastrophe. Soon both pieces were firing point-blank, supported by the platoon fire of the line and the suburbs, while two more cannon in the Rue Saint-Denis and the Rue Aubry-le-Boucher riddled the Saint-Merry barricade and the four guns barked at each other across Paris. One gun fired grapeshot, the other balls aimed high to crumble the crest onto the insurgents, the object being to drive them from the summit and from the windows so that the columns could rush the street and scale the redoubt by surprise, as on the evening before.","Enjolras ordered fire on the artillerymen. The long-silent barricade poured out seven or eight volleys with rage and joy, and when the smoke thinned two-thirds of the gunners lay beneath the wheels of the cannon; the survivors served the pieces with grave tranquillity, but the fire had slackened. Bossuet called it success. Enjolras shook his head: another quarter of an hour of this success and there would not be a cartridge left in the barricade. Gavroche, it appears, overheard him."],["Courfeyrac saw someone at the foot of the barricade, outside in the street amid the bullets: Gavroche, with a bottle basket from the wine-shop, calmly emptying the cartridge boxes of the National Guardsmen killed on the slope. Told to come in, he answered \\"Instanter\\" and bounded further into the street, where Fannicot's company had left some twenty corpses, a provision of cartridges for the barricade. The smoke lay in the street like a fog, and thanks to it and his small size he rifled the first seven or eight boxes almost unseen, crawling on his belly, galloping on all fours, basket in his teeth, winding from body to body and opening each cartouche like a monkey opening a nut. On a corporal he found a powder-flask and pocketed it \\"for thirst.\\" The insurgents dared not call him back for fear of drawing attention to him.","He went too far, to where the smoke thinned, and the sharpshooters behind their stone dike and the suburban guards at the corner pointed out something moving. A bullet struck the body he was robbing; a second struck sparks beside him; a third overturned his basket. Gavroche sprang to his feet, hands on hips, hair in the wind, and sang at the National Guardsmen that people were ugly at Nanterre and it was the fault of Voltaire, and stupid at Palaiseau and it was the fault of Rousseau. He picked up every fallen cartridge and went on plundering, answering each missed shot with a new couplet: he was not a notary, he was a little bird, joy was his character and misery his trousseau, all the fault of Voltaire and Rousseau. The soldiers laughed as they aimed, and always missed; he lay down, leapt up, hid in doorways, thumbed his nose at the grapeshot, a gamin-fairy, the invulnerable dwarf of the fray, while the barricade watched panting.","One bullet, better aimed or more treacherous, struck him at last. Gavroche staggered and sank, and the whole barricade cried out; but he rose to a sitting position, a thread of blood down his face, raised both arms towards where the shot had come from and began to sing that he had fallen to the ground, the fault of Voltaire, with his nose in the gutter, the fault of... He did not finish. A second bullet from the same marksman stopped him. He fell face down on the pavement and moved no more. That grand little soul had taken its flight."],["The drama's gaze shifts to the Luxembourg, closed by police order because of the insurrection and emptied of the troops that had bivouacked there. Two ragged children, about seven and five, were walking hand in hand on the sunny side of the paths, the elder leading with a little stick, the younger saying he was hungry. They were the two Gavroche had taken trouble over: children of the Thénardiers, leased to Magnon, attributed to M. Gillenormand, and now leaves blown from all those rootless branches, their once-clean clothes turned to rags. The locked gates had let them slip in and stay; the inspectors, more concerned with the outside than the inside, had not seen them.","Hugo gives the garden at eleven o'clock on the 6th of June its due: rain-washed foliage, lilies and carnations, tulips like flame, bees, the amorous old crows of Marie de Medici, the whole of nature breakfasting with not a beast's stomach empty. He pauses on the thinkers who are content with the infinite and forget to love, the magnificent egoists like Horace and Goethe who do not see the weeping child because the zodiac fills their eyes, great and pitiable at once; and he asks what there is above a sun that may be blind. The god.","The children had reached the great basin and hidden behind the swans' hutch when a bourgeois of about fifty arrived with his six-year-old son, who was dressed as a National Guardsman and carried a big brioche. Hugo suggests he was the same man Marius once overheard counselling his son to avoid excesses. The father, who walked like the swans and smiled because his mouth would not close, held forth on living content with little, called the distant firing the Saturnalia, and, catching sight of the two ragged boys, said that anarchy was entering the garden. His son was no longer hungry and found his cake stale; the father took it and flung it to the swans to teach compassion for animals, then, as the tumult grew sinister and a black cloud veiled the sun, hurried the boy home, saying the Tuileries were being attacked and that from the Tuileries to the Luxembourg was only the distance from royalty to the peerage.","As soon as they had gone the elder boy lay flat on the rim of the basin and reached out with his stick; the swans, hurrying towards the enemy, pushed a ripple before them that floated the brioche to his wand. He rapped it in, frightened the swans away, seized the wet cake, broke it into a large piece and a small one, kept the small one for himself, gave the large one to his brother and told him to ram it into his muzzle."],["Marius dashed out of the barricade with Combeferre behind him, but Gavroche was dead. Combeferre brought back the basket of cartridges; Marius carried the child, thinking that what Thénardier had done for his father he was now doing for Thénardier's son, except that Thénardier had brought back his father alive and he was bringing back the child dead. When he re-entered the redoubt his face, like Gavroche's, was covered in blood: a bullet had grazed his head as he stooped, and he had not noticed. Courfeyrac bandaged his brow with his cravat. They laid Gavroche on Mabeuf's table and spread the black shawl over both; there was enough of it for the old man and the child.","Combeferre distributed the cartridges, fifteen rounds a man. Jean Valjean, still motionless on his stone post, shook his head when offered his share. Combeferre called him a rare eccentric who found a way of not fighting in the barricade; Enjolras answered that this did not prevent him defending it, and Courfeyrac added that he was another sort from Father Mabeuf.","Hugo notes how little the bombardment disturbed the interior; men went and came, talked and jested, and the glow of heroism deepened as the position went from critical to menacing to desperate. Enjolras dominated it gravely; Combeferre in an apron dressed wounds; Bossuet and Feuilly made cartridges from the powder-flask Gavroche had taken from the dead corporal, Bossuet remarking that they would soon take the diligence for another planet; Courfeyrac laid out his arsenal of sword-cane, gun, two pistols and a cudgel; a workman tied Mother Hucheloup's straw hat on against sunstroke; Joly examined his tongue in the widow's mirror; some devoured mouldy crusts found in a drawer. Jean Valjean stared silently at the wall. Marius was troubled about what his father was going to say to him."],["Hugo first insists that a barricade, whatever its inner calm, remains a vision to those inside it: one emerges from civil war as from a dream, with blood under one's nails and no clear memory. Then the clock struck midday, and before the twelve strokes ended Enjolras shouted for stones to be carried up to the windows and roofs: a squad of sappers with axes had appeared at the end of the street, the head of the assault column. In two minutes the spare paving-stones walled the first-floor window and the attic windows to half their height, with loopholes planned by Feuilly for the gun barrels; the two cannon were now firing ball at the centre of the barrier to open a breach. Enjolras had the fifteen bottles from under Mabeuf's table carried to the first floor. Asked who was to drink them, he said, \\"They.\\" The lower window was blocked, the iron bars of the door made ready; the barricade was the rampart and the wine-shop the keep.","While the assailants made their preparations with irritating leisure, Enjolras reviewed everything, feeling that since such men were to die their death should be a masterpiece. He told Marius that they were the two leaders, that he would give the last orders inside and Marius should watch outside. He had the kitchen door nailed up so that the wounded would not be splashed, then gave his orders to Feuilly, who answered for all: axes ready on the first floor to cut the staircase, twenty-six men on foot, thirty-four guns, the eight spare ones kept loaded, twenty men to the barricade and six at the loopholes, all to rush to the barricade when the drum beat the assault.","Then he turned to Javert: \\"I am not forgetting you.\\" He laid a pistol on the table and said the last man to leave the room should smash the spy's skull, not there, so as not to mix the corpses, but over the four-foot barricade in the Mondétour lane. Javert was more impassive than Enjolras himself. Here Jean Valjean stepped out of the group and asked whether the commander thought he deserved a recompense. Enjolras said the barricade had two saviours, Marius Pontmercy and himself. Valjean asked for one thing: to blow that man's brains out. Javert raised his head, saw him, made an almost imperceptible movement and said, \\"That is just.\\" Enjolras, reloading, saw no objection and told him to take the spy. Valjean sat on the end of the table, took the pistol and cocked it. Almost at once trumpets sounded and Marius shouted a warning from the top of the barricade. Javert laughed his noiseless laugh, told the insurgents they were in no better case than he, and as they poured out at Enjolras's cry threw after them: \\"We shall meet again shortly!\\""],["Left alone with Javert, Jean Valjean untied the rope that bound him to the table and signed to him to rise. Javert obeyed with the smile of enchained authority. Valjean took him by the martingale like a beast by its breast-band and led him slowly, pistol in hand, across the inner court of the barricade; the insurgents, intent on the coming attack, had their backs turned, and only Marius, posted at the far left, saw the victim and executioner pass. Valjean made Javert climb the low entrenchment in the Mondétour lane, and they were alone. Among the heap of corpses lay a livid face with streaming hair, a pierced hand and a half-bare breast: Éponine. Javert glanced at it and said calmly that he thought he knew that girl.","Then Valjean put the pistol under his arm and fixed on Javert a look that needed no words: Javert, it is I. \\"Take your revenge,\\" said Javert. Valjean drew a clasp-knife, and Javert remarked that it suited him better. But Valjean cut the martingale at his neck, the cords at his wrists and the cord at his feet, straightened up and said, \\"You are free.\\" Javert, master of himself as he was, could not repress a start and stood open-mouthed. Valjean added that he did not expect to leave this place alive, but that if by chance he did, he lived under the name of Fauchelevent at No. 7, Rue de l'Homme Armé. Javert snarled like a tiger, muttered \\"Have a care,\\" repeated the address and the number, buttoned his coat, folded his arms and walked off towards the Halles. A few minutes later he turned and shouted, \\"You annoy me. Kill me, rather,\\" not noticing that he had stopped addressing Valjean as \\"thou.\\" \\"Be off with you,\\" said Valjean, and Javert turned the corner of the Rue des Prêcheurs. Valjean fired the pistol into the air, went back to the barricade and said, \\"It is done.\\"","Meanwhile Marius, seeing the pinioned spy in daylight for the first time, had recognised him: the inspector of the Rue de Pontoise who had given him the two pistols he had used in this very barricade. The memory was misty, a question rather than a certainty, but perhaps there was still time to intervene. He called to Enjolras at the other end of the barricade and asked the police agent's name. \\"Javert.\\" Marius sprang to his feet. At that moment the pistol shot rang out and Jean Valjean reappeared crying that it was done. A gloomy chill went through Marius's heart."],["The death agony of the barricade was about to begin amid the splendour of the June day, the sobbing tocsin of Saint-Merry and the alarming silence of the houses. Since the evening before the two rows of houses in the Rue de la Chanvrerie had become walls. Hugo explains that when the moment is ripe the inhabitant becomes the combatant's ally and the house fraternises with the fortress; when the masses disown the movement the city turns to a desert, doors are nailed up, and the street becomes a defile that helps the army. Behind those shut doors families eat and sleep and are afraid; fear turns to fury, and the people inside say it is the insurgents' own fault and above all do not open. The wall has ears of flesh and the men bowels of stone. Whom to reproach? No one and everyone; the incomplete times.","Utopia that grows impatient and becomes revolt almost always comes too soon and must accept catastrophe in place of triumph. Is the people's abandonment ingratitude? Yes from the point of view of the human race, no from that of the individual, who has his own pardonable interests and his own short life. Hugo names progress as the permanent life of peoples and, recalling Gerard de Nerval's \\"God is dead, perhaps,\\" insists that progress sleeps but always wakes taller. He adds a severe reservation: Utopia at war borrows battle from the lie of yesterday, shoots spies and executes traitors, and every blade has two edges. Even so he venerates the confessors of Utopia, most of all in failure, preferring martyrdom to success, John Brown to Washington.","There are accepted revolutions and refused revolutions called riots; an insurrection is an idea sitting its examination before the people. Peoples are positive and dislike insurrection because it ends in catastrophe and starts from an abstraction, yet it is always for the ideal that men sacrifice themselves. What the young men of the Rue de la Chanvrerie were fighting was not precisely Louis-Philippe, whom none of them hated, but the younger branch of divine right and, through it, the usurpation of man over man everywhere. Such men march with heads down hoping for victory and accepting Thermopylae. Hugo praises France as the artist among nations, the bearer of the torch after Greece and Italy, warns against both dogma and lucre, grants peoples the right to an eclipse, and insists that the stomach must not be the sole wisdom.","He ends by naming his book's subject. The battle he is describing is a convulsion towards the ideal, an epilepsy of trammelled progress. The whole work, from end to end, is the march from evil to good, from night to day, from appetite to conscience, from nothingness to God: matter at the point of departure, the soul at the point of arrival; the hydra at the beginning, the angel at the end."],["The attack came as a hurricane. In broad daylight, surprise impossible, a powerful column of the line interspersed with National and Municipal Guard, with sappers at its head and drums and trumpets sounding, charged straight at the barricade with the weight of a brazen beam. The wall held. For a moment it was flooded with assailants, then shook them off as a lion shakes off dogs, and the repulsed column, massed in the street, answered with a horizontal bouquet of musketry that picked off men one after another. On both sides the bravery was almost barbarous; National Guardsmen fought like Zouaves, and the street was strewn with corpses.","Enjolras held one end of the barricade and Marius the other. Enjolras, who carried the whole defence in his head, sheltered himself, and three soldiers fell under his embrasure without seeing him; Marius fought unprotected, more than half his body above the breastwork, formidable and pensive, a phantom firing a gun. The cartridges were giving out but not the sarcasms. Courfeyrac, bareheaded, said the cannonballs had finally taken his hat; Feuilly bitterly named the well-known men who had sworn to join them and had abandoned them, and Combeferre answered that some people observe honour as one observes the stars, from a great distance. The interior was so strewn with torn cartridges that it looked like snow.","The attackers had numbers, the insurgents position, and the army closed in step by step like a vice. Assault followed assault. These ragged, starving, sleepless men, nearly all wounded, with a few rounds left and notched swords, became Titans, and the barricade was ten times attacked, scaled and never captured; Hugo compares it to the Forest of Swords of the Veda and renounces any attempt to depict the whole slaughter. They fought hand to hand from roofs, windows and cellar gratings, one against sixty, and the façade of Corinthe was half demolished.","Bossuet was killed; Feuilly was killed; Courfeyrac was killed; Combeferre, pierced by three bayonet thrusts in the breast as he lifted a wounded soldier, had only time to glance at heaven before he died. Marius, still fighting, was so riddled with wounds, especially in the head, that his face vanished under blood. Enjolras alone was untouched; when he had no weapon left, a hand thrust some arm into his fist, and all he had were the stumps of four swords. Hugo closes with Homer's roll of the slain and the old frescoes of iron-clad dukes, and insists that a peasant recruit and a pale student, each believing he fights for his country, cast a shadow in the epic field of humanity as long as any Ajax."],["When no leader was left alive but Enjolras and Marius at the two ends, the centre that Courfeyrac, Joly, Bossuet, Feuilly and Combeferre had so long sustained gave way. The cannon had hollowed the middle of the redoubt into two slopes, the outer one an inclined plane for attack, and a last assault up it succeeded; the bayonets came over the crest through the smoke and the defenders of the centre fell back in confusion. Then the love of life woke in some of them: hemmed in by the six-storey house at the back of the redoubt, they battered at its door with gun-butts and kicks, begging to be let in. No one opened. From the third-floor window the dead man's head looked down on them.","Enjolras and Marius and the seven or eight rallied round them sprang to protect these men. Enjolras shouted to the soldiers not to advance, and when an officer disobeyed he killed him. Then, back against the Corinthe building, sword in one hand and rifle in the other, he held the wine-shop door open, shielding the fugitives with his body against an entire battalion, whirling his rifle like a singlestick round his head, and was the last in. The door slammed so violently that it left five fingers of a soldier who had clung to it glued to the post.","Marius stayed outside. A shot had broken his collarbone; he felt himself fainting and falling, and with his eyes already shut felt a vigorous hand seize him, having only time to think, with a last memory of Cosette, that he was taken prisoner and would be shot. Enjolras, not seeing Marius among those in the wine-shop, thought the same, but there was no time; he barred, bolted and chained the door while muskets and sappers' axes beat on it. The soldiers were full of rage, both for the dead artillery sergeant and because of a false rumour that the insurgents had been mutilating prisoners and had a headless soldier in the wine-shop.","Enjolras said, \\"Let us sell our lives dearly,\\" then bent over the table where Mabeuf and Gavroche lay under the black cloth and kissed the old man's hand that hung to the floor, as he had kissed his brow the evening before, the only two kisses of his life. Hugo abridges: the barricade had fought like a gate of Thebes, the wine-shop fought like a house of Saragossa. Paving-stones rained from the windows and roof; when the door yielded the assailants found no one alive below, the staircase cut down with axes, and a terrific fire pouring through the hole in the ceiling: the last cartridges. When those were gone, each man on the first floor took two of the reserved bottles and held the scaling party off with these fragile clubs. They were bottles of aquafortis. It resembled Milton and Dante rather than Homer; demons attacked and spectres resisted. It was heroism become monstrous."],["Climbing on each other's backs, up the skeleton of the staircase and the walls, a score of soldiers and guardsmen, most of them disfigured by wounds and blind with blood, burst into the first-floor room. Only one man was still standing: Enjolras, without cartridges or sword, holding the barrel of his gun whose stock he had broken on the heads of the first to enter, the billiard table between him and his attackers, head high in a corner, still so alarming that a space emptied round him. They cried that this was the leader who had killed the artilleryman and that he should be shot where he stood. \\"Shoot me,\\" said Enjolras, and threw away his stump of gun-barrel, folded his arms and offered his breast.","The audacity of a fine death stilled the room. Enjolras, bloody but unwounded, fresh and rosy after twenty-four hours of combat, seemed to compel his killers to kill him respectfully; a witness later spoke of an insurgent they called Apollo, and one guardsman lowered his gun, saying it seemed to him he was about to shoot a flower. Twelve men formed a squad opposite him, a sergeant shouted the aim, and an officer intervened to ask whether he wished his eyes bandaged. \\"No.\\" Was it he who had killed the artillery sergeant? \\"Yes.\\"","Grantaire had woken a few moments before. He had been dead drunk in that upper room since the evening before, head on his arms among the glasses and bottles, snoring through fusillade, cannonballs and assault, so like the corpses around him that no one had noticed him; noise does not rouse a drunkard, silence does, and the sudden halt of the tumult woke him. His intoxication tore away like a curtain and he understood everything at once. As the sergeant was about to repeat the order, a strong voice beside the soldiers shouted, \\"Long live the Republic! I'm one of them.\\" Grantaire crossed the room with a firm stride, placed himself before the guns beside Enjolras and said, \\"Finish both of us at one blow.\\" Then, gently, to Enjolras: \\"Do you permit it?\\" Enjolras pressed his hand with a smile.","The smile had not ended when the volley sounded. Enjolras, pierced by eight bullets, remained leaning against the wall as though nailed there, his head bowed. Grantaire fell at his feet as if struck by a thunderbolt. A few moments later the soldiers dislodged the last insurgents from the top of the house, firing into the attic, flinging bodies still alive from the windows; two light-infantrymen were shot lifting the omnibus, a soldier and an insurgent fell from the roof locked together, and a similar struggle ended in the cellar. Then silence. The barricade was captured, and the soldiers began to search the houses round about and pursue the fugitives."],["Marius was indeed a prisoner, but the hand that gripped him as he fell was Jean Valjean's. Valjean had taken no part in the fighting except to expose himself in it; thanks to him, everywhere present like a providence, the fallen had been picked up, carried to the taproom and cared for, and no blow or attack or even act of self-defence came from his hands. He received only a few scratches; the bullets would have none of him. Hugo doubts he had come there to seek suicide, an irreligious act. He had seemed not to see Marius and had never taken his eyes off him; when the shot laid Marius low, Valjean leapt on him like a tiger on its prey and bore him off, and in the fury concentrated on Enjolras and the wine-shop door no one saw him cross the barricade and disappear behind the angle of the Corinthe building.","That angle made a little cape sheltered from bullets and eyes, the fold in which Éponine had breathed her last. Valjean set Marius down against the wall and looked about him. For a moment or two the spot was safe, but the implacable house in front of him was shut, the low barricade on his right towards the Rue de la Petite Truanderie had a line of bayonets behind it that would greet any head with sixty shots, and on his left was the battle; if a single soldier turned the corner, all was over. He remembered his escape from the Rue Polonceau eight years before; that had been difficult, this was impossible. Only a bird could have got out.","Staring at the ground as though to pierce a hole in it with his eyes, he made out at the base of the little barricade, under a disordered heap of paving-stones, an iron grating about two feet square, lying flat and loosened from its frame, with a dark shaft below it like a chimney flue or a cistern pipe. His old art of escape rose in him like an illumination. He thrust aside the stones, raised the grating, lifted the inert Marius onto his shoulders, climbed down with elbows and knees into the well, let the heavy trap fall back with the stones rolling over it, and stood on a flagged floor three metres down, all in a few minutes, with the strength of a giant and the speed of an eagle.","He found himself with the unconscious Marius in a long underground corridor of profound peace, absolute silence and night. The feeling of his fall into the convent garden came back to him, only what he carried now was not Cosette but Marius. The tumult of the wine-shop taken by assault reached him overhead only as a vague murmur."],["Hugo begins with an accountant's thesis: Paris casts twenty-five millions a year into the water, without metaphor, by means of its intestine, the sewer. Science now knows that human manure is the richest fertiliser; the Chinese have always known it, and their soil is as young as in Abraham's day. Fleets go to the South Pole for the dung of petrels while the detritus of a capital, which could feed the world if returned to the land, is swept into the abyss. The filth carted through the streets at night is in truth the flowering meadow, the cattle, the golden wheat, the bread on the table and the blood in the veins.","France alone, he says, deposits half a milliard a year in the Atlantic through its rivers, a quarter of the budget, with two results: the land impoverished and the water poisoned, hunger from the furrow and disease from the stream; the Thames is poisoning London, and Paris has had to move its sewer mouths below the last bridge. A simple double system of pipes, already working in English towns, would bring the fields' pure water to the city and send the city's rich water back to the fields. The sewer is a mistake; drainage that restores what it takes would increase the earth's yield tenfold and lighten the problem of misery. Paris, the model city, is a spendthrift, and in this it imitates Rome, whose sewer, as Liebig says, absorbed the well-being of the Roman peasant and then engulfed Italy, Sicily, Sardinia and Africa.","For this operation Paris has beneath it another Paris of sewers, with its streets, crossroads, squares and blind alleys, a circulation of mire without the human form. If Paris contains Athens, Tyre, Sparta and Nineveh, it also contains Lutetia, the city of mud, a grandiose vileness like Machiavelli or Mirabeau among men. The subsoil, apart from catacombs, gas pipes and water mains, is a colossal madrepore, a shadowy labyrinth under both banks whose only guiding thread is its slope. In its humid mist appears the rat, the creature Paris seems to have given birth to."],["Seen from above with Paris lifted off like a lid, the sewers would outline a great branch grafted on the river, the belt sewer its trunk and the dead-end ducts its twigs, though Hugo prefers the image of an eccentric oriental alphabet, misshapen letters welded together at random against a background of shadow. Sinks and sewers were objects of almost religious dread in antiquity and the Middle Ages, from the vermin ditch of Benares to the sink of Nineveh; false prophets drew their false moons and suns from cesspools.","The history of men is reflected in the history of sewers. The Paris sewer has been a sepulchre and an asylum: crime, intelligence, social protest, liberty of conscience, thought and theft, everything the law has persecuted, has hidden there, the Maillotins in the fourteenth century, the Huguenots in the sixteenth, the chauffeurs in the eighteenth. A hundred years ago the pickpocket in danger slipped into it as into a bedroom; the forest had its cave and Paris its sewer, where Villon within converses with Rabelais without.","The sewer, Hugo says, is the conscience of the city, where everything converges and nothing keeps a secret. Filth does not lie: there the bottle-end confesses drunkenness, the apple-core that had literary opinions becomes an apple-core again, the gaming-house louis jostles the suicide's nail, a foetus rolls in last Shrove Tuesday's spangles. All that was rouged is washed clean; a sewer is a cynic and tells everything, and its sincerity rests the soul after the great airs of reasons of state and incorruptible robes. History too passes through it: the Saint Bartholomews filter through its stones drop by drop, and the thinker sees Louis XI with Tristan, Charles IX with his mother, Richelieu with Louis XIII, kneeling in its gloom with a scrap of shroud for an apron, sponging out their work. The social observer must enter these shadows; philosophy, the microscope of thought, reconstructs the city from the cesspool as it infers the amphora from the potsherd."],["The medieval sewer of Paris was legendary; Henri II attempted a survey in the sixteenth century and failed, and a hundred years ago the cesspool was simply left to itself. Old Paris, Hugo says, had not much head, and could no more sweep out filth than abuses; above the unintelligible city lay the inextricable sewer, Daedalus backing up Babel. Sometimes the sewer overflowed like an enraged Nile, and the city tasted its own filth, a resemblance to remorse that served as a warning badly received. The inundation of 1802, still remembered by Parisians of eighty, spread mud over the Place des Victoires and into the Rue Saint-Honoré, reached three feet in the Rue Saint-Pierre, and on the left bank stopped a few paces from the house where Racine had lived.","At the start of the century the sewer was still a place of mystery and terror. Paris knew in a confused way that it had a cavern beneath it, talked of as of the monstrous bed of Thebes; sewermen's boots never went beyond certain known points; carts were tipped straight into it and cleaning was left to the rain. Rome had called its sewer the Gemoniae, Paris called hers the Polypus-Hole. Hygiene and legend agreed in horror: the goblin bred under the Mouffetard sewer, the fever of 1685 was blamed on the gaping hiatus of the Marais sewer, and the toothed grating of the Rue de la Mortellerie breathed hell like a dragon's maw. The sewer had no bottom; it was the lower world, and not even the police thought of exploring it. Yet the cesspool found its Christopher Columbus.","One day in 1805, during one of the Emperor's rare appearances in Paris, with the veterans of the Rhine, the Nile and Lodi crowding the Carrousel in that splendid moment between Marengo and Austerlitz, the Minister of the Interior told Napoleon at his levee that he had seen the most intrepid man in his Empire the day before. What had he done? He wanted to do something: to visit the sewers of Paris. This man existed and his name was Bruneseau."],["The visit was a formidable campaign, a night battle against suffocation and a voyage of discovery; one young workman who survived it later told the details Bruneseau left out of his official report. Eight of his twenty labourers refused to go further at the first turnings. The work was double: to cleanse and to survey at once, noting inflows, counting gratings and vents, mapping branches, sounding side-drains and measuring every vault. Lanterns pined in the foul air, fainting sewermen were carried out, the floor gave way into bottomless wells and one man vanished and was got out with difficulty; on Fourcroy's advice they burned cages of resinous tow where the air had been cleared, and in places the stone itself seemed diseased with misshapen fungi.","Going downhill, Bruneseau read on a stone at the Grand-Hurleur the date 1550, where Philibert Delorme's survey for Henri II had halted; he found seventeenth-century vaulting in the Ponceau drain and eighteenth-century work of 1740 in the collector, both more cracked than the belt sewer of 1412, when the brook of Ménilmontant was raised to the dignity of the Grand Sewer. Under the Courthouse they thought they recognised ancient dungeons cut into the sewer itself, with an iron collar still hanging in one, and walled them up. They found a perfectly preserved ragpicker's basket, jewels, coins, a Huguenot medal of a pig in a cardinal's hat, and the skeleton of an orangutan that had vanished from the Jardin des Plantes in 1800, probably the devil who had appeared in the Rue des Bernardins.","The most surprising encounter was at the entrance to the Grand Sewer, where a shapeless rag hung from the hinge of a vanished grating. It was very fine batiste, and in one corner they made out a marquis's coronet and the letters LAVBESP: Laubespine. It was a piece of Marat's shroud, the sheet he had kept from a youthful love affair with a great lady when he was physician to the Comte d'Artois's stables, and the only fine linen in his house when he died; old women had wrapped him in it, and it had passed through the Pantheon to end with the rats. They left it hanging, from scorn or respect, both of which Marat deserved.","The whole visit lasted seven years, from 1805 to 1812, and as he went Bruneseau built: lowering the Ponceau arch in 1808, pushing the sewer under the Rue Saint-Denis in 1809, under the Salpêtrière in 1810, the Place Royale in 1811, the Rue de la Paix in 1812, disinfecting the whole network and taking on his son-in-law Nargaud in the second year. Thus old society performed the toilet of its double bottom. Hugo closes with a portrait of the antique sewer as it had been: tortuous, cracked, unpaved, fetid and wild, a titanic mole-burrow where the past prowls blindly in the filth that was once splendour."],["The modern sewer is clean, cold, straight and correct, almost what the English call respectable; it is proper and greyish, laid out by rule and line, like a tradesman who has become a councillor of state. The mire there behaves with decency. One might mistake it for one of those underground corridors so useful to fleeing kings in the good old days; the classical alexandrine, driven out of poetry, seems to have taken refuge in its long whitish vault, and even the Rue de Rivoli serves as a pattern underground. The administrative vocabulary has grown dignified: what was a gut is a gallery, what was a hole is a surveying orifice. Villon would no longer find his old lodging there.","Its immemorial population of rats swarms more than ever, but even they grow tame in their subterranean palace, and the rain that once soiled the sewer now washes it. Yet one should not trust it too far: miasmas still live there, and for all the efforts of the prefecture and the health commission it exhales a vague, suspicious odour, like Tartuffe after confession. Still, Tartuffe's conscience is an advance on the Augean stables, and the sweeping is a homage the sewer pays to civilisation.","It is more than progress; it is transmutation, a revolution between the ancient and the present sewer. And the man who effected it is the man everyone forgets, whom Hugo has just named: Bruneseau."],["The sewer grows with Paris, a polyp stretching out an arm every time the city cuts a street. The old monarchy had built twenty-three thousand three hundred metres by 1806; Napoleon added under five thousand, Louis XVIII nearly six, Charles X nearly eleven, Louis-Philippe eighty-nine thousand, the Republic of 1848 twenty-three thousand, the present government seventy thousand: in all two hundred and twenty-six thousand metres, sixty leagues, more than ten times what existed at the century's start. The soil of Paris resists the pick: liquid clays, springs, hard rock and the deep quagmires called moutardes; rivulets burst into half-built vaults, marl rolls down like a cataract, quicksands at Belleville swallow a man visibly, and typhus slowly impregnates the workmen. Hugo names the superintendent Monnot, who died after building the sewer from the Barrière Blanche to Aubervilliers in four months of day-and-night work, and the engineer Duleau, who died after vaulting three thousand metres across the city; there are no bulletins for such bravery, more useful than that of the battlefield.","The sewers of 1832 were far from today's. Bruneseau had given the impulse, but it took the cholera to force the great reconstruction. In 1821 part of the belt sewer still lay open to the sky in the Rue des Gourdes, and the absorbing wells only date from 1836. At the time of the insurrection of the 5th and 6th of June many streets were still sunken causeways with heavy square gratings, called cassis, polished and slippery under horses' hooves, and in a long list of streets, from the Rue du Temple to the Champs-Élysées and the Rue de Tournon, the old gothic sewer still displayed its maw with monumental effrontery. From 1806 to 1831 some seven hundred and fifty metres a year were built; since then eight to ten thousand a year, in small stone and hydraulic mortar at two hundred francs the metre.","Beyond economics lie questions of public health. Paris sits between a sheet of water, pure and fed by the Seine, Marne, Yonne and Loire oozing through the green clay beneath the chalk, and a sheet of air that comes from the sewer and is unhealthy; the air above a dung-heap is purer than the air above Paris, and disease radiates fifty leagues around the Louvre. In time the sheet of water will be used to wash the sewer, by which Hugo means restoring the filth to the soil, and the whole community will gain in health and lose in misery. For ten centuries the cesspool has been the disease of Paris, held in a horror like the knacker's trade; the proverb said that to descend into the sewer was to enter the grave, and the sink bears the traces of every cataclysm from the shells of the Deluge to the rag of Marat."],["In the heart of the city Jean Valjean had escaped from the city, passing in the time it took to lift and replace a grating from midday to midnight and from the whirlwind of murder to the stagnation of the tomb; the waste-trap of safety had opened beneath him, an adorable ambush of providence. He stood blind and deaf for a moment, not knowing whether the man on his shoulders was alive or a corpse, then felt the narrow walls and the wet floor and smelt where he was. When his eyes adjusted he saw that the passage behind him was walled up, a blind branch, and that the faint light from the manhole died a dozen paces ahead. The soldiers might notice the grating too, so there was not a minute to lose; he hoisted Marius again and plunged into the dark.","After fifty paces he met a cross passage and had to choose. The labyrinth's clue is its slope: to follow it is to reach the river. He reasoned that he was probably in the sewer of the Halles, and that to go left and downhill would bring him in a quarter of an hour to some mouth on the Seine between the Pont au Change and the Pont-Neuf, two bleeding men emerging from the ground in the most crowded spot in Paris, to be seized at once. He climbed the incline and turned right instead. Marius's arms were round his neck and his feet dragged; his cheek clung to Valjean's, bleeding, and a warm breath at Valjean's ear told him he was alive. The rain of the day before ran in a little torrent down the middle, so he hugged the wall.","Hugo lays out what Valjean did not know. He thought he was under the Rue Saint-Denis, whose old stone sewer runs straight to the Grand Sewer; in fact the gut of the Petite-Truanderie led into the Montmartre sewer, one of the most labyrinthine of the old network, with the Plâtrière sewer on his left like a Chinese puzzle, the three-toothed Rue du Cadran gallery on his right, the zigzag branch of the Mail and the dead end of the Rue des Jeûneurs between him and the belt sewer, the only way to a safe distant outlet. Had he known, the cheap porous stone under his hand would have told him he was not under the Rue Saint-Denis. Horror seized him by degrees as he walked in an enigma, asking whether Marius would die of haemorrhage and he of hunger, two skeletons in a corner of the night; like the prophet, he was in the belly of the monster. Then the water began to beat his heels instead of his toes: the sewer was descending. He feared he was going to the Seine, but he had in fact crossed the watershed and was heading for the belt sewer, on the right path without knowing it. At every branch he felt the angles and avoided any opening narrower than his own passage, judging rightly that it must be a dead end, and so escaped the fourfold trap.","He heard a distant continuous thunder overhead, the rumbling of vehicles: he was passing from the Paris petrified by the uprising into the living city. He had walked half an hour, only shifting Marius from hand to hand, when suddenly he saw his own shadow ahead of him on a faint reddish glow that gilded the viscous walls. He turned. Far behind him, piercing the dark, flamed a horrible star that seemed to be watching him, and behind it eight or ten black upright forms moved confusedly. It was the gloomy star of the police rising in the sewer."],["On the 6th of June a battue of the sewers had been ordered: while General Bugeaud swept public Paris, Prefect Gisquet was to search the occult city for vanquished insurgents who might have taken refuge there. Three squads of agents and sewermen, armed with carbines, bludgeons, swords and poniards, explored the right bank, the left bank and the Cité. The lantern aimed at Jean Valjean belonged to the right-bank patrol, which had just searched the curving gallery and the three blind alleys under the Rue du Cadran, the very opening Valjean had passed by because it was narrower than his passage. Emerging, the police thought they heard footsteps towards the belt sewer, and the sergeant raised his lantern.","It was an indescribable moment for Valjean, but if he saw the lantern well, the lantern saw him badly: it was light and he was shadow, far off and mingled with the dark. He hugged the wall and stopped, and when he stopped the sound stopped. Half-visionary from hunger and sleeplessness, he did not understand what the gleam and the forms behind him were. The patrol listened and heard nothing, looked and saw nothing, and gathered in a circle at a service crossroads of the Montmartre sewer, where Valjean saw the bulldog heads whispering together. They concluded they had been mistaken, that it was useless to get tangled in the belt sewer, and that any \\"bousingot\\" to be hunted was towards Saint-Merry. The sergeant ordered a turn to the left, towards the Seine. Had they split into two squads, Valjean would have been taken; the prefecture's instructions, anticipating insurgents in force, had probably forbidden it. All hung on that thread.","Before leaving, to satisfy his policeman's conscience, the sergeant fired his gun in Valjean's direction. The detonation rolled from echo to echo through the crypt, and a fragment of plaster splashing into the stream a few paces from Valjean told him the ball had struck the vault over his head. Measured steps died away along the timber-work, the reddish glow faded, blindness and deafness resumed possession of the shadows, and Valjean, not daring to stir, stayed a long time leaning on the wall with straining ears and dilated pupils, watching the phantom patrol vanish."],["Hugo credits the police of that period with never letting a revolt distract them from ordinary duty: on the afternoon of the 6th of June, under the pressure of a possible revolution, an agent was still \\"spinning\\" a thief. On the slope of the right bank a little beyond the Pont des Invalides, where there is no longer any shore today, two men walked some distance apart, the one in front trying to get away without seeming to, the one behind trying to overtake him without hurrying, like a game of checkers played at a distance. Seen from the opposite quay, the first was a bristling, tattered, equivocal figure in a ragged blouse, furious as well as afraid; the second a classic, official personage in the frock-coat of authority buttoned to the chin. Hugo hints that the reader might recognise them at closer range, but does not name them.","The pursuer's object, Hugo suggests, was to clothe the first man more warmly, in red rather than blue; if he had not yet seized him, it was in the hope of being led to some meeting-place and some group worth catching, the delicate operation called spinning. Seeing an empty hackney-coach pass on the quay above, he made a sign, and the driver, who evidently knew with whom he was dealing, turned and followed the two men at a walking pace; the police instruct their agents always to have a coach on hand. The man in the blouse, to his follower's surprise, did not take the watering ramp up to the Champs-Élysées but kept on along the bank, which narrowed towards the Pont de Jéna into a tongue that ended in the water, blocked by the quay wall on one side and the river on the other, with only a heap of demolition rubbish six or seven feet high to hide the end.","The fugitive went round the heap and was lost to sight; the pursuer, no longer seen, walked fast, rounded it in his turn, and stopped in amazement. The man was gone. The shore ran only thirty paces further into the water, and no one could have jumped into the Seine unseen. Then he struck his brow: where the land met the water was a large, low, arched iron grating with a heavy lock and three massive hinges, a door at the base of the quay through which a blackish stream ran into the river. Beyond its rusty bars a dark vaulted corridor could be made out. He shook it; it held firm. It had evidently just been opened, silently, and closed again, which meant the man had not a hook but a key. \\"That is too much! A government key!\\" he exclaimed, then composed himself with a string of ironic monosyllables and settled behind the rubbish to watch with the patient rage of a pointer, while the coach halted on the quay above and its driver put the oat-bags on his horses, and passers-by on the Pont de Jéna glanced at the two motionless items in the landscape."],["Valjean resumed his march and the going grew harder. The vaults averaged five and a half feet, so he had to bend at every step to keep Marius's head from the stone and rise again, groping along walls too slimy to hold. The gleams from the air-holes were rare and so wan that full sunlight looked like moonlight. He was hungry and above all thirsty, in a place full of water where a man cannot drink; his prodigious strength, little diminished by age thanks to his chaste and sober life, began to give way, and Marius, perhaps dead, grew heavier as it ebbed. Rats slid between his legs and one bit him. About three in the afternoon he reached the belt sewer and was astonished by the sudden width: eight feet by seven, a gallery where his outstretched hands could not touch both walls.","At the junction with the galleries of the Rue de Provence and the Abattoir he chose the broadest passage, the belt sewer, and, judging that he must reach the Seine at any risk, turned left to descend. Hugo explains that this saved him: the Grand Sewer, the old brook of Ménilmontant, ends upstream in a blind sack at its source, and had he climbed it he would have died at a wall; only a man who knew the whole madrepore could have reached the Amelot outlet by the Filles-du-Calvaire passage. Asked where he was, Valjean would have answered, \\"In the night.\\" His instinct served him. He passed the claw-shaped branches under the Rue Laffitte and the Rue Saint-Georges and the long corridor of the Chaussée d'Antin.","A little beyond the Madeleine branch, under a large air-hole, probably in the Rue d'Anjou, he halted, exhausted, and laid Marius down on the banquette with the gentleness of a brother. Under the wan light Marius's face was like ashes at the bottom of a tomb, his eyes closed, his hair plastered red, his hands limp, blood clotted in his cravat and at the corners of his mouth, his coat chafing the open gashes. Valjean laid a hand on his breast: the heart still beat. He tore up his shirt, bound the wounds as best he could and stopped the bleeding, then, bending over the unconscious young man in that half light, gazed at him with inexpressible hatred.","In Marius's pockets he had found the roll forgotten there the evening before and his pocketbook. He ate the roll and opened the book to the lines Marius had written: that his name was Marius Pontmercy and his body should be carried to his grandfather, M. Gillenormand, No. 6 Rue des Filles-du-Calvaire, in the Marais. He read them by the air-hole, repeated the address in a low voice, put the book back, and, his strength restored, took Marius on his back again with his head on his right shoulder. Hugo notes that the torch of street names lighting this march for the reader was one Valjean did not possess; only the paling light and the thinning rumble of vehicles told him that evening was coming and that he was leaving central Paris for some solitary region near the outer quays. The air-holes grew fewer and the gloom deepened. Suddenly the darkness became terrible."],["He felt that he was entering water and that there was no longer pavement under his feet, only mud. Hugo turns to the beaches of Brittany and Scotland where a traveller walking at low tide notices that the sand has become birdlime and that each footprint fills with water, sinks in two or three inches, tries to turn back and sinks deeper, and recognises with terror that he is caught in a quicksand. He flings away his burden too late; he shouts and waves his hat; if the beach is deserted he is condemned to a long, implacable burial that drags him down by the feet while leaving him time to see the horizon, the villages, the sails and the birds, until the sand reaches his belly, his breast, his throat, fills his shouting mouth and closes his eyes, and a hand pierces the surface, waves and disappears. The earth, permeated with the ocean, drowns a man.","This fate was possible thirty years ago in the sewers of Paris. Before the works of 1833, water seeping into friable strata dissolved the underpinning of the flagged or cemented floor, which cracked and crumbled, and the gulf of mire so formed was called a fontis: the quicksand of Mont Saint-Michel inside a sewer, neither earth nor water, sometimes three or four feet deep, sometimes bottomless. If water predominated death was quick, if earth, slow. Hugo makes the reader picture it: instead of open sky and the hope of passers-by, deafness, blindness, a black vault, slow suffocation by filth with the whole city unaware overhead. Death on a pyre or in a shipwreck can be great; death in mire is filthy and humiliating, and the dying man does not know whether he is becoming a spectre or a frog. Everywhere else the sepulchre is sinister; here it is deformed.","Fontis varied with the subsoil: a man would take a day to disappear in the Lunière fontis and five minutes in the Philippeaux slough; a child could escape where a man perished, and the first law of safety was to throw off every load, which is why a sewerman who felt the ground yield dropped his tools first. They were caused by friable soil, deep slips, summer downpours, winter floods and the weight of houses forcing out the vaults; sometimes a saw-toothed gap between paving-stones betrayed the break above, often nothing did, and woe to the sewermen then. The registers name the drowned, among them Blaise Poutrain under the Rue Carême-Prenant, brother of the last gravedigger of the Innocents, and the young Vicomte d'Escoubleau, a hero of Lérida, who, surprised one night at his cousin the Duchesse de Sourdis's, took refuge from the Duke in the Beautreillis sewer and was swallowed; Madame de Sourdis, told of his death, called for her smelling-bottle and forgot to weep. The sewer extinguishes love: Hero refuses to wash Leander, and Thisbe stops her nose at Pyramus."],["Jean Valjean was facing a fontis. Hugo notes that such quagmires were common in the fluid subsoil of the Champs-Élysées, which in 1836 would hold up the rebuilding of this very sewer under the Faubourg Saint-Honoré for six months amid rain and floods. This one had been made by the downpour of the day before: the sand beneath the pavement had given way, the water had backed up, and the floor had sunk into the ooze to a depth no one could guess. It was a pit of mire in a cavern of night.","Valjean felt the pavement vanish and entered the slime, water on top and mud beneath. Retreat was impossible: Marius was dying and he was exhausted, and where else was there to go? For the first steps it seemed shallow, then the mud reached his calves and the water his knees, then the mud his knees and the water his waist, and he could no longer go back. Mud dense enough to bear one man could not bear two; singly, each might have got out. He went on, holding the dying man, perhaps a corpse, as high as he could. The water reached his armpits and he felt himself sinking, the density that held him up also hindering him; soon only his head was above the water, with his two arms raising Marius, like the mother holding up her child in the old paintings of the Deluge.","He sank further, turned his face backwards to breathe, caught a glimpse of Marius's drooping, livid head above him, made a desperate effort and thrust his foot forward. It struck something solid, a point of support, and high time. He rooted himself on it with a kind of fury, like the first step of a staircase back to life. It was the beginning of the other slope of the pavement, which had bent under the water like a plank without breaking, an inclined plane up which he climbed to the far side of the quagmire. As he came out of the water he struck a stone and fell on his knees, and, reflecting that this was only just, he stayed there some time with his soul absorbed in words addressed to God. Then he rose, shivering, chilled, foul-smelling, bowed under the dying man he dragged after him, dripping with slime and with his soul filled with a strange light."],["He had left his strength in the fontis if not his life. He had to stop for breath every three or four steps and lean on the wall; once he sat on the banquette to shift Marius and thought he would never rise, but his energy outlived his vigour. Walking on desperately, almost fast, he struck his head against a wall at an elbow of the sewer, looked up, and far away at the end of the vault saw a light, this time not the terrible light of the lantern but good white daylight. Jean Valjean saw the outlet. Like a damned soul glimpsing the way out of Gehenna he ran rather than walked, no longer feeling his fatigue or Marius's weight, towards an arch that narrowed like a funnel, a construction copied from prison wickets.","He reached it and halted. It was the outlet, but he could not get out. The arch was closed by a heavy grating that plainly seldom turned on its rusty hinges, clamped to the stone by a thick lock red with rust like a brick, double-locked, one of the prison locks old Paris loved. Beyond the bars lay the open air, the river, the daylight and a narrow shore, with the Pont de Jéna downstream to the right and the Pont des Invalides upstream to the left, one of the most solitary spots in Paris, opposite the Gros-Caillou, a perfect place to wait for night and escape. Flies came and went through the bars. It was about half past eight in the evening and the day was declining.","He laid Marius on the dry part of the vault and seized the bars with both fists and shook them in a frenzy; nothing moved. He tried each bar in turn, hoping to tear one loose for a lever; not one stirred. No lever, no prying; the obstacle was invincible. He could not retrace his steps through the quagmire he had barely escaped and past a patrol he could not escape twice, and every other outlet was surely barred in the same way; chance had unsealed the grating he came in by, but he had only escaped into a prison. All was over; everything he had done was useless, and exhaustion had ended in failure. Caught with Marius in the great web of death, feeling the spider run along its black strands, he turned his back to the grating and fell to the pavement beside the motionless young man, his head between his knees, at the last drop of anguish. He was thinking neither of himself nor of Marius. He was thinking of Cosette."],["In the depths of his prostration a hand touched Valjean's shoulder and a low voice said, \\"Half shares.\\" He had heard no footsteps and thought he was dreaming, but a man stood before him in a blouse, barefoot, his shoes in his left hand so as to approach unheard. Valjean knew him at once: Thénardier. Steeled to shocks, he recovered himself; nothing could make this night blacker. Thénardier shaded his eyes and screwed them up, trying to recognise the other, and failed: Valjean had his back to the light and was so bemired and bloody that he would have been unrecognisable at noon, while Thénardier stood lit by the cellar-light of the grating. The duel began between Valjean veiled and Thénardier unmasked.","Thénardier asked how he meant to get out, since the lock could not be picked, then explained his terms: \\"You have killed that man; that's all right. I have the key.\\" He took Valjean for an assassin and a colleague. Give him half of what was in the dead man's pockets and he would open the door; he half drew a huge key from under his blouse to show how a key to liberty is made. Valjean, as Corneille's phrase has it, remained stupid: here was Providence in horrible guise, his good angel springing from the earth in the shape of Thénardier. Thénardier added a rope, explaining to the idiot that he would need a stone too, from the rubbish outside, to sink the stiff in the river, and Valjean took it mechanically. Thénardier chattered on, approving his silence as good practice for the examining magistrate, asking how he had crossed the slough and why he had not tossed the man into it, then answering himself: the workmen would have found the body, but the river is the true grave, Paris the killer, and justice asks no questions. He spoke low, with a finger to his lips and sudden hushes, and Valjean supposed other ruffians lurked nearby with whom he did not care to share.","Asked to show the money, Valjean searched his pockets. He always carried money, but dressing as a National Guardsman the evening before he had forgotten his pocketbook, and he laid on the banquette a louis d'or, two five-franc pieces and five or six large sous. \\"You knocked him over cheap,\\" said Thénardier, and searched both men's pockets with the greatest familiarity, Valjean letting him so as to keep his back to the light. Handling Marius's coat, Thénardier tore off a strip with a pickpocket's skill and hid it under his blouse, probably thinking it might later identify the murdered man and the murderer; Valjean did not notice. Finding only the thirty francs, Thénardier forgot his motto of half shares and took it all, hesitating only over the sous before pocketing them too, grumbling that people cut throats too cheap altogether.","He helped Valjean get Marius onto his shoulders, crept barefoot to the grating, peered out with a finger on his lips, and turned the key; the gate swung open without a sound. Its oiled hinges showed that it opened more often than anyone supposed, the accomplice of some mysterious band, a receiver of stolen goods. Hugo doubts that Thénardier's intention in letting another man out was purely to rescue an assassin. He opened the gate just wide enough, closed it, double-locked it, and melted back into the dark with the velvet tread of a tiger. Jean Valjean found himself in the open air."],["Valjean let Marius slide onto the shore. Behind him lay miasma and darkness; around him the pure evening air, the river flowing at his feet with the sound of a kiss, the nests saying good night in the elms of the Champs-Élysées, the first stars. For a few seconds he was overcome by that caressing serenity and bathed in prayer; then duty returned, and he bent to sprinkle water from his palm on Marius's face. The eyelids did not open, but the half-open mouth still breathed. As he was about to dip his hand again he felt someone behind him and turned. A tall man in a long coat stood a few paces off, arms folded, a leaded bludgeon in his fist. Jean Valjean recognised Javert.","Hugo explains. Thénardier's pursuer had been Javert, who after his unlooked-for release from the barricade had reported to the Prefect in person and gone straight back on duty, which included, as the note found on him had indicated, a surveillance of this stretch of the right bank; there he had seen Thénardier and followed him. The obligingly opened grating was a stroke of cleverness: Thénardier, who sensed that Javert was still there, had flung the sleuthhound a bone, putting an assassin outside in his own place, forcing the police off his scent, earning thirty francs and counting on escaping under cover of the diversion. Valjean had fallen from one danger onto another.","Javert did not recognise the disfigured man and asked who he was. \\"I.\\" \\"Who is 'I'?\\" \\"Jean Valjean.\\" Javert put his bludgeon between his teeth, clamped both hands on Valjean's shoulders like vices and scrutinised him face to face. Valjean stayed inert like a lion under a lynx's claws and said that Inspector Javert had him in his power, that he had considered himself his prisoner since the morning and had not given his address to escape him; he asked one favour. Javert, in a kind of savage reverie, released him and murmured, as though in a dream, \\"What are you doing here? And who is this man?\\", still not addressing him as \\"thou.\\" Valjean asked only that Javert help him carry the man home before disposing of him as he saw fit. Javert's face contracted as it always did when anyone thought him capable of a concession, but he did not say no. He wet his handkerchief in the river, wiped Marius's bloody brow and said, as if to himself, that this man had been at the barricade and was the one they called Marius; he had played the spy even in his death agony and taken notes. He felt the pulse and pronounced him a dead man. \\"No. Not yet,\\" said Valjean. Javert's preoccupation must have been profound, for he did not press the question of how the man had been brought from the barricade.","Valjean said the young man lived in the Marais with his grandfather but could not recall the name, and drew out the pocketbook open at Marius's pencilled lines; Javert read them with his night-bird's eyes and muttered the address, Gillenormand, Rue des Filles-du-Calvaire, No. 6, then shouted for the coachman, whose hackney-coach had been waiting all along. He kept the pocketbook. The carriage came down the watering ramp, Marius was laid on the back seat, Javert sat beside Valjean, and they drove rapidly up the quays towards the Bastille in a glacial silence, the lamplight flashing at intervals on the corpse, the spectre and the statue."],["A drop of blood fell from Marius's hair at every jolt. Night had fully come when the carriage stopped at No. 6 Rue des Filles-du-Calvaire. Javert got down first, checked the number, hammered the old knocker with its goat and satyr, and pushed the gate open on a yawning porter with a candle; the whole Marais goes to bed early on days of revolt, hiding its head under the covers like a child who hears the Bugaboo. Valjean and the coachman lifted Marius out, one under the arms and one under the knees, and as they carried him Valjean slipped a hand inside his torn clothes and found the heart beating, a little less feebly than before.","Javert addressed the porter in the tone of government to the servant of a factious house: was there a person called Gillenormand? His son was being brought back. He was dead. Valjean, mud-covered and tattered behind Javert, shook his head at the porter to say it was not so, but the man understood neither. Javert went on: he had gone to the barricade and got himself killed; go and wake his father; there would be a funeral here tomorrow. For Javert, Hugo notes, every incident of the public highway had its drawer: uproar, revolt, carnival, funeral.","The porter woke Basque, Basque woke Nicolette, and Nicolette roused great-aunt Gillenormand; the grandfather they let sleep, thinking he would learn soon enough. Marius was carried to the first floor and laid on an old sofa in M. Gillenormand's antechamber, and while Basque went for a doctor and Nicolette opened the linen presses, Valjean felt Javert touch his shoulder. He understood and went down with Javert's step behind him, the porter watching them go in terrified somnolence. In the carriage Valjean asked Inspector Javert for one more favour: to let him go home for an instant, after which Javert might do with him as he liked. Javert was silent a few moments, chin in his collar, then lowered the front glass and told the driver: Rue de l'Homme Armé, No. 7."],["They did not speak during the drive. Valjean wanted only to finish what he had begun: to warn Cosette, tell her where Marius was, and take what final measures he could. For himself all was over; he had been seized and had not resisted. Another man might have thought of the rope Thénardier had given him and of the bars of his first cell, but since the Bishop there had been in Valjean a profound hesitation before any violence, even against himself, and suicide, a violence against the unknown that may contain the death of the soul, was impossible to him.","The carriage stopped at the mouth of the Rue de l'Homme Armé, too narrow for vehicles. The coachman humbly pointed out to the inspector that his Utrecht velvet was stained with the murdered man's blood and the murderer's mud, that was how he understood it, and asked for an attestation; Javert pushed the certificate book aside, asked the fare for seven and a quarter hours of waiting and driving, eighty francs, paid four napoleons and dismissed him. Valjean supposed Javert meant to walk him to the nearby post of the Blancs-Manteaux or the Archives.","They walked down the deserted street to No. 7. Valjean knocked and the door opened. \\"It is well,\\" said Javert. \\"Go upstairs.\\" Then, with a strange expression and as though it cost him an effort: \\"I will wait for you here.\\" It was unlike Javert's habits, but Valjean, who had made up his mind to give himself up, was not greatly surprised that Javert should show him the haughty confidence of a cat that lets the mouse go to the length of its claws. He called \\"It is I!\\" to the porter and climbed the stairs. On the first floor he paused; the landing window was open onto the short street, lit from end to end by the lantern opposite, and he leaned out, for air or mechanically. He was overwhelmed with amazement. There was no longer anyone there. Javert had taken his departure."],["Marius had been carried into the drawing-room and the doctor had come. Aunt Gillenormand went to and fro wringing her hands, saying \\"Heavens! is it possible?\\" and that everything would be covered with blood, and at last that it was bound to end this way, stopping short of \\"I told you so\\"; when they began undressing Marius she withdrew to tell her beads. The doctor found the pulse beating and no deep wound in the chest, had Marius laid flat on a camp bed with his head low and his chest bare, and took stock: a bullet deadened by the pocketbook had torn round the ribs without entering, the broken collarbone had been badly dislocated by the long underground journey, the arms were slashed by sabre cuts, the face was unmarked but the head was covered with cuts whose depth could not yet be judged, and the swoon and the loss of blood were grave. Basque and Nicolette tore and rolled bandages, wadding stopped the bleeding for want of lint, and a pail of water reddened at once. The doctor shook his head from time to time, a bad sign.","A door opened at the end of the room and a long, pale figure appeared: the grandfather. The revolt had kept M. Gillenormand in a fever for two days; he had gone to bed early, exhausted, and the noise and the rift of light under his door had woken him. He stood on the threshold in a white dressing-gown as straight as a shroud, like a phantom looking into a tomb, and saw on the mattress the young man bleeding, waxen, eyes shut, slashed with crimson wounds under the candles. He trembled from head to foot, his face took the angles of a skull, and he murmured, \\"Marius!\\" Basque began to explain that Monsieur had been brought back from the barricade, and the old man cried in a terrible voice, \\"He is dead! Ah! The rascal!\\"","A sepulchral transformation straightened him like a young man. He demanded that the doctor tell him whether the boy was dead; the doctor, in the extreme of anxiety, said nothing. Gillenormand broke into terrible laughter: dead, killed on the barricades, out of hatred for him, to spite him, this was how he came home. He threw the window open and harangued the night: the villain knew his room had been kept ready and his childhood portrait hung at the head of his grandfather's bed, that he had only to come back and say \\"It is I\\" to be master of the house; and he had said no, the old man is a Royalist, and had gone to get himself killed to avenge a remark about the Duc de Berry. The doctor took his arm, and the old man told him calmly that he was composed, that he had seen Louis XVI die, that it was the newspapers and progress and the rights of man that brought children home like this, and then talked of the child he had raised: his little shovel in the Tuileries, the holes the grandfather filled with his cane so the inspectors would not grumble, the blond head people gathered to admire by the Hercules Farnese, the boy who could not say his Ds and chirped like a bird and was sunlight to him every morning. Children take hold of you and never let go.","Bending over the still, livid Marius he muttered reproaches in the voice of a dying man, heartless lad, clubbist, Septembrist, then, his voice coming as from the far side of an abyss, raged that he would die too, that every girl in Paris would have been glad to make the wretch happy and instead he had gone and got himself shot for the Republic and for General Lamarque's handsome eyes, that there would be two funerals under one carriage gate, that at a hundred years old this blow was what he had hoped for, and that he would not even grieve, did he hear, assassin? At that moment Marius slowly opened his eyes, and his gaze, still dim with lethargic wonder, rested on M. Gillenormand. \\"Marius! My little Marius! My child! My well-beloved son! You open your eyes, you gaze upon me, you are alive, thanks!\\" cried the old man, and fell fainting."],["Javert walks slowly down the Rue de l'Homme Armé with his head bowed and his hands behind his back, two things he has never done in his life. He makes for the Seine and stops at the angle of the Pont Notre-Dame, above the stretch of river that mariners dread, where the current between two bridges forms a whirlpool from which no one who falls in reappears. Leaning on the parapet, he examines himself. For the first time his conscience is divided: he owes his life to a convict and has just repaid the debt by letting that convict go. To arrest Jean Valjean is wrong; to leave him free is wrong. Either way Javert is dishonoured.","Hugo follows the collapse of a mind that has never had to think. All Javert's axioms crumble before this man. M. Madeleine reappears behind Jean Valjean and the two figures fuse into one venerable figure, and Javert finds himself admiring a galley-slave. He recalls that twenty times in the carriage he was tempted to seize Valjean's collar, and each time his hand fell back as under an enormous weight, while a voice told him to deliver up his saviour and then wash his hands like Pilate. He discovers that there is something beyond duty, a superior above the Prefect, God, and he does not know how to hand in his resignation to that superior. Infallibility is not infallible; the law may err; there is a gulf on high. Only two ways out remain: to go back and arrest Jean Valjean, or the other.","Javert leaves the parapet with his head erect, enters the police post in the Place du Châtelet, and in his calmest handwriting composes 'A few observations for the good of the service': ten numbered complaints about barefoot prisoners catching cold, chairs forbidden at the Madelonettes, the canteen bars, the 'barkers' who extort two sous, gendarmes gossiping about interrogations, a woman keeping the wicket to the secret cells. He signs it 'Javert, Inspector of the 1st class, June 7th, 1832, about one o'clock in the morning', seals it as a 'Note for the administration', and returns to exactly the same paving-stone of the parapet.","The night is black and starless, the river swollen with rain. Javert stares down into the roaring dark for several minutes, takes off his hat and sets it on the edge of the quay, and a tall black figure appears upright on the parapet, bends towards the Seine, and falls straight down into the shadows. A dull splash follows. Only the darkness knows what became of the shape that vanished beneath the water."],["Hugo returns to Boulatruelle, the road-mender and part-time thief of Montfermeil who dreams of treasure buried in the forest. He had been picked up with the other ruffians in Jondrette's garret, but his well-attested drunkenness that night saved him: the courts could not tell whether he was a robber or a victim, dropped the charge, and he went back to breaking stones on the Gagny–Lagny road, his taste for theft somewhat cooled and his taste for wine undiminished.","One morning before daybreak he sees through the branches a man whose back and shoulders seem familiar, though he cannot place him; he recalls only that many years before a man had given him the same uneasy feeling. The stranger has clearly walked all night from Paris, with no bundle. Boulatruelle thinks of the treasure, takes his sharp pickaxe, and follows the trail of footprints and bent branches. When the track fails he climbs a great beech and spots his man slipping into a distant glade he knows well, the Blaru-bottom, marked by a heap of porous stones and an ailing chestnut-tree bandaged with a sheet of zinc nailed to its bark.","He drops from the tree and, trusting the straight line through the brambles rather than the winding paths, takes forty minutes to reach the glade, scratched, soaked and furious. The man is gone. The heap of stones is in place, but behind it, before the zinc-plastered tree, lies freshly turned earth, a pickaxe left behind, and a hole. The hole is empty. 'Thief!' Boulatruelle shrieks at the horizon."],["For weeks after the night of the barricade Marius lies in fever and delirium at the Rue des Filles-du-Calvaire, repeating Cosette's name, his head wounds and suppurating lesions a constant danger. The doctor forbids all emotion. Every day, sometimes twice, a very well-dressed gentleman with white hair calls at the porter's lodge to ask after the wounded man and leaves a large package of lint for the dressings. On the 7th of September, four months to the day, the doctor answers for Marius's life; two more months on a long chair follow because of his broken collarbone. The long illness also shields him from prosecution: public indignation at the order requiring doctors to inform on the wounded, shared by the King himself, means the councils of war leave the insurgents who were not taken in arms alone.","M. Gillenormand passes from anguish to ecstasy. He has his armchair carried to the bedside, insists on the finest linen for lint, cries 'Aïe!' when dead flesh is cut away, and asks the doctor the same questions over and over. On the day Marius is pronounced out of danger he gives the porter three louis, dances a gavotte, sings a bawdy song, and is seen by Basque kneeling on a chair in prayer, he who had never believed in God. He sends a bouquet to a pretty neighbour, calls Marius 'M. le Baron', shouts 'Long live the Republic!', and becomes, in Hugo's phrase, the grandson of his grandson.","Marius, meanwhile, has one fixed idea: Cosette. He does not know what has become of her, remembers the barricade as a cloud of shadows (Éponine, Gavroche, Mabeuf, the Thénardiers, his friends), finds the passage of M. Fauchelevent through it a puzzle, and has no idea how or by whom he was saved; all anyone can tell him is that he was brought home at night in a hackney-coach. He is barely softened by his grandfather's tenderness, distrusts it as a ploy, and never once calls him 'father'. As his strength returns so does the memory of his father's wrongs. He tests the ground by praising the men of '93; the old man falls silent all day, which Marius reads as suppressed wrath. He resolves that if he is refused he will tear off his bandages and refuse food: he will have Cosette or die."],["One day, as M. Gillenormand gently recommends a cutlet rather than fried sole, Marius gathers all his strength, sits up, fixes him with a terrible look and declares: 'I wish to marry.' 'Agreed,' says the grandfather, and bursts out laughing. He has made his inquiries: the girl comes every day in the shape of an old gentleman to ask after Marius, she has spent the time weeping and making lint, she lives at No. 7 Rue de l'Homme Armé, the story about the lancer was false, she is a jewel. He had thought of simply planting her at the bedside, but that is only done in novels. 'Take her. Such is my ferocity.' He confesses he saw that Marius did not love him and hoped that giving him Cosette would make him love him a little.","The old man dissolves into sobs, clasps Marius's head to his breast, and both weep. 'Father!' cries Marius; 'Ah, so you love me!' answers the grandfather. Marius asks whether he may see her; tomorrow, says the old man, then today, since Marius has said 'father' three times and it is worth it.","The chapter ends in comedy. The grandfather quotes André Chénier, blunders into saying his throat was cut by 'the ras... by the giants of '93', sees a frown on Marius's face, tries to retract and tangles himself hopelessly, and rushes purple-faced from the room to seize Basque by the collar in the anteroom and bellow that 'those ruffians did assassinate him!' 'Who, sir?' 'André Chénier!' 'Yes, sir,' says Basque in alarm."],["Cosette enters Marius's chamber before the whole household, including Basque and Nicolette, and the grandfather, caught mid-way through blowing his nose, exclaims 'Adorable!' She is frightened and in heaven, longing to throw herself into Marius's arms and not daring. Behind her comes a grave white-haired man with a heartrending smile, 'Monsieur Fauchelevent': the narrator says plainly that it is Jean Valjean, in new black clothes and a white cravat. The porter is far from recognising in this correct bourgeois the mud-covered, bloodied man who brought Marius home on the night of the 7th of June, though something about the face nags at him. Valjean stands apart by the door with a mouldy-looking package under his arm that Mademoiselle Gillenormand takes for a book.","M. Gillenormand, careless with names, bows to 'Monsieur Tranchelevent' and asks on behalf of his grandson, Baron Marius Pontmercy, for the hand of Mademoiselle. The other bows. 'Permission to adore each other!' The lovers chatter in low voices: Cosette scolds him for going to fight, tells him she has been dead for four months, that they still live in the Rue de l'Homme Armé with no garden, and shows the callus on her fingers from making lint. The grandfather tells everyone else to make a noise so the children can talk, and lectures Cosette on marriage, churches and the folly of spinsterhood. Asked about his close friend, Marius names Courfeyrac and says he is dead; 'That is good,' says the grandfather absently. Aunt Gillenormand watches this irruption of light into her failed life with stupefied innocence.","Taking all four hands in his, the old man suddenly turns gloomy: more than half his fortune is in an annuity, and after his death the children will not have a sou. A grave, tranquil voice answers from behind them: 'Mademoiselle Euphrasie Fauchelevent possesses six hundred thousand francs.' 'I am she,' says Cosette. Jean Valjean opens the package: five hundred thousand-franc notes and one hundred and sixty-eight of five hundred, five hundred and eighty-four thousand francs in all. 'This is a fine book,' says M. Gillenormand, and jokes that Marius has found a millionaire grisette in his tree of dreams, while the aunt murmurs the sum over and over. Marius and Cosette, gazing at each other, scarcely notice."],["Hugo ties up the forest mystery. After the Champmathieu affair, during his first brief escape, Jean Valjean reached Paris and withdrew from Laffitte's bank the six hundred and thirty thousand francs he had earned at Montreuil-sur-Mer under the name of Madeleine. Fearing recapture, which indeed followed, he buried the banknotes in a box packed in a coffer of chestnut shavings in the forest of Montfermeil, in the glade called the Blaru-bottom, and with them the Bishop's candlesticks, which he had carried off when he fled Montreuil-sur-Mer. The man Boulatruelle once saw in the forest at evening was Valjean; so was the man he saw at dawn in the last chapter. Whenever he needed money he went to the Blaru-bottom, which accounts for the absences mentioned earlier. Seeing Marius convalescent and the moment at hand, he dug up the whole sum and left Boulatruelle his pickaxe.","The actual sum was five hundred and eighty-four thousand five hundred francs; Valjean kept back five hundred for himself, thinking 'We shall see hereafter.' The difference from the original six hundred and thirty thousand represents ten years of expenses from 1823 to 1833, the five years in the convent having cost only five thousand francs. He sets the two candlesticks on his chimneypiece, where Toussaint admires them.","He also knows that he is delivered from Javert. The story has been told in his hearing and he has checked it in the Moniteur: a police inspector named Javert was found drowned under a laundry-boat between the Pont au Change and the Pont-Neuf, and a paper he left behind, from a man otherwise irreproachable and esteemed by his superiors, pointed to mental derangement and suicide. 'In fact,' Valjean thinks, 'since he let me go once he had me in his power, he must already have been mad.'"],["The doctor allows the wedding for February; it is now December, and weeks of perfect happiness pass. Marius and Cosette have gone from the sepulchre to paradise without transition and understand nothing of it; Cosette thinks the good God is looking after them. Jean Valjean smooths every difficulty. Having been a mayor, he knows how to solve the problem only he understands, Cosette's civil status: he invents for her a family of dead people. She is the daughter not of himself but of the other Fauchelevent, his brother, both once gardeners at the Petit-Picpus; the nuns, never clear which Fauchelevent she belonged to, give glowing references, and an acte de notoriété makes her legally Mademoiselle Euphrasie Fauchelevent, orphan. Valjean, as Fauchelevent, is appointed her guardian, with M. Gillenormand supervising. The five hundred and eighty-odd thousand francs become a legacy from a dead benefactor who wished to remain unknown, reduced from five hundred and ninety-four thousand by the cost of her education. The oddities go unnoticed, one party blinded by love and the others by the money.","Cosette learns that the old man she has so long called father is only a kinsman. At any other time it would have broken her heart; now it is a faint passing cloud, for she has Marius, and she has long been used to enigmas around her. She goes on calling him father nonetheless. She is enchanted by Father Gillenormand, who rifles his lacquered chests for Binche guipure, lace, moiré, India kerchiefs and antique jewels, adds a tea-rose moiré antique gown after Marius calls the revolutionaries 'antique memories', and expounds his philosophy of the superfluous: happiness is only the necessary, and dry happiness is dry bread. He delivers a vast tirade on how the nineteenth century has forgotten how to marry, with garters, violins under the trees, Dryads and Nereids.","Aunt Gillenormand surveys it all with placidity; the six hundred thousand francs were her last surprise. Piqued that her father never consulted her about the marriage, she had reserved the question of her inheritance, and would likely have left a poor nephew poor; but one owes consideration to six hundred thousand francs, so she will leave her fortune to the young people, who do not need it. The couple are to live with the grandfather, who gives up his own chamber, hangs it with buttercup satin he says once draped a duchess's bed, and turns his library into the lawyer's study the Order requires of Marius."],["The lovers meet every day, Cosette chaperoned by M. Fauchelevent, which Mademoiselle Gillenormand finds backwards: the bride coming to court. Marius and Fauchelevent see each other but hardly speak; Fauchelevent is the condition attached to Cosette and Marius accepts him. They edge towards conversation only on general politics and once, on free and compulsory education, nearly agree. Fauchelevent talks well, even loftily, yet lacks something indefinable: something less and something more than a man of the world.","Marius surrounds this cold, benevolent man with silent questions. Four months of agony have dug a black hole in his memory, and he has reached the point of wondering whether he really saw so calm and serious a man in the barricade. Other ghosts trouble him too: Mabeuf falling, Gavroche singing in the grapeshot, Éponine's cold brow under his lips, Enjolras, Courfeyrac, Prouvaire, Combeferre, Bossuet, Grantaire rising and dispersing like smoke. Were they dreams? Is he the same man, the poor man now rich, the abandoned man now with a family, who entered a tomb black and came out white while the others stayed inside?","Fauchelevent almost belongs among these vanished beings, and Marius half decides the Fauchelevent of the barricade was a nightmare of his delirium. Both men are too rigid for a direct question. Once only Marius tries, mentioning the Rue de la Chanvrerie and asking, 'Of course you know that street?' 'I have no idea of the name of that street,' Fauchelevent replies in the most natural manner. The answer, which bears on the name and not the street, strikes Marius as more conclusive than it is. 'Decidedly,' he thinks, 'I have been dreaming. M. Fauchelevent was not there.'"],["Before entering joyfully into the future Marius wants a quittance from the past. He owes two debts of gratitude, one for his father and one for himself: to Thénardier, who saved Colonel Pontmercy at Waterloo (Marius does not know the real scene there, and that his father owed Thénardier his life without owing him thanks), and to the unknown man who brought him home. His agents find no trace of Thénardier. Madame Thénardier has died in prison awaiting trial; Thénardier and his daughter Azelma have sunk back into the dark. The Gorbeau ambush case has collapsed: with Claquesous vanished and the chief accused escaped, the Assizes could only sentence two underlings, Panchaud alias Bigrenaille and Demi-Liard, to ten years in the galleys; the fugitives got hard labour for life in their absence, and Thénardier, as ringleader, was condemned to death in absentia, which drives him deeper into hiding.","The search for his rescuer begins well and stops dead. The hackney-coachman is found: on the 6th of June, on a police agent's orders, he waited from three in the afternoon until nightfall on the Quai des Champs-Élysées above the outlet of the Grand Sewer; towards nine the grating opened and a man came out carrying another who seemed dead; the agent arrested the living man and seized the body; they drove to the Rue des Filles-du-Calvaire and left the dead man, who was Marius, then on near the Archives, where the agent paid the coachman and led the other man away. Marius remembers only a hand seizing him from behind as he fell in the barricade. Someone carried him from the Halles to the Champs-Élysées through the sewer. Who?","The prefecture knows nothing of any arrest at the sewer mouth on the 6th of June and treats the coachman's story as a fable invented for a tip. Yet Marius cannot doubt it without doubting his own existence. Why does this man give no sign? Did he escape the agent? Is he dead? Basque and Nicolette saw only their bleeding master; the porter, the only witness, can say just that 'that man was terrible.' Marius keeps his bloodstained clothes in case they help; one skirt of the coat has a piece torn away.","One evening, before Cosette and Jean Valjean, Marius describes the fruitless search and, irritated by 'Monsieur Fauchelevent's' cold face, exclaims that the man was sublime, an archangel who carried a corpse a league and a half through the sewers for the sake of a spark of life, and was arrested for it. 'Oh, if Cosette's six hundred thousand francs were mine...' 'They are yours,' Valjean interrupts. 'Well, I would give them all to find that man again.' Jean Valjean remains silent."],["The night of the 16th to 17th of February 1833 is the wedding night of Marius and Cosette, and Hugo opens with a mock-elegy for the old fashion of marrying at home, before France learned from England to flee in a post-chaise. The banns and papers could not be ready before the 16th, which fell on Shrove Tuesday; the aunt hesitates, the grandfather quotes a proverb promising that a Mardi Gras marriage has no ungrateful children, and the day is kept despite the public merrymaking. It rains. The evening before, Jean Valjean handed Marius the five hundred and eighty-four thousand francs in M. Gillenormand's presence; the marriage being under community of property, the papers were simple. Toussaint passes to Cosette as lady's maid, and a fine chamber in the Gillenormand house is furnished for Valjean, whom Cosette has almost made promise to occupy it.","A few days before the wedding Valjean crushed the thumb of his right hand, would let no one dress or even see the hurt, and has carried the arm in a sling ever since, which prevents him from signing; M. Gillenormand, as supervising guardian, signed in his place. Repaving blocks the direct road to Saint-Paul, so the carriages turn through the boulevard among the Carnival crowds, the grandfather delighted that the young folk will see 'a bit of the masquerade'. The first coach carries Cosette, Aunt Gillenormand, M. Gillenormand and Jean Valjean; Marius, by custom, follows in the second. Hugo digresses on the cartloads of maskers, their ancient tradition and the suspicious, government-sponsored laughter of the Carnival.","At the Pont-aux-Choux both files of vehicles stop, and a calash full of maskers halts opposite the bride's carriage. Two of them, an old 'Spaniard' with a huge nose and black moustache and a gaunt young fishwife in a half-mask, talk under the uproar. The Spaniard is certain he knows 'that old cove' in the first carriage with his arm 'hung up in a black cravat', and guesses from his place that he is the bride's father. He wants the girl to jump down and follow the wedding; she cannot, because she is hired for the day by the prefecture and would be arrested if she left the cart, and he himself can only go out masked and must go back into his hole on Ash Wednesday. He insists she find out what that wedding is, whom the old man belongs to and where the couple live. 'You understand me, Azelma.' The files move on and the maskers lose sight of the bride's 'trap'."],["Cosette, dressed by Toussaint and Nicolette in white taffeta, Binche guipure, English point, pearls and orange blossom, is dazzling at the mayor's office and at church; beneath Marius's curls the pale scars of the barricade show. The grandfather, resplendent in the elegance of the Barras era, escorts the bride, taking the place of Jean Valjean, whose arm is still in its sling. Valjean follows them in black, smiling, while M. Gillenormand decrees joy and the abolition of unhappiness. After the vows, the registers, the rings and the white moiré pall, the couple emerge hand in hand, Cosette still unable to believe it is real. In the carriage home the grandfather hails 'Monsieur le Baron and Madame la Baronne, with an income of thirty thousand livres', and Cosette whispers to Marius, 'My name is Marius. I am Madame Thou.' They promise each other to go back and look at their little garden in the Rue Plumet.","At the Rue des Filles-du-Calvaire Marius climbs, triumphant, the staircase up which he was carried dying; the poor at the door bless them; flowers are everywhere. Captain Théodule Gillenormand has come from Chartres for his cousin's wedding; Cosette does not recognise him and he remembers her no more than any other woman, so the grandfather congratulates himself on never having believed the lancer story. Cosette has never been more tender with Jean Valjean, speaking to him in the inflections of her childhood. Before dinner she finds him on a chair half hidden behind the drawing-room door, curtsies deeply in her bridal dress, and asks, 'Father, are you satisfied?' 'Yes, I am content.' 'Well, then, laugh.' And Jean Valjean laughs.","In the brilliant dining-room, with its Venetian chandelier of coloured glass birds and a Haydn quartet playing in the antechamber, two armchairs flank the bride: one for M. Gillenormand, one for Jean Valjean. The second stays empty. Basque reports that M. Fauchelevent asked him to say his injured hand was paining him, that he could not dine with the Baron and Baroness, begged to be excused and would come tomorrow, and has just left. The empty chair chills the table for a moment; then the grandfather, beaming for two, gives the seat to Marius, 'Fortunatus beside Fortunata', and Cosette, saddened at first, is soon content to have her husband beside her and sets her white satin foot on his. Within five minutes the whole table is laughing with the animation of forgetfulness.","At dessert the grandfather rises with a half-filled glass and delivers his sermon: adore each other, give rein to your joys, down with the philosophers who preach moderation; woman is the one royalty no revolution has toppled; love is six thousand years old and still new; be Daphnis and Chloe while waiting to become Philemon and Baucis. The evening is gay, there is dancing, and a little after midnight the married pair disappear and the Gillenormand house becomes a temple. Hugo pauses on the threshold of the wedding night with a meditation on love as the one true happiness."],["Immediately after laughing at Cosette's command, while no one is watching, Jean Valjean rises and slips into the antechamber, the very room he entered eight months earlier black with mud and blood, carrying the grandson to the grandfather; now the musicians sit on the sofa where Marius was laid down, and Basque in knee-breeches arranges roses round the dishes. Valjean points to his sling, charges Basque to explain his absence, and goes out. He stands for several minutes in the dark beneath the radiant dining-room windows, listening to the grandfather's voice, the violins, the plates, the laughter, and through it all Cosette's sweet, joyous voice.","He walks back to the Rue de l'Homme Armé by the slightly longer way through the Rue Saint-Louis and the Blancs-Manteaux, the road he has taken every day for three months because Cosette passed along it. The apartment is empty; even Toussaint is gone. The cupboards stand open. In Cosette's room the bed is stripped, the pillow bare, all her small feminine belongings carried away; only one bed in the flat is made up, his own. He wanders from room to room closing cupboard doors, then sets his candle in his chamber. He takes his arm out of the sling and uses the right hand as if it did not hurt at all.","His eyes fall on the little portmanteau that never leaves him, the 'inseparable' of which Cosette was jealous, set on a round table by his bed since the 4th of June. He unlocks it and slowly draws out the clothes in which, ten years before, Cosette left Montfermeil: the little gown, the black fichu, the stout coarse shoes, the thick fustian bodice, the knitted petticoat, the apron with pockets, the woollen stockings no longer than his hand, all black. He had brought those mourning clothes to Montfermeil for her himself, that cold December when she shivered in rags with red feet in wooden shoes, and he remembers walking through the leafless forest hand in hand with a child who had no one in the world but him, her big doll in her arms and a louis d'or in her apron pocket. He lays the garments out on the bed, looks at them one by one, and then his white head falls forward into them and that stoical old heart breaks; anyone on the stairs would have heard frightful sobs."],["The old, formidable struggle begins again. Jacob wrestled with the angel for one night; Hugo recalls how many times Jean Valjean has been seized by his conscience in the dark, overthrown by the truth, dazzled against his will by the spark the Bishop lit in him, and left at the end bleeding but at peace. Tonight he feels he is passing through his final combat. Once more two roads open before him, one tempting and one alarming, the terrible port and the smiling ambush, and the mysterious finger in the darkness points him to the one that alarms him.","The question is this: how is he to behave towards the happiness of Cosette and Marius? He willed it and made it; they have everything, even riches, and it is his doing. But must he now force himself on that happiness? Remain the half-seen, respected father he has been? Bring his past, unspoken, to their future, and seat himself veiled at that bright fireside, placing feet that drag the shadow of the law on the Gillenormand fender? Be the silent mute of destiny beside two happy beings? Cosette is the raft of his shipwreck: if he clings to it he is saved and lives; if he lets go, the abyss.","He fights, now against his will, now against his conviction, and the past surges up before the present. Hugo pleads on his behalf: is there no limit to the obedience of the soul, can perpetual self-sacrifice be exacted, who would blame Sisyphus or Jean Valjean for saying 'It is enough'? What was the Champmathieu affair beside this; what is a return to the galleys beside entrance into the void? Martyrdom has its throne, crown and sceptre of red-hot iron, but the mantle of flame still remains to be put on. At last he reaches the peace of exhaustion and weighs the two sacrifices, Cosette's or his own.","What he resolves the chapter does not say. He stays until daylight bent double over the bed, arms flung out like a man unnailed from a cross and thrown face down, twelve hours of a winter night, ice-cold, motionless as a corpse, his thoughts crawling and soaring. Once he shudders and his mouth, pressed to Cosette's garments, kisses them, and so one sees he is alive. Who could see? No one was there, except the One who is in the shadows."],["A little past midday on the 17th of February, Basque admits M. Fauchelevent to the disordered drawing-room, where fallen flowers and guttered candles still speak of the feast. Valjean asks that his name not be announced. He is deathly pale, hollow-eyed, his coat creased from a night without sleep. Marius bounds in, radiant and sleepless too, and calls him 'father' for the first time, pouring out plans: they will have no more of the Rue de l'Homme Armé; his chamber here is ready, facing south on the garden, with a nightingale in the acacias, a place of honour for the little valise, whist with the grandfather, walks with Cosette as in the Luxembourg days. 'Sir,' says Jean Valjean, 'I have something to say to you. I am an ex-convict.'","Marius cannot take the words in. Valjean unties the black cravat, unrolls the linen from his hand and shows the thumb: there was never anything wrong with it; he invented the injury so as not to sign and put a forgery into the marriage papers. He was nineteen years in the galleys for theft, then condemned for life as a second offender, and has broken his ban. Marius, horrified, cries, 'You are Cosette's father!' Valjean draws himself up: before God, no. He is a peasant of Faverolles, a pruner of trees; his name is not Fauchelevent but Jean Valjean; he is nothing to Cosette, a passer-by who ten years ago did not know she existed, who took in an orphan who needed him. Cosette now passes out of his life into a better providence. The six hundred thousand francs were a deposit, and he restores it, completing the restitution by giving his true name.","Pressed by Marius as to why he confesses when no one pursues him, Valjean answers: out of honesty, and because of a thread in his heart he could not break. He could have stayed Monsieur Fauchelevent, had the chamber, lived as one family; and at that word he becomes wild. He belongs to no family. To keep silent would have been a lie lived every day, the galleys introduced to their fireside, Cosette condemned to him forever, three heads in the green cap. Conscience is a grasp like a fist in his own collar: 'I am tracked. By whom? By myself.' Fauchelevent could lend him his name but he could not take it; a name is an I; in days gone by he stole a loaf to live, today to live he will not steal a name. He points to his dragging leg: 'Now you understand why.' And he asks Marius to imagine the police one day tearing off the mask in front of them all. Marius, convinced, offers a hand that Valjean does not offer back, and promises to procure his pardon through the grandfather's friends; Valjean says it is useless, he is believed dead, and the only pardon he needs is his conscience's.","Cosette, in a voluminous white dressing-gown, puts her head round the door, accuses them of talking politics on the morning after a wedding, demands a kiss from her father (who gives her the smile of a spectre) and is sent away by Marius with talk of investing her six hundred thousand francs. When she is gone Marius murmurs, 'Poor Cosette, when she finds out...', and Valjean, trembling, begs him on his most sacred word never to tell her; she saw the chain-gang pass once. He weeps silently, murmuring that he wishes he could die. Marius promises to keep the secret for himself alone, and, with cooling distance, offers a recompense for the honestly returned deposit, which Valjean gently declines. Then Valjean asks whether he ought not to see Cosette any more; 'I think that would be better,' Marius says coldly. 'I shall never see her more,' Valjean murmurs, reaches the door, and turns back livid: if he had not wished to go on seeing Cosette he would simply have gone away; it was in order to stay near her that he had to tell the truth. He asks to come rarely, briefly, by the ordinary door, to be received in the little ground-floor waiting-room at nightfall. 'You shall come every evening,' says Marius, 'and Cosette will be waiting for you.' Happiness escorts despair to the door."],["Marius is upset. The estrangement he always felt towards the man beside Cosette is explained: M. Fauchelevent is the convict Jean Valjean, a scorpion found in a nest of turtledoves. Has he wedded the convict as well? He asks whether he has anything to reproach himself with and admits his dreamy, visionary side: in the six or seven ecstatic weeks of the Rue Plumet he never even told Cosette about the Gorbeau drama or named the Thénardiers or Éponine, half out of intoxication and half out of a dull instinct to bury an adventure in which he could only have been an accuser. Yet had he known everything, would he have loved or married her any less? No. Love bandaged his eyes to lead him to paradise, though that paradise now has an infernal accompaniment.","His horror is mixed with pity and surprise. This twice-convicted thief restored a deposit of six hundred thousand francs that he alone knew of; he revealed himself when nothing forced him, rejecting the shelter of a false name, out of a scruple of conscience. Whatever he is, he is a conscience awakening, and his sincerity makes inquiry useless. Strange reversal: M. Fauchelevent inspired distrust, Jean Valjean inspires confidence. But the cloud returns. Why did that man flee the police at the Jondrette garret instead of lodging a complaint? Because he was a fugitive who had broken his ban. Why had he come to the barricade? Marius now distinctly remembers Valjean dragging the bound Javert out of the barricade and the pistol shot behind the corner of the Rue Mondétour. Obviously there was hatred between the spy and the galley-slave; Valjean went there for vengeance and, Marius concludes, killed Javert.","One question has no answer: how did this man's life come to run alongside Cosette's for so long? What bound the lamb to the wolf, and, stranger still, the wolf to the lamb, so that for nine years the angel had the monster as her support? Marius sees in this Jean Valjean's secret and also God's: the workman was horrible, the work admirable; the dung-heap has helped the spring make the rose before. He clings to the declaration 'I am nothing to Cosette', decides Valjean's part is finished and that he, Marius, is now Cosette's providence, and still returns to horror. On penal questions Marius, democrat though he is, holds the inexorable view that a convict is lower than the lowest rung, and his final gesture is to turn away his head.","Hugo insists on one thing: Marius did not put the two or three decisive questions (the Jondrette attic, the barricade, Javert) because he was afraid of the answers and of where a hideous glare might reach, perhaps to Cosette. He already knew too much and preferred to dull his senses. He now regrets the promise he let himself be drawn into; he should simply have rejected the man and freed his house of him. Still, one must keep one's word even to a convict. He hides his trouble from Cosette and, questioning her casually about her childhood, becomes ever more convinced that this convict was everything good and paternal a man could be to her: the sinister nettle had loved and protected the lily."],["At nightfall Basque is waiting in the courtyard, as though on orders, to ask whether monsieur wishes to go upstairs or remain below. 'I will remain below.' The waiting-room is a small vaulted cellar paved with red tiles and lit by a grated window, with a black spider's web wheel on one pane, a heap of empty bottles in a corner, a flaking ochre wall and a narrow black chimneypiece where, tellingly, a fire has already been lit: his answer was foreseen. Two armchairs and a threadbare rug stand by the hearth. Valjean, who has neither eaten nor slept for days, sinks into a chair and does not notice Basque bring a candle.","Suddenly Cosette is beside him. She knows he is peculiar but never expected this; Marius told her he wished to be received here. She demands a kiss and offers her cheek, then, quoting Christ, the other; he does not move, as if nailed to the floor. She orders him upstairs to dine; impossible. He slips into 'thou', catches himself, and says, 'You know, madame, that I am peculiar.' Madame! She is to call him 'Monsieur Jean', or 'Jean'; since she is Madame Pontmercy he can be Monsieur Jean. Bewildered and hurt, she presses his hands to her neck and begs him to be good, which means coming to live with them, leaving the Rue de l'Homme Armé, and being her father. 'You no longer need a father, you have a husband.' He takes refuge in Toussaint's authority for his old ways and changes the subject to a pretty rosewood toilet table he saw in a shop window, and she blows at him like an angry cat.","She rages that everyone has been tormenting her since yesterday, that her carefully arranged chamber is left on her hands, that he wants to be received in a cellar where the walls have beards. Then, suddenly serious: 'Are you angry with me because I am happy?' The question, simple for her, lacerates him. He turns pale and murmurs, half to himself, that her happiness was the object of his life, that God may sign his dismissal, 'Cosette, thou art happy; my day is over.' 'Ah, you have said thou to me!' She springs to his neck and he strains her wildly to him as if taking her back, then gently detaches her arms, takes his hat, and from the threshold says: 'I have said thou to you. Tell your husband that this shall not happen again. Pardon me.' He leaves Cosette stupefied by this enigmatic farewell."],["The next day Valjean returns at the same hour and Cosette asks nothing, no longer complains of the cold or the cellar, avoids both 'father' and 'Monsieur Jean', and lets herself be called 'you' and 'Madame'; only her joy is slightly dimmed. She has evidently had with Marius one of those conversations in which the beloved man explains nothing and satisfies the beloved woman. Basque has removed the bottles and Nicolette the spiders. Valjean comes every day because he cannot take Marius's words other than literally; Marius arranges to be out at those hours. The house grows used to 'M. Fauchelevent's' ways: Toussaint says monsieur has always been like that, and the grandfather, who at ninety-six wants no new attachments, settles the matter with 'He's an original', citing a marquis who bought a palace to live in its garret.","No one glimpses the sinister foundation. Hugo compares such a man to an Indian marsh that ripples without wind, a surface disturbed by a hydra on the bottom; many men carry a secret dragon, and the small singularities of the solitary, the side door, the deserted street, the candle left at the porter's lodge, often proceed from a formidable depth.","Weeks pass. Cosette's new life fills with visits, housekeeping and, above all, being with Marius, walking arm in arm with him in the open street before the whole world. Toussaint cannot get on with Nicolette and leaves. Valjean comes every day. The 'you', the 'Madame', the 'Monsieur Jean' make him another person to her; the care he has taken to detach her is succeeding, and she becomes gayer and less tender, though she still loves him and he feels it. One day she says: you were my father, then my uncle, then Monsieur Fauchelevent, now Jean; who are you? If I did not know how good you are I should be afraid of you. He still lives in the Rue de l'Homme Armé because he cannot bear to move away from her quarter, and as the days lengthen his visits grow longer. Once she chances to call him 'father'; a flash of joy lights his face and he catches her up: 'Say Jean.' 'Ah! truly, Monsieur Jean,' she laughs. 'That is right,' he says, and turns away so she will not see him wipe his eyes."],["That was the last time; after that flash, extinction. Valjean has lost Cosette wholly in one day and must now lose her again in detail, yet his whole life is concentrated in the daily hour when he sits beside her and talks of her childhood, the convent and her little friends. One warm afternoon in early April Marius proposes they go back to their garden in the Rue Plumet, whose lease still belongs to Cosette, and they fly off like two swallows; that evening Basque tells Valjean that Madame has gone out with Monsieur and has not returned. He waits an hour and leaves with drooping head. Next day Cosette talks of nothing but having 'lived a whole day in her past' and does not notice she missed him.","Valjean has observed the young couple's severe economy and is troubled. Why no carriage of their own, when a coupé costs only five hundred francs a month? Why has Toussaint not been replaced? 'Have I not Marius?' says Cosette. He urges her to profit by her riches; she does not answer. To prolong his visits he praises Marius, and she outdoes him; Basque has to come twice to say dinner is served. One day there is no fire in the hearth; he tells himself it is April. Cosette exclaims at the cold and learns he told Basque not to light it 'since it is May'; next day there is a fire but the armchairs have been moved to the far end by the door, and he carries them back. Then Cosette reports something odd her husband said: of their thirty thousand livres, twenty-seven are hers and three his grandfather's; would she have the courage to live on the three? She said yes, on nothing, with him.","Valjean finds no word to answer and, walking home, enters the wrong house and climbs two floors before noticing. He concludes that Marius doubts the origin of the six hundred thousand francs, perhaps has even discovered it comes from him, and would rather be poor than rich on unclean money; and he begins to sense that he is being shown the door. The next day the armchairs are gone altogether, and he stammers that he himself told Basque to remove them because he can only stay a few minutes. Cosette shrugs at his oddities. 'Adieu!' he murmurs, unable to say either 'Adieu, Cosette' or 'Adieu, Madame'. This time he has understood.","He does not come the next day; Cosette notices only in the evening and is at once diverted by a kiss. Nor the day after. She sends Nicolette to ask whether he is ill: M. Jean replies that he is not ill but busy, will come soon, is about to take one of his little journeys, and they are not to think of him. Nicolette repeats that Madame wants to know why he did not come 'yesterday evening'. 'It is two days since I have been there,' Valjean says gently; the remark goes unnoticed and unreported."],["In the last months of spring and the first of summer 1833 the shopkeepers and loungers of the Marais notice an old man neatly dressed in black who comes out of the Rue de l'Homme Armé every day towards nightfall, passes the Blancs-Manteaux and the Rue Culture-Sainte-Catherine, and turns into the Rue Saint-Louis. There he walks slowly, head thrust forward, his eyes fixed on one point that seems a star to him, the corner of the Rue des Filles-du-Calvaire. The nearer he comes the more his eyes light up and the slower he goes, as though he fears the moment of arrival, like a magnetic needle trembling towards the pole. At last he reaches the corner, puts his head round the last house with melancholy timidity, gazes into the street with a look like the reflection of a closed paradise, and a single tear rolls down and sometimes stops at his mouth, where he tastes its bitterness. Then he goes back by the same road, his glance dying as he retreats.","Little by little he stops short of the corner: halfway along the Rue Saint-Louis, then at the Rue Culture-Sainte-Catherine, where he looks at the distant street and shakes his head as if refusing himself something; then the Rue Pavée, the Rue des Trois-Pavillons, finally no further than the Blancs-Manteaux. He is a pendulum no longer wound up, its swings shortening before they cease. Every day he leaves at the same hour on the same walk and never completes it, shortening it perhaps without knowing. His face says one thing: what is the use? His eye is dim and his tears are exhausted; his chin trembles; the folds of his gaunt neck are painful to see; in bad weather he carries an umbrella he never opens. The good women of the quarter say he is an innocent, and the children follow him and laugh."],["It is a terrible thing to be happy, Hugo begins: in possessing happiness one forgets duty. Yet the reader would be wrong to blame Marius. He put no questions to M. Fauchelevent before the marriage and has been afraid to put any to Jean Valjean since; he regrets the concession he made to despair, and has confined himself to gradually estranging Valjean from the house and effacing him from Cosette's mind, always placing himself between the two, so that the effacement became an eclipse. He believes he has serious reasons, some the reader has seen and others yet to be shown.","One of them is new: in a case he argued, Marius chanced to meet a former employee of the Laffitte bank and, without seeking it, acquired mysterious information which, out of respect for the secret he promised to keep and for Valjean's perilous position, he has not been able to probe. He believes he now has a grave duty, to restore the six hundred thousand francs to someone he is discreetly seeking; meanwhile he does not touch the money.","Cosette has been told none of these secrets, but it would be harsh to condemn her either. An all-powerful magnetism makes her do instinctively what Marius wishes; sensing his will towards 'Monsieur Jean', she conforms, and what is shadowed in his mind grows overcast in hers. Her forgetting is superficial, heedlessness rather than ingratitude; at bottom she is sincerely attached to the man she so long called father, but she loves her husband more. When she wonders aloud about him, Marius reminds her that he said he was going on a journey; two or three times she sends Nicolette to the Rue de l'Homme Armé, and Valjean has the answer 'no' returned. The couple themselves have been away, to Vernon, where Marius took Cosette to his father's grave. Marius gradually wins Cosette away from Jean Valjean, and Cosette allows it. Hugo closes on the 'ingratitude of nature': the young turn towards the light and the old towards the shadows, branches growing away from the trunk without breaking from it; let us not blame these poor children."],["One day Jean Valjean goes down his staircase, takes three steps into the street, and sits on the stone post where Gavroche found him meditating on the night of the 5th to 6th of June; after a few moments he goes back up. It is the last swing of the pendulum. The next day he does not leave his apartment; the day after, he does not leave his bed. His portress, who cooks his scanty meals of cabbage or potatoes with bacon, finds the plate untouched; he protests that the water jug is empty, and she retorts that drinking without eating is called fever. He takes her hand and promises in his kind voice to eat the potatoes. He sees no other human being; he is in one of those Paris streets through which no one passes and houses to which no one comes. While he still went out he bought a little copper crucifix for a few sous and hung it opposite his bed.","A week passes and he has not taken a step in his room. The portress tells her husband the good man upstairs is not eating and will not last long; she is sure his daughter has made a bad marriage. The porter pronounces, with marital sovereignty, that if the man has a doctor he will die and if he has none he will die. Scraping grass from her pavement with an old knife, she grumbles that it is a shame, such a neat old man, white as a chicken. She spots the doctor of the quarter at the end of the street and sends him up; the door is always unlocked.","The doctor sees Jean Valjean and talks with him. Coming down, he tells the portress that her sick man is very ill indeed. What is wrong with him? 'Everything and nothing. He is a man who, to all appearances, has lost some person who is dear to him. People die of that.' He told the doctor he was in good health. The doctor will come again, 'but someone else besides must come.'"],["One evening Jean Valjean can barely raise himself on his elbow, feels for his pulse and cannot find it, and knows he is weaker than he has ever been. Under some supreme preoccupation he forces himself up and dresses in the old workman's clothes he has returned to since he stopped going out, sweating and pausing at every movement. Since living alone he has moved his bed into the antechamber to inhabit the empty flat as little as possible. He opens the valise, spreads Cosette's little outfit on the bed, puts two wax candles into the Bishop's candlesticks on the chimneypiece and lights them, though it is still broad summer daylight, as candles are lit by day in rooms where there is a corpse.","He drops into the chair before the mirror in which he once read Cosette's reversed writing on the blotter, and does not recognise himself: he is eighty, and this one year has counted for thirty; the mark on his brow is not age but death. His sorrow has coagulated into a clot of despair. By night he drags a table and the armchair to the fireside, sets out pen, ink and paper, faints, tips the jug to his mouth because he cannot lift it, moistens the dried ink with water, and writes with the back of a curled pen, gazing for hours at the little black gown.","At last he writes: 'Cosette, I bless thee.' Her husband was right to make him understand he should go, though there is a little error in what he believed; he is excellent, and she must love him well after her father is dead. Then, so that she will know the money is really hers, he sets down the trade: white jet from Norway, black jet from England, black glass jewellery from Germany; the little anvil and spirit-lamp; the wax of resin and lampblack at four livres a pound that he learned to make from shellac and turpentine for thirty sous; buckles of violet glass fixed to black iron; Spain, the country of jet, buying a great deal. The pen falls from his fingers and he is seized by a sob from the depths of his being: all is over; she was a smile that passed over him; one minute, one instant, to hear her voice and touch her dress and then die; it is nothing to die, what is frightful is to die without seeing her. At that moment there is a knock at the door."],["That same evening Basque brings Marius a coarse letter whose tobacco smell and handwriting bring the Jondrette garret before him at once. Signed 'Thénard', it offers, for a consideration, to drive from his honourable family an individual who has no right there. Marius pockets some banknotes and has the man shown in: a stranger in green spectacles, plastered grey hair, a thick nose and a threadbare black suit too big for him, which Hugo identifies, in a digression on 'the Changer' of the Rue Beautreillis who rents respectable costumes to rogues at thirty sous a day, as the 'statesman, ex-ambassador' outfit. The visitor claims acquaintance with princesses and Chateaubriand, describes a fortified village called la Joya near Panama where gold is found, and says he needs money to emigrate there with his wife and daughter. He then offers, gratis, the news that Marius has a thief and assassin in his house named Jean Valjean, an ex-convict. 'I know it,' says Marius to each point, and when the man offers the secret of Madame la Baronne's fortune for twenty thousand francs, then ten, then twenty francs, Marius names him: Thénardier, also Jondrette, Fabantou, Genflot, Don Alvarès and Mistress Balizard, the innkeeper of Montfermeil. He throws him five hundred francs. Thénardier laughs, pulls off the spectacles and the quills from his nostrils, straightens his back and admits it. He has never seen Marius before; through Azelma's pursuit of the wedding party on Shrove Tuesday and his own inductions he has learned who the man in the sewer was and that the Baroness is Cosette, though he does not know exactly who Cosette is and thinks better of trading on Fantine's story.","Marius, glad at last to be able to pay his father's debt and to probe the origin of Cosette's fortune, tells Thénardier what he himself knows: Jean Valjean is a thief because he robbed and ruined a wealthy manufacturer, M. Madeleine of the Pas-de-Calais, extracting over half a million from Laffitte by a forged signature (Marius has this from the cashier himself), and an assassin because he shot the police agent Javert, as Marius saw at the barricade. 'Monsieur le Baron, we are on the wrong track,' says Thénardier, and gives two reasons: Jean Valjean did not rob M. Madeleine because Jean Valjean was M. Madeleine, and he did not kill Javert because Javert killed Javert. He produces two yellowed newspapers from a tobacco-soaked envelope: a Drapeau Blanc of the 25th of July 1823 establishing the identity of Madeleine and Valjean, and a Moniteur of the 15th of June 1832 announcing Javert's suicide and adding, from Javert's own report to the prefect, that he owed his life at the barricade to an insurgent who fired into the air instead of blowing out his brains. Marius cannot doubt printed proof; the cashier's information was false. He cries out with joy that the wretch is an admirable man, Madeleine, the providence of a whole countryside, Javert's saviour, a hero, a saint.","'He's not a saint,' says Thénardier, 'he's an assassin and a robber,' and he tells his real story. On the 6th of June 1832 a man who had made the Grand Sewer his hiding-place and held a key to it saw a bent figure approaching the outlet grating with a corpse on his back: an ex-convict who had crossed a frightful quagmire rather than leave the body where the sewermen would find it. The convict demanded the key; the other, parleying, tore a strip from the dead man's coat as evidence, let him out, and ran. The man with the corpse was Jean Valjean; the man with the key is Thénardier himself; and he holds up the strip of torn black cloth, stained dark. Marius, pale and breathless, fumbles in a cupboard without taking his eyes from the rag. As Thénardier concludes that the murdered young man was some rich stranger lured into a trap, Marius cries, 'The young man was myself, and here is the coat!', flings down his bloodstained coat, and fits the strip exactly to the tear.","Thénardier is petrified. Marius, trembling and radiant, thrusts a fistful of notes at him: he came to accuse the man and has justified him, he is himself the thief and the assassin, Marius saw him in the lair on the Rue de l'Hôpital and could send him to the galleys; here is a thousand francs; Waterloo protects him. ('Of a General,' Thénardier corrects, when Marius says he saved a colonel.) Marius adds three thousand more and promises twenty thousand on the day he leaves for America with his daughter, 'for your wife is dead, you abominable liar'. Hugo finishes with the man: two days later Thénardier sails under a false name with Azelma and a draft for twenty thousand francs on New York, and with Marius's money sets up in America as a slave-dealer. Marius rushes to the garden crying for Cosette, a carriage and her shawl: 'It was he who saved my life!' In the cab to No. 7 Rue de l'Homme Armé he pours it out: the letter sent by Gavroche must have reached him; he went to the barricade to save Marius, saved Javert too, carried him through the sewer and the quagmire to give him to Cosette; 'Thy father, Cosette, thy father more than ever'; they will bring him back whether he is willing or not. Cosette understands not a word and says, 'You are right.'"],["At the knock Valjean feebly says 'Come in', and Cosette rushes into the room while Marius stays leaning on the door-jamb. Valjean sits erect with trembling outstretched arms and an immense joy in his eyes; Cosette falls on his breast crying 'Father!' and he stammers, 'It is thou! Thou dost pardon me then!' Marius, holding back tears, murmurs 'My father!', and Valjean asks his pardon too and says, 'Thanks.' Cosette tosses off her shawl and hat, sits on his knees, parts his white hair and kisses his brow. He marvels at how stupid he was: at the very moment they were climbing the stairs he was telling himself he would never see her again; one reckons without the good God, who sends an angel to a good man who needs one. He admits he needed to see Cosette a little now and then, a heart needs a bone to gnaw, but he knew he was in the way. Cosette scolds him for his long 'journey' and his cold hands.","When Valjean again asks Marius's pardon, Marius bursts out to Cosette: this man saved his life and gave her to him and then sacrificed himself, and he, the ingrate, the pitiless one, is thanked by him; Cosette, that man is an angel. 'Hush, why tell all that?' Why, Marius demands, did he not say he was Monsieur Madeleine, that he saved Javert, that Marius owed him his life? Because, Valjean answers, he thought as Marius did, that he ought to go away; had Marius known about the sewer he would have made him stay. Marius declares they will carry him off, that he is Cosette's father and his, and he shall not pass another day in this dreadful house. 'Tomorrow I shall not be here, but I shall not be with you.' Cosette threatens to run away with him by force and chatters about his waiting chamber, the azaleas, her strawberries, the robin eaten by a cat, and the end of 'Madame' and 'Monsieur Jean' now that everyone says 'thou'. He hears the music of her voice rather than the words, murmurs that the proof God is good is that she is here, says how charming it would be to live together, and adds gently, 'It is a pity.' Then: 'I am going to die presently.' Cosette cries that she forbids it; he answers, 'Oh yes, forbid me to die. Who knows? Perhaps I shall obey'; Marius kneels and begs his forgiveness; Valjean says God has thought as they all have and does not change his mind, death is a good arrangement, and he drank the whole jug of water last night.","The doctor enters; Valjean greets him with 'Good day, and farewell', and the doctor answers Marius's one-word question with a glance and, feeling the pulse, whispers 'Too late'. 'It is nothing to die; it is dreadful not to live,' Valjean says. In a last access of strength he walks firmly to the wall, takes down the little copper crucifix and lays it on the table: 'Behold the great martyr.' Then he sinks, rallies, kisses a fold of Cosette's sleeve, and tells them what pained him most: that Marius would not touch the money. It is honestly Cosette's; he explains the jet and the iron slides for bracelets and refers them to the paper he was writing. When the portress asks whether he wants a priest he answers, 'I have had one,' pointing above his head; Hugo thinks it probable the Bishop was present at this death.","His breath failing, he calls them close for his last words: he loves them dearly, Cosette must weep for him a little but not too much, the buckles without tongues paid best of all so the six hundred thousand francs are honest money and she may have a carriage, a box at the theatre and fine dresses; she will find his letter; he leaves her the two candlesticks, silver but to him gold and diamonds, and does not know whether the one who gave them is pleased with him on high. He is to be buried in the first plot of earth they find, under a stone with no name on it. He asks Marius's pardon for not always having loved him; there is a five-hundred-franc note in the chest of drawers for the poor. He points to the little gown on the bed and remembers Montfermeil: the forest, the handle of the water-bucket, her cold red hand, the doll Catherine, the straws floated on the gutters, the battledore and shuttlecock, cherries in her ears. The Thénardiers were wicked and she must forgive them. 'Cosette, the moment has come to tell thee the name of thy mother. She was called Fantine. Kneel whenever thou utterest it. She suffered much. She loved thee dearly.' Love each other well and always, there is nothing else in the world. He confesses he went as far as the corner of the street like a madman, once without his hat. 'I die happy. Give me your dear and well-beloved heads, so that I may lay my hands upon them.' Cosette and Marius kneel under his hands, which no longer move. He has fallen back, lit by the candles, his white face turned to heaven. He is dead. The night is starless; no doubt some immense angel stands in the gloom with wings outspread, awaiting that soul."],["In the cemetery of Père-Lachaise, near the common grave and far from the fashionable tombs, in a deserted corner by an old wall, under a great yew climbed by wild convolvulus, among dandelions and moss, lies a stone. Time, damp and lichen have worked on it as on any other; the water greens it and the air blackens it. It is near no path, the grass is high and wet, lizards come when there is sun, weeds quiver all around, and in spring linnets sing in the trees.","The stone is perfectly plain, cut only to be long enough and narrow enough to cover a man. No name is to be read on it. Only, many years ago, a hand pencilled four lines of French verse upon it, which rain and dust have gradually made illegible and which are probably effaced today. They say that he sleeps; that though fate was strange to him, he lived; that he died when he no longer had his angel; and that the thing came about simply of itself, as night falls when the day departs."]]`);export{e as default};